  "summary": "After 2000, wave 1. The archive as object. A new Research-field entry archival_turn (period_4) gathers the readership that the History PhD citation graph identifies as the broadest post-2000 framework without an opposing side: the archive as a site of power (Trouillot, Mbembe, Schwartz and Cook), Stoler's reading along the grain and the Dutch reviewers' doubts, the archive's allure, fever and dust (Farge, Derrida, Steedman), absence and critical fabulation (Hartman, Fuentes), archive stories and the ethnography of archives (Burton, Dirks, Zeitlyn), and the archivists' turn in Canada and South Africa, in six strands, a roster of 16, and a chronology of 27 events from 1989 to 2024. Ann Laura Stoler (promoted from a people record; 7 new edges, 16 events) and Michel-Rolph Trouillot (new; 8 edges, 26 events) become person entries in the historians layer. edge_386 and edge_417 are re-sourced from post to ann_laura_stoler; derrida_archive_primary loses its mirror-host URL. Settler colonial studies after 2000, and Patrick Wolfe (period-4 wave 1, Agent B). The entry settler_colonialism is reviewed through 2024: its description, scope note and figures are rewritten from texts read in full (Wolfe 2006, Veracini 2011 and 2016, Kauanui 2016, Englert 2020, Goeman 2016; Tuck and Yang 2012 and Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014 in most pages; Macoun and Strakosch 2013 in the authors' version); six strands are corrected and three added (the Wolfe–Veracini structure debate; Canadian history beside and under the frame, 2009–2020; the field's institutions); its chronology grows from 6 to 48 events (2008–2022) and its coverage to 2024; thirteen roster rows are amended and eleven added. Patrick Wolfe's entry is backfilled: description, scope note and figures from the 2006 essay read in full, a chronology of 24 events (1994–2022), six new incident edges (indigenous, racial formation, Holocaust historiography, postcolonial critique, Indigenous–newcomer relations, Harris's