    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "focus",
      "old": "Wolfe's account treats invasion and elimination as a continuing structure. Kauanui's abstract attests the formulation and its critique; Wolfe's own pages are not checked here.",
      "new": "Wolfe's programme, now read in his own pages. Settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it 'is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'the primary motive for elimination is not race … but access to territory. Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; 'settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event'; elimination has a 'positive' aspect (allotment, citizenship, child abduction, assimilation) that persists after the frontier, so that the form is 'relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' names its convergence with genocide without 'hierarchy among victims' (2006). The formula goes back to the 1999 book (p. 2) and the 1994 essay (p. 96, per his note 7). Kauanui: the phrase 'often seems to stand-in for a serious engagement of his theory'.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: the 2006 essay is read in full; the earlier caveat is withdrawn.",
      "strand": "structure_and_elimination"
    },
    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "title",
      "old": "Settler colonial theory and field formation",
      "new": "Veracini: the field, the journal and the self-superseding structure",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.",
      "strand": "theory_and_field"
    },
    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "focus",
      "old": "Veracini disentangles settler colonial from colonial phenomena and introduces a dedicated journal (2011); a 2013 essay traces the concept's earlier career. Field-making, not a claim of single origin.",
      "new": "Veracini founded Settler Colonial Studies (2011; open access, volunteer-run, on an ARC grant; to Taylor & Francis in 2013) 'to contribute to the consolidation of a new scholarly field'. Colonialism says 'you, work for me'; settler colonialism says 'you, go away'; the two 'should be understood in their dialectical relation'. Where Wolfe's structure endures, Veracini's 'persistently pursues a specific end point': 'settler colonialism, by contrast, extinguishes itself', is 'least visible in the settler cities', and 'ends with an indigenous ultimate permanence'; 'decolonisation is not settler decolonisation', and the politics of recognition 'cannot be considered a genuinely decolonising move' (2011). His history of the concept (2013) finds three earlier careers of the term and a consolidation 'from approximately the mid 1990s', with 'both the Canadian and US historiographies … late in adopting' it. Englert calls him Wolfe's 'self-styled intellectual successor'; Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel find in his history 'Indigenous studies and Indigenous resistance … pretty much erased'.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Veracini 2011 read in full, 2013 in its author manuscript.",
      "strand": "theory_and_field"
    },
    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "title",
      "old": "Indigenous critiques of recognition, sovereignty and resurgence",
      "new": "The Indigenous reading: land, refusal, incommensurability",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.",
      "strand": "recognition_refusal_resurgence"
    },
    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "focus",
      "old": "Indigenous political theory read alongside the paradigm. These works have their own genealogies in Indigenous thought and are not a subset of settler-colonial studies. Open question: these may belong in Indigenous history instead.",
      "new": "Indigenous political theory that takes up Wolfe's formula and redirects the field. Tuck and Yang: settler colonialism's violence 'is not temporally contained in the arrival of the settler but is reasserted each day of occupation. This is why Patrick Wolfe (1999) emphasizes that settler colonialism is a structure and not an event'; 'decolonization brings about the repatriation of Indigenous land and life; it is not a metaphor'; the settler–native–slave triad; 'settler moves to innocence'; 'an ethic of incommensurability' against reconciliation, which 'is about rescuing settler normalcy' (2012). Simpson: 'refusal' as 'an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society', consent 'as a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession' (2017, abstract; the 2014 book is known through its publisher page). Coulthard: settler colonialism 'territorially acquisitive in perpetuity'; in Canada the means of elimination changed but 'the ends have always remained the same: to shore up continued access to Indigenous peoples' territories' (2014, p. 125, as quoted by Englert); a 'place-based' reworking of primitive accumulation (publisher). Byrd, L. B. Simpson and Moreton-Robinson remain at publisher-page level. These works have their own genealogies; in Canadian and US theses they are read with Wolfe (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23), and the user ruled (2026-10-12) that they may sit in more than one field.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Tuck and Yang read; Simpson and Coulthard in their own words at abstract or quoted level.",
      "strand": "recognition_refusal_resurgence"
    },
    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "title",
      "old": "Indigenous scholars on settler colonial studies",
      "new": "Indigenous studies and the field: who made it, whom it displaces",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.",
      "strand": "indigenous_scholars_critique"
    },
    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "focus",
      "old": "Whether the field recentres settlers and displaces Indigenous studies and Indigenous-authored histories.",
      "new": "Kauanui: Wolfe 'insisted … that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did', yet 'he tends to be most frequently cited as if he had'; 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies'; indigeneity is 'a category of analysis that is distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality'; Warrior counted ASA panels and feared the field had become 'an answer to the chronic need for more attention to … Native and Indigenous studies' (2016). Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel: the field 'can displace, overshadow, or even mask over Indigenous studies'; 'when settlers take up these questions, it's suddenly considered a legitimate field of study' (2014). Goeman answers from the same field: Wolfe 'critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner' and his work 'became a place to engage Indigenous studies concerns in relation to settler colonial studies' (2016). Konishi surveys Indigenous scholars who advocate, interrogate and challenge the field (2019, abstract).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Kauanui, Snelgrove et al. and Goeman read.",
      "strand": "indigenous_scholars_critique"
    },
