    {
      "node": "settler_colonialism",
      "field": "focus",
      "old": "Structural inevitability (Macoun and Strakosch), a teleology of elimination (Rowse), regional and periodic fit with other modes of colonialism (Greer), universal theory or English heritage (Shoemaker).",
      "new": "From inside the theory, Macoun and Strakosch: SCT 'posits a structural inevitability', 'can depict colonization as structurally inevitable', risks 'colonial fatalism' and 'does not account for Indigenous lives'; yet 'the fact that settler colonialism struggles to narrate its own ending does not mean that it cannot end' (2013). Rowse: a 'teleology of elimination' (2014, abstract). Greer: one mode among several, dominant in some periods and regions (2019). Shoemaker: universal theory or English heritage? (2019). Englert: 'the elimination of the native … does not accurately define the overall logic of settler colonialism'; settler colonies in Africa and South America 'were built on the exploitation of indigenous peoples'; accumulation by dispossession 'carried out by all classes of settlers, including by workers' (2020, with Kelley and Speed 2017).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Macoun and Strakosch and Englert read.",
      "strand": "critiques_and_limits"
    },
