      "field": "works",
      "old": "Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999)",
      "new": "Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999); “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006); “After the Frontier” (2011)",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3.",
      "person_id": "patrick_wolfe"
    },
    {
      "node": "patrick_wolfe",
      "field": "description",
      "old": "Reads the history of anthropology through settler colonialism. Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999) studies virgin birth in anthropology and in Australian settler discourse, and Victorian anthropology on mother-right, totemism and E. B. Tylor. Later essays address land, labour and race (2001) and the elimination of the native (2006).",
      "new": "An Australian historian of settler colonialism, race and the history of anthropology (1949–2016). His first book read Victorian and Australian anthropology through 'the elimination of native societies': 'the colonizers come to stay—invasion is a structure not an event' (1999). The 2006 essay made the formula a programme: settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it is 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; after the frontier, elimination 'turned inwards' through allotment, blood quantum and assimilation, so the form stays 'relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' names its convergence with genocide without 'hierarchy among victims'. Race, he argued, 'is made in the targeting' (2001, 2006; Traces of History, 2016). Veracini called him the field's founder 'in a fit of absentmindedness'; Kauanui records that he 'insisted … that he did not create the field … that Native scholars did'. Critics find in his structure a 'colonial fatalism' (Macoun and Strakosch) and ask whether exploitation, not elimination, defines settler colonies (Englert).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7: description rewritten from the 2006 essay (read in full) and named readers."
    },
    {
      "node": "patrick_wolfe",
      "field": "representative_figures_and_works",
      "old": "Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016); Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology: The Politics and Poetics of an Ethnographic Event (1999). Later readings: “Land, Labor, and Difference: Elementary Structures of Race” (2001); “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006).",
      "new": "Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016); “Nation and MiscegeNation” (Social Analysis 36, 1994); Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology: The Politics and Poetics of an Ethnographic Event (1999); “Land, Labor, and Difference: Elementary Structures of Race” (AHR 2001); “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (JGR 2006); “Structure and Event” (in Moses, ed., 2008); “After the Frontier” (2011); “Purchase by Other Means” (2012); ed., “Recuperating Binarism” (2013); with David Lloyd, “Settler Colonial Logics and the Neoliberal Regime” (2015); Traces of History: Elementary Structures of Race (2016); ed., The Settler Complex (2016). Readers: Veracini (2011, 2016), Macoun and Strakosch (2013), Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel (2014), Kauanui (2016), Goeman (2016), Birch (2016), the American Quarterly forum (2017), Englert (2020), Russell (2022).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7."
    },
    {
      "node": "patrick_wolfe",
      "field": "scope_note",
      "old": "This entry presents the 1999 book. Its introduction (pp. 1–2, read) distinguishes settler from franchise colonies, holds that settler colonies 'were (are) premised on the elimination of native societies' and states that 'invasion is a structure not an event'; the rest of the book is checked at contents and catalogue level. The 2001 and 2006 essays are later readings. He did not originate the term, which has earlier usage (Veracini 2013), and he is not presented as the field's founder.",
      "new": "Read on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md): the 2006 essay in full (an Internet Archive capture of the journal's free-to-read page; page numbers from Kauanui's citations), the 1999 introduction pp. 1–2 (earlier), Veracini's obituary, Goeman's memorial, Kauanui's essay, Macoun and Strakosch, Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel, Englert, in full or in most pages. Unread: the 1994 essay (JSTOR), the 1999 chapters (contents and catalogue checked), the 2001 AHR essay beyond a sentence he quotes himself, the 2008 chapter, the Settler Colonial Studies essays, Traces of History (British Library contents note only), The Settler Complex, Birch's obituary (restricted file), the 2017 forum, Russell's chapter. Life dates and places from Wikidata Q109284663 (checked by QLever SPARQL); posts from the Victoria University collection page (Victoria University, University of Melbourne, La Trobe University; undated) and Veracini's obituary (fellowships at Harvard and Stanford; undated), so no post is drawn as an event; Kauanui's 'English scholar' is noted against Wikidata's Australian historian born in Yorkshire. He did not coin the term (Veracini 2013; Kauanui 2016, n. 7) and said he did not found the field; the entry presents a programme, not an origin. Canadian PhD figures are in the settler_colonialism scope_note, not here.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7."
    },
    {
      "node": "environment",
      "field": "description",
      "old": "Explains historical relations among people and the nonhuman world through ecological exchange, agriculture, property, commodity flows, conservation and cultural ideas of nature. These approaches disagree about causal emphasis. Cases from New England, the Great Plains, Mexico, India, colonial islands and South Africa connect ecological processes with power without supplying one universal model. Bullard’s environmental-justice inquiry and Rita and Jackie Huggins’s situated life histories broaden comparison toward unequal burdens, displacement and authority over historical accounts. Dust Bowl debates distinguish agricultural capitalism, technology and soils, federal intervention, regional scale and household survival; primary criticism and later social history complicate a single causal summary.",
      "new": "Explains historical relations among people and the nonhuman world through ecological exchange, agriculture, property, commodity flows, conservation and cultural ideas of nature; the approaches disagree about causal emphasis, and cases from New England, the Great Plains, Mexico, India, colonial islands and South Africa connect ecology with power without one model. Bullard and the Huggins life histories add unequal burdens and authority over accounts; the Dust Bowl debates set capitalism, technology, policy and survival against one another. After 1995 the field argued with itself and its neighbours: Cronon called wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'; Foreman answered that this 'Received Wilderness Idea is a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (2000); Spence (1999) and Jacoby (2001) took the removal of Indigenous people from 'uninhabited wilderness' into the archive. A merged journal (1996), McNeill's world synthesis (2000) and a Canadian network (NiCHE, 2004) widened it; by 2013 Sutter found nature and culture replaced by 'environmental hybridity' (editors' summary). Chakrabarty (2009) and the Anthropocene histories of 2016 set its questions at planetary scale.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1: the field after 1995."
    },
    {
      "node": "environment",
      "field": "representative_figures_and_works",
      "old": "Alfred Crosby; Donald Worster; Carolyn Merchant; William Cronon. ASEH founded 1977. Expanded contextual readings include Leopold’s precursor essays, Melville’s Mexican study, Gadgil and Guha’s Indian history, Grove’s colonial conservation and Carruthers’s South African park history.",
      "new": "Alfred Crosby; Donald Worster; Carolyn Merchant; William Cronon. ASEH founded 1977. Expanded contextual readings include Leopold’s precursor essays, Melville’s Mexican study, Gadgil and Guha’s Indian history, Grove’s colonial conservation and Carruthers’s South African park history. After 2000: J. R. McNeill, Something New Under the Sun (2000) and 'Observations' (2003); Spence, Dispossessing the Wilderness (1999); Jacoby, Crimes against Nature (2001); Sutter, 'The World with Us' (JAH 2013); Isenberg, ed., The Oxford Handbook of Environmental History (2014); in Canada, Wynn (series editor), MacEachern, Loo, Piper, Sandlos, Evenden, Colpitts, Parr; the Anthropocene: Chakrabarty (2009), McNeill and Engelke, Malm, Bonneuil and Fressoz (2016). Institutions: Environmental History (journal, 1996), NiCHE (2004).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1."
    },
    {
      "node": "environment",
      "field": "scope_note",
      "old": "Ecological exchange, political economy, and feminist critiques of nature overlap; institutional society history does not document every intellectual route. Crosby’s temperate-settler argument differs from Melville’s Mexican case; Gadgil and Guha’s resource conflicts differ from Grove’s conservation networks and Carruthers’s park politics. Cronon’s cultural account of wilderness does not deny nonhuman nature. The selection remains incomplete, especially in environmental justice and Indigenous scholarship. A bounded second-pass addition now compares Bullard and the Huggins collaboration; primary-excerpt, publisher-description and metadata limits remain distinct, and neither selection represents every community.",
      "new": "Ecological exchange, political economy, and feminist critiques of nature overlap; institutional society history does not document every intellectual route. Crosby’s temperate-settler argument differs from Melville’s Mexican case; Gadgil and Guha’s resource conflicts differ from Grove’s conservation networks and Carruthers’s park politics. Cronon’s cultural account of wilderness does not deny nonhuman nature. The selection remains incomplete, especially in environmental justice and Indigenous scholarship. A bounded second-pass addition now compares Bullard and the Huggins collaboration; primary-excerpt, publisher-description and metadata limits remain distinct, and neither selection represents every community. Extended to 2024 on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md). Read in full: Foreman's 'The Real Wilderness Idea' (2000), Cronon's CV, 'Storytelling' (2013, passages) and 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (2003), the NiCHE and UBC Press series pages, publisher pages and abstracts for the Canadian books and articles, two open reviews of Worster's Shrinking the Earth. Unread: the 1996 comments by Hays, Cohen and Dunlap and Cronon's response; Sutter's 2013 essay and its six responses (the editors' summary is quoted instead); McNeill 2000 and 2003 (the latter known from the earlier record); the 2005 'What's Next' forum including Linda Nash on agency; the bodies of every Canadian book; Malm, Moore, Bonneuil and Fressoz beyond descriptions. The History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23 dates the field's readership: 1.6% of Canada + US theses cited two or more of its authors in the 1990s, 5.7% in 2000–09, 9.5% in 2010–14, 11.3% in 2015–19, 9.9% in 2020–25; the network of works co-cited with Cronon, Worster, White, Crosby and Merchant peaked in Canada in 2010–14 (37 works) and halved by 2020–25 (17), its new members being Wolfe and Tuck and Yang; in the US it is stable (17–19 works) and its entrants are Indigenous, borderlands and slavery works. The field is read with its neighbours rather than renewed from within.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1: reading limits and the PhD citation figures."
    },
