    {
      "node": "environment",
      "field": "context",
      "old": "Writes the political history of conservation and environmentalism. Cronon (1993) says his two books 'speak just as powerfully to major trends in conservation and environmental politics' and draws from them a contrast between conservationists' 'economically efficient production' and environmentalists' 'ecologically responsible consumption'. Books unread here.",
      "new": "Writes the political history of conservation and environmentalism. Cronon (1993) says his two books 'speak just as powerfully to major trends in conservation and environmental politics' and draws from them a contrast between conservationists' 'economically efficient production' and environmentalists' 'ecologically responsible consumption'. Books unread here. He answered Cronon's wilderness essay in the first number of Environmental History (1996): 'Cronon's wilderness is a world of abstracted ideas … divorced from the values and ideas inherent in wilderness action' (as quoted by Foreman, 2000; the comment itself unread).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P3: Hays's 1996 comment, as quoted by Foreman; marked for re-verification.",
      "person_id": "samuel_p_hays"
    },
    {
      "node": "william_cronon",
      "field": "description",
      "old": "A historian of North American environments who treats ecological change as a two-way relation between people and the rest of nature, worked out through property, markets and ideas of nature. Changes in the Land (1983) assumes that 'the interactions of the two are dialectical' and finds in colonial New England not a choice between landscapes with and without people but 'two human ways of living, two ways of belonging to an ecosystem'. Nature’s Metropolis (1991) follows grain, lumber and meat between Chicago and its hinterland on the premise that 'city and country have a common history, so their stories are best told together'. 'A Place for Stories' (1992) asks environmental historians for 'stories about stories about nature', and 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995) calls wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'. Reviewers found the Chicago book distant from farmers and townspeople, or moralising; he replied that he saw 'no pure heroes or pure villains'.",
      "new": "A historian of North American environments who treats ecological change as a two-way relation between people and the rest of nature, worked out through property, markets and ideas of nature. Changes in the Land (1983) assumes that 'the interactions of the two are dialectical' and finds in colonial New England not a choice between landscapes with and without people but 'two human ways of living, two ways of belonging to an ecosystem'. Nature’s Metropolis (1991) follows grain, lumber and meat between Chicago and its hinterland on the premise that 'city and country have a common history, so their stories are best told together'. 'A Place for Stories' (1992) asks environmental historians for 'stories about stories about nature', and 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995) calls wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'. Reviewers found the Chicago book distant from farmers and townspeople, or moralising; he replied that he saw 'no pure heroes or pure villains'. After 1995 the essay drew its own debate: Hays found in it 'a world of abstracted ideas' (1996, as quoted by Foreman), and Foreman told a wilderness-science conference that the 'Received Wilderness Idea' of 'Callicott, Cronon and other postmodern deconstructionist scholars' was 'a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (2000). Cronon answered in practice, asking that the Apostle Islands be managed 'as a historical wilderness', 'a storied wilderness' (2003). He edited the Weyerhaeuser Environmental Books (1994–2014) and, as 'the first self-described environmental historian ever to serve as president of the AHA', told the profession that 'Our core business is resurrection: helping the dead past live again' (2013).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1/P6: the debate and the later career."
    },
    {
      "node": "william_cronon",
      "field": "scope_note",
      "old": "This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-13: chapter 1 of Changes in the Land in an unpaginated web transcription; 'A Place for Stories' (JAH 1992; pp. 1347–1351 and 1369–1376 read) and 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993; OCR of the author’s scan, journal pagination); 'Kennecott Journey' (1992); passages of 'The Trouble with Wilderness' in the author’s reflow; and the 1992 Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response read in full. Nature’s Metropolis itself is unread: its words appear here only as forum contributors quote them with page numbers. 'Modes of Prophecy and Production', his 1990 response to Worster in the JAH round table, is paywalled and unread. He is not presented as a founder. He places environmental history among the 'new' histories 'born or reenergized in the wake of the 1960s' (1993, p. 2), names Nash, Hays, Worster, Merchant, Tarr and Pyne as practitioners (pp. 2–3), footnotes lessons to Crosby, Richard White, McEvoy and others (pp. 8–10), and echoes Leopold (n. 11). On Mahoney’s reading (1992, p. 492), Nature’s Metropolis demonstrates 'this fundamental urban historical premise' rather than inventing it. His approaches were contested in period: Danbom (1992, p. 503) finds 'environmental moralism'; Mahoney (p. 491) that urban and economic history 'seem only to serve the interests of environmental history'; Sessions (1996) that the wilderness essay underweights wild habitat. His 2003 afterword, McNeill (2003) and Canadian uses after 2000 are later readings.",
      "new": "This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-13: chapter 1 of Changes in the Land in an unpaginated web transcription; 'A Place for Stories' (JAH 1992; pp. 1347–1351 and 1369–1376 read) and 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993; OCR of the author’s scan, journal pagination); 'Kennecott Journey' (1992); passages of 'The Trouble with Wilderness' in the author’s reflow; and the 1992 Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response read in full. Nature’s Metropolis itself is unread: its words appear here only as forum contributors quote them with page numbers. 'Modes of Prophecy and Production', his 1990 response to Worster in the JAH round table, is paywalled and unread. He is not presented as a founder. He places environmental history among the 'new' histories 'born or reenergized in the wake of the 1960s' (1993, p. 2), names Nash, Hays, Worster, Merchant, Tarr and Pyne as practitioners (pp. 2–3), footnotes lessons to Crosby, Richard White, McEvoy and others (pp. 8–10), and echoes Leopold (n. 11). On Mahoney’s reading (1992, p. 492), Nature’s Metropolis demonstrates 'this fundamental urban historical premise' rather than inventing it. His approaches were contested in period: Danbom (1992, p. 503) finds 'environmental moralism'; Mahoney (p. 491) that urban and economic history 'seem only to serve the interests of environmental history'; Sessions (1996) that the wilderness essay underweights wild habitat. His 2003 afterword, McNeill (2003) and Canadian uses after 2000 are later readings. Extended on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): his CV read in full; 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (2003) read in full in OCR; 'Storytelling' (2013) read in passages from the AHA's open text; Foreman's 'The Real Wilderness Idea' (2000) read in full. Unread: his 1996 response to Hays, Cohen and Dunlap (paywalled), whose side of the second round is therefore missing; the 2012 Perspectives columns (titles only); Callicott and Nelson's anthology (publisher description). Hays's 1996 sentence is known only as Foreman quotes it. Wikidata Q8007305 confirmed by QLever.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): reading record."
    },
    {
      "node": "william_cronon",
      "field": "representative_figures_and_works",
      "old": "William Cronon (b. 1954); Changes in the Land: Indians, Colonists, and the Ecology of New England (1983); “Modes of Prophecy and Production: Placing Nature in History” (JAH 1990); Nature’s Metropolis: Chicago and the Great West (1991); “Kennecott Journey: The Paths out of Town” (1992); “A Place for Stories: Nature, History, and Narrative” (JAH 1992); “The Uses of Environmental History” (1993); “The Trouble with Wilderness” (1995). Readings: the Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response (1992); Sessions’s reply on wilderness (1996); his own afterword to Changes in the Land (2003).",
      "new": "William Cronon (b. 1954); Changes in the Land: Indians, Colonists, and the Ecology of New England (1983); “Modes of Prophecy and Production: Placing Nature in History” (JAH 1990); Nature’s Metropolis: Chicago and the Great West (1991); “Kennecott Journey: The Paths out of Town” (1992); “A Place for Stories: Nature, History, and Narrative” (JAH 1992); “The Uses of Environmental History” (1993); “The Trouble with Wilderness” (1995). Readings: the Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response (1992); Sessions’s reply on wilderness (1996); his own afterword to Changes in the Land (2003). Later: 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (Orion, 2003); 'Storytelling' (AHR, 2013); Weyerhaeuser Environmental Books, series editor 1994–2014. Readers: Hays (1996, as quoted by Foreman); Callicott and Nelson, eds. (1998); Foreman (2000).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md)."
    },
    {
      "node": "donald_worster",
      "field": "description",
      "old": "A historian of the American Great Plains and arid West who set out a three-level model of environmental history. In 'Doing Environmental History' (1988) he wrote that 'there are three levels on which the new history proceeds': 'understanding nature itself', 'the socioeconomic realm as it interacts with the environment', and 'the purely mental or intellectual', which together 'constitute a single dynamic inquiry'. His 1990 Journal of American History essay turned to 'that middle level, the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena', chiefly the capitalist transformation of agriculture, which Dust Bowl (1979) had called 'the decisive factor in this nation's use of nature'; Rivers of Empire (1985) described the West as 'a modern hydraulic society'. Merchant's 1990 response called the levels 'a significant framework for research and writing in this emerging field' but found that the essay 'lacks a gender analysis'. Cronon (1992) read Dust Bowl as a story whose 'chief agent' is capitalism and whose plot 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy'.",
      "new": "A historian of the American Great Plains and arid West who set out a three-level model of environmental history. In 'Doing Environmental History' (1988) he wrote that 'there are three levels on which the new history proceeds': 'understanding nature itself', 'the socioeconomic realm as it interacts with the environment', and 'the purely mental or intellectual', which together 'constitute a single dynamic inquiry'. His 1990 Journal of American History essay turned to 'that middle level, the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena', chiefly the capitalist transformation of agriculture, which Dust Bowl (1979) had called 'the decisive factor in this nation's use of nature'; Rivers of Empire (1985) described the West as 'a modern hydraulic society'. Merchant's 1990 response called the levels 'a significant framework for research and writing in this emerging field' but found that the essay 'lacks a gender analysis'. Cronon (1992) read Dust Bowl as a story whose 'chief agent' is capitalism and whose plot 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy'. After the round table he turned to lives and to limits: biographies of John Wesley Powell (2001) and John Muir (2008; the Muir essay of 2005), and Shrinking the Earth (2016), which Collomb read as a history of the 'Second Earth' of 1500 that gave settlers 'the false impression that there was no limit', by 'the greatest US environmental historian' — while finding its 'plea for an earth ethic almost rings hollow'. He held the Hall chair at Kansas (1989–2012) and received the AHA's Award for Scholarly Distinction (2012).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P6/P7: the later career."
    },
    {
      "node": "donald_worster",
      "field": "scope_note",
      "old": "This entry presents the three-level model in Worster's 1988 wording, checked in an abridged reprint and by full-text search of the 1988 edition, and the 1990 essay in his own 1993 reprint; the Journal of American History pages of 1990 were not reachable and are given only where another writer cites them (Isenberg 2013). Dust Bowl and Rivers of Empire are quoted at the pages given by Cronon (1992) and Hennessey (1987), with the wording confirmed by search of the original editions. He is not presented as the founder of the field or the sole author of the scheme: he grounds the idea in Aldo Leopold's call for 'an ecological interpretation of history', and Arthur McEvoy's ecology, production and cognition triad appeared in the same 1988 volume. The scheme was contested at once: Merchant (1990) added gender and 'a fourth analytical level, that of reproduction', Cronon (1992) treated Dust Bowl as a declensionist narrative, and McDean (1986) criticised its generalisation across the Plains. The 1990 objections of White and Cronon in the round table are known here only through Isenberg (2013), a later reading.",
      "new": "This entry presents the three-level model in Worster's 1988 wording, checked in an abridged reprint and by full-text search of the 1988 edition, and the 1990 essay in his own 1993 reprint; the Journal of American History pages of 1990 were not reachable and are given only where another writer cites them (Isenberg 2013). Dust Bowl and Rivers of Empire are quoted at the pages given by Cronon (1992) and Hennessey (1987), with the wording confirmed by search of the original editions. He is not presented as the founder of the field or the sole author of the scheme: he grounds the idea in Aldo Leopold's call for 'an ecological interpretation of history', and Arthur McEvoy's ecology, production and cognition triad appeared in the same 1988 volume. The scheme was contested at once: Merchant (1990) added gender and 'a fourth analytical level, that of reproduction', Cronon (1992) treated Dust Bowl as a declensionist narrative, and McDean (1986) criticised its generalisation across the Plains. The 1990 objections of White and Cronon in the round table are known here only through Isenberg (2013), a later reading. Extended on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): the Oxford record of The Wealth of Nature (contents; the opening paragraph of 'Transformations' as its abstract), the Origins author page, Columbia's Bancroft list and the AHA award list read; Collomb (2017) and Gratale (2017) on Shrinking the Earth read in full. The biographies of Powell and Muir and 'John Muir and the Modern Passion for Nature' (2005) are known by metadata only, so no edge rests on them; the retirement year (2012) and the Renmin appointment rest on a search snippet and the Origins page; the ASEH presidency has no years found. The Internet Archive routes in the older records for Nature's Economy, Rivers of Empire and The Wealth of Nature are not reused; those records now point to publisher pages, their earlier reading notes kept. Wikidata Q1240502 confirmed by QLever.",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): reading record."
    },
    {
      "node": "donald_worster",
      "field": "representative_figures_and_works",
      "old": "Donald Worster (b. 1941); Nature's Economy: The Roots of Ecology (1977); Dust Bowl: The Southern Plains in the 1930s (1979); Rivers of Empire: Water, Aridity, and the Growth of the American West (1985); 'Appendix: Doing Environmental History', in The Ends of the Earth (1988); 'Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History', Journal of American History (1990), reprinted in The Wealth of Nature (1993). Readings: Merchant's round-table response (1990); Cronon, 'A Place for Stories' (1992); Hennessey's review of Rivers of Empire (1987); McDean (1986).",
      "new": "Donald Worster (b. 1941); Nature's Economy: The Roots of Ecology (1977); Dust Bowl: The Southern Plains in the 1930s (1979); Rivers of Empire: Water, Aridity, and the Growth of the American West (1985); 'Appendix: Doing Environmental History', in The Ends of the Earth (1988); 'Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History', Journal of American History (1990), reprinted in The Wealth of Nature (1993). Readings: Merchant's round-table response (1990); Cronon, 'A Place for Stories' (1992); Hennessey's review of Rivers of Empire (1987); McDean (1986). Later: A River Running West (2001); 'John Muir and the Modern Passion for Nature' (2005); A Passion for Nature (2008); Shrinking the Earth (2016). Readers: Collomb (2017); Gratale (2017).",
      "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md)."
    }
