ed by Gouda); 'sexual control was both an instrumental image for the body politic … and itself fundamental to how racial policies were secured' (2002, p. 78, as quoted by Ottevaere)","map_label":"Sex, race and colonial rule, 1995–2002","source_ids":["stoler_desire","p4a_gouda_bki_2009","p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004"],"evidence_note":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.9 2) re-sourced this arrow from post to the new person entry ann_laura_stoler, keeping its id and kind. Earlier note retained: the 1995 project both uses and questions Foucault; this contribution is the specific colonial-historical intervention described by the publisher, not the origin of queer history or proof that all queer historians adopted it. Added: Gouda 2009, p. 553 (read in full), quoting the 1995 book without a page; Ottevaere 2004 (read in full), quoting Carnal Knowledge, p. 78.","classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"queer/colonial"},"edge_892":{"source":"patrick_wolfe","target":"settler_colonialism","type":"connection","directed":true,"relationship_kind":"contribution","relationship":"Settler colonialism as 'a structure not an event': settler colonizers 'come to stay'; the form 'destroys to replace', is 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal', has territory as its 'irreducible element' and persists after the frontier as 'structural genocide' (1994–2006)","map_label":"The logic of elimination, 1999–2006","source_ids":["screq_wolfe_1999","cpl129_wolfe_1999_intro","cpl129_wolfe_1999_contents","cpl129_wolfe_1999_catalogue","screq_wolfe_2006","p4b_wolfe_1994","screq_kauanui_2016","screq_veracini_2013","p4b_veracini_2013_deposit"],"evidence_note":"Revision of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_01): the 2006 essay, read in full in the Internet Archive capture of the journal's free-to-read page (screq_wolfe_2006 addendum), joins the 1999 introduction (pp. 1–2, read earlier: settler colonies 'were (are) premised on the elimination of native societies'; 'invasion is a structure not an event'; contents and catalogue checked). Wolfe 2006, in his words: 'Settler colonialism is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal' (p. 387, pages per Kauanui 2016); 'Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; 'settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event' (p. 388; his note 7 refers the formula to the 1999 book, p. 2, and to 'Nation and MiscegeNation', 1994, p. 96); 'Settler colonialism destroys to replace'; 'elimination refers to more than the summary liquidation of Indigenous people, though it includes that' (p. 390); 'settler colonialism is relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' (p. 403). A contribution, not a founding: earlier settler-society studies (1983, 1995) and earlier usage (Veracini 2013; Kauanui 2016, note 7: Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973) precede it, and Kauanui records that Wolfe 'insisted … that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did'. The earlier note's Canadian PhD counts are moved to the entry's scope_note (user rule, 2026-10-23); bibliographies record consultation, not influence.","classification_basis":"editorial_review"},"edge_893":{"source":"patrick_wolfe","target":"anthropology","type":"connection","directed":true,"relationship_kind":"contribution","relationship":"A history of Victorian and Australian anthropology in its settler-colonial setting","map_label":"Anthropology's settler-colonial setting","source_ids":["screq_wolfe_1999","cpl129_wolfe_1999_intro","cpl129_wolfe_1999_contents","cpl129_wolfe_1999_catalogue","p4b_veracini_wolfe_obituary_2016","p4b_goeman_2016"],"evidence_note":"In-period evidence (1999). The contents page lists chapters on mother-right ('Sex and Property in Victorian Anthropology'), totemism ('Victorian Anthropology's Eternal Dichotomy') and E. B. Tylor ('Survival in a Paradigm Shift: E.B. Tylor and the Problem of the Text'), alongside virgin birth in anthropology and in Australian settler discourse. Catalogue subjects: Anthropology -- Australia -- History; Racism in anthropology -- Australia. Introduction p. 1 read: the book is 'a history not so much of anthropology itself as of its ideological entanglements'. The chapters' stance toward these anthropologists is not read here. If the introduction confirms a critical argument, a critique edge to anthropology/evolution may be the better reading. A historian's account of the discipline, not anthropological fieldwork. Addendum 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_02): Veracini's obituary (read in full) reports that the 1999 book 'was ostensibly a history of Australian anthropology', that 'settler colonialism' was 'only added at the very end and at the request of the publisher', and that 'to explain the evolution of anthropology Patrick defined settler colonialism as distinct from colonialism'; Goeman (read in full) records Audra Simpson's memory of him as a historian 'of anthropology itself, and its relationship to politics'. The chapters remain unread, so the critique/contribution question stays open.","classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"anthropology/colonial_knowledge"},"edge_882":{"source":"post","target":"settler_colonialism","type":"critique","directed":true,"relationship_kind":"critique","relationship":"Postcolonial studies 'failed to detect the settler colonial \"situation\" in its specific operation' (Veracini 2011); 'relying solely on postcolonial literatures … will not help to envision the shape that decolonization must take in settler colonial contexts' (Tuck and Yang 2012); postcolonial theory 'has tended to obscure the ongoing hierarchies of settler states' (Macoun and Strakosch 2013)","map_label":"Against postcolonial studies' blind spot, 2011–13","source_ids":["screq_veracini_2011","p4b_veracini_2011_deposit","screq_tuck_yang_2012","p4b_macoun_strakosch_2013_qut","screq_hoxie_2008"],"evidence_note":"Revision of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_11): the arrow changes from an undirected comparison to a critique from the field toward the umbrella, in three read texts' own words. Veracini 2011, p. 5 (read in full): 'colonial, imperial and postcolonial studies have primarily focused on something else … these scholarly literatures have ultimately failed to detect the settler colonial \"situation\" in its specific operation'; p. 1: 'colonial and settler colonial phenomena be analytically disentangled'. Tuck and Yang 2012, p. 5 (read): 'relying solely on postcolonial literatures or theories of coloniality that ignore settler colonialism will not help to envision the shape that decolonization must take in settler colonial contexts'. Macoun and Strakosch 2013 (author version, read), introduction: 'For many decades, postcolonial theory has shaped global scholarship of colonialism, and this has tended to obscure the ongoing hierarchies of settler states'; 'postcolonial does not \"mean the same thing as post-settler colonial\"'. Counter-reading kept: Hoxie 2008 (abstract) presents settler colonialism as the most promising aspect of a postcolonial critique. The earlier note's Canadian PhD counts are moved to the entry's scope_note.","classification_basis":"editorial_review"},"edge_881":{"source":"indigenous","target":"settler_colonialism","type":"connection","directed":false,"relationship_kind":"comparison","relationship":"Indigenous historical authority and settler-colonial analysis","map_label":"Indigenous history and settler colonialism","source_ids":["screq_konishi_2019","screq_kauanui_2016","screq_snelgrove_dhamoon_corntassel_2014","p4b_goeman_2016","p4b_veracini_2013_deposit"],"evidence_note":"Konishi surveys Indigenous scholars who advocate, interrogate and challenge settler colonial studies (abstract). Revision of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_12) adds the read words on both sides. Kauanui 2016 (read in full): 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies'; Wolfe 'insisted … that Native scholars did' create the field; indigeneity 'a category of analysis that is distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality'. Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014, pp. 9–11 (read): the field 'can displace, overshadow, or even mask over Indigenous studies'; 'when settlers take up these questions, it's suddenly considered a legitimate field of study'. Goeman 2016 (read in full): Wolfe's work 'became a place to engage Indigenous studies concerns in relation to settler colonial studies that at times leaves out Indigenous epistemologies and ontologies'. Veracini 2013 (author manuscript, read): 'indigenous studies, and especially history became crucial disciplinary sites in the development of \"settler colonialism\" as a conceptual category'. Comparison only: Indigenous history is not a subset of the paradigm, and the paradigm did not originate there. The earlier note's Canadian PhD counts are moved to the entry's scope_note.","classification_basis":"editorial_review"},"edge_883":{"source":"anticolonial","target":"settler_colonialism","type":"connection","directed":false,"relationship_kind":"comparison","relationship":"Decolonization-era usage and later theory","map_label":"Anti-colonial usage of 'settler colonialism'","source_ids":["screq_veracini_2013","screq_mamdani_2001","p4b_veracini_2011_deposit","screq_tuck_yang_2012"],"evidence_note":"Veracini dates a usage, until the late 1970s, naming a diehard hyper-colonialism challenged by anti-colonial insurgencies. Mamdani revisits the first postcolonial generation's political economy (Rodney) to ask how settler and native became political identities. Comparison: no line of descent from liberation politics to the later field is asserted. Addendum 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_13): Veracini 2011, pp. 5–7 (read in full), 'Decolonisation is not settler decolonisation': 'arguing against colonialism is not the same thing as condemning settler colonialism'; 'if colonialism ends with the coloniser's departure … settler colonialism ends with an indigenous ultimate permanence'. Tuck and Yang 2012, p. 31 (read): 'Decolonization is not equivocal to other anti-colonial struggles. It is incommensurable.' Both keep the two decolonisations apart, which is why a comparison and not a descent is drawn.","classification_basis":"editorial_review"},"edge_112":{"id":"edge_112","source":"environment","target":"global","relationship":"Crosby and Melville connect introduced organisms with colonial expansion; Grove traces conservation through colonial networks; after 2000 McNeill's Something New Under the Sun (2000) and, with Engelke, The Great Acceleration (2016) write the twentieth century and the Anthropocene at world scale","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"source_ids":["global","cronon","crosby_ecological","melville_plague","grove_green","subrahmanyam_connected","p4c_mcneill_something_new_2000","p4c_mcneill_great_acceleration_2016"],"evidence_note":"Ecological change and cross-border material relations provide one route beyond national containers; examples require attention to scale. Crosby’s temperate regions, Melville’s Mexican case and Grove’s island networks operate at different scales. Their books provide resources for histories across borders; this does not prove that all global historians adopted them. Subrahmanyam738n10 explicitly cites Crosby and Grove, adding named primary reception for this route; it does not establish every proposed Melville reception. Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) (edge_112 revision): the Harvard University Press description of The Great Acceleration read ('The Earth has entered a new age—the Anthropocene'; 'human dependence on fossil fuels inaugurated the Anthropocene'); McNeill 2000 by metadata and prizes only.","classification_basis":"editorial_review","map_label":"Organisms, conquest, colonial networks and the Anthropocene, 1986–2016"},"edge_720":{"id":"edge_720","source":"environment","target":"urbanhistory","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"Environmental perspectives on city–hinterland connections, assessed in period by an urban historian as a contribution to urban history (1991–92); Castonguay and Evenden's Urban Rivers (2012) carry the city–river question across Europe and North America","evidence_note":"In-period evidence: the 1992 Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis (open PDF, text layer read). The urban historian Timothy Mahoney judges that the book 'makes significant theoretical and substantive contributions to both economic and urban history by placing urban and economic development in an environmental context', and that Cronon 'has, perhaps inadvertently, brought urbanization as a “process” forward from its current relegation as a kind of assumed stage set for monographs on local occurrences in cities' (p. 491). Of the premise that 'to really understand how and why cities developed, one must study how they organized their hinterlands', he writes: 'Cronon’s study of Chicago demonstrates the explanatory power of this fundamental urban historical premise' (p. 492). He also finds that in the book 'urban and economic history seem only to serve the interests of environmental history' (p. 491). Cronon’s response: 'One of my most important agendas in Nature’s Metropolis was to try to convince environmental historians that the city is among the most important historical loci of environmental change'; 'Surely such an argument can only strengthen the claims of urban history as a field, setting the process of urbanization in a broader context that is as important to urban historians as it is to environmental ones' (p. 520). The arrow rests on one book, one urban historian’s in-period assessment and the author’s reply; it does not claim that urban history as a field adopted environmental questions. Context only, not support for the claim: Cronon’s 1993 ASEH address names Joel Tarr’s 'many studies of pollution and waste streams' (p. 3, n. 6) and cites Melosi’s Garbage in the Cities (n. 24). cronon_urban_reception (2022) is a later reading. Cronon’s own contribution is the separate edge from his person entry (revision 1.131). Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) (edge_720 revision): the Pittsburgh page read (Mauch's blurb: 'the best environmental history of urban industrial rivers'); body unread.","source_ids":["pe131_cronon_nm_forum_1992","cronon_urban_reception","pe131_cronon_uses_1993","p4c_castonguay_evenden_urban_2012"],"map_label":"Environmental perspectives on city–hinterland connections, 1991–92","classification_basis":"editorial_review"},"edge_901":{"id":"edge_901","source":"william_cronon","target":"environment","relationship":"Ecological change as a two-way relation of people and nature, traced through property, markets and ideas of nature (1983–1995); and the field's institutions: ASEH president 1989–93, editor of the Weyerhaeuser Environmental Books 1994–2014 ('acquiring, editing, and writing forewords for all titles'), and in 2013 'the first self-described environmental historian ever to serve as president of the AHA'","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"source_ids":["pe131_cronon_changes_1983","pe131_cronon_uses_1993","pe131_cronon_kennecott_1992","pe131_cronon_nm_forum_1992","cronon_wilderness","cronon_changes_afterword","p4c_cronon_cv","p4c_cronon_storytelling_2013"],"evidence_note":"Cronon’s own in-period statements. Changes in the Land (1983), ch. 1 'The View from Walden', read in full in an unpaginated web transcription (page numbers not verified): 'The replacement of Indians by predominantly European populations in New England was as much an ecological as a cultural revolution'; 'An ecological history begins by assuming a dynamic and changing relationship between environment and culture, one as apt to produce contradictions as continuities. Moreover, it assumes that the interactions of the two are dialectical'; 'The choice is not between two landscapes, one with and one without a human influence; it is between two human ways of living, two ways of belonging to an ecosystem'; 'The pig was not merely a pig but a creature bound among other things to the fence, the dandelion, and a very special definition of property'; a 'world capitalist system' draws people 'into trade and market relations which lie well beyond the boundaries of their local ecosystems'. He sets this against species-transfer accounts 'as Alfred Crosby and William H. McNeill have done', which are 'bound to be incomplete', and against functionalist ecological anthropology, which risks 'a static and ahistorical view'. In 1992 he calls Nature’s Metropolis 'a natural sequel' to that book, which 'described a comparable set of environmental changes that occurred in New England as that region became linked to London’s markets' (Annals of Iowa forum, p. 517). 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993): the lesson 'When people buy and sell things in a market, they link together ecosystems and encourage change' is 'a central concern of my own work' (p. 8, n. 14, citing Changes in the Land, Nature’s Metropolis and 'Kennecott Journey'); 'The chief moral of my own version of environmental history is the one I tried to embed in the title of my book Changes in the Land. To live as human beings on this planet is to change the world around us' (p. 19); 'the material history of environmental change is simultaneously a spiritual history of human consciousness and a political economic history of human society' (p. 19); 'All environmental knowledge is culturally constructed and historically contingent—including our own' (p. 14), held together with the claim that 'the natural world exists quite apart from what we believe about it' (p. 15). 'Kennecott Journey' (1992, p. 33): 'The special task of environmental historians is to tell stories that carry us back and forth across the boundary between people and nature to reveal just how culturally constructed that boundary is—and how dependent upon natural systems it remains.' 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995, author’s reflow p. 1): wilderness is 'quite profoundly a human creation'. A contribution, not a founding: he makes the claim for materialist analysis that tries 'to finesse a full-blown determinism' on behalf of the field ('One important contribution of environmental history', 1993, p. 13), and places the field among the 'new' histories of the 1960s (p. 2). Contested in period: Danbom (1992) reads Nature’s Metropolis for its 'environmental moralism' (p. 503) and as 'a book about evil unleavened by good' (p. 506); Cronon answers 'I see no pure heroes or pure villains in the stories I tell' (p. 522). His 1990 JAH response to Worster is unread. The 2003 afterword (pp. 182–185: property analysis learned from anthropology, economics and legal theory; his own criticism of schematic capitalism and source asymmetry) is a later reading. edge_342 (anthropology → environment) and edge_343 (environment → culture) stay: they record anthropology as a resource and environmental history’s contribution to cultural history; this arrow is his own framework. Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) (edge_901 revision): CV pp. 2, 14–15 read in full; the AHA's open text of 'Storytelling' read in passages.","classification_basis":"editorial_review","map_label":"Ecology, markets, ideas of nature and the field's institutions, 1983–2014","target_strand":"environment/colonial_property","proposal_id":"pe131_cronon_rel_01"},"edge_903":{"id":"edge_903","source":"william_cronon","target":"truthdebate","relationship":"Narrative bounded by evidence, ecology and the community of scholars: an environmental historian’s answer to postmodern critiques of narrative (1992); twenty years on, 'Storytelling' (2013) restated the case: 'How do we make the past come alive? By telling stories about it', referring readers to the 1992 essay for 'this theoretical background'","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"source_ids":["pe131_cronon_place_stories_1992","pe131_cronon_uses_1993","p4c_cronon_storytelling_2013"],"evidence_note":"'A Place for Stories: Nature, History, and Narrative', JAH 78:4 (March 1992), author-hosted copy in journal pagination (pp. 1347–1351 and 1369–1376 read). Writing about narrative, 'we cannot avoid encountering the postmodernist assault on narrative, which calls into question not just the stories we tell but the deeper purpose that motivated us in the first place' (p. 1349). The argument is meant for all historians: 'I am urging historians to acknowledge storytelling as the necessary core even of longue durée histories … Environmental history is but one example of these, and most of my arguments apply just as readily to the others' (p. 1349, n. 5). 'I, like most practicing historians, am only willing to follow the postmodernists so far'; 'My goal throughout has been to acknowledge the immense power of narrative while still defending the past (and nature) as real things to which our storytelling must somehow conform lest it cease being history altogether' (p. 1372). Three bounds: 'our stories cannot contravene known facts about the past' and 'our stories must make ecological sense' (p. 1372); 'We write as members of communities' (p. 1373). 'To just that extent, nature coauthors our stories' (p. 1373). Against 'an endless postmodernist deconstruction of texts that fails to ground itself in history, in community, in politics, and finally in the moral problem of living on earth' he asserts 'the virtues of narrative' (p. 1374), and he asks environmental historians for 'not just stories about nature, but stories about stories about nature' (p. 1375). He reads with Hayden White’s Metahistory, Tropics of Discourse and The Content of the Form and with Novick (nn. 6, 7, 43), and credits Richard White with the argument about criticism (n. 46). The 1993 address restates it: historical wisdom 'usually comes in the form of parables, not policy recommendations or certainties' (p. 16). This is his own text; no in-period reading that places the essay in the postmodernism debate was read. Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) (edge_903 revision): the 2013 address read in passages (AHA open text; n. 14 refers to 'A Place for Stories').","classification_basis":"editorial_review","map_label":"Stories and their limits, 1992–2013","proposal_id":"pe131_cronon_rel_03"},"edge_904":{"id":"edge_904","source":"donald_worster","target":"environment","relationship":"Three levels of environmental history (nature, modes of production, ideas), with the capitalist transformation of agriculture as the middle level (1979–1990); and, at the end, the 'Second Earth' of 1500 whose abundance gave 'the false impression that there was no limit' (Shrinking the Earth, 2016, as read by Collomb)","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"source_ids":["pe131_worster_ends_1988","pe131_worster_jah_1990","pe131_worster_wealth_1993","worster_dust_bowl","pe131_merchant_jah_1990","pe131_cronon_place_stories_1992","mcdean_dust_historiography","pe131_crosby_isenberg_2013","pe131_extras_mcneill_2003","p4c_worster_shrinking_2016","p4c_collomb_2017","p4c_worster_origins_bio","p4c_aha_scholarly_distinction"],"evidence_note":"Worster's own statements, in-period first. 'Appendix: Doing Environmental History', The Ends of the Earth (1988), read in an abridged reprint and checked by full-text search of the 1988 edition, whose running heads place the passage between pp. 292 and 294 (Isenberg 2013 cites p. 293): 'There are three levels on which the new history proceeds, three clusters of issues it addresses'; 'The first deals with understanding nature itself, as organized and functioning in past times'; 'The second level in this history brings in the socioeconomic realm as it interacts with the environment. Here we are concerned with tools and work, with the social relations that grow out of that work, with the various modes people have devised of producing goods from natural resources'; the third is 'the purely mental or intellectual, in which perceptions, ethics, laws, myths, and other structures of meaning become part of an individual's or group's dialogue with nature'; together they 'constitute a single dynamic inquiry'. 'Transformations of the Earth' (JAH 1990; English text checked in Worster's 1993 reprint in The Wealth of Nature, ch. 4; JAH page as cited by Isenberg): 'most environmental historians seem to have settled philosophically on a position that is at once materialist and idealist', yet the essay turns to 'that middle level, the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena' (p. 1091), and proposes that 'the single most important task for scholars in the history of modern agroecology is to trace what Karl Polanyi has called “the great transformation”'. Dust Bowl (1979) had argued: 'Capitalism, it is my contention, has been the decisive factor in this nation's use of nature' (p. 5, as quoted by Cronon 1992, p. 1363; wording confirmed in the 1979 edition). In-period readings: Merchant's 1990 response (read) calls his 'conceptual levels of ecology (natural history), production (technology and its socioeconomic relations), and cognition' 'a significant framework for research and writing in this emerging field', while the essay 'lacks a gender analysis' and environmental history 'needs a fourth analytical level, that of reproduction' (p. 1117). Cronon (1992, read) finds that in Dust Bowl 'the chief agent of the story is not “the pioneers” or “civilization” or “man”; it is capitalism', a plot that 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy' (p. 1363). McDean (1986) criticises the generalisation across the Plains (existing source, not re-read). A contribution, not a founding: Worster takes the idea from Aldo Leopold, who 'spoke of “an ecological interpretation of history”' (1988), and McEvoy's ecology, production and cognition triad appeared in the same 1988 volume. Later readings, not evidence for this edge: Isenberg (2013, pp. 88–89) reports White's and Cronon's 1990 objections ('superstructure in the old vulgar Marxist sense'; 'potentially excessive materialism'), whose texts are unread here; McNeill (2003, p. 37) writes of environmental historians' theories that 'The most cogent was probably the one elaborated in slightly different versions by both Donald Worster and Arthur McEvoy', but that such theories 'have sparked no groundswells of enthusiasm'. Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) (edge_904 revision): Collomb 2017 para. 2 read in full; Shrinking the Earth itself unread; the Origins page (Hall chair 1989–) and the AHA award list (2012) read.","classification_basis":"editorial_review","map_label":"Three levels, agroecology and limits, 1979–2016","target_strand":"environment/agriculture_capitalism","proposal_id":"pe131_worster_rel_01"}}
text_revisions [{"node":"post","field":"context","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler","old":"Reworks Foucault through colonial archives, race and sexuality.","new":"Reworks Foucault through colonial archives, race and sexuality (1995); treats 'colonizer/colonized' as 'a historically shifting pair of social categories that needs to be explained' (2002, p. 13, as quoted by Ottevaere); asks scholars to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002). Gouda: homage to her work 'has become de rigueur' in postcolonial studies (2009).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.6): the row rested on one publisher description; Ottevaere 2004 and Gouda 2009 read in full, the 2002 abstract read."},{"node":"post","field":"works","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler","old":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995)","new":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995); 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002)","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.6)."},{"node":"queer","field":"context","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler","old":"Colonial archives and racial formation expose limits and possibilities in Foucauldian histories of sexuality.","new":"Colonial archives and racial formation expose limits and possibilities in Foucauldian histories of sexuality (1995); 'sexual control was both an instrumental image for the body politic … and itself fundamental to how racial policies were secured' (2002, p. 78, as quoted by Ottevaere).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.6): Ottevaere 2004 read in full."},{"node":"queer","field":"works","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler","old":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995)","new":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002)","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.6)."},{"node":"gender","field":"context","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler","old":"Makes colonial race and sexuality central to a critical extension of Foucault.","new":"Makes colonial race and sexuality central to a critical extension of Foucault (1995); 'Ethnographies of empire should attend both to changing sensibilities and to sex' (2002, p. 78, as quoted by Ottevaere), who compares her evaluation of 'historically designated categories' to Joan Scott's.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.6): Ottevaere 2004 read in full."},{"node":"gender","field":"works","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler","old":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995)","new":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002), ch. 3","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.6)."},{"node":"post","field":"focus","strand":"colonial_intimacies","old":"Empire becomes part of the history of European bourgeois sexuality.","new":"Empire becomes part of the history of European bourgeois sexuality (1995). Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002) made 'the intimate' a matter of state and 'colonizer/colonized' a shifting pair of categories (p. 13, as quoted by Ottevaere); the same year her 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' moved 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject', the programme now carried by the entry archival_turn.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.9 3): the strand rested on one publisher description; cross-reference to the new entry."},{"node":"post","field":"works","strand":"colonial_intimacies","old":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995)","new":"Race and the Education of Desire (1995); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002); 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002)","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.9 3)."},{"node":"memory","field":"focus","strand":"archive_authority","old":"Combe’s inquiry into access supplies a named resource to Derrida. His account differentiates archives from spontaneous memory and examines institutional authority and technical inscription.","new":"Combe’s inquiry into access supplies a named resource to Derrida. His account differentiates archives from spontaneous memory and examines institutional authority and technical inscription. From the archival profession, Schwartz and Cook called archives 'active sites where social power is negotiated' whose appraisal and access shape collective memory (2002, abstract); the wider readership is on the entry archival_turn.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.9 4): cross-reference and the Schwartz–Cook abstract (read)."},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"description","old":"Historical inquiry into colonial formations in which newcomers came to stay, acquired land and claimed sovereignty over Indigenous territories. Patrick Wolfe's account of settler colonialism as a structure rather than an event treats invasion as a continuing relation rather than a completed frontier past. Lorenzo Veracini's work and the journal Settler Colonial Studies (2011) distinguished settler colonial from colonial phenomena. Comparative studies of settler capitalism and settler societies, and decolonization-era uses of the term, came earlier. In Canadian historical writing the framework meets an older historiography of dispossession, reserves, gender and the state in the West and British Columbia. It also meets Indigenous political theory of recognition, sovereignty and resurgence. Its uses extend to labour, science, memory and settler identity. The field is contested from several directions. Indigenous scholars ask whether it recentres settlers and displaces Indigenous studies and Indigenous-authored histories. Historians question its structural inevitability and its fit across regions and periods, proposing other modes such as commercial penetration and extractivism. Black studies scholars question how it places slavery. Settler colonialism overlaps with Indigenous history, postcolonial critique and historical geography without being equivalent to any of them.","new":"Historical inquiry into colonial formations in which newcomers came to stay, took land and claimed sovereignty over Indigenous territories. Patrick Wolfe gave the field its programme: settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it is 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'invasion is a structure not an event' (1999, 2006). Lorenzo Veracini founded Settler Colonial Studies (2011) and distinguished colonialism ('you, work for me') from settler colonialism ('you, go away'), adding that the structure 'persistently pursues a specific end point': it 'extinguishes itself'. Two debates run through the field. Veracini's self-superseding structure and Wolfe's enduring one; and the Indigenous reading, which adopts Wolfe's formula while insisting that 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies' (Kauanui), that 'decolonization is not a metaphor' (Tuck and Yang) and that refusal, not recognition, answers the settler state (Simpson, Coulthard). Settler scholars warn against 'colonial fatalism' (Macoun and Strakosch); Marxists ask whether elimination or exploitation defines the form (Englert). In Canada the frame met an older historiography of reserves, treaties, gender and the state, and the TRC's finding of 'cultural genocide' (2015).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.1: description rewritten from the texts read in full; every quotation attributed."},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"scope_note","old":"A selective research field whose theoretical consolidation came mostly after 2000; the entry covers its roots through 2000 and cites selected later works as readings. Settler-colonial theory, Indigenous political thought, Canadian histories of dispossession and comparative imperial history have different genealogies. Roster inclusion is not adoption of the paradigm: Carter, Perry, Harris and Daschuk wrote earlier or beside it. No single founder, origin or Canadian consensus is asserted. Critics appear as critics. Francophone uptake in the PhD corpus is near zero.","new":"A research field whose consolidation came after 2000; reviewed through 2024 in the period-4 wave (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md). Read in full: Wolfe 2006 (in an Internet Archive capture of the journal's free-to-read page), Veracini 2011 and his 2016 obituary of Wolfe, Kauanui 2016, Englert 2020, Goeman 2016; in most pages, Tuck and Yang 2012 and Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014; Macoun and Strakosch 2013 in the authors' version; Veracini 2013 in its author manuscript (abstract, opening, conclusion); the TRC summary pp. 1–3. Unread, known through publisher pages or named readers: Wolfe 1999 beyond pp. 1–2, 2001, 2008, 2016; Veracini 2010, 2015; Coulthard; A. Simpson (book; 2017 abstract read); Byrd; Moreton-Robinson; L. B. Simpson; Rowse; Konishi; Sexton; Garba and Sorentino; King; Daschuk; Miller 2009; Perry 2015; Carter 2016; McCallum; Mawani; Stanger-Ross; Lowman and Barker; Regan. Settler-colonial theory, Indigenous political thought, Canadian histories of dispossession and comparative imperial history have different genealogies; Veracini (2013) and Kauanui (2016) both record uses of the term before Wolfe (Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973, Denoon 1983, Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis 1995). Roster inclusion is not adoption of the paradigm: Carter, Perry, Harris, Miller, Daschuk and McCallum wrote earlier or beside it, and their publisher descriptions do not use the phrase. Critics appear as critics; Kauanui and Goeman defend Wolfe's theory against shallow citation while asking the field to attend to Indigenous studies. History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23: two or more of the field's authors are cited by 3.2% of Canada + US theses in 2000–09 and 11.0% in 2020–25 (Canada 15.2%, US 6.8%); Wolfe and Veracini are co-cited by 3.0% of theses in 2020–25 and Wolfe with Tuck and Yang, Simpson or Coulthard by 2.3%, the only rising two-sided citing after 2000; the Canadian neighbourhood of the founders is the largest and youngest of any field (51 works, median year 2006), its 2020–25 entrants Coulthard, Simpson, Carter, Miller, Daschuk, Kauanui and Greer; among Canadian History PhDs 2009–26, 54 cite a work of Wolfe's and 17 the 1999 book (first in 2012), figures that say more about Canadian history than about him. Bibliographies record consultation, not influence. Francophone uptake in the corpus remains near zero.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.1: scope note rewritten; the dissertation figures (and the counts struck from edge_892, 881 and 882) live here."},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"representative_figures_and_works","old":"Earlier: Denoon, Settler Capitalism (1983); Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis, eds., Unsettling Settler Societies (1995). Wolfe, Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999) and “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006); Elkins and Pedersen, eds. (2005); Veracini, Settler Colonialism (2010) and Settler Colonial Studies (2011). Read with Coulthard (2014), A. Simpson (2014), L. B. Simpson (2011, 2017), Byrd (2011) and Tuck and Yang (2012). Canadian historiography: Carter (1990, 2008), Harris (2002, 2004, 2020), Perry (2001), Regan (2010), Daschuk (2013), Battell Lowman and Barker (2015), Burrill (2019). Critiques: Macoun and Strakosch (2013), Rowse (2014), Kauanui (2016), Greer (2019), Shoemaker (2019), Konishi (2019), Garba and Sorentino (2020).","new":"Earlier: Sayegh (1965), Rodinson (1973), Denoon, Settler Capitalism (1983); Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis, eds., Unsettling Settler Societies (1995). Wolfe, Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999), “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006), “Structure and Event” (2008), Traces of History (2016); Veracini, Settler Colonialism: A Theoretical Overview (2010), “Introducing settler colonial studies” (2011), “Career of a Concept” (2013), The Settler Colonial Present (2015); Elkins and Pedersen, eds. (2005); Cavanagh and Veracini, eds. (2016). Indigenous critique and theory: Byrd (2011), L. B. Simpson (2011, 2017), Tuck and Yang (2012), Coulthard (2014), A. Simpson (2014, 2017), Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel (2014), Moreton-Robinson (2015), Kauanui (2016), Goeman (2016). Critiques: Macoun and Strakosch (2013), Rowse (2014), Greer (2019), Shoemaker (2019), Konishi (2019), Englert (2020), Garba and Sorentino (2020). Canada: Carter (1990, 2008, 2016), Harris (2002, 2004, 2020), Perry (2001, 2015), Miller (2009), Mawani (2009), Regan (2010), Daschuk (2013), McCallum (2014), Battell Lowman and Barker (2015), TRC Summary (2015), Hugill (2017), Burrill (2019), Stanger-Ross, ed. (2020).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.1."},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","old":"Structure, not event","new":"Structure, not event: the logic of elimination","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"structure_and_elimination"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Wolfe's account treats invasion and elimination as a continuing structure. Kauanui's abstract attests the formulation and its critique; Wolfe's own pages are not checked here.","new":"Wolfe's programme, now read in his own pages. Settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it 'is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'the primary motive for elimination is not race … but access to territory. Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; 'settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event'; elimination has a 'positive' aspect (allotment, citizenship, child abduction, assimilation) that persists after the frontier, so that the form is 'relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' names its convergence with genocide without 'hierarchy among victims' (2006). The formula goes back to the 1999 book (p. 2) and the 1994 essay (p. 96, per his note 7). Kauanui: the phrase 'often seems to stand-in for a serious engagement of his theory'.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: the 2006 essay is read in full; the earlier caveat is withdrawn.","strand":"structure_and_elimination"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","old":"Settler colonial theory and field formation","new":"Veracini: the field, the journal and the self-superseding structure","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"theory_and_field"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Veracini disentangles settler colonial from colonial phenomena and introduces a dedicated journal (2011); a 2013 essay traces the concept's earlier career. Field-making, not a claim of single origin.","new":"Veracini founded Settler Colonial Studies (2011; open access, volunteer-run, on an ARC grant; to Taylor & Francis in 2013) 'to contribute to the consolidation of a new scholarly field'. Colonialism says 'you, work for me'; settler colonialism says 'you, go away'; the two 'should be understood in their dialectical relation'. Where Wolfe's structure endures, Veracini's 'persistently pursues a specific end point': 'settler colonialism, by contrast, extinguishes itself', is 'least visible in the settler cities', and 'ends with an indigenous ultimate permanence'; 'decolonisation is not settler decolonisation', and the politics of recognition 'cannot be considered a genuinely decolonising move' (2011). His history of the concept (2013) finds three earlier careers of the term and a consolidation 'from approximately the mid 1990s', with 'both the Canadian and US historiographies … late in adopting' it. Englert calls him Wolfe's 'self-styled intellectual successor'; Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel find in his history 'Indigenous studies and Indigenous resistance … pretty much erased'.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Veracini 2011 read in full, 2013 in its author manuscript.","strand":"theory_and_field"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","old":"Indigenous critiques of recognition, sovereignty and resurgence","new":"The Indigenous reading: land, refusal, incommensurability","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"recognition_refusal_resurgence"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Indigenous political theory read alongside the paradigm. These works have their own genealogies in Indigenous thought and are not a subset of settler-colonial studies. Open question: these may belong in Indigenous history instead.","new":"Indigenous political theory that takes up Wolfe's formula and redirects the field. Tuck and Yang: settler colonialism's violence 'is not temporally contained in the arrival of the settler but is reasserted each day of occupation. This is why Patrick Wolfe (1999) emphasizes that settler colonialism is a structure and not an event'; 'decolonization brings about the repatriation of Indigenous land and life; it is not a metaphor'; the settler–native–slave triad; 'settler moves to innocence'; 'an ethic of incommensurability' against reconciliation, which 'is about rescuing settler normalcy' (2012). Simpson: 'refusal' as 'an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society', consent 'as a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession' (2017, abstract; the 2014 book is known through its publisher page). Coulthard: settler colonialism 'territorially acquisitive in perpetuity'; in Canada the means of elimination changed but 'the ends have always remained the same: to shore up continued access to Indigenous peoples' territories' (2014, p. 125, as quoted by Englert); a 'place-based' reworking of primitive accumulation (publisher). Byrd, L. B. Simpson and Moreton-Robinson remain at publisher-page level. These works have their own genealogies; in Canadian and US theses they are read with Wolfe (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23), and the user ruled (2026-10-12) that they may sit in more than one field.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Tuck and Yang read; Simpson and Coulthard in their own words at abstract or quoted level.","strand":"recognition_refusal_resurgence"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","old":"Indigenous scholars on settler colonial studies","new":"Indigenous studies and the field: who made it, whom it displaces","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"indigenous_scholars_critique"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Whether the field recentres settlers and displaces Indigenous studies and Indigenous-authored histories.","new":"Kauanui: Wolfe 'insisted … that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did', yet 'he tends to be most frequently cited as if he had'; 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies'; indigeneity is 'a category of analysis that is distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality'; Warrior counted ASA panels and feared the field had become 'an answer to the chronic need for more attention to … Native and Indigenous studies' (2016). Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel: the field 'can displace, overshadow, or even mask over Indigenous studies'; 'when settlers take up these questions, it's suddenly considered a legitimate field of study' (2014). Goeman answers from the same field: Wolfe 'critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner' and his work 'became a place to engage Indigenous studies concerns in relation to settler colonial studies' (2016). Konishi surveys Indigenous scholars who advocate, interrogate and challenge the field (2019, abstract).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Kauanui, Snelgrove et al. and Goeman read.","strand":"indigenous_scholars_critique"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","old":"Critiques: structure, region and period","new":"Critiques: fatalism, region, period, exploitation","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"critiques_and_limits"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Structural inevitability (Macoun and Strakosch), a teleology of elimination (Rowse), regional and periodic fit with other modes of colonialism (Greer), universal theory or English heritage (Shoemaker).","new":"From inside the theory, Macoun and Strakosch: SCT 'posits a structural inevitability', 'can depict colonization as structurally inevitable', risks 'colonial fatalism' and 'does not account for Indigenous lives'; yet 'the fact that settler colonialism struggles to narrate its own ending does not mean that it cannot end' (2013). Rowse: a 'teleology of elimination' (2014, abstract). Greer: one mode among several, dominant in some periods and regions (2019). Shoemaker: universal theory or English heritage? (2019). Englert: 'the elimination of the native … does not accurately define the overall logic of settler colonialism'; settler colonies in Africa and South America 'were built on the exploitation of indigenous peoples'; accumulation by dispossession 'carried out by all classes of settlers, including by workers' (2020, with Kelley and Speed 2017).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Macoun and Strakosch and Englert read.","strand":"critiques_and_limits"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Co-citation places Daschuk's Plains history next to Wolfe and Veracini (19 of 58 Wolfe/Veracini-citing PhDs). Its own framing is unchecked, so this is a comparison strand.","new":"Daschuk's Plains history is the work most co-cited with Wolfe and Veracini by Canadian PhDs (19 of 58, lift 10.2; a 2020–25 entrant with support 12; History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23). Its publisher describes 'a policy of starvation' that 'sparked widespread debate about genocide in Canada' and does not use 'settler colonialism'; the 2019 edition adds responses by Kelm, Mosby, Neylan and Woolford. His own framing remains unchecked; the TRC summary's 'cultural genocide' (2015) is the public text beside which the book is read. A comparison strand.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: publisher page read; framing still unchecked.","strand":"health_starvation"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Comparative settler economies (1983) and gender, race, ethnicity and class in settler societies (1995) are earlier comparative scholarship, before the field's theoretical consolidation; Veracini's history of the concept records decolonization-era usage of the term itself.","new":"Comparative settler economies (1983) and gender, race, ethnicity and class in settler societies (1995) are earlier comparative scholarship, before the field's theoretical consolidation; Veracini's history of the concept records decolonization-era usage of the term itself. Veracini (2013) reads the 'settler/dominion capitalism' literature as writing 'indigenous people and indigenous history off the conceptual map'; Kauanui (2016, n. 7) adds Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973, Sayigh 1979 and Trask 1992/2000 before Wolfe.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor.","strand":"earlier_settler_societies"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"How settler-colonial and decolonial frameworks place slavery and Black people.","new":"How settler-colonial and decolonial frameworks place slavery and Black people. Wolfe 2006 (read) sets out the opposed racialisations of Black and Indigenous people in the United States; Tuck and Yang's settler–native–slave triad (read) is what Garba and Sorentino answer. Both authors' words are now read; the critics' remain at abstract level.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor.","strand":"slavery_black_studies"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"works","old":"Adele Perry, On the Edge of Empire: Gender, Race, and the Making of British Columbia, 1849–1871 (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 2001).; Sarah Carter, The Importance of Being Monogamous: Marriage and Nation Building in Western Canada to 1915 (Edmonton: University of Alberta Press / Athabasca University Press, 2008).; Margaret D. Jacobs, White Mother to a Dark Race: Settler Colonialism, Maternalism, and the Removal of Indigenous Children in the American West and Australia, 1880–1940 (Lincoln: University of Nebraska Press, 2009).","new":"Adele Perry, On the Edge of Empire: Gender, Race, and the Making of British Columbia, 1849–1871 (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 2001).; Sarah Carter, The Importance of Being Monogamous: Marriage and Nation Building in Western Canada to 1915 (Edmonton: University of Alberta Press / Athabasca University Press, 2008).; Margaret D. Jacobs, White Mother to a Dark Race: Settler Colonialism, Maternalism, and the Removal of Indigenous Children in the American West and Australia, 1880–1940 (Lincoln: University of Nebraska Press, 2009).; Adele Perry, Colonial Relations: The Douglas-Connolly Family and the Nineteenth-Century Imperial World (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2015).; Sarah Carter, Imperial Plots: Women, Land, and the Spadework of British Colonialism on the Canadian Prairies (Winnipeg: University of Manitoba Press, 2016).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor: publisher pages.","strand":"gender_family_nation"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","old":"Settler self-examination after residential schools and in contemporary Canada; Regan's contents page names the 'peacemaker myth'.","new":"Settler self-examination after residential schools and in contemporary Canada; Regan's contents page names the 'peacemaker myth'. The TRC summary (2015, pp. 1–3 read) finds that residential schools were part of a policy 'which can best be described as \"cultural genocide\"'; Regan is described by her publisher as the Commission's director of research.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor.","strand":"settler_identity_reconciliation"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Formulates settler colonialism as a continuing structure of dispossession; the 'structure, not an event' phrasing is attested through Kauanui's abstract. Already a comparison participant in Indigenous history.","new":"Gave the field its programme: 'destroys to replace'; 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'invasion is a structure not an event'; 'structural genocide' (2006, read in full; the formula from 1994 and 1999). Denied that he created the field, per Kauanui. Australian historian (1949–2016); also on the Indigenous, racial formation, Holocaust and Indigenous–newcomer entries.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Distinguishes settler colonial from colonial phenomena and introduces Settler Colonial Studies (2011); traces the concept's earlier career.","new":"Founded the journal (2011) and the theory of a self-superseding structure: colonialism says 'you, work for me', settler colonialism 'you, go away'; 'settler colonialism … extinguishes itself'; 'decolonisation is not settler decolonisation' (2011, read in full). His history of the concept (2013) dates the field's consolidation to the mid-1990s. Wikidata Q112150999; a person entry is deferred to wave 2.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"lorenzo_veracini"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Indigenous political theory rejecting the colonial politics of recognition; contributes to, rather than belongs to, settler-colonial studies. Metadata-level only.","new":"Rejects 'the colonial politics of recognition' and reworks Marx's primitive accumulation as 'place-based' (publisher); settler colonialism 'territorially acquisitive in perpetuity', its Canadian means changing while 'the ends have always remained the same' (2014, p. 125, as quoted by Englert). Political scientist, UBC; the publisher identifies him as Yellowknives Dene. Book unread; Wikidata Q91710018; a person entry is deferred to wave 2.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"glen_coulthard"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Mohawk political life across the borders of settler states; metadata-level only.","new":"'Refusal' as 'an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society'; consent as 'a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession' (2017, abstract read); Mohawk Interruptus (2014) known through its publisher page (nested sovereignty, ethnographic refusal, prizes 2014–15). Anthropologist, Columbia; Wikidata Q112536616 describes her as a Kahnawà:ke Mohawk anthropologist; a person entry is deferred to wave 2.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"audra_simpson"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Coauthor of a widely cited argument against metaphorical uses of decolonization; argument read only through a critic's abstract.","new":"'Decolonization brings about the repatriation of Indigenous land and life; it is not a metaphor'; adopts Wolfe's 'structure and not an event' (1999) and his 2006 account of opposed racialisations; 'settler moves to innocence'; 'an ethic of incommensurability' (2012, read). SUNY New Paltz on the article, Toronto per Wikidata (Q28924966). The article's note 1 describes its authors as 'an Indigenous scholar and a settler/trespasser/scholar writing together'; no further identity assertion.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"eve_tuck"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Coauthor; see Eve Tuck.","new":"Coauthor with Eve Tuck (2012, read); UC San Diego on the article. See Eve Tuck.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"k_wayne_yang"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Argues that engagement with Wolfe's structure thesis demands rethinking indigeneity and Native American and Indigenous Studies; a critical interlocutor rather than an adherent.","new":"Defends Wolfe's theory against its reduction to a slogan and against the charge of an Anglo binary, while insisting that 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies' and that indigeneity is a category of analysis distinct from race (2016, read in full). Co-founder of NAISA (2008). Wesleyan; Wikidata Q59537273.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"j_kehaulani_kauanui"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Disease, starvation policy and the loss of Indigenous life on the Plains; strongly co-cited with Wolfe and Veracini, but whether the book uses the framework is unchecked.","new":"Disease, starvation policy and the loss of Indigenous life on the Plains; the work most co-cited with Wolfe and Veracini by Canadian PhDs. The publisher's description speaks of 'a policy of starvation' and a 'debate about genocide', not of settler colonialism; whether the book uses the framework is unchecked (2019 edition with responses by Kelm, Mosby, Neylan and Woolford).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"james_daschuk"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Coauthor of a critique of the theory's structural inevitability, written from within the theory.","new":"Coauthor of a critique from inside the theory: 'colonial fatalism'; SCT 'does not account for Indigenous lives'; yet 'the fact that settler colonialism struggles to narrate its own ending does not mean that it cannot end' (2013, authors' version read).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"alissa_macoun"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Coauthor; see Alissa Macoun.","new":"Coauthor; see Alissa Macoun (2013, read).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"elizabeth_strakosch"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Residential schools, truth telling and the 'peacemaker myth'; contents page only.","new":"Residential schools, truth telling and the 'peacemaker myth' (ch. 3 'Deconstructing Canada's Peacemaker Myth'); publisher page read (EPUB 2010, paperback January 2011; foreword by Taiaiake Alfred; TRC director of research). Book unread.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"paulette_regan"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Settler identity in contemporary Canada; catalogue-level only.","new":"Settler identity in contemporary Canada: 'Why Say Settler', 'Canada and Settler Colonialism', 'It's Always All About the Land' (contents, publisher page); book unread.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"emma_battell_lowman"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","old":"Coauthor; see Emma Battell Lowman.","new":"Coauthor; see Emma Battell Lowman. Also cited by Snelgrove et al. (2014) for settler colonialism as 'a distinct method of colonizing'.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"adam_j_barker"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"works","old":"Lost Harvests (1990); The Importance of Being Monogamous (2008)","new":"Lost Harvests (1990); The Importance of Being Monogamous (2008); Imperial Plots (2016)","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3.","person_id":"sarah_carter"},{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"works","old":"On the Edge of Empire (2001); “‘Oh I’m Just Sick of the Faces of Men’” (1995); “Presence, Absence, and the Writing of BC History” (2001/02)","new":"On the Edge of Empire (2001); “‘Oh I’m Just Sick of the Faces of Men’” (1995); “Presence, Absence, and the Writing of BC History” (2001/02); Colonial Relations (2015)","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3.","person_id":"adele_perry"},{"node":"indigenous","field":"role","old":"comparison","new":"contributor","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: linked to p4B_rel_03.","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"},{"node":"indigenous","field":"context","old":"Related settler-colonial and anthropological scholarship, admitted as a bibliographic connection.","new":"His programme engaged Native American and Indigenous Studies directly (Goeman 2016; Kauanui 2016, both read): 'a key scholar in conceiving of a settler-colonial analytic in relation to Indigenous thinking'; US Indian policy read as modalities of elimination (2006; 'After the Frontier', 2011, unread). A contribution to the conversation, not an origin of the field's methods.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3.","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"},{"node":"indigenous","field":"works","old":"Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999)","new":"Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999); “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006); “After the Frontier” (2011)","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3.","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"},{"node":"patrick_wolfe","field":"description","old":"Reads the history of anthropology through settler colonialism. Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999) studies virgin birth in anthropology and in Australian settler discourse, and Victorian anthropology on mother-right, totemism and E. B. Tylor. Later essays address land, labour and race (2001) and the elimination of the native (2006).","new":"An Australian historian of settler colonialism, race and the history of anthropology (1949–2016). His first book read Victorian and Australian anthropology through 'the elimination of native societies': 'the colonizers come to stay—invasion is a structure not an event' (1999). The 2006 essay made the formula a programme: settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it is 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; after the frontier, elimination 'turned inwards' through allotment, blood quantum and assimilation, so the form stays 'relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' names its convergence with genocide without 'hierarchy among victims'. Race, he argued, 'is made in the targeting' (2001, 2006; Traces of History, 2016). Veracini called him the field's founder 'in a fit of absentmindedness'; Kauanui records that he 'insisted … that he did not create the field … that Native scholars did'. Critics find in his structure a 'colonial fatalism' (Macoun and Strakosch) and ask whether exploitation, not elimination, defines settler colonies (Englert).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7: description rewritten from the 2006 essay (read in full) and named readers."},{"node":"patrick_wolfe","field":"representative_figures_and_works","old":"Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016); Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology: The Politics and Poetics of an Ethnographic Event (1999). Later readings: “Land, Labor, and Difference: Elementary Structures of Race” (2001); “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006).","new":"Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016); “Nation and MiscegeNation” (Social Analysis 36, 1994); Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology: The Politics and Poetics of an Ethnographic Event (1999); “Land, Labor, and Difference: Elementary Structures of Race” (AHR 2001); “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (JGR 2006); “Structure and Event” (in Moses, ed., 2008); “After the Frontier” (2011); “Purchase by Other Means” (2012); ed., “Recuperating Binarism” (2013); with David Lloyd, “Settler Colonial Logics and the Neoliberal Regime” (2015); Traces of History: Elementary Structures of Race (2016); ed., The Settler Complex (2016). Readers: Veracini (2011, 2016), Macoun and Strakosch (2013), Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel (2014), Kauanui (2016), Goeman (2016), Birch (2016), the American Quarterly forum (2017), Englert (2020), Russell (2022).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7."},{"node":"patrick_wolfe","field":"scope_note","old":"This entry presents the 1999 book. Its introduction (pp. 1–2, read) distinguishes settler from franchise colonies, holds that settler colonies 'were (are) premised on the elimination of native societies' and states that 'invasion is a structure not an event'; the rest of the book is checked at contents and catalogue level. The 2001 and 2006 essays are later readings. He did not originate the term, which has earlier usage (Veracini 2013), and he is not presented as the field's founder.","new":"Read on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md): the 2006 essay in full (an Internet Archive capture of the journal's free-to-read page; page numbers from Kauanui's citations), the 1999 introduction pp. 1–2 (earlier), Veracini's obituary, Goeman's memorial, Kauanui's essay, Macoun and Strakosch, Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel, Englert, in full or in most pages. Unread: the 1994 essay (JSTOR), the 1999 chapters (contents and catalogue checked), the 2001 AHR essay beyond a sentence he quotes himself, the 2008 chapter, the Settler Colonial Studies essays, Traces of History (British Library contents note only), The Settler Complex, Birch's obituary (restricted file), the 2017 forum, Russell's chapter. Life dates and places from Wikidata Q109284663 (checked by QLever SPARQL); posts from the Victoria University collection page (Victoria University, University of Melbourne, La Trobe University; undated) and Veracini's obituary (fellowships at Harvard and Stanford; undated), so no post is drawn as an event; Kauanui's 'English scholar' is noted against Wikidata's Australian historian born in Yorkshire. He did not coin the term (Veracini 2013; Kauanui 2016, n. 7) and said he did not found the field; the entry presents a programme, not an origin. Canadian PhD figures are in the settler_colonialism scope_note, not here.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7."},{"node":"environment","field":"description","old":"Explains historical relations among people and the nonhuman world through ecological exchange, agriculture, property, commodity flows, conservation and cultural ideas of nature. These approaches disagree about causal emphasis. Cases from New England, the Great Plains, Mexico, India, colonial islands and South Africa connect ecological processes with power without supplying one universal model. Bullard’s environmental-justice inquiry and Rita and Jackie Huggins’s situated life histories broaden comparison toward unequal burdens, displacement and authority over historical accounts. Dust Bowl debates distinguish agricultural capitalism, technology and soils, federal intervention, regional scale and household survival; primary criticism and later social history complicate a single causal summary.","new":"Explains historical relations among people and the nonhuman world through ecological exchange, agriculture, property, commodity flows, conservation and cultural ideas of nature; the approaches disagree about causal emphasis, and cases from New England, the Great Plains, Mexico, India, colonial islands and South Africa connect ecology with power without one model. Bullard and the Huggins life histories add unequal burdens and authority over accounts; the Dust Bowl debates set capitalism, technology, policy and survival against one another. After 1995 the field argued with itself and its neighbours: Cronon called wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'; Foreman answered that this 'Received Wilderness Idea is a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (2000); Spence (1999) and Jacoby (2001) took the removal of Indigenous people from 'uninhabited wilderness' into the archive. A merged journal (1996), McNeill's world synthesis (2000) and a Canadian network (NiCHE, 2004) widened it; by 2013 Sutter found nature and culture replaced by 'environmental hybridity' (editors' summary). Chakrabarty (2009) and the Anthropocene histories of 2016 set its questions at planetary scale.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1: the field after 1995."},{"node":"environment","field":"representative_figures_and_works","old":"Alfred Crosby; Donald Worster; Carolyn Merchant; William Cronon. ASEH founded 1977. Expanded contextual readings include Leopold’s precursor essays, Melville’s Mexican study, Gadgil and Guha’s Indian history, Grove’s colonial conservation and Carruthers’s South African park history.","new":"Alfred Crosby; Donald Worster; Carolyn Merchant; William Cronon. ASEH founded 1977. Expanded contextual readings include Leopold’s precursor essays, Melville’s Mexican study, Gadgil and Guha’s Indian history, Grove’s colonial conservation and Carruthers’s South African park history. After 2000: J. R. McNeill, Something New Under the Sun (2000) and 'Observations' (2003); Spence, Dispossessing the Wilderness (1999); Jacoby, Crimes against Nature (2001); Sutter, 'The World with Us' (JAH 2013); Isenberg, ed., The Oxford Handbook of Environmental History (2014); in Canada, Wynn (series editor), MacEachern, Loo, Piper, Sandlos, Evenden, Colpitts, Parr; the Anthropocene: Chakrabarty (2009), McNeill and Engelke, Malm, Bonneuil and Fressoz (2016). Institutions: Environmental History (journal, 1996), NiCHE (2004).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1."},{"node":"environment","field":"scope_note","old":"Ecological exchange, political economy, and feminist critiques of nature overlap; institutional society history does not document every intellectual route. Crosby’s temperate-settler argument differs from Melville’s Mexican case; Gadgil and Guha’s resource conflicts differ from Grove’s conservation networks and Carruthers’s park politics. Cronon’s cultural account of wilderness does not deny nonhuman nature. The selection remains incomplete, especially in environmental justice and Indigenous scholarship. A bounded second-pass addition now compares Bullard and the Huggins collaboration; primary-excerpt, publisher-description and metadata limits remain distinct, and neither selection represents every community.","new":"Ecological exchange, political economy, and feminist critiques of nature overlap; institutional society history does not document every intellectual route. Crosby’s temperate-settler argument differs from Melville’s Mexican case; Gadgil and Guha’s resource conflicts differ from Grove’s conservation networks and Carruthers’s park politics. Cronon’s cultural account of wilderness does not deny nonhuman nature. The selection remains incomplete, especially in environmental justice and Indigenous scholarship. A bounded second-pass addition now compares Bullard and the Huggins collaboration; primary-excerpt, publisher-description and metadata limits remain distinct, and neither selection represents every community. Extended to 2024 on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md). Read in full: Foreman's 'The Real Wilderness Idea' (2000), Cronon's CV, 'Storytelling' (2013, passages) and 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (2003), the NiCHE and UBC Press series pages, publisher pages and abstracts for the Canadian books and articles, two open reviews of Worster's Shrinking the Earth. Unread: the 1996 comments by Hays, Cohen and Dunlap and Cronon's response; Sutter's 2013 essay and its six responses (the editors' summary is quoted instead); McNeill 2000 and 2003 (the latter known from the earlier record); the 2005 'What's Next' forum including Linda Nash on agency; the bodies of every Canadian book; Malm, Moore, Bonneuil and Fressoz beyond descriptions. The History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23 dates the field's readership: 1.6% of Canada + US theses cited two or more of its authors in the 1990s, 5.7% in 2000–09, 9.5% in 2010–14, 11.3% in 2015–19, 9.9% in 2020–25; the network of works co-cited with Cronon, Worster, White, Crosby and Merchant peaked in Canada in 2010–14 (37 works) and halved by 2020–25 (17), its new members being Wolfe and Tuck and Yang; in the US it is stable (17–19 works) and its entrants are Indigenous, borderlands and slavery works. The field is read with its neighbours rather than renewed from within.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1: reading limits and the PhD citation figures."},{"node":"environment","field":"works","old":"“The Trouble with Wilderness” (1995)","new":"“The Trouble with Wilderness” (1995; journal reprint with comments and a response, Environmental History 1:1, January 1996)","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P2: the 1996 journal reprint and forum.","strand":"wilderness_culture"},{"node":"environment","field":"context","old":"Writes the political history of conservation and environmentalism. Cronon (1993) says his two books 'speak just as powerfully to major trends in conservation and environmental politics' and draws from them a contrast between conservationists' 'economically efficient production' and environmentalists' 'ecologically responsible consumption'. Books unread here.","new":"Writes the political history of conservation and environmentalism. Cronon (1993) says his two books 'speak just as powerfully to major trends in conservation and environmental politics' and draws from them a contrast between conservationists' 'economically efficient production' and environmentalists' 'ecologically responsible consumption'. Books unread here. He answered Cronon's wilderness essay in the first number of Environmental History (1996): 'Cronon's wilderness is a world of abstracted ideas … divorced from the values and ideas inherent in wilderness action' (as quoted by Foreman, 2000; the comment itself unread).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P3: Hays's 1996 comment, as quoted by Foreman; marked for re-verification.","person_id":"samuel_p_hays"},{"node":"william_cronon","field":"description","old":"A historian of North American environments who treats ecological change as a two-way relation between people and the rest of nature, worked out through property, markets and ideas of nature. Changes in the Land (1983) assumes that 'the interactions of the two are dialectical' and finds in colonial New England not a choice between landscapes with and without people but 'two human ways of living, two ways of belonging to an ecosystem'. Nature’s Metropolis (1991) follows grain, lumber and meat between Chicago and its hinterland on the premise that 'city and country have a common history, so their stories are best told together'. 'A Place for Stories' (1992) asks environmental historians for 'stories about stories about nature', and 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995) calls wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'. Reviewers found the Chicago book distant from farmers and townspeople, or moralising; he replied that he saw 'no pure heroes or pure villains'.","new":"A historian of North American environments who treats ecological change as a two-way relation between people and the rest of nature, worked out through property, markets and ideas of nature. Changes in the Land (1983) assumes that 'the interactions of the two are dialectical' and finds in colonial New England not a choice between landscapes with and without people but 'two human ways of living, two ways of belonging to an ecosystem'. Nature’s Metropolis (1991) follows grain, lumber and meat between Chicago and its hinterland on the premise that 'city and country have a common history, so their stories are best told together'. 'A Place for Stories' (1992) asks environmental historians for 'stories about stories about nature', and 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995) calls wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'. Reviewers found the Chicago book distant from farmers and townspeople, or moralising; he replied that he saw 'no pure heroes or pure villains'. After 1995 the essay drew its own debate: Hays found in it 'a world of abstracted ideas' (1996, as quoted by Foreman), and Foreman told a wilderness-science conference that the 'Received Wilderness Idea' of 'Callicott, Cronon and other postmodern deconstructionist scholars' was 'a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (2000). Cronon answered in practice, asking that the Apostle Islands be managed 'as a historical wilderness', 'a storied wilderness' (2003). He edited the Weyerhaeuser Environmental Books (1994–2014) and, as 'the first self-described environmental historian ever to serve as president of the AHA', told the profession that 'Our core business is resurrection: helping the dead past live again' (2013).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1/P6: the debate and the later career."},{"node":"william_cronon","field":"scope_note","old":"This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-13: chapter 1 of Changes in the Land in an unpaginated web transcription; 'A Place for Stories' (JAH 1992; pp. 1347–1351 and 1369–1376 read) and 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993; OCR of the author’s scan, journal pagination); 'Kennecott Journey' (1992); passages of 'The Trouble with Wilderness' in the author’s reflow; and the 1992 Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response read in full. Nature’s Metropolis itself is unread: its words appear here only as forum contributors quote them with page numbers. 'Modes of Prophecy and Production', his 1990 response to Worster in the JAH round table, is paywalled and unread. He is not presented as a founder. He places environmental history among the 'new' histories 'born or reenergized in the wake of the 1960s' (1993, p. 2), names Nash, Hays, Worster, Merchant, Tarr and Pyne as practitioners (pp. 2–3), footnotes lessons to Crosby, Richard White, McEvoy and others (pp. 8–10), and echoes Leopold (n. 11). On Mahoney’s reading (1992, p. 492), Nature’s Metropolis demonstrates 'this fundamental urban historical premise' rather than inventing it. His approaches were contested in period: Danbom (1992, p. 503) finds 'environmental moralism'; Mahoney (p. 491) that urban and economic history 'seem only to serve the interests of environmental history'; Sessions (1996) that the wilderness essay underweights wild habitat. His 2003 afterword, McNeill (2003) and Canadian uses after 2000 are later readings.","new":"This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-13: chapter 1 of Changes in the Land in an unpaginated web transcription; 'A Place for Stories' (JAH 1992; pp. 1347–1351 and 1369–1376 read) and 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993; OCR of the author’s scan, journal pagination); 'Kennecott Journey' (1992); passages of 'The Trouble with Wilderness' in the author’s reflow; and the 1992 Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response read in full. Nature’s Metropolis itself is unread: its words appear here only as forum contributors quote them with page numbers. 'Modes of Prophecy and Production', his 1990 response to Worster in the JAH round table, is paywalled and unread. He is not presented as a founder. He places environmental history among the 'new' histories 'born or reenergized in the wake of the 1960s' (1993, p. 2), names Nash, Hays, Worster, Merchant, Tarr and Pyne as practitioners (pp. 2–3), footnotes lessons to Crosby, Richard White, McEvoy and others (pp. 8–10), and echoes Leopold (n. 11). On Mahoney’s reading (1992, p. 492), Nature’s Metropolis demonstrates 'this fundamental urban historical premise' rather than inventing it. His approaches were contested in period: Danbom (1992, p. 503) finds 'environmental moralism'; Mahoney (p. 491) that urban and economic history 'seem only to serve the interests of environmental history'; Sessions (1996) that the wilderness essay underweights wild habitat. His 2003 afterword, McNeill (2003) and Canadian uses after 2000 are later readings. Extended on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): his CV read in full; 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (2003) read in full in OCR; 'Storytelling' (2013) read in passages from the AHA's open text; Foreman's 'The Real Wilderness Idea' (2000) read in full. Unread: his 1996 response to Hays, Cohen and Dunlap (paywalled), whose side of the second round is therefore missing; the 2012 Perspectives columns (titles only); Callicott and Nelson's anthology (publisher description). Hays's 1996 sentence is known only as Foreman quotes it. Wikidata Q8007305 confirmed by QLever.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): reading record."},{"node":"william_cronon","field":"representative_figures_and_works","old":"William Cronon (b. 1954); Changes in the Land: Indians, Colonists, and the Ecology of New England (1983); “Modes of Prophecy and Production: Placing Nature in History” (JAH 1990); Nature’s Metropolis: Chicago and the Great West (1991); “Kennecott Journey: The Paths out of Town” (1992); “A Place for Stories: Nature, History, and Narrative” (JAH 1992); “The Uses of Environmental History” (1993); “The Trouble with Wilderness” (1995). Readings: the Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response (1992); Sessions’s reply on wilderness (1996); his own afterword to Changes in the Land (2003).","new":"William Cronon (b. 1954); Changes in the Land: Indians, Colonists, and the Ecology of New England (1983); “Modes of Prophecy and Production: Placing Nature in History” (JAH 1990); Nature’s Metropolis: Chicago and the Great West (1991); “Kennecott Journey: The Paths out of Town” (1992); “A Place for Stories: Nature, History, and Narrative” (JAH 1992); “The Uses of Environmental History” (1993); “The Trouble with Wilderness” (1995). Readings: the Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response (1992); Sessions’s reply on wilderness (1996); his own afterword to Changes in the Land (2003). Later: 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (Orion, 2003); 'Storytelling' (AHR, 2013); Weyerhaeuser Environmental Books, series editor 1994–2014. Readers: Hays (1996, as quoted by Foreman); Callicott and Nelson, eds. (1998); Foreman (2000).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md)."},{"node":"donald_worster","field":"description","old":"A historian of the American Great Plains and arid West who set out a three-level model of environmental history. In 'Doing Environmental History' (1988) he wrote that 'there are three levels on which the new history proceeds': 'understanding nature itself', 'the socioeconomic realm as it interacts with the environment', and 'the purely mental or intellectual', which together 'constitute a single dynamic inquiry'. His 1990 Journal of American History essay turned to 'that middle level, the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena', chiefly the capitalist transformation of agriculture, which Dust Bowl (1979) had called 'the decisive factor in this nation's use of nature'; Rivers of Empire (1985) described the West as 'a modern hydraulic society'. Merchant's 1990 response called the levels 'a significant framework for research and writing in this emerging field' but found that the essay 'lacks a gender analysis'. Cronon (1992) read Dust Bowl as a story whose 'chief agent' is capitalism and whose plot 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy'.","new":"A historian of the American Great Plains and arid West who set out a three-level model of environmental history. In 'Doing Environmental History' (1988) he wrote that 'there are three levels on which the new history proceeds': 'understanding nature itself', 'the socioeconomic realm as it interacts with the environment', and 'the purely mental or intellectual', which together 'constitute a single dynamic inquiry'. His 1990 Journal of American History essay turned to 'that middle level, the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena', chiefly the capitalist transformation of agriculture, which Dust Bowl (1979) had called 'the decisive factor in this nation's use of nature'; Rivers of Empire (1985) described the West as 'a modern hydraulic society'. Merchant's 1990 response called the levels 'a significant framework for research and writing in this emerging field' but found that the essay 'lacks a gender analysis'. Cronon (1992) read Dust Bowl as a story whose 'chief agent' is capitalism and whose plot 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy'. After the round table he turned to lives and to limits: biographies of John Wesley Powell (2001) and John Muir (2008; the Muir essay of 2005), and Shrinking the Earth (2016), which Collomb read as a history of the 'Second Earth' of 1500 that gave settlers 'the false impression that there was no limit', by 'the greatest US environmental historian' — while finding its 'plea for an earth ethic almost rings hollow'. He held the Hall chair at Kansas (1989–2012) and received the AHA's Award for Scholarly Distinction (2012).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P6/P7: the later career."},{"node":"donald_worster","field":"scope_note","old":"This entry presents the three-level model in Worster's 1988 wording, checked in an abridged reprint and by full-text search of the 1988 edition, and the 1990 essay in his own 1993 reprint; the Journal of American History pages of 1990 were not reachable and are given only where another writer cites them (Isenberg 2013). Dust Bowl and Rivers of Empire are quoted at the pages given by Cronon (1992) and Hennessey (1987), with the wording confirmed by search of the original editions. He is not presented as the founder of the field or the sole author of the scheme: he grounds the idea in Aldo Leopold's call for 'an ecological interpretation of history', and Arthur McEvoy's ecology, production and cognition triad appeared in the same 1988 volume. The scheme was contested at once: Merchant (1990) added gender and 'a fourth analytical level, that of reproduction', Cronon (1992) treated Dust Bowl as a declensionist narrative, and McDean (1986) criticised its generalisation across the Plains. The 1990 objections of White and Cronon in the round table are known here only through Isenberg (2013), a later reading.","new":"This entry presents the three-level model in Worster's 1988 wording, checked in an abridged reprint and by full-text search of the 1988 edition, and the 1990 essay in his own 1993 reprint; the Journal of American History pages of 1990 were not reachable and are given only where another writer cites them (Isenberg 2013). Dust Bowl and Rivers of Empire are quoted at the pages given by Cronon (1992) and Hennessey (1987), with the wording confirmed by search of the original editions. He is not presented as the founder of the field or the sole author of the scheme: he grounds the idea in Aldo Leopold's call for 'an ecological interpretation of history', and Arthur McEvoy's ecology, production and cognition triad appeared in the same 1988 volume. The scheme was contested at once: Merchant (1990) added gender and 'a fourth analytical level, that of reproduction', Cronon (1992) treated Dust Bowl as a declensionist narrative, and McDean (1986) criticised its generalisation across the Plains. The 1990 objections of White and Cronon in the round table are known here only through Isenberg (2013), a later reading. Extended on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): the Oxford record of The Wealth of Nature (contents; the opening paragraph of 'Transformations' as its abstract), the Origins author page, Columbia's Bancroft list and the AHA award list read; Collomb (2017) and Gratale (2017) on Shrinking the Earth read in full. The biographies of Powell and Muir and 'John Muir and the Modern Passion for Nature' (2005) are known by metadata only, so no edge rests on them; the retirement year (2012) and the Renmin appointment rest on a search snippet and the Origins page; the ASEH presidency has no years found. The Internet Archive routes in the older records for Nature's Economy, Rivers of Empire and The Wealth of Nature are not reused; those records now point to publisher pages, their earlier reading notes kept. Wikidata Q1240502 confirmed by QLever.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): reading record."},{"node":"donald_worster","field":"representative_figures_and_works","old":"Donald Worster (b. 1941); Nature's Economy: The Roots of Ecology (1977); Dust Bowl: The Southern Plains in the 1930s (1979); Rivers of Empire: Water, Aridity, and the Growth of the American West (1985); 'Appendix: Doing Environmental History', in The Ends of the Earth (1988); 'Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History', Journal of American History (1990), reprinted in The Wealth of Nature (1993). Readings: Merchant's round-table response (1990); Cronon, 'A Place for Stories' (1992); Hennessey's review of Rivers of Empire (1987); McDean (1986).","new":"Donald Worster (b. 1941); Nature's Economy: The Roots of Ecology (1977); Dust Bowl: The Southern Plains in the 1930s (1979); Rivers of Empire: Water, Aridity, and the Growth of the American West (1985); 'Appendix: Doing Environmental History', in The Ends of the Earth (1988); 'Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History', Journal of American History (1990), reprinted in The Wealth of Nature (1993). Readings: Merchant's round-table response (1990); Cronon, 'A Place for Stories' (1992); Hennessey's review of Rivers of Empire (1987); McDean (1986). Later: A River Running West (2001); 'John Muir and the Modern Passion for Nature' (2005); A Passion for Nature (2008); Shrinking the Earth (2016). Readers: Collomb (2017); Gratale (2017).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md)."}]
