{"id":"archival_turn","label":"The archive as object","date_label":"Le goût de l'archive 1989 · Archive Fever 1994/1996 · Silencing the Past 1995 · Dust 2001 · Archival Science 2 'archives, records, and power' 2002 · Archive Stories 2005 · 'Venus in Two Acts' 2008 · Along the Archival Grain 2009 · Dispossessed Lives 2016 · Trouillot Remixed 2021","layer":"historiographical_developments","period":"period_4","hunt_core_paradigm":false,"description":"A way of reading that takes the archive as an object of study rather than a quarry of facts. Trouillot's Silencing the Past (1995) located the silences of history in the making of sources, archives, narratives and 'history in the final instance', and made the Haitian Revolution the case of an event 'unthinkable' as it happened. Stoler asked colonial historians to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002) and to read 'along the archival grain' before reading against it, finding in Dutch colonial records a 'repository of good taste and bad faith' (2009, as quoted by Gouda). Farge's Le goût de l'archive (1989), Derrida's Archive Fever (1994) and Steedman's Dust (2001) wrote the archive's allure, fever and dust; Schwartz and Cook, from the archival profession, called archives 'active sites where social power is negotiated' (2002); Burton's Archive Stories (2005) collected historians' encounters with their sources; Hartman's 'critical fabulation' (2008) and Fuentes (2016) confronted the violence of slavery's archive. No opposing side formed: the History PhD citation graph finds Stoler the strongest riser of 2015–25 and the cluster a framework without a debate.","representative_figures_and_works":"Founding texts: Arlette Farge, Le goût de l'archive (1989; The Allure of the Archives, 2013, foreword Natalie Zemon Davis); Thomas Richards, The Imperial Archive (1993); Jacques Derrida, Mal d'archive / Archive Fever (lecture 1994; Diacritics 1995; Chicago 1996); Michel-Rolph Trouillot, Silencing the Past (1995; 20th-anniversary edition with Hazel V. Carby's foreword, 2015); Saidiya Hartman, Scenes of Subjection (1997). The turn named: the History of the Human Sciences issue 'The Archive' (1999); Terry Cook, 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth' (Archivaria 51, 2001); Carolyn Steedman, Dust and 'Something She Called a Fever' (2001); Archival Science 2:1–2, 'archives, records, and power' (2002: Joan M. Schwartz and Terry Cook; Ann Laura Stoler, 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance'); Refiguring the Archive (2002: Achille Mbembe, 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits'; Stoler); From the Margins (2002: Nicholas B. Dirks, 'Annals of the Archive'); Diana Taylor, The Archive and the Repertoire (2003); Ann Cvetkovich, An Archive of Feelings (2003); Marlene Manoff, 'Theories of the Archive from Across the Disciplines' (2004); Antoinette Burton, ed., Archive Stories (2005: Durba Ghosh, Tony Ballantyne, Adele Perry, Ann Curthoys). The programme: Stoler, Along the Archival Grain (2009) and the Bijdragen debate (Gouda, Raben, Schulte Nordholt, 2009); Anjali Arondekar, For the Record (2009); Hartman, 'Venus in Two Acts' (Small Axe 26, 2008); Francis X. Blouin and William G. Rosenberg, Processing the Past (2011); David Zeitlyn, 'Anthropology in and of the Archives' (2012); Marisa J. Fuentes, Dispossessed Lives (2016); Trouillot Remixed (2021). Readers: Frances Gouda, Remco Raben, Henk Schulte Nordholt (2009); Alyssa Goldstein Sepinwall (2013); Marlene L. Daut (2021); Bastien Craipain (2024).","source_ids":["derrida_archive_primary","p4a_arondekar_record_2009","p4a_bulamah_vibrant_2020","p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005","p4a_cohorts_release_2026","p4a_cook_archivaria_1997","p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","p4a_craipain_hnet_2024","p4a_daut_beyond_2021","p4a_davidson_overland_2020","p4a_derrida_archive_fever_chicago_1996","p4a_derrida_diacritics_1995","p4a_farge_allure_2013","p4a_from_the_margins_2002","p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016","p4a_gouda_bki_2009","p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019","p4a_hartman_venus_2008","p4a_jhs_19_2_2013","p4a_manoff_2004","p4a_mbembe_power_2002","p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004","p4a_raben_bki_2009","p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009","p4a_schwartz_cook_2002","p4a_sepinwall_2013","p4a_steedman_dust_2002","p4a_steedman_fever_2001","p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009","p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_stoler_bki_reply_2009","p4a_stoler_carnal_2002","p4a_stoler_haunted_2006","p4a_stoler_refiguring_2002","p4a_taylor_archive_repertoire_2003","p4a_trouillot_global_2003","p4a_trouillot_remixed_2021","p4a_trouillot_savage_slot_2003","p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995","p4a_trouillot_uchicago_obit_2012","p4a_zeitlyn_2012","w3_spivak_rani_1985"],"entry_kind":"group","entry_type":"Research field","scope_note":"A Research-field entry gathering a readership rather than a school: in the History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23) the share of Canada + US theses citing two or more of Stoler, Trouillot, Farge, Derrida, Hartman, Steedman, Burton, Fuentes, Blouin and Rosenberg rose from 0 (1990s) to 3.3% (2000–09), 7.1%, 7.6% and 10.4% (2020–25; US 11.8%, Canada 9.0%; Europe 2.1%), with Stoler cited by 10.7% of theses in 2020–25 across 11 of 18 fields and Trouillot by 5.9%; no opposing side is co-cited (cohorts README §5), and Along the Archival Grain entered the global/transnational neighbourhood of PhDs in 2020–25. Six strands: the archive as a site of power (Trouillot, Mbembe, Schwartz and Cook); reading along the grain (Stoler); allure, fever and dust (Farge, Derrida, Steedman); absence, violence and critical fabulation (Hartman, Fuentes); archive stories and the ethnography of archives (Burton, Dirks, Zeitlyn); the archivists' turn in Canada and South Africa (Cook, Schwartz, Harris, Brothman). Research of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md): read in full — Gouda (2009), Schulte Nordholt (2009), Ottevaere (2004), Schultz (2015), Craipain (2024), the Chicago obituary and the finding aid for Trouillot; in part — Cook (2001), the Bulamah–Goyatá–Pereira introduction (2020), Davidson (2020); abstracts and contents only — Stoler 2002, Schwartz and Cook 2002, Mbembe 2002, Manoff 2004, Trouillot 2003, Along the Archival Grain, Archive Stories, From the Margins, Haunted by Empire, Duress, For the Record, The Archive and the Repertoire. Unread: the bodies of Silencing the Past (the four-moments passage is given as later writers list it and as Davidson quotes it, unpaginated; wording awaits re-verification on a library copy of ch. 1), Along the Archival Grain, Carnal Knowledge, Archive Fever, Dust and the AHR essay, Le goût de l'archive, 'Venus in Two Acts', Dispossessed Lives, The Imperial Archive, Processing the Past, Zeitlyn 2012, the HHS 1999 issue, Sepinwall 2013, Daut 2021, Carby's foreword. Richards (1993), Blouin and Rosenberg (2011) and the HHS issue are named in the works list but not dated as events (no page reached). Indigenous data sovereignty and the digital archive are out of scope (see web_history for the 2024 uptake of Stoler's phrase).","representative_people":[{"person_id":"michel_rolph_trouillot","role":"historian","context":"Silences enter history at the making of sources, archives, narratives and 'history in the final instance'; 'in history, power begins at the source' (as quoted by Davidson, 2020, unpaginated; wording awaits a library copy); the Haitian Revolution 'unthinkable' as it happened (ch. 3 title); 'an unavoidable reference' three decades on (Craipain, 2024). Body unread.","works":"Silencing the Past (1995; 20th-anniversary edition 2015)","source_ids":["p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995","p4a_davidson_overland_2020","p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"ann_laura_stoler","role":"historian","context":"'From archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002, abstract); 'the pulse of the archive' and 'an ethnography of the archives' (2009, pp. 35, 45, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt); read along the grain before against it (Gouda, 2009). Her Dutch reviewers' doubts are recorded in the strand.","works":"'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002); Along the Archival Grain (2009)","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009","p4a_gouda_bki_2009","p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"arlette_farge","role":"precursor","context":"Le goût de l'archive (1989): the sensory and emotional experience of working in the judicial records of eighteenth-century Paris (Yale description); 'an eloquent testimony to the materiality of the archive' (Hunt, TLS). English 2013 with Davis's foreword. Unread.","works":"Le goût de l'archive (1989); The Allure of the Archives (2013)","source_ids":["p4a_farge_allure_2013"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"derrida","role":"contributor","context":"Archive Fever (1994/1996), on the memory, freud and language rosters already; Cook's 'Derrida aftershock' (2001, p. 21) dates its reception by archivists; Burton's introduction is 'Archive Fever, Archive Stories' (2005).","works":"Archive Fever (lecture 1994; Diacritics 1995; Chicago 1996)","source_ids":["derrida_archive_primary","p4a_derrida_archive_fever_chicago_1996","p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"carolyn_steedman","role":"historian","context":"Dust (2001) and the AHR essay on 'Michelet, Derrida, and Dust' (2001): the archive as the repository of 'that which will not go away'; 'archivization' (publisher description). Unread.","works":"Dust (2001/2002); 'Something She Called a Fever' (2001)","source_ids":["p4a_steedman_dust_2002","p4a_steedman_fever_2001"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"saidiya_hartman","role":"historian","context":"'Venus in Two Acts' (2008) and 'critical fabulation', a 'speculative method … which questions historical archives as the sole source of credible information about the past' (MacArthur, 2019). Unread.","works":"Scenes of Subjection (1997); Lose Your Mother (2007); 'Venus in Two Acts' (2008)","source_ids":["p4a_hartman_venus_2008","p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"marisa_j_fuentes","role":"historian","context":"Dispossessed Lives (2016) 'interrogates the archive and its historical production' for enslaved and free women in Bridgetown (publisher description). Unread.","works":"Dispossessed Lives (2016)","source_ids":["p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"antoinette_burton","role":"historian","context":"Edited Archive Stories (2005): historians' 'actual encounters' with archives, against 'the claims to objectivity associated with the traditional archive' (publisher); introduction 'Archive Fever, Archive Stories'. Unread.","works":"Archive Stories (2005)","source_ids":["p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"terry_cook","role":"contributor","context":"Canadian archivist: 'When Jacques Derrida … devotes an entire book, his 1996 Archive Fever, to the very raison d'être of the archival profession, something significant is happening' (2001, p. 21); with Schwartz, archives as 'active sites where social power is negotiated' (2002, abstract).","works":"'What Is Past Is Prologue' (1997); 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth' (2001); with Schwartz, 'Archives, Records, and Power' (2002)","source_ids":["p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","p4a_cook_archivaria_1997","p4a_schwartz_cook_2002"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"joan_m_schwartz","role":"contributor","context":"Co-editor of Archival Science's 'archives, records, and power' issues (2002); National Archives of Canada / Queen's. Abstract read.","works":"'Archives, Records, and Power' (2002)","source_ids":["p4a_schwartz_cook_2002"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"achille_mbembe","role":"contributor","context":"'The Power of the Archive and its Limits' (2002): the archive 'first refers to a building, a symbol of a public institution' and cannot be defined without it (abstract).","works":"'The Power of the Archive and its Limits' (2002)","source_ids":["p4a_mbembe_power_2002"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"nicholas_b_dirks","role":"contributor","context":"'Annals of the Archive: Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History' opened 'Ethnography and the Archive' in From the Margins (2002); Stoler's 2002 notes cite his 'Colonial Histories and Native Informants: Biography of an Archive'. Unread.","works":"'Annals of the Archive' (2002)","source_ids":["p4a_from_the_margins_2002","p4a_stoler_archives_2002"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"adele_perry","role":"contributor","context":"'The Colonial Archive on Trial: Possession, Dispossession, and History in Delgamuukw v. British Columbia' in Archive Stories (2005). Contents only; chapter unread.","works":"'The Colonial Archive on Trial' (2005)","source_ids":["p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"frances_gouda","role":"critic","context":"Found Along the Archival Grain 'resplendent with questions and insights' but with 'so little engagement with the work of contemporary Dutch historians' (2009, pp. 552, 555). Read in full.","works":"'A race horse among work horses in Dutch archival pastures' (2009)","source_ids":["p4a_gouda_bki_2009"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"henk_schulte_nordholt","role":"critic","context":"'I am not quite sure how to identify these \"pulses\"'; the case studies 'have not yet set a new standard'; archives in Indonesia ignored: 'an unexpected colonial flavour' (2009, pp. 560–562). Read in full.","works":"'How colonial is this effort to establish a new standard for an ethnography of the archive?' (2009)","source_ids":["p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009"],"basis":"source_review"},{"person_id":"marlene_l_daut","role":"critic","context":"'Beyond Trouillot: Unsettling Genealogies of Historical Thought' (Small Axe 25:1, 2021), as cited by Craipain (2024, n. 8). Unread.","works":"'Beyond Trouillot' (2021)","source_ids":["p4a_daut_beyond_2021","p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"],"basis":"source_review"}],"strands":[{"id":"site_of_power","title":"The archive as a site of power: Trouillot, Mbembe, Schwartz and Cook","focus":"Trouillot's Silencing the Past (1995) holds that silences enter historical production at four moments — the making of sources, of archives, of narratives and of 'history in the final instance', as every later summary lists them — and that 'in history, power begins at the source' and 'archives are the institutions that organise facts and sources' (as quoted by Davidson, 2020, unpaginated; wording awaits re-verification on a library copy of ch. 1). Craipain (2024) reads the book as a 'critical indictment of Western historiography' whose 'historical guild' relegated 'racism, slavery and colonialism' to 'an historical backburner' (Trouillot, p. 98, as quoted by Craipain). From the archival profession, Schwartz and Cook opened Archival Science's 'archives, records, and power' issues (2002) with the claim that archives 'are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated' (abstract); Mbembe's 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits' (2002) begins from the archive as 'a building, a symbol of a public institution' (abstract).","person_ids":["michel_rolph_trouillot","achille_mbembe","joan_m_schwartz","terry_cook"],"works":"Silencing the Past (1995); 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits' (2002); 'Archives, Records, and Power' (2002)","source_ids":["p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995","p4a_davidson_overland_2020","p4a_craipain_hnet_2024","p4a_schwartz_cook_2002","p4a_mbembe_power_2002"],"basis":"editorial_distinction"},{"id":"along_the_grain","title":"Reading along the grain: Stoler's ethnography of the colonial archive","focus":"Stoler's 2002 essay asked anthropologists in colonial studies, whose 'archival activity tends to remain more an extractive than an ethnographic one', to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (abstract). Along the Archival Grain (2009) treated Dutch colonial records 'not just as repositories of state power, but as unquiet movements in a field of force' (pp. 32–33, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt), looked 'for the pulse of the archive in the quiescence and quickened pace of its own production' (p. 35) and aimed 'to set a new standard for an ethnography of the archives' (p. 45) rather than an ethnography in them. Gouda: she 'underscores the necessity to read along the grain before trying to read against it, as many postcolonial scholars in recent years have advocated' (2009, p. 552). The reviewers' doubts are part of the record: Schulte Nordholt was 'not quite sure how to identify these \"pulses\"' and found 'an unexpected colonial flavour' in a book that ignored archives in Indonesia and Indonesian scholars (pp. 560, 562); Gouda found 'so little engagement with the work of contemporary Dutch historians' (p. 555).","person_ids":["ann_laura_stoler","frances_gouda","henk_schulte_nordholt","remco_raben"],"works":"'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002); Along the Archival Grain (2009); the Bijdragen debate (2009)","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009","p4a_gouda_bki_2009","p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009","p4a_raben_bki_2009","p4a_stoler_bki_reply_2009"],"basis":"editorial_distinction"},{"id":"allure_fever_dust","title":"Allure, fever and dust: Farge, Derrida, Steedman","focus":"Three texts made the historian's experience of the archive their subject. Farge's Le goût de l'archive (1989; English 2013, with Natalie Zemon Davis's foreword) is, in Yale's words, a reflection on the sensory and emotional experience of archival work in the judicial records of eighteenth-century Paris; Arnold Hunt called it 'an eloquent testimony to the materiality of the archive and its power to astonish and delight'. Derrida's Archive Fever (lecture 1994; Diacritics 1995; Chicago 1996), already on the derrida, memory and freud entries, is 'an extended meditation on remembrance, religion, time, and technology' that starts from archiving (publisher). Steedman's Dust (2001) and 'Something She Called a Fever: Michelet, Derrida, and Dust' (AHR 106:4, 2001) answered Derrida with the archive as the repository of 'that which will not go away' and named 'archivization' as the practice of attending to it (publisher description). Terry Cook dated the 'Derrida aftershock', in which historians and others were 'subjecting the archival world to a detailed critique such as it has never before experienced', from inside the profession (2001, p. 21). All three bodies unread.","person_ids":["arlette_farge","derrida","carolyn_steedman","natalie_zemon_davis"],"works":"Le goût de l'archive (1989); Archive Fever (1994/1996); Dust (2001); 'Something She Called a Fever' (2001)","source_ids":["p4a_farge_allure_2013","p4a_derrida_archive_fever_chicago_1996","p4a_derrida_diacritics_1995","p4a_steedman_fever_2001","p4a_steedman_dust_2002","p4a_cook_archivaria_2001"],"basis":"editorial_distinction"},{"id":"absence_fabulation","title":"Absence, violence and critical fabulation: Hartman, Fuentes","focus":"Hartman's 'Venus in Two Acts' (Small Axe 26, June 2008) examines a girl's presence and silence in the archive of Atlantic slavery; the MacArthur Foundation describes her 'speculative method she terms \"critical fabulation,\" which questions historical archives as the sole source of credible information about the past' (2019). Scenes of Subjection (1997) 'traces continuities between pre- and post-emancipation eras' (MacArthur). Fuentes's Dispossessed Lives (2016), on enslaved and free women in eighteenth-century Bridgetown, 'interrogates the archive and its historical production to expose the ongoing effects' of slavery's violence, 'combining fragmentary sources with interdisciplinary methodologies that include black feminist theory' (publisher). Both are unread here; the strand records their place in the readership (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23: Hartman 4.3% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25, from 0 in 2000–09; Fuentes a 2020–25 entrant to the US capitalism and Indigenous networks).","person_ids":["saidiya_hartman","marisa_j_fuentes"],"works":"Scenes of Subjection (1997); 'Venus in Two Acts' (2008); Dispossessed Lives (2016)","source_ids":["p4a_hartman_venus_2008","p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019","p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016","p4a_cohorts_release_2026"],"basis":"editorial_distinction"},{"id":"archive_stories_ethnography","title":"Archive stories and the ethnography of archives: Burton, Dirks, Zeitlyn","focus":"Burton's Archive Stories (Duke, 2005) collected, in the press's words, ethnographies of the archival world by historians that challenge 'the claims to objectivity associated with the traditional archive'; its introduction is titled 'Archive Fever, Archive Stories', and its contributors include Durba Ghosh on miscegenation in Britain and India, Tony Ballantyne on 'Mr. Peal's Archive', Jennifer Milligan on 'What Is an Archive?' in modern France, Adele Perry on 'The Colonial Archive on Trial' in Delgamuukw v. British Columbia, and Ann Curthoys. Dirks's 'Annals of the Archive: Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History' opened Part 1, 'Ethnography and the Archive', of From the Margins (2002), the volume that also printed Stoler and Trouillot. Zeitlyn's 'Anthropology in and of the Archives' (Annual Review of Anthropology 41, 2012) and Manoff's survey of 'theories of the archive from across the disciplines' (2004), which found 'a confluence of interests among scholars, archivists, and librarians', mark the turn's consolidation. All bodies unread; the strand rests on contents and abstracts.","person_ids":["antoinette_burton","nicholas_b_dirks","durba_ghosh","adele_perry"],"works":"From the Margins (2002); The Archive and the Repertoire (2003); 'Theories of the Archive from Across the Disciplines' (2004); Archive Stories (2005); 'Anthropology in and of the Archives' (2012)","source_ids":["p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005","p4a_from_the_margins_2002","p4a_taylor_archive_repertoire_2003","p4a_zeitlyn_2012","p4a_manoff_2004"],"basis":"editorial_distinction"},{"id":"archivists_turn_canada","title":"The archivists' turn: Canada and South Africa","focus":"The profession's own turn ran beside and partly before the historians'. Cook names 'the pioneering postmodern archivists before Cook' as 'also Canadian, Brien Brothman and Richard Brown' (2001, n. 13). His plenary to the Association of Canadian Archivists (Winnipeg, 8 June 2001), printed as 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth', argued that appraisal should let archives 'acquire in its holdings multiple voices, and not by default only the voices of the powerful', while, 'as Verne Harris notes', not romanticising the marginalised (pp. 31–32), and that 'the Canadian parallel way of archival remembering through macro-appraisal may speak strongly to archivists in this new century' (p. 32). Schwartz (National Archives of Canada / Queen's) and Cook (Manitoba) then edited the 'archives, records, and power' issues of Archival Science (2002), citing Derrida and Harris, not Foucault or Stoler (reference list). Harris's South African work and Refiguring the Archive (2002) are the other pole. Perry's chapter on the colonial archive before the courts in Delgamuukw (2005) is the Canadian historians' link, unread.","person_ids":["terry_cook","joan_m_schwartz","adele_perry"],"works":"'What Is Past Is Prologue' (1997); 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth' (2001); 'Archives, Records, and Power' (2002); Refiguring the Archive (2002)","source_ids":["p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","p4a_cook_archivaria_1997","p4a_schwartz_cook_2002","p4a_mbembe_power_2002","p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"],"basis":"editorial_distinction"}],"coverage":{"reviewed_through":2024,"basis":"Surveyed 1989–2024 in the research of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md) through publisher pages, abstracts, open reviews and the History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23); the founding books themselves are unread beyond their contents and named readers, so the chronology's dates are firm and its characterisations rest on readers. The latest items are Craipain's 2024 review and the 2024 web-archives essay that borrows Stoler's phrase."},"chronology":{"version":1,"label":"The archive as object: selected contributions and readings, 1989–2024","selection_note":"Selected founding texts, the profession's and the historians' responses, editions and readings; not a field origin, lifespan or complete history.","events":[{"id":"at_farge_1989","label":"Farge, Le goût de l'archive (Seuil)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":1989,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_farge_allure_2013"],"support":"Yale page: \"the original was published in 1989\"; Seuil page 404; unread."},{"id":"at_derrida_lecture_1994","label":"Derrida, 'Mal d'archive', lecture (Freud Museum, London)","kind":"lecture_delivery","track":"context","date":{"year":1994,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:derrida_archive_primary"],"support":"Existing graph event on the derrida entry; not re-verified this wave."},{"id":"at_derrida_diacritics_1995","label":"Derrida, 'Archive Fever: A Freudian Impression', Diacritics 25:2, trans. Prenowitz","kind":"publication","track":"context","date":{"year":1995,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_derrida_diacritics_1995"],"support":"Crossref: vol. 25, no. 2, from p. 9, 1995."},{"id":"at_trouillot_silencing_1995","label":"Trouillot, Silencing the Past: Power and the Production of History (Beacon)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":1995,"month":11,"day":21,"precision":"day"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995"],"support":"Beacon contents read; the day (21 November 1995) from a trade listing seen in a search rendering; body unread."},{"id":"at_derrida_chicago_1996","label":"Derrida, Archive Fever (University of Chicago Press, English book)","kind":"edition_publication","track":"editions","date":{"year":1996,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_derrida_archive_fever_chicago_1996"],"support":"Chicago page: © 1996, viii + 114 pp."},{"id":"at_brothman_cook_1997","label":"Brothman's review of Archive Fever and Cook's 'What Is Past Is Prologue', Archivaria 43","kind":"reception_publication","track":"reception","date":{"year":1997,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","graph:p4a_cook_archivaria_1997"],"support":"Cook 2001, n. 13 (read) for Brothman's review; Archivaria page for Cook 1997 (abstract)."},{"id":"at_hartman_scenes_1997","label":"Hartman, Scenes of Subjection (OUP)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":1997,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019"],"support":"MacArthur page (read); unread."},{"id":"at_cook_plenary_2001","label":"Cook, plenary to the Association of Canadian Archivists, Winnipeg; printed as 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth', Archivaria 51 (Spring 2001)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2001,"month":6,"day":8,"precision":"day"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_cook_archivaria_2001"],"support":"Note *, p. 14 (read): plenary 8 June 2001."},{"id":"at_steedman_2001","label":"Steedman, 'Something She Called a Fever: Michelet, Derrida, and Dust', AHR 106:4; Dust (Manchester 2001; Rutgers 2002)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2001,"month":10,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_steedman_fever_2001","graph:p4a_steedman_dust_2002"],"support":"Crossref (October 2001, pp. 1159–1180); Rutgers page (1 February 2002); unread."},{"id":"at_archival_science_2002","label":"Archival Science 2:1–2, 'archives, records, and power': Schwartz and Cook, 1–19; Stoler, 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance', 87–109","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2002,"month":3,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_schwartz_cook_2002","graph:p4a_stoler_archives_2002"],"support":"Crossref (March 2002); abstracts and reference lists read."},{"id":"at_refiguring_2002","label":"Refiguring the Archive (Hamilton, Harris et al., eds; Kluwer): Mbembe, 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits'; Stoler's essay reprinted","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2002,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_mbembe_power_2002","graph:p4a_stoler_refiguring_2002"],"support":"Crossref chapter records; Mbembe's abstract opening read."},{"id":"at_from_the_margins_2002","label":"From the Margins (Axel, ed.; Duke): Dirks, 'Annals of the Archive'; Stoler, 'Developing Historical Negatives'; Trouillot, 'Culture on the Edges'","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2002,"month":6,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_from_the_margins_2002"],"support":"Duke page, contents (read)."},{"id":"at_stoler_carnal_2002","label":"Stoler, Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (UC Press)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2002,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_stoler_carnal_2002","graph:p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004"],"support":"UC Press page (2010 edition); Ottevaere gives \"University of California Press, 2002. xi + 335 pp.\""},{"id":"at_manoff_2004","label":"Manoff, 'Theories of the Archive from Across the Disciplines', portal 4:1","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2004,"month":1,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_manoff_2004"],"support":"Crossref (January 2004); DSpace abstract read; body unread."},{"id":"at_burton_2005","label":"Burton, ed., Archive Stories (Duke; released January 2006)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2005,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"],"support":"Duke page (contents; \"January 2006\"); Crossref issued 2005."},{"id":"at_hartman_venus_2008","label":"Hartman, 'Venus in Two Acts', Small Axe 26","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2008,"month":6,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_hartman_venus_2008"],"support":"Crossref (1 June 2008, pp. 1–14); unread."},{"id":"at_stoler_grain_2009","label":"Stoler, Along the Archival Grain (Princeton; © 2008)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2009,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009","graph:p4a_gouda_bki_2009"],"support":"MUSE record (contents); Gouda: \"Princeton University Press, 2009. xiii + 314 pp.\""},{"id":"at_bki_debate_2009","label":"Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 'Debate': Gouda, Raben, Schulte Nordholt on Along the Archival Grain, with Stoler's reply","kind":"reception_publication","track":"reception","date":{"year":2009,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_gouda_bki_2009","graph:p4a_raben_bki_2009","graph:p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009","graph:p4a_stoler_bki_reply_2009"],"support":"Gouda and Schulte Nordholt read in full; Raben's first page; Stoler's first paragraph."},{"id":"at_trouillot_death_2012","label":"Death of Michel-Rolph Trouillot, Chicago","kind":"death","track":"context","date":{"year":2012,"month":7,"day":5,"precision":"day"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_trouillot_uchicago_obit_2012"],"support":"Chicago News, 10 July 2012 (read)."},{"id":"at_zeitlyn_2012","label":"Zeitlyn, 'Anthropology in and of the Archives', Annual Review of Anthropology 41","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2012,"month":10,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_zeitlyn_2012"],"support":"Crossref (21 October 2012); unread."},{"id":"at_sepinwall_2013","label":"Sepinwall, 'Still Unthinkable? The Haitian Revolution and the Reception of … Silencing the Past', Journal of Haitian Studies 19:2 (special issue on Trouillot)","kind":"reception_publication","track":"reception","date":{"year":2013,"month":9,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_sepinwall_2013","graph:p4a_jhs_19_2_2013"],"support":"Crossref (September 2013); contents page read; article unread."},{"id":"at_farge_english_2013","label":"Farge, The Allure of the Archives (Yale; trans. Scott-Railton; foreword Davis)","kind":"translation_publication","track":"editions","date":{"year":2013,"month":9,"day":24,"precision":"day"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_farge_allure_2013"],"support":"Yale page (e-book 24 September 2013; paperback 24 February 2015)."},{"id":"at_silencing_2015","label":"Silencing the Past, 20th-anniversary edition with Hazel V. Carby's foreword (Beacon)","kind":"edition_publication","track":"editions","date":{"year":2015,"month":3,"day":17,"precision":"day"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995"],"support":"Beacon page (17 March 2015); foreword unread."},{"id":"at_fuentes_2016","label":"Fuentes, Dispossessed Lives (Penn)","kind":"publication","track":"contributions","date":{"year":2016,"precision":"year"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016"],"support":"Google Books (CA) record; Penn page 403; unread."},{"id":"at_daut_2021","label":"Daut, 'Beyond Trouillot: Unsettling Genealogies of Historical Thought', Small Axe 25:1","kind":"reception_publication","track":"reception","date":{"year":2021,"month":3,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_daut_beyond_2021","graph:p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"],"support":"As cited by Craipain (2024, n. 8); unread."},{"id":"at_remixed_2021","label":"Trouillot Remixed: The Michel-Rolph Trouillot Reader (Bonilla, Beckett and Fernando, eds; Duke)","kind":"retrospective_publication","track":"editions","date":{"year":2021,"month":12,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_trouillot_remixed_2021"],"support":"Duke page (contents)."},{"id":"at_craipain_2024","label":"Craipain's review of Stirring the Pot of Haitian History (H-Caribbean): Silencing the Past 'an unavoidable reference'","kind":"reception_publication","track":"reception","date":{"year":2024,"month":10,"precision":"month"},"source_ids":["graph:p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"],"support":"Read in full."}],"intervals":[],"continuation":{"status":"not_asserted","basis":"The readership is still growing in the dissertation data through 2025; no cessation is established and no interval is drawn."},"interpretation":"Dates mark selected texts and events. Farge (1989), Derrida (1994–96) and Trouillot (1995) are the texts the later writers answered; the entry is not dated from them as a founding. The 2009 Bijdragen debate and the 2013 and 2021 readings of Trouillot are dated reception, not intervals.","prepared_on":"2026-10-23","editorial_record":"data/production-batches/group-revision-1.156/acceptance.json"},"aliases":["Archival turn","The archive as subject","Ethnography of the archive","Archive studies"]}
proposed_edges
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_01","source":"post","target":"archival_turn","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"influence","directed":true,"relationship":"Colonial studies supplied the turn's founding texts: Stoler's call to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002) came from 'anthropologists engaged in post-colonial studies'; Dirks's 'Annals of the Archive' (2002), Burton's Archive Stories (2005) and Arondekar (2009) worked colonial archives","map_label":"Colonial archives as the turn's first object, 2002–09","evidence_note":"Stoler 2002 abstract (read): its first sentence names post-colonial studies; From the Margins and Archive Stories contents (Duke pages read); Arondekar's Duke page (read). No body read; the claim is about where the texts came from, not their arguments. data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.1.4.","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_from_the_margins_2002","p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005","p4a_arondekar_record_2009"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"archival_turn/archive_stories_ethnography"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_02","source":"derrida","target":"archival_turn","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"influence","directed":true,"relationship":"Archive Fever (1994/1996) is the text the turn answers: Cook dated a 'Derrida aftershock' in which historians and anthropologists subjected 'the archival world to a detailed critique such as it has never before experienced' (2001); Steedman's AHR essay (2001) and Burton's introduction (2005) carry his title","map_label":"The Derrida aftershock, 1996–2005","evidence_note":"Cook 2001, p. 21 (read); Steedman's and Burton's titles (Crossref; Duke contents); Stoler 2002, n. 21, and Schwartz and Cook 2002, nn. 15 and 17, cite Archive Fever (reference lists read). The existing arrow edge_588 (derrida → memory) is not repeated; this one carries the archive readership.","source_ids":["p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","p4a_steedman_fever_2001","p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005","p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_schwartz_cook_2002"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"archival_turn/allure_fever_dust","roster_row":{"node":"archival_turn","person_id":"derrida"}}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_03","source":"foucault","target":"archival_turn","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"influence","directed":true,"relationship":"The Archaeology's 'The Statement and the Archive' and 'Nietzsche, Genealogy, History' are the Foucault the turn cites: Stoler's 2002 notes open with the latter and twice cite the former; Cook names 'Foucault's historical methodology' among his own exposures (2001); Manoff's survey begins from Derrida and Foucault (2004)","map_label":"The archive after the Archaeology, 2001–04","evidence_note":"Stoler 2002 reference list (nn. 1, 30, 42; read); Cook 2001, n. 23 (read); Manoff's abstract (DSpace record). Thin: rests on citations, not on a passage stating the debt.","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","p4a_manoff_2004"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"archival_turn/site_of_power"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_04","source":"archival_turn","target":"memory","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"Schwartz and Cook put the archivist's own choices into 'the making of modern memory': archives 'are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated', and appraisal, description and access shape collective memory (2002); Trouillot's 'The Presence in the Past' and 'Abortive Rituals' (2000) addressed commemoration and apology","map_label":"Archives in the making of memory, 2000–02","evidence_note":"Schwartz and Cook 2002 abstract (read); Trouillot's chapter and essay titles (Beacon and Duke contents, read); the memory entry's strand archive_authority (Combe, Derrida) is the existing hook.","source_ids":["p4a_schwartz_cook_2002","p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995","p4a_trouillot_remixed_2021"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"memory/archive_authority"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_05","source":"archival_turn","target":"atlantic","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"The archive of slavery became the turn's hardest case: Trouillot's 'unthinkable' Haitian Revolution (1995), Hartman's 'Venus in Two Acts' (2008) and 'critical fabulation', Fuentes's Dispossessed Lives (2016) on Bridgetown's enslaved women; Craipain: 'an unavoidable reference' for 'Black and Caribbean studies' (2024)","map_label":"Slavery's archive, 1995–2016","evidence_note":"Craipain 2024 (read in full); MacArthur's description of critical fabulation (read); Fuentes's publisher description (read); Trouillot's ch. 3 title (Beacon contents). Hartman and Fuentes unread.","source_ids":["p4a_craipain_hnet_2024","p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019","p4a_hartman_venus_2008","p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016","p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"atlantic/slave_society"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_06","source":"archival_turn","target":"anthropology","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"'An ethnography of the archives' rather than in them: Stoler's standard (2009, p. 45, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt), after her 2002 complaint that anthropologists' 'archival activity tends to remain more an extractive than an ethnographic one'; Dirks's 'Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History' (2002); Zeitlyn's review (2012)","map_label":"Ethnography of the archive, 2002–12","evidence_note":"Stoler 2002 abstract (read); Schulte Nordholt 2009, pp. 560–562 (read: 'less often accustomed to making archives into an object of ethnographic research'); Dirks and Zeitlyn by title (Duke contents; Crossref).","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009","p4a_from_the_margins_2002","p4a_zeitlyn_2012"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"anthropology/colonial_knowledge"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_07","source":"archival_turn","target":"language","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"comparison","directed":false,"relationship":"The turn is the linguistic turn's successor in the archive: Cook presented the profession's engagement as 'postmodernism and the practice of archives' (2001); Manoff found 'a confluence of interests among scholars, archivists, and librarians' started by Derrida and Foucault (2004); but Stoler's programme reads 'along the grain' first, against 'reading against the grain' (Gouda)","map_label":"After the linguistic turn: the archive, 2001–09","evidence_note":"Cook 2001 title and abstract (read); Manoff's abstract (DSpace record); Gouda 2009, p. 552 (read). A comparison, not a critique: no text read names the linguistic turn as its target.","source_ids":["p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","p4a_manoff_2004","p4a_gouda_bki_2009"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"language/discourse"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_08","source":"subaltern","target":"archival_turn","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"comparison","directed":false,"relationship":"Reading 'against the grain' for the subaltern (Spivak's 'Rani of Sirmur', 1985) is the practice Stoler's 'along the grain' answers: Gouda, 'the necessity to read along the grain before trying to read against it, as many postcolonial scholars in recent years have advocated' (2009)","map_label":"Against and along the grain, 1985–2009","evidence_note":"Gouda 2009, p. 552 (read); Spivak 1985 by title (graph source w3_spivak_rani_1985, metadata only). Thin: Gouda does not name Subaltern Studies; the Spivak essay is unread. The coordinator may hold it.","source_ids":["p4a_gouda_bki_2009","w3_spivak_rani_1985"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"archival_turn/along_the_grain"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_09","source":"archival_turn","target":"black","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"Hartman's 'critical fabulation' and Fuentes's black-feminist reading of 'fragmentary sources' (2016) take the archive's violence as the problem of Black history; Trouillot 'remains an unavoidable reference for anyone navigating the streams of Black and Caribbean studies' (Craipain, 2024)","map_label":"The violence of the archive in Black history, 2008–16","evidence_note":"MacArthur's description (read); Fuentes's publisher description (read); Craipain 2024 (read). The black entry's strand archives ('Collecting and diaspora bibliography') is the hook.","source_ids":["p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019","p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016","p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"black/archives"}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_10","source":"archival_turn","target":"global","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"Trouillot's Global Transformations (2003) set 'North Atlantic universals' and the 'savage slot' against the anthropology of globalization, beginning from the claim that 'anthropology discovered globalization before the term became fashionable'; Stoler's 'Tense and Tender Ties' (2001) carried the colonial archive's questions into North American history","map_label":"The archive in global history, 2001–03","evidence_note":"Springer abstract of the Savage Slot chapter (read) and the Bulamah, Goyatá and Pereira reading (para. 5, read); Duke description of Haunted by Empire (read). The dissertation finding that Along the Archival Grain entered the global/transnational neighbourhood of PhDs in 2020–25 is kept in the scope note, not here.","source_ids":["p4a_trouillot_global_2003","p4a_trouillot_savage_slot_2003","p4a_bulamah_vibrant_2020","p4a_stoler_haunted_2006"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"global/connected"}
edges_considered_not_proposed
{"pair":"archival_turn → indigenous (contribution)","reason":"Held. Perry's 'The Colonial Archive on Trial' (Delgamuukw) and Curthoys's chapter in Archive Stories are the evidence, contents only; no chapter read."}
{"pair":"archival_turn → annales","reason":"Not drawn. Farge is placed as a precursor row on this entry; no Annales statement was read."}
{"pair":"archival_turn → gender / queer","reason":"Held. Arondekar's For the Record and Cvetkovich's An Archive of Feelings would carry them; both unread."}
{"pair":"michel_rolph_trouillot ↔ ann_laura_stoler","reason":"Held. Stoler's 2002 notes cite Silencing the Past three times (nn. 24, 38, 49), but no sentence of hers on Trouillot was read; recorded as a citation fact in her scope note."}
{"pair":"ann_laura_stoler → foucault / → queer (p4A_rel_13, p4A_rel_14)","reason":"Not drawn: the coordinator chose to re-source edge_386 and edge_417 to the person entry instead (batch-fragment-A.json, edge_revisions)."}
new_sources
{"id":"p4a_stoler_archives_2002","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance,\" Archival Science 2:1–2 (March 2002): 87–109.","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance,\" Archival Science 2:1–2 (March 2002): 87–109.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02435632","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Springer page: abstract, keywords and reference list read for period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §1); the body is paywalled and unread. Crossref: vol. 2, issue 1–2, pp. 87–109, March 2002. The abstract carries \"from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject\"; the notes cite Foucault, Derrida, Trouillot, Richards, Dirks and Cohn."}}
{"id":"p4a_stoler_refiguring_2002","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance: On the Content in the Form,\" in Carolyn Hamilton et al., eds, Refiguring the Archive (Dordrecht: Kluwer, 2002), 83–102.","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance: On the Content in the Form,\" in Carolyn Hamilton et al., eds, Refiguring the Archive (Dordrecht: Kluwer, 2002), 83–102.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-010-0570-8_7","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref only; the reprint of the Archival Science essay. Unread."}}
{"id":"p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, Along the Archival Grain: Epistemic Anxieties and Colonial Common Sense (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 2009).","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, Along the Archival Grain: Epistemic Anxieties and Colonial Common Sense (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 2009).","url":"https://muse.jhu.edu/book/61029","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Project MUSE book record: summary and contents with page ranges read (Prologue in Two Parts 1–16; The Pulse of the Archive 17–54; chs 3–7; appendices; © 2008 on the record; Gouda gives \"2009. xiii + 314 pp.\"). Princeton's page and chapter PDF returned 403; the body is unread; every quotation is as given by Gouda or Schulte Nordholt with their page numbers (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §2)."}}
{"id":"p4a_gouda_bki_2009","title":"Frances Gouda, \"A race horse among work horses in Dutch archival pastures\" [review of Stoler, Along the Archival Grain], Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 552–556.","citation":"Frances Gouda, \"A race horse among work horses in Dutch archival pastures\" [review of Stoler, Along the Archival Grain], Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 552–556.","url":"https://hdl.handle.net/11245/1.310957","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Read in full as the final published version in the University of Amsterdam repository (UvA-DARE) for period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §2a); the PDF also prints the first page of Raben's review (p. 556). The journal's own host (kitlv-journals.nl) has lapsed; Brill's issue page returned 403."}}
{"id":"p4a_raben_bki_2009","title":"Remco Raben, \"Ambiguities of reading and writing\" [review of Stoler, Along the Archival Grain], Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 556–560.","citation":"Remco Raben, \"Ambiguities of reading and writing\" [review of Stoler, Along the Archival Grain], Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 556–560.","url":null,"verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"First page (p. 556) read at the foot of Gouda's PDF (UvA-DARE); the rest unread; the page range is inferred from the adjacent reviews. No URL of its own found."}}
{"id":"p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009","title":"Henk Schulte Nordholt, \"How colonial is this effort to establish a new standard for an ethnography of the archive?\" [review of Stoler, Along the Archival Grain], Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 560–562.","citation":"Henk Schulte Nordholt, \"How colonial is this effort to establish a new standard for an ethnography of the archive?\" [review of Stoler, Along the Archival Grain], Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 560–562.","url":"https://hdl.handle.net/11245/1.311281","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Read in full (UvA-DARE final published version) for period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §2a); quotes the book's pp. 32–33, 35 and 45. The PDF also prints the first paragraph of Stoler's reply (p. 562)."}}
{"id":"p4a_stoler_bki_reply_2009","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Thinking through colonial ontologies,\" Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 562–.","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Thinking through colonial ontologies,\" Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 (2009): 562–.","url":null,"verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"First paragraph read at the foot of Schulte Nordholt's PDF; the end page is unknown and the reply unread; Brill's issue page returned 403. No URL of its own found."}}
{"id":"p4a_schwartz_cook_2002","title":"Joan M. Schwartz and Terry Cook, \"Archives, Records, and Power: The Making of Modern Memory,\" Archival Science 2:1–2 (March 2002): 1–19.","citation":"Joan M. Schwartz and Terry Cook, \"Archives, Records, and Power: The Making of Modern Memory,\" Archival Science 2:1–2 (March 2002): 1–19.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02435628","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Abstract, keywords and reference list read on Springer (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §3): \"Archives, then, are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated\"; references cite Derrida and Verne Harris, not Foucault, Stoler or Trouillot. Body paywalled and unread."}}
{"id":"p4a_cook_archivaria_2001","title":"Terry Cook, \"Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth: Postmodernism and the Practice of Archives,\" Archivaria 51 (Spring 2001): 14–35.","citation":"Terry Cook, \"Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth: Postmodernism and the Practice of Archives,\" Archivaria 51 (Spring 2001): 14–35.","url":"https://archivaria.ca/index.php/archivaria/article/view/12792","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Open-access PDF read in part for period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §5): the opening and note * (pp. 14–15), p. 21, pp. 31–32, notes 13, 23, 33, 36–37; the rest skimmed by keyword. Origin: plenary to the Association of Canadian Archivists, Winnipeg, 8 June 2001."}}
{"id":"p4a_cook_archivaria_1997","title":"Terry Cook, \"What Is Past Is Prologue: A History of Archival Ideas Since 1898, and the Future Paradigm Shift,\" Archivaria 43 (Spring 1997): 17–63.","citation":"Terry Cook, \"What Is Past Is Prologue: A History of Archival Ideas Since 1898, and the Future Paradigm Shift,\" Archivaria 43 (Spring 1997): 17–63.","url":"https://archivaria.ca/index.php/archivaria/article/view/12175","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Landing page and abstract read; body unread."}}
{"id":"p4a_stoler_carnal_2002","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power: Race and the Intimate in Colonial Rule (Berkeley: University of California Press, 2002; 2nd ed. with a new preface, 2010).","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power: Race and the Intimate in Colonial Rule (Berkeley: University of California Press, 2002; 2nd ed. with a new preface, 2010).","url":"https://www.ucpress.edu/books/carnal-knowledge-and-imperial-power/paper","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"UC Press page for the 2010 edition read: description, contents (chs 1–7 and epilogue), author line. The 2002 edition's pagination is taken from Ottevaere's review (p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004). Body unread."}}
{"id":"p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004","title":"Dawn Ottevaere, review of Ann Stoler, Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power, H-Women, H-Net Reviews (June 2004).","citation":"Dawn Ottevaere, review of Ann Stoler, Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power, H-Women, H-Net Reviews (June 2004).","url":"https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=9492","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Read in full for period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §6a); fetched with curl (H-Net permits redistribution with attribution). Quotes the book's pp. 9, 13, 15, 78, 83, 141, 162, 203 and gives \"Berkeley: University of California Press, 2002. xi + 335 pp.\""}}
{"id":"p4a_schultz_hnet_2015","title":"Ronald Schultz, review of Ann Laura Stoler, ed., Imperial Debris: On Ruins and Ruination, H-Empire, H-Net Reviews (February 2015).","citation":"Ronald Schultz, review of Ann Laura Stoler, ed., Imperial Debris: On Ruins and Ruination, H-Empire, H-Net Reviews (February 2015).","url":"https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=41934","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Read in full (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §6c); quotes pp. 7 and 9 of Stoler's \"The Rot Remains\" and gives the book's ISBNs and 384 pp."}}
{"id":"p4a_stoler_debris_2013","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, ed., Imperial Debris: On Ruins and Ruination (Durham: Duke University Press, 2013).","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, ed., Imperial Debris: On Ruins and Ruination (Durham: Duke University Press, 2013).","url":"https://www.dukeupress.edu/imperial-debris","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Duke page not fetched this session; the details (2013, 384 pp., ISBN 978-0-8223-5361-4 paper) are from Schultz's review. Body unread."}}
{"id":"p4a_stoler_haunted_2006","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, ed., Haunted by Empire: Geographies of Intimacy in North American History (Durham: Duke University Press, 2006).","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, ed., Haunted by Empire: Geographies of Intimacy in North American History (Durham: Duke University Press, 2006).","url":"https://www.dukeupress.edu/haunted-by-empire","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Description and contents read (May 2006, 568 pp.; Stoler's \"Intimidations of Empire\" p. 1 and \"Tense and Tender Ties\" p. 23; Hall's commentary, Cott's afterword). Bodies unread."}}
{"id":"p4a_stoler_tense_2001","title":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Tense and Tender Ties: The Politics of Comparison in North American History and (Post) Colonial Studies,\" Journal of American History 88:3 (December 2001): 829–865.","citation":"Ann Laura Stoler, \"Tense and Tender Ties: The Politics of Comparison in North American History and (Post) Colonial Studies,\" Journal of American History 88:3 (December 2001): 829–865.","url":"https://doi.org/10.2307/2700385","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref (first page 829, December 2001); the last page is not confirmed; unread."}}
