{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_11","source":"ann_laura_stoler","target":"archival_turn","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"Asked colonial studies to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' and from 'extractive' to 'ethnographic' archival work (2002); read the Dutch colonial archive 'along the grain' for 'the pulse of the archive' and set out 'to set a new standard for an ethnography of the archives' (2009, pp. 35, 45, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt)","map_label":"Archive as subject, 2002–09","evidence_note":"Own words: the 2002 abstract (read); the 2009 book's pp. 32–33, 35 and 45 as quoted by Schulte Nordholt (read in full), and pp. 3, 5, 24, 41, 43, 53 as quoted by Gouda (read in full). Both books unread. data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §§1–2.","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009","p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009","p4a_gouda_bki_2009"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"archival_turn/along_the_grain","roster_row":{"node":"archival_turn","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler"}}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_12","source":"ann_laura_stoler","target":"post","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"With Cooper, an agenda for reading metropole and colony together (1989; 1997); Carnal Knowledge argued that 'students of colonialism, anthropologists in particular, have taken the politically constructed dichotomy colonizer/colonized as a given rather than as a historically shifting pair of social categories' (2002, p. 13); Gouda: homage to her work 'has become de rigueur' in postcolonial studies","map_label":"Colonial categories as the problem, 1989–2009","evidence_note":"Ottevaere 2004 (read in full), quoting pp. 9, 13 and 15; Gouda 2009, pp. 553–554 (read); Crossref for the 1989 introduction; the graph's existing record of Tensions of Empire (contents). The 1989 and 1997 introductions are unread. The post entry's strands colonial_intimacies and metropole_colony already name her.","source_ids":["p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004","p4a_stoler_carnal_2002","p4a_gouda_bki_2009","p4a_cooper_stoler_ae_1989","cooper_stoler_tensions_1997"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"post/metropole_colony","roster_row":{"node":"post","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler"}}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_15","source":"ann_laura_stoler","target":"gender","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"'Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power: Gender and Morality in the Making of Race' (ch. 3, 2002): 'Ethnographies of empire should attend both to changing sensibilities and to sex, to racialized regimes that were realized on a macro and micro scale' (p. 78); métissage as 'the tension between a form of authority simultaneously predicated on incorporating and distancing' (p. 83); 'Like Joan Scott', she evaluates 'historically designated categories' (Ottevaere)","map_label":"Gender in the making of race, 2002","evidence_note":"Ottevaere 2004 (read in full), quoting pp. 78 and 83 and making the Scott comparison; UC Press contents (read). Chapter unread.","source_ids":["p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004","p4a_stoler_carnal_2002"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"gender/colonial_categories","roster_row":{"node":"gender","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler"}}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_16","source":"ann_laura_stoler","target":"anthropology","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"'An ethnography of the archives' as a standard for the discipline (2009, p. 45, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt), against 'doing an ethnography in the archives'; her 2002 complaint that anthropologists' 'archival activity tends to remain more an extractive than an ethnographic one'; Schulte Nordholt: good historians 'are less often accustomed to making archives into an object of ethnographic research'","map_label":"Ethnography of, not in, the archive, 2002–09","evidence_note":"Stoler 2002 abstract (read); Schulte Nordholt 2009, pp. 560–562 (read in full).","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_archives_2002","p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"anthropology/colonial_knowledge","roster_row":{"node":"anthropology","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler"}}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_17","source":"ann_laura_stoler","target":"memory","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"'Memory-Work in Java: A Cautionary Tale' (2002, ch. 7) 'situates memories of domestic service by those who served against the density of the archives around them' (p. 162) and 'invites more work on colonial memory itself while making the \"colonial\" a subject rather than an assumed category of analysis' (p. 203, as quoted by Ottevaere)","map_label":"Colonial memory against the archive, 2002","evidence_note":"Ottevaere 2004 (read in full), quoting pp. 162 and 203; UC Press contents (read). Chapter unread.","source_ids":["p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004","p4a_stoler_carnal_2002"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"memory/subjectivity","roster_row":{"node":"memory","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler"}}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_18","source":"ann_laura_stoler","target":"global","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"'Tense and Tender Ties: The Politics of Comparison in North American History and (Post) Colonial Studies' (JAH 2001; Haunted by Empire, 2006) brought postcolonial critiques to North American history and questioned the conventions of comparison (Duke description); Imperial Debris (2013) asked colonial scholars 'to move beyond the theoretical and empirical limitations of postcolonial and subaltern studies' toward ongoing 'ruination' (Schultz)","map_label":"The politics of comparison and ruination, 2001–16","evidence_note":"Duke page for Haunted by Empire (contents and description, read); Crossref for the 2001 article; Schultz 2015 (read in full), quoting 'violent verb' (p. 7) and 'what people are left with' (p. 9); Duke page for Duress (contents: 'On Degrees of Imperial Sovereignty'). Bodies unread; the comparison claim rests on a publisher description.","source_ids":["p4a_stoler_tense_2001","p4a_stoler_haunted_2006","p4a_schultz_hnet_2015","p4a_stoler_debris_2013","p4a_stoler_duress_2016"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"global/comparison","roster_row":{"node":"global","person_id":"ann_laura_stoler"}}
{"proposal_id":"p4A_rel_19","source":"ann_laura_stoler","target":"adele_perry","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"influence","directed":true,"relationship":"Perry named Stoler (with McClintock, Said and Burton) in the 'international literature on gender and colonialism' that inspired On the Edge of Empire (2001), with a caution about analysing 'white discourse and white discourse alone' (1995); Perry later wrote 'The Colonial Archive on Trial' in Burton's Archive Stories (2005)","map_label":"Stoler in Perry's reading, 1995–2005","evidence_note":"Rests on the evidence already recorded for edge_1023 (post → adele_perry): Perry's own 2001 statement and her 1995 caution, read in an earlier revision (pe139_perry_presence_2001, pe139_perry_sick_1995); Burton's contents (Duke page, read). The person-level arrow makes the influence that edge_1023 attributes to a field attributable to its named author; edge_1023 itself is unchanged. Perry's chapter is unread.","source_ids":["pe139_perry_presence_2001","pe139_perry_sick_1995","p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review"}
