node
strands [{"id": "site_of_power", "title": "The archive as a site of power: Trouillot, Mbembe, Schwartz and Cook", "focus": "Trouillot's Silencing the Past (1995) holds that silences enter historical production at four moments — the making of sources, of archives, of narratives and of 'history in the final instance', as every later summary lists them — and that 'in history, power begins at the source' and 'archives are the institutions that organise facts and sources' (as quoted by Davidson, 2020, unpaginated; wording awaits re-verification on a library copy of ch. 1). Craipain (2024) reads the book as a 'critical indictment of Western historiography' whose 'historical guild' relegated 'racism, slavery and colonialism' to 'an historical backburner' (Trouillot, p. 98, as quoted by Craipain). From the archival profession, Schwartz and Cook opened Archival Science's 'archives, records, and power' issues (2002) with the claim that archives 'are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated' (abstract); Mbembe's 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits' (2002) begins from the archive as 'a building, a symbol of a public institution' (abstract).", "person_ids": ["michel_rolph_trouillot", "achille_mbembe", "joan_m_schwartz", "terry_cook"], "works": "Silencing the Past (1995); 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits' (2002); 'Archives, Records, and Power' (2002)", "source_ids": ["p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995", "p4a_davidson_overland_2020", "p4a_craipain_hnet_2024", "p4a_schwartz_cook_2002", "p4a_mbembe_power_2002"], "basis": "editorial_distinction"}, {"id": "along_the_grain", "title": "Reading along the grain: Stoler's ethnography of the colonial archive", "focus": "Stoler's 2002 essay asked anthropologists in colonial studies, whose 'archival activity tends to remain more an extractive than an ethnographic one', to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (abstract). Along the Archival Grain (2009) treated Dutch colonial records 'not just as repositories of state power, but as unquiet movements in a field of force' (pp. 32–33, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt), looked 'for the pulse of the archive in the quiescence and quickened pace of its own production' (p. 35) and aimed 'to set a new standard for an ethnography of the archives' (p. 45) rather than an ethnography in them. Gouda: she 'underscores the necessity to read along the grain before trying to read against it, as many postcolonial scholars in recent years have advocated' (2009, p. 552). The reviewers' doubts are part of the record: Schulte Nordholt was 'not quite sure how to identify these \"pulses\"' and found 'an unexpected colonial flavour' in a book that ignored archives in Indonesia and Indonesian scholars (pp. 560, 562); Gouda found 'so little engagement with the work of contemporary Dutch historians' (p. 555).", "person_ids": ["ann_laura_stoler", "frances_gouda", "henk_schulte_nordholt", "remco_raben"], "works": "'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002); Along the Archival Grain (2009); the Bijdragen debate (2009)", "source_ids": ["p4a_stoler_archives_2002", "p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009", "p4a_gouda_bki_2009", "p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009", "p4a_raben_bki_2009", "p4a_stoler_bki_reply_2009"], "basis": "editorial_distinction"}, {"id": "allure_fever_dust", "title": "Allure, fever and dust: Farge, Derrida, Steedman", "focus": "Three texts made the historian's experience of the archive their subject. Farge's Le goût de l'archive (1989; English 2013, with Natalie Zemon Davis's foreword) is, in Yale's words, a reflection on the sensory and emotional experience of archival work in the judicial records of eighteenth-century Paris; Arnold Hunt called it 'an eloquent testimony to the materiality of the archive and its power to astonish and delight'. Derrida's Archive Fever (lecture 1994; Diacritics 1995; Chicago 1996), already on the derrida, memory and freud entries, is 'an extended meditation on remembrance, religion, time, and technology' that starts from archiving (publisher). Steedman's Dust (2001) and 'Something She Called a Fever: Michelet, Derrida, and Dust' (AHR 106:4, 2001) answered Derrida with the archive as the repository of 'that which will not go away' and named 'archivization' as the practice of attending to it (publisher description). Terry Cook dated the 'Derrida aftershock', in which historians and others were 'subjecting the archival world to a detailed critique such as it has never before experienced', from inside the profession (2001, p. 21). All three bodies unread.", "person_ids": ["arlette_farge", "derrida", "carolyn_steedman", "natalie_zemon_davis"], "works": "Le goût de l'archive (1989); Archive Fever (1994/1996); Dust (2001); 'Something She Called a Fever' (2001)", "source_ids": ["p4a_farge_allure_2013", "p4a_derrida_archive_fever_chicago_1996", "p4a_derrida_diacritics_1995", "p4a_steedman_fever_2001", "p4a_steedman_dust_2002", "p4a_cook_archivaria_2001"], "basis": "editorial_distinction"}, {"id": "absence_fabulation", "title": "Absence, violence and critical fabulation: Hartman, Fuentes", "focus": "Hartman's 'Venus in Two Acts' (Small Axe 26, June 2008) examines a girl's presence and silence in the archive of Atlantic slavery; the MacArthur Foundation describes her 'speculative method she terms \"critical fabulation,\" which questions historical archives as the sole source of credible information about the past' (2019). Scenes of Subjection (1997) 'traces continuities between pre- and post-emancipation eras' (MacArthur). Fuentes's Dispossessed Lives (2016), on enslaved and free women in eighteenth-century Bridgetown, 'interrogates the archive and its historical production to expose the ongoing effects' of slavery's violence, 'combining fragmentary sources with interdisciplinary methodologies that include black feminist theory' (publisher). Both are unread here; the strand records their place in the readership (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23: Hartman 4.3% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25, from 0 in 2000–09; Fuentes a 2020–25 entrant to the US capitalism and Indigenous networks).", "person_ids": ["saidiya_hartman", "marisa_j_fuentes"], "works": "Scenes of Subjection (1997); 'Venus in Two Acts' (2008); Dispossessed Lives (2016)", "source_ids": ["p4a_hartman_venus_2008", "p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019", "p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016", "p4a_cohorts_release_2026"], "basis": "editorial_distinction"}, {"id": "archive_stories_ethnography", "title": "Archive stories and the ethnography of archives: Burton, Dirks, Zeitlyn", "focus": "Burton's Archive Stories (Duke, 2005) collected, in the press's words, ethnographies of the archival world by historians that challenge 'the claims to objectivity associated with the traditional archive'; its introduction is titled 'Archive Fever, Archive Stories', and its contributors include Durba Ghosh on miscegenation in Britain and India, Tony Ballantyne on 'Mr. Peal's Archive', Jennifer Milligan on 'What Is an Archive?' in modern France, Adele Perry on 'The Colonial Archive on Trial' in Delgamuukw v. British Columbia, and Ann Curthoys. Dirks's 'Annals of the Archive: Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History' opened Part 1, 'Ethnography and the Archive', of From the Margins (2002), the volume that also printed Stoler and Trouillot. Zeitlyn's 'Anthropology in and of the Archives' (Annual Review of Anthropology 41, 2012) and Manoff's survey of 'theories of the archive from across the disciplines' (2004), which found 'a confluence of interests among scholars, archivists, and librarians', mark the turn's consolidation. All bodies unread; the strand rests on contents and abstracts.", "person_ids": ["antoinette_burton", "nicholas_b_dirks", "durba_ghosh", "adele_perry"], "works": "From the Margins (2002); The Archive and the Repertoire (2003); 'Theories of the Archive from Across the Disciplines' (2004); Archive Stories (2005); 'Anthropology in and of the Archives' (2012)", "source_ids": ["p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005", "p4a_from_the_margins_2002", "p4a_taylor_archive_repertoire_2003", "p4a_zeitlyn_2012", "p4a_manoff_2004"], "basis": "editorial_distinction"}, {"id": "archivists_turn_canada", "title": "The archivists' turn: Canada and South Africa", "focus": "The profession's own turn ran beside and partly before the historians'. Cook names 'the pioneering postmodern archivists before Cook' as 'also Canadian, Brien Brothman and Richard Brown' (2001, n. 13). His plenary to the Association of Canadian Archivists (Winnipeg, 8 June 2001), printed as 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth', argued that appraisal should let archives 'acquire in its holdings multiple voices, and not by default only the voices of the powerful', while, 'as Verne Harris notes', not romanticising the marginalised (pp. 31–32), and that 'the Canadian parallel way of archival remembering through macro-appraisal may speak strongly to archivists in this new century' (p. 32). Schwartz (National Archives of Canada / Queen's) and Cook (Manitoba) then edited the 'archives, records, and power' issues of Archival Science (2002), citing Derrida and Harris, not Foucault or Stoler (reference list). Harris's South African work and Refiguring the Archive (2002) are the other pole. Perry's chapter on the colonial archive before the courts in Delgamuukw (2005) is the Canadian historians' link, unread.", "person_ids": ["terry_cook", "joan_m_schwartz", "adele_perry"], "works": "'What Is Past Is Prologue' (1997); 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth' (2001); 'Archives, Records, and Power' (2002); Refiguring the Archive (2002)", "source_ids": ["p4a_cook_archivaria_2001", "p4a_cook_archivaria_1997", "p4a_schwartz_cook_2002", "p4a_mbembe_power_2002", "p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"], "basis": "editorial_distinction"}]
coverage {"reviewed_through": 2024, "basis": "Surveyed 1989–2024 in the research of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md) through publisher pages, abstracts, open reviews and the History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23); the founding books themselves are unread beyond their contents and named readers, so the chronology's dates are firm and its characterisations rest on readers. The latest items are Craipain's 2024 review and the 2024 web-archives essay that borrows Stoler's phrase."}
chronology {"version": 1, "label": "The archive as object: selected contributions and readings, 1989–2024", "selection_note": "Selected founding texts, the profession's and the historians' responses, editions and readings; not a field origin, lifespan or complete history.", "events": [{"id": "at_farge_1989", "label": "Farge, Le goût de l'archive (Seuil)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 1989, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_farge_allure_2013"], "support": "Yale page: \"the original was published in 1989\"; Seuil page 404; unread."}, {"id": "at_derrida_lecture_1994", "label": "Derrida, 'Mal d'archive', lecture (Freud Museum, London)", "kind": "lecture_delivery", "track": "context", "date": {"year": 1994, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:derrida_archive_primary"], "support": "Existing graph event on the derrida entry; not re-verified this wave."}, {"id": "at_derrida_diacritics_1995", "label": "Derrida, 'Archive Fever: A Freudian Impression', Diacritics 25:2, trans. Prenowitz", "kind": "publication", "track": "context", "date": {"year": 1995, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_derrida_diacritics_1995"], "support": "Crossref: vol. 25, no. 2, from p. 9, 1995."}, {"id": "at_trouillot_silencing_1995", "label": "Trouillot, Silencing the Past: Power and the Production of History (Beacon)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 1995, "month": 11, "day": 21, "precision": "day"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995"], "support": "Beacon contents read; the day (21 November 1995) from a trade listing seen in a search rendering; body unread."}, {"id": "at_derrida_chicago_1996", "label": "Derrida, Archive Fever (University of Chicago Press, English book)", "kind": "edition_publication", "track": "editions", "date": {"year": 1996, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_derrida_archive_fever_chicago_1996"], "support": "Chicago page: © 1996, viii + 114 pp."}, {"id": "at_brothman_cook_1997", "label": "Brothman's review of Archive Fever and Cook's 'What Is Past Is Prologue', Archivaria 43", "kind": "reception_publication", "track": "reception", "date": {"year": 1997, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_cook_archivaria_2001", "graph:p4a_cook_archivaria_1997"], "support": "Cook 2001, n. 13 (read) for Brothman's review; Archivaria page for Cook 1997 (abstract)."}, {"id": "at_hartman_scenes_1997", "label": "Hartman, Scenes of Subjection (OUP)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 1997, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019"], "support": "MacArthur page (read); unread."}, {"id": "at_cook_plenary_2001", "label": "Cook, plenary to the Association of Canadian Archivists, Winnipeg; printed as 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth', Archivaria 51 (Spring 2001)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2001, "month": 6, "day": 8, "precision": "day"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_cook_archivaria_2001"], "support": "Note *, p. 14 (read): plenary 8 June 2001."}, {"id": "at_steedman_2001", "label": "Steedman, 'Something She Called a Fever: Michelet, Derrida, and Dust', AHR 106:4; Dust (Manchester 2001; Rutgers 2002)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2001, "month": 10, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_steedman_fever_2001", "graph:p4a_steedman_dust_2002"], "support": "Crossref (October 2001, pp. 1159–1180); Rutgers page (1 February 2002); unread."}, {"id": "at_archival_science_2002", "label": "Archival Science 2:1–2, 'archives, records, and power': Schwartz and Cook, 1–19; Stoler, 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance', 87–109", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2002, "month": 3, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_schwartz_cook_2002", "graph:p4a_stoler_archives_2002"], "support": "Crossref (March 2002); abstracts and reference lists read."}, {"id": "at_refiguring_2002", "label": "Refiguring the Archive (Hamilton, Harris et al., eds; Kluwer): Mbembe, 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits'; Stoler's essay reprinted", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2002, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_mbembe_power_2002", "graph:p4a_stoler_refiguring_2002"], "support": "Crossref chapter records; Mbembe's abstract opening read."}, {"id": "at_from_the_margins_2002", "label": "From the Margins (Axel, ed.; Duke): Dirks, 'Annals of the Archive'; Stoler, 'Developing Historical Negatives'; Trouillot, 'Culture on the Edges'", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2002, "month": 6, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_from_the_margins_2002"], "support": "Duke page, contents (read)."}, {"id": "at_stoler_carnal_2002", "label": "Stoler, Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (UC Press)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2002, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_stoler_carnal_2002", "graph:p4a_ottevaere_hnet_2004"], "support": "UC Press page (2010 edition); Ottevaere gives \"University of California Press, 2002. xi + 335 pp.\""}, {"id": "at_manoff_2004", "label": "Manoff, 'Theories of the Archive from Across the Disciplines', portal 4:1", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2004, "month": 1, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_manoff_2004"], "support": "Crossref (January 2004); DSpace abstract read; body unread."}, {"id": "at_burton_2005", "label": "Burton, ed., Archive Stories (Duke; released January 2006)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2005, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005"], "support": "Duke page (contents; \"January 2006\"); Crossref issued 2005."}, {"id": "at_hartman_venus_2008", "label": "Hartman, 'Venus in Two Acts', Small Axe 26", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2008, "month": 6, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_hartman_venus_2008"], "support": "Crossref (1 June 2008, pp. 1–14); unread."}, {"id": "at_stoler_grain_2009", "label": "Stoler, Along the Archival Grain (Princeton; © 2008)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2009, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009", "graph:p4a_gouda_bki_2009"], "support": "MUSE record (contents); Gouda: \"Princeton University Press, 2009. xiii + 314 pp.\""}, {"id": "at_bki_debate_2009", "label": "Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde 165:4 'Debate': Gouda, Raben, Schulte Nordholt on Along the Archival Grain, with Stoler's reply", "kind": "reception_publication", "track": "reception", "date": {"year": 2009, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_gouda_bki_2009", "graph:p4a_raben_bki_2009", "graph:p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009", "graph:p4a_stoler_bki_reply_2009"], "support": "Gouda and Schulte Nordholt read in full; Raben's first page; Stoler's first paragraph."}, {"id": "at_trouillot_death_2012", "label": "Death of Michel-Rolph Trouillot, Chicago", "kind": "death", "track": "context", "date": {"year": 2012, "month": 7, "day": 5, "precision": "day"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_trouillot_uchicago_obit_2012"], "support": "Chicago News, 10 July 2012 (read)."}, {"id": "at_zeitlyn_2012", "label": "Zeitlyn, 'Anthropology in and of the Archives', Annual Review of Anthropology 41", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2012, "month": 10, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_zeitlyn_2012"], "support": "Crossref (21 October 2012); unread."}, {"id": "at_sepinwall_2013", "label": "Sepinwall, 'Still Unthinkable? The Haitian Revolution and the Reception of … Silencing the Past', Journal of Haitian Studies 19:2 (special issue on Trouillot)", "kind": "reception_publication", "track": "reception", "date": {"year": 2013, "month": 9, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_sepinwall_2013", "graph:p4a_jhs_19_2_2013"], "support": "Crossref (September 2013); contents page read; article unread."}, {"id": "at_farge_english_2013", "label": "Farge, The Allure of the Archives (Yale; trans. Scott-Railton; foreword Davis)", "kind": "translation_publication", "track": "editions", "date": {"year": 2013, "month": 9, "day": 24, "precision": "day"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_farge_allure_2013"], "support": "Yale page (e-book 24 September 2013; paperback 24 February 2015)."}, {"id": "at_silencing_2015", "label": "Silencing the Past, 20th-anniversary edition with Hazel V. Carby's foreword (Beacon)", "kind": "edition_publication", "track": "editions", "date": {"year": 2015, "month": 3, "day": 17, "precision": "day"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995"], "support": "Beacon page (17 March 2015); foreword unread."}, {"id": "at_fuentes_2016", "label": "Fuentes, Dispossessed Lives (Penn)", "kind": "publication", "track": "contributions", "date": {"year": 2016, "precision": "year"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016"], "support": "Google Books (CA) record; Penn page 403; unread."}, {"id": "at_daut_2021", "label": "Daut, 'Beyond Trouillot: Unsettling Genealogies of Historical Thought', Small Axe 25:1", "kind": "reception_publication", "track": "reception", "date": {"year": 2021, "month": 3, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_daut_beyond_2021", "graph:p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"], "support": "As cited by Craipain (2024, n. 8); unread."}, {"id": "at_remixed_2021", "label": "Trouillot Remixed: The Michel-Rolph Trouillot Reader (Bonilla, Beckett and Fernando, eds; Duke)", "kind": "retrospective_publication", "track": "editions", "date": {"year": 2021, "month": 12, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_trouillot_remixed_2021"], "support": "Duke page (contents)."}, {"id": "at_craipain_2024", "label": "Craipain's review of Stirring the Pot of Haitian History (H-Caribbean): Silencing the Past 'an unavoidable reference'", "kind": "reception_publication", "track": "reception", "date": {"year": 2024, "month": 10, "precision": "month"}, "source_ids": ["graph:p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"], "support": "Read in full."}], "intervals": [], "continuation": {"status": "not_asserted", "basis": "The readership is still growing in the dissertation data through 2025; no cessation is established and no interval is drawn."}, "interpretation": "Dates mark selected texts and events. Farge (1989), Derrida (1994–96) and Trouillot (1995) are the texts the later writers answered; the entry is not dated from them as a founding. The 2009 Bijdragen debate and the 2013 and 2021 readings of Trouillot are dated reception, not intervals.", "prepared_on": "2026-10-23", "editorial_record": "data/production-batches/group-revision-1.156/acceptance.json"}
proposed_edges
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_01", "source": "post", "target": "archival_turn", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "influence", "directed": true, "relationship": "Colonial studies supplied the turn's founding texts: Stoler's call to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002) came from 'anthropologists engaged in post-colonial studies'; Dirks's 'Annals of the Archive' (2002), Burton's Archive Stories (2005) and Arondekar (2009) worked colonial archives", "map_label": "Colonial archives as the turn's first object, 2002–09", "evidence_note": "Stoler 2002 abstract (read): its first sentence names post-colonial studies; From the Margins and Archive Stories contents (Duke pages read); Arondekar's Duke page (read). No body read; the claim is about where the texts came from, not their arguments. data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.1.4.", "source_ids": ["p4a_stoler_archives_2002", "p4a_from_the_margins_2002", "p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005", "p4a_arondekar_record_2009"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "archival_turn/archive_stories_ethnography"}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_02", "source": "derrida", "target": "archival_turn", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "influence", "directed": true, "relationship": "Archive Fever (1994/1996) is the text the turn answers: Cook dated a 'Derrida aftershock' in which historians and anthropologists subjected 'the archival world to a detailed critique such as it has never before experienced' (2001); Steedman's AHR essay (2001) and Burton's introduction (2005) carry his title", "map_label": "The Derrida aftershock, 1996–2005", "evidence_note": "Cook 2001, p. 21 (read); Steedman's and Burton's titles (Crossref; Duke contents); Stoler 2002, n. 21, and Schwartz and Cook 2002, nn. 15 and 17, cite Archive Fever (reference lists read). The existing arrow edge_588 (derrida → memory) is not repeated; this one carries the archive readership.", "source_ids": ["p4a_cook_archivaria_2001", "p4a_steedman_fever_2001", "p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005", "p4a_stoler_archives_2002", "p4a_schwartz_cook_2002"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "archival_turn/allure_fever_dust", "roster_row": {"node": "archival_turn", "person_id": "derrida"}}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_03", "source": "foucault", "target": "archival_turn", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "influence", "directed": true, "relationship": "The Archaeology's 'The Statement and the Archive' and 'Nietzsche, Genealogy, History' are the Foucault the turn cites: Stoler's 2002 notes open with the latter and twice cite the former; Cook names 'Foucault's historical methodology' among his own exposures (2001); Manoff's survey begins from Derrida and Foucault (2004)", "map_label": "The archive after the Archaeology, 2001–04", "evidence_note": "Stoler 2002 reference list (nn. 1, 30, 42; read); Cook 2001, n. 23 (read); Manoff's abstract (DSpace record). Thin: rests on citations, not on a passage stating the debt.", "source_ids": ["p4a_stoler_archives_2002", "p4a_cook_archivaria_2001", "p4a_manoff_2004"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "archival_turn/site_of_power"}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_04", "source": "archival_turn", "target": "memory", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "contribution", "directed": true, "relationship": "Schwartz and Cook put the archivist's own choices into 'the making of modern memory': archives 'are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated', and appraisal, description and access shape collective memory (2002); Trouillot's 'The Presence in the Past' and 'Abortive Rituals' (2000) addressed commemoration and apology", "map_label": "Archives in the making of memory, 2000–02", "evidence_note": "Schwartz and Cook 2002 abstract (read); Trouillot's chapter and essay titles (Beacon and Duke contents, read); the memory entry's strand archive_authority (Combe, Derrida) is the existing hook.", "source_ids": ["p4a_schwartz_cook_2002", "p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995", "p4a_trouillot_remixed_2021"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "memory/archive_authority"}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_05", "source": "archival_turn", "target": "atlantic", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "contribution", "directed": true, "relationship": "The archive of slavery became the turn's hardest case: Trouillot's 'unthinkable' Haitian Revolution (1995), Hartman's 'Venus in Two Acts' (2008) and 'critical fabulation', Fuentes's Dispossessed Lives (2016) on Bridgetown's enslaved women; Craipain: 'an unavoidable reference' for 'Black and Caribbean studies' (2024)", "map_label": "Slavery's archive, 1995–2016", "evidence_note": "Craipain 2024 (read in full); MacArthur's description of critical fabulation (read); Fuentes's publisher description (read); Trouillot's ch. 3 title (Beacon contents). Hartman and Fuentes unread.", "source_ids": ["p4a_craipain_hnet_2024", "p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019", "p4a_hartman_venus_2008", "p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016", "p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "atlantic/slave_society"}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_06", "source": "archival_turn", "target": "anthropology", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "contribution", "directed": true, "relationship": "'An ethnography of the archives' rather than in them: Stoler's standard (2009, p. 45, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt), after her 2002 complaint that anthropologists' 'archival activity tends to remain more an extractive than an ethnographic one'; Dirks's 'Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History' (2002); Zeitlyn's review (2012)", "map_label": "Ethnography of the archive, 2002–12", "evidence_note": "Stoler 2002 abstract (read); Schulte Nordholt 2009, pp. 560–562 (read: 'less often accustomed to making archives into an object of ethnographic research'); Dirks and Zeitlyn by title (Duke contents; Crossref).", "source_ids": ["p4a_stoler_archives_2002", "p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009", "p4a_from_the_margins_2002", "p4a_zeitlyn_2012"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "anthropology/colonial_knowledge"}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_07", "source": "archival_turn", "target": "language", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "comparison", "directed": false, "relationship": "The turn is the linguistic turn's successor in the archive: Cook presented the profession's engagement as 'postmodernism and the practice of archives' (2001); Manoff found 'a confluence of interests among scholars, archivists, and librarians' started by Derrida and Foucault (2004); but Stoler's programme reads 'along the grain' first, against 'reading against the grain' (Gouda)", "map_label": "After the linguistic turn: the archive, 2001–09", "evidence_note": "Cook 2001 title and abstract (read); Manoff's abstract (DSpace record); Gouda 2009, p. 552 (read). A comparison, not a critique: no text read names the linguistic turn as its target.", "source_ids": ["p4a_cook_archivaria_2001", "p4a_manoff_2004", "p4a_gouda_bki_2009"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "language/discourse"}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_08", "source": "subaltern", "target": "archival_turn", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "comparison", "directed": false, "relationship": "Reading 'against the grain' for the subaltern (Spivak's 'Rani of Sirmur', 1985) is the practice Stoler's 'along the grain' answers: Gouda, 'the necessity to read along the grain before trying to read against it, as many postcolonial scholars in recent years have advocated' (2009)", "map_label": "Against and along the grain, 1985–2009", "evidence_note": "Gouda 2009, p. 552 (read); Spivak 1985 by title (graph source w3_spivak_rani_1985, metadata only). Thin: Gouda does not name Subaltern Studies; the Spivak essay is unread. The coordinator may hold it.", "source_ids": ["p4a_gouda_bki_2009", "w3_spivak_rani_1985"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "archival_turn/along_the_grain"}
{"proposal_id": "p4A_rel_09", "source": "archival_turn", "target": "black", "type": "connection", "relationship_kind": "contribution", "directed": true, "relationship": "Hartman's 'critical fabulation' and Fuentes's black-feminist reading of 'fragmentary sources' (2016) take the archive's violence as the problem of Black history; Trouillot 'remains an unavoidable reference for anyone navigating the streams of Black and Caribbean studies' (Craipain, 2024)", "map_label": "The violence of the archive in Black history, 2008–16", "evidence_note": "MacArthur's description (read); Fuentes's publisher description (read); Craipain 2024 (read). The black entry's strand archives ('Collecting and diaspora bibliography') is the hook.", "source_ids": ["p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019", "p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016", "p4a_craipain_hnet_2024"], "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "target_strand": "black/archives"}
