description "An American anthropologist of Dutch and French colonial rule whose work moved from the plantation belt of Sumatra to the colonial archive itself. With Frederick Cooper she set an agenda for reading metropole and colony together (1989; Tensions of Empire, 1997). Race and the Education of Desire (1995) asked why 'Foucault's elusive and suggestive treatment of race still remains so marginal to what colonial historians take from him' (2002, p. 141, as quoted by Ottevaere); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002) treated 'colonizer/colonized' not 'as a given' but as 'a historically shifting pair of social categories that needs to be explained' (p. 13). 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002) asked scholars to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject'; Along the Archival Grain (2009) read Dutch records 'along the grain' for 'the pulse of the archive' and 'the habits of a colonial heart'. Dutch reviewers found the pulses hard to identify and Indonesian archives absent. Imperial Debris (2013) and Duress (2016) turned to ruination and 'imperial durabilities'. Gouda: in postcolonial studies 'paying homage to Ann Stoler's work has become de rigueur' (2009)."
representative_figures_and_works "Ann Laura Stoler (b. 1949); Capitalism and Confrontation in Sumatra's Plantation Belt, 1870–1979 (1985); with Frederick Cooper, 'Tensions of Empire' (American Ethnologist, 1989) and Tensions of Empire (eds, 1997); Race and the Education of Desire (1995); 'Tense and Tender Ties' (JAH, 2001); 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (Archival Science, 2002); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002; 2nd ed. 2010); ed., Haunted by Empire (2006); with McGranahan and Perdue, eds, Imperial Formations (2007); Along the Archival Grain (2009); ed., Imperial Debris (2013); Duress (2016); Interior Frontiers (2022). Readers: Dawn Ottevaere (2004); Frances Gouda, Remco Raben, Henk Schulte Nordholt (2009); Ronald Schultz (2015)."
entry_kind "person"
entry_type "Individual thinker or historian"
scope_note "Read on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §§1–2, 6): the abstract and reference list of the 2002 Archival Science essay; the contents of Along the Archival Grain and three reviews from the Bijdragen debate (Gouda and Schulte Nordholt in full, Raben's first page) with the first paragraph of Stoler's reply; Ottevaere's H-Net review of Carnal Knowledge (in full) and the UC Press contents; Schultz's H-Net review of Imperial Debris (in full); the Duke pages for Haunted by Empire and Duress. Unread: every book's body, the 2002 essay's body, 'Tense and Tender Ties' (2001), the Cooper–Stoler introductions of 1989 and 1997 (metadata and contents only, as the graph already records), Imperial Formations (2007), Interior Frontiers (2022), and Stoler's reply to her Dutch critics beyond its opening. Her 2002 notes cite Trouillot's Silencing the Past three times; no sentence of hers on Trouillot was read. Quotations from the 2009 book are 'as quoted by' Gouda or Schulte Nordholt, from Carnal Knowledge 'as quoted by' Ottevaere, from Imperial Debris 'as quoted by' Schultz, with their page numbers. Wikidata Q16203101 checked by QLever on 2026-10-23 (born 1949; anthropologist, historian; VIAF 263247736). Posts from the New School's pages (search rendering; the page itself returned 429) and the UC Press author line; the Wisconsin years (1983–89) and degrees (Barnard BA 1972, Columbia PhD 1982) from Wikipedia's citation of New School pages, orientation only; the 1985 book is not dated as an event because no publisher page was fetched. History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23): cited by 10.7% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25, the strongest riser of 2015–25, in 11 of 18 fields; Along the Archival Grain 4.4% and Carnal Knowledge 3.8% of theses 2015–25 (figures for this note, not for arrows)."
