id "michel_rolph_trouillot"
label "Michel-Rolph Trouillot"
date_label "Ti dife boule sou istwa Ayiti 1977 · Peasants and Capital 1988 · Haiti: State against Nation 1990 · 'The Odd and the Ordinary' 1990 · 'Anthropology and the Savage Slot' 1991 · Silencing the Past 1995 · Global Transformations 2003 · Trouillot Remixed 2021"
layer "historians"
period null
hunt_core_paradigm false
description "A Haitian anthropologist and historian who left Port-au-Prince for New York in 1968 and wrote his first history of the Haitian Revolution in Creole (Ti dife boule, 1977). Peasants and Capital (1988) and Haiti: State against Nation (1990) studied Dominica's economy and Duvalierism; 'The Odd and the Ordinary' (1990) refused Haitian exceptionalism. Silencing the Past (1995) asked how power enters 'the production of history': silences enter at four moments, in the making of sources, archives, narratives and 'history in the final instance'; 'in history, power begins at the source' (as quoted by Davidson); the Haitian Revolution was 'unthinkable' as it happened, and the 'historical guild' relegated 'racism, slavery and colonialism' to 'an historical backburner' (p. 98, as quoted by Craipain). 'Anthropology and the Savage Slot' (1991) and Global Transformations (2003) turned the same question on anthropology's 'North Atlantic universals'. Craipain: three decades on, Silencing the Past 'remains an unavoidable reference' (2024); Daut asked readers to go 'beyond Trouillot' (2021). The 2015 edition carries Hazel Carby's foreword; Trouillot Remixed (2021) collects the essays."
representative_figures_and_works "Michel-Rolph Trouillot (1949–2012); Ti dife boule sou istwa Ayiti (1977; Stirring the Pot of Haitian History, 2021); Peasants and Capital: Dominica in the World Economy (1988); Haiti: State against Nation: The Origins and Legacy of Duvalierism (1990); 'The Odd and the Ordinary: Haiti, the Caribbean, and the World' (Cimarrón, 1990); 'Anthropology and the Savage Slot' (1991); Silencing the Past: Power and the Production of History (1995; 20th-anniversary edition 2015, foreword Hazel V. Carby); Open the Social Sciences (Gulbenkian Commission, 1996); 'Abortive Rituals: Historical Apologies in the Global Era' (2000); 'The Anthropology of the State in the Age of Globalization' (Current Anthropology, 2001); 'Culture on the Edges' (2002); Global Transformations: Anthropology and the Modern World (2003); Trouillot Remixed (2021). Readers: Alyssa Goldstein Sepinwall (2013); Colin Dayan, J. Michael Dash, Greg Beckett (2013); Hazel V. Carby (2015); Rodrigo Bulamah, Júlia Goyatá and Bethânia Pereira (2020); Marlene L. Daut (2021); Yarimar Bonilla (2021); Bastien Craipain (2024)."
entry_kind "person"
entry_type "Individual thinker or historian"
scope_note "Read on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §§4, 13): the University of Chicago obituary (2012) and the Chicago Library finding aid's biographical note (in full); the contents and descriptions of Silencing the Past (Beacon), Global Transformations (Springer, with the 'Savage Slot' abstract) and Trouillot Remixed (Duke); the Journal of Haitian Studies 19:2 (2013) contents; Craipain's H-Net review of Stirring the Pot (2024, in full); the Bulamah–Goyatá–Pereira introduction to 'The Odd and the Ordinary' (Vibrant, 2020, in part); Davidson's Overland essay (2020), which quotes chapter 1 without page numbers. Unread: every book's body — the four-moments passage of Silencing the Past is given as later writers list it and as Davidson quotes it, unpaginated, and its wording awaits re-verification on a library copy of Silencing the Past, ch. 1; chapter 3, 'An Unthinkable History', by title and Craipain's quotation (p. 98) only; Haiti: State against Nation and Peasants and Capital by obituary citations only; Sepinwall (2013), Daut (2021), Carby (2015), Bonilla's prelude (2021) and Dayan (2012–13) are named as readers but unread. Wikidata Q6836249 checked by QLever on 2026-10-23. Born 26 November 1949, Port-au-Prince (Wikidata); son and nephew of the historians Ernest and Hénock Trouillot (Bulamah et al., n. 3); Duke 1983–88, Johns Hopkins 1988–98, Chicago 1998–2012 (finding aid); died 5 July 2012 aged 62 after an aneurysm (obituaries). History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23): cited by 5.9% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25 (0.8% in 2000–09), Silencing the Past by 4.3% in 2015–25, in 5 of 18 fields; a figure for this note, not for arrows."
source_ids ["p4a_bulamah_vibrant_2020", "p4a_craipain_hnet_2024", "p4a_daut_beyond_2021", "p4a_davidson_overland_2020", "p4a_from_the_margins_2002", "p4a_jhs_19_2_2013", "p4a_sepinwall_2013", "p4a_stoler_archives_2002", "p4a_trouillot_global_2003", "p4a_trouillot_papers_ead", "p4a_trouillot_remixed_2021", "p4a_trouillot_savage_slot_2003", "p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995", "p4a_trouillot_uchicago_obit_2012"]
