proposed_edges [{"proposal_id":"p4B_rel_03","source":"patrick_wolfe","target":"indigenous","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"A settler-colonial analytic worked out in conversation with Native American and Indigenous Studies: US Indian policy (removal, allotment, blood quantum, termination) read as successive modalities of elimination (2006, 2011); 'a key scholar in conceiving of a settler-colonial analytic in relation to Indigenous thinking' (Goeman, 2016)","map_label":"Wolfe and Indigenous studies, 2006–16","evidence_note":"Goeman 2016 (read in full): \"a key scholar in conceiving of a settler-colonial analytic in relation to Indigenous thinking and scholarship that exists far longer than settler nations\"; \"He critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner\". Kauanui 2016 (read in full), note 7: \"his long time, direct intellectual engagement with scholars in Native American and Indigenous Studies\", and para. 2: he insisted \"that Native scholars did\" create the field. Wolfe 2006 (read in full), sections on discovery and preemption, the Cherokee removal, allotment, blood quantum (\"statistical extermination\", quoting Jaimes) and termination; \"After the Frontier\" (2011) is unread. A contribution to the conversation, not an origin: the entry's Indigenous methods are not derived from him, and the dissent of Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel (2014) is recorded on the entry. Replaces the former comparison row. (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md)","source_ids":["screq_wolfe_2006","p4b_wolfe_2011_after_frontier","p4b_goeman_2016","screq_kauanui_2016"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"indigenous/settler_colonial_interlocutor","roster_row":{"node":"indigenous","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"}},{"proposal_id":"p4B_rel_04","source":"patrick_wolfe","target":"racial_formation","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"Race as the outcome of 'differentiation, which is an intensely conflictual process' (2001): 'a race cannot be taken as given. It is made in the targeting'; Black people 'racialized as slaves', Indians eliminated 'as Indians' (2006); a comparative typology of colonial race regimes in Traces of History (2016)","map_label":"Elementary structures of race, 2001–16","evidence_note":"Wolfe 2006 (read in full), opening section: \"different racial regimes encode and reproduce the unequal relationships into which Europeans coerced the populations concerned\"; \"Black people's enslavement produced an inclusive taxonomy … For Indians, in stark contrast, non-Indian ancestry compromised their indigeneity\"; \"a race cannot be taken as given. It is made in the targeting\"; and note 62 quoting his own 2001 essay, p. 894: \"Difference … is not simply given. It is the outcome of differentiation, which is an intensely conflictual process.\" The 2001 essay is otherwise unread (Crossref: AHR 106:3, June 2001). Traces of History (2016) known through the British Library contents note (chapters on Australia, the United States, central Europe, Brazil, Palestine) and Veracini's obituary (read): it \"utilises his analysis of racism under settler colonialism in order to explain racism everywhere else\" and provides \"a compelling typology of racial formations\". Strengthens the entry-level comparison edge_885, which stays. (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md)","source_ids":["screq_wolfe_2001","screq_wolfe_2006","screq_wolfe_2016","p4b_wolfe_traces_bl_2016","p4b_veracini_wolfe_obituary_2016"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"racial_formation/race","roster_row":{"node":"racial_formation","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"}},{"proposal_id":"p4B_rel_05","source":"patrick_wolfe","target":"holocaust","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"comparison","directed":false,"relationship":"Settler colonialism set beside genocide studies: the Holocaust as 'the non-paradigmatic paradigm' against which 'qualified genocides' are devalued; 'structural genocide' proposed to retain settler colonialism's specificity without 'hierarchy among victims'; a chapter in Moses's Empire, Colony, Genocide (2006–08)","map_label":"Settler colonialism and genocide, 2006–08","evidence_note":"Wolfe 2006 (read in full): \"Settler colonialism is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal\"; \"The best known of all genocides was internal to Europe\"; Lemkin's definition (note 6); Hull, Pohl, Zimmerer and \"the modernity of colonialism\"; \"the Holocaust, the non-paradigmatic paradigm that, being the indispensable example, can never merely exemplify\"; \"the term 'structural genocide' avoids the questions of degree—and, therefore, of hierarchy among victims\" (p. 403 per Kauanui). The 2008 chapter in Moses ed. is unread (Berghahn contents; Docker's \"Are Settler-Colonies Inherently Genocidal?\" in the same volume). The holocaust entry is historiographical; this compares frames and does not claim he wrote Holocaust history. (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md)","source_ids":["screq_wolfe_2006","p4b_wolfe_2008_structure_event"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","roster_row":{"node":"holocaust","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"}},{"proposal_id":"p4B_rel_08","source":"patrick_wolfe","target":"post","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"comparison","directed":false,"relationship":"Settler against franchise colonialism: settler colonies 'were not primarily established to extract surplus value from indigenous labour' but 'premised on displacing indigenes from (or replacing them on) the land' (1999), 'a specific social formation' whose specificity is to be retained (2006)","map_label":"Settler versus franchise colonialism, 1999–2006","evidence_note":"Wolfe 1999, pp. 1–2 (existing reading, cpl129_wolfe_1999_intro: the introduction distinguishes settler from franchise colonies) and p. 3 as quoted by Englert 2020 (read): \"In contrast to the kind of colonial formation that Cabral or Fanon confronted settler colonies were not primarily established to extract surplus value from indigenous labour. Rather, they are premised on displacing indigenes from (or replacing them on) the land\". Wolfe 2006 (read in full): \"settler colonialism is a specific social formation and it is desirable to retain that specificity\". Englert finds the Fanon/Algeria example contradictory (Algeria depended on Indigenous labour). A comparison, not a critique: Hoxie 2008 (existing abstract) places the frame inside postcolonial critique, while Veracini's claim that postcolonial studies missed the settler situation is drawn at entry level (edge_882 revised). (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md)","source_ids":["cpl129_wolfe_1999_intro","screq_wolfe_2006","p4b_englert_2020","screq_hoxie_2008"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"post/metropole_colony"},{"proposal_id":"p4B_rel_09","source":"patrick_wolfe","target":"native_newcomer","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"comparison","directed":false,"relationship":"Treaties, preemption, removal and allotment read as 'the logic of elimination' rather than as relations and encounter: 'the murderous activities of the frontier rabble constitute [the colonial state's] principal means of expansion' (2006); set against the fur-trade historiography's 'middle ground', which Veracini records as a rival recovery (2013)","map_label":"Elimination or encounter","evidence_note":"Wolfe 2006 (read in full), second section (the doctrine of discovery, preemption, the Cherokee removal: \"The American right to buy always superseded the Indian right not to sell\" (Rosenthal, quoted); \"Rather than something separate from or running counter to the colonial state, the murderous activities of the frontier rabble constitute its principal means of expansion\") and sixth section (allotment, blood quantum, termination). Veracini 2013, concluding section (author manuscript, read): \"the recovery of a long lasting 'middle ground' between indigenous and settler polities in the past, sustained the possibility of renewed relationships\"; \"both the Canadian and US historiographies were late in adopting settler colonialism as a category of analysis\". edge_869 already compares the two fields; this names the person's own reading. Canadian material is not his. (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md)","source_ids":["screq_wolfe_2006","p4b_veracini_2013_deposit"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"native_newcomer/settler_colonial_reading","roster_row":{"node":"native_newcomer","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"}},{"proposal_id":"p4B_rel_10","source":"cole_harris","target":"patrick_wolfe","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"influence","directed":true,"relationship":"Wolfe took from Harris the sentence that 'the principal momentum of settler colonialism comes into focus' when capital's interest in uncluttered access to land is combined with settlers' interest in land as livelihood (2004, quoted 2006): a use as evidence, moderate","map_label":"Harris read by Wolfe, 2006","evidence_note":"Wolfe 2006 (read in full), third section and note 36, quoting Harris, \"How Did Colonialism Dispossess?\" (2004), p. 179: \"Combine capital's interest in uncluttered access to land and settlers' interest in land as livelihood, and the principal momentum of settler colonialism comes into focus.\" Englert 2020 (read) also uses Harris 2004, p. 172, against Wolfe. The direction matters for edge_1014, which found no derivation of Harris from Wolfe: the citation runs the other way. A single quotation; influence in the sense of a use as evidence, as for locB_rel_26 in revision 1.152. (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md)","source_ids":["screq_wolfe_2006","screq_harris_2004","p4b_englert_2020"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review"}]
edges_considered_not_proposed [{"pair":"patrick_wolfe → lorenzo_veracini (p4B_rel_06)","reason":"Held by the coordinator (2026-10-23): Veracini has no entry this wave; his debt is recorded on his roster row and in the strand wolfe_veracini_structure_debate."},{"pair":"j_kehaulani_kauanui / sai_englert / macoun and strakosch → patrick_wolfe","reason":"No entries for the critics; carried by settler_colonialism → patrick_wolfe (p4B_rel_07, in the settler_colonialism draft)."},{"pair":"patrick_wolfe → james_daschuk; → adele_perry","reason":"No statement of debt read; edge_1020 already records Perry's 2016 self-description."},{"pair":"patrick_wolfe → allan_greer","reason":"edge_1012 already carries Greer's retention of the formula."},{"pair":"patrick_wolfe → dependency","reason":"Drawn at entry level (p4B_rel_14): Englert's frame is Harvey's, not Frank's."}]
new_sources [{"id":"p4b_wolfe_1994","title":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Nation and MiscegeNation: Discursive Continuity in the Post-Mabo Era,\" Social Analysis 36 (1994): 93–152.","citation":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Nation and MiscegeNation: Discursive Continuity in the Post-Mabo Era,\" Social Analysis 36 (1994): 93–152.","url":null,"verification":{"status":"not_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Not read. Known as cited by Wolfe 2006, notes 1, 7 (p. 96 for the structure formula), 66 (p. 111) and 70 (pp. 110–118), all read in the 2006 text, and by Kauanui 2016. JSTOR blocked access in the 2026-10-12 round; no Crossref record."}},{"id":"p4b_wolfe_2008_structure_event","title":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Structure and Event: Settler Colonialism, Time, and the Question of Genocide,\" in A. Dirk Moses, ed., Empire, Colony, Genocide: Conquest, Occupation, and Subaltern Resistance in World History (New York and Oxford: Berghahn, 2008), 102–132.","citation":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Structure and Event: Settler Colonialism, Time, and the Question of Genocide,\" in A. Dirk Moses, ed., Empire, Colony, Genocide: Conquest, Occupation, and Subaltern Resistance in World History (New York and Oxford: Berghahn, 2008), 102–132.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1515/9781782382140-005","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Berghahn page (https://www.berghahnbooks.com/title/MosesEmpire) read: War and Genocide 12; hardback June 2008, paperback December 2009; contents (Wolfe ch. 4; Docker ch. 3; Veracini ch. 6). Page range from the Crossref chapter record. Chapter unread."}},{"id":"p4b_wolfe_2011_after_frontier","title":"Patrick Wolfe, \"After the Frontier: Separation and Absorption in US Indian Policy,\" Settler Colonial Studies 1, no. 1 (2011): 13–51.","citation":"Patrick Wolfe, \"After the Frontier: Separation and Absorption in US Indian Policy,\" Settler Colonial Studies 1, no. 1 (2011): 13–51.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2011.10648800","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record (issued January 2011); unread."}},{"id":"p4b_wolfe_2012_purchase","title":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Purchase by Other Means: The Palestine Nakba and Zionism's Conquest of Economics,\" Settler Colonial Studies 2, no. 1 (2012): 133–171.","citation":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Purchase by Other Means: The Palestine Nakba and Zionism's Conquest of Economics,\" Settler Colonial Studies 2, no. 1 (2012): 133–171.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2012.10648830","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record (issued January 2012); unread."}},{"id":"p4b_wolfe_2012_arabic","title":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Arabic Translation: Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native (2006),\" Settler Colonial Studies 2, no. 1 (2012): 226–252.","citation":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Arabic Translation: Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native (2006),\" Settler Colonial Studies 2, no. 1 (2012): 226–252.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2012.10648834","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record (issued January 2012)."}},{"id":"p4b_wolfe_2013_recuperating","title":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Recuperating Binarism: A Heretical Introduction,\" Settler Colonial Studies 3, nos. 3–4 (2013): 257–279.","citation":"Patrick Wolfe, \"Recuperating Binarism: A Heretical Introduction,\" Settler Colonial Studies 3, nos. 3–4 (2013): 257–279.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2013.830587","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record (November 2013); unread. Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014, p. 10 (read) note that Wolfe edited this issue and the paired American Indian Culture and Research Journal 37:2 (2013)."}},{"id":"p4b_lloyd_wolfe_2015","title":"David Lloyd and Patrick Wolfe, \"Settler Colonial Logics and the Neoliberal Regime,\" Settler Colonial Studies 6, no. 2 (2016): 109–118 (online 7 May 2015).","citation":"David Lloyd and Patrick Wolfe, \"Settler Colonial Logics and the Neoliberal Regime,\" Settler Colonial Studies 6, no. 2 (2016): 109–118 (online 7 May 2015).","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2015.1035361","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record (online 7 May 2015; volume 6, issue 2); unread."}},{"id":"p4b_wolfe_traces_bl_2016","title":"British Library legal-deposit record: Patrick Wolfe, Traces of History: Elementary Structures of Race (London and New York: Verso, 2016), electronic edition.","citation":"British Library legal-deposit record: Patrick Wolfe, Traces of History: Elementary Structures of Race (London and New York: Verso, 2016), electronic edition.","url":"https://eld.bl.uk/catalog/018878064","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Catalogue record read: Verso, London and New York, 2016; \"title from PDF title page (EBSCO, viewed January 27, 2016)\"; ISBNs 9781781689189 and 9781781689196 (electronic), related 9781781689172 (paperback); contents note: Introduction; 1 Racialisation of Indigenous People in Australia; 2 Race and Democracy in the United States; 3 Antisemitism in Central Europe; 4 Africans, Natives and Colour in Brazil; 5 Dispossessing Indians, Making the United States; 6 Racialising Indians in the United States; 7 Dispossessing the Natives in Palestine; 8 Racialising the Jewish State; Conclusion. Confirms the 2016 imprint year queried in screq_wolfe_2016. Book unread."}},{"id":"p4b_wolfe_settler_complex_2016","title":"Patrick Wolfe, ed., The Settler Complex: Recuperating Binarism in Colonial Studies (Los Angeles: UCLA American Indian Studies Center, 2016).","citation":"Patrick Wolfe, ed., The Settler Complex: Recuperating Binarism in Colonial Studies (Los Angeles: UCLA American Indian Studies Center, 2016).","url":null,"verification":{"status":"not_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"No publisher page found. Known through Kauanui 2016, note 8 (\"Wolfe responded to the charge of perpetuating a binary between settler and native with his recently edited volume\"), Goeman 2016, note 6 (both read), and Smithers's review (Australian Historical Studies 48:1, 2017; Crossref). Unread."}},{"id":"p4b_veracini_wolfe_obituary_2016","title":"Lorenzo Veracini, \"Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016),\" Settler Colonial Studies 6, no. 3 (2016): 189–190.","citation":"Lorenzo Veracini, \"Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016),\" Settler Colonial Studies 6, no. 3 (2016): 189–190.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2016.1176393","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Read in full on 2026-10-23 in the Internet Archive capture of 26 March 2024 of the publisher's page (http://web.archive.org/web/20240326050636/https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/2201473X.2016.1176393; the live page returned 403). Crossref: online 10 May 2016. Passages: \"fellowships at Harvard and Stanford\"; \"Dialectics and binaries … worked for him\"; \"still a Marxist\" of \"the young Marx\"; the 1999 book \"ostensibly a history of Australian anthropology\", \"settler colonialism\" \"only added at the very end and at the request of the publisher\"; \"No one had theorised it before and in a systematic way\"; \"practically established what is now a burgeoning scholarly subfield\"; \"kick started a scholarly field in a fit of absentmindedness. Or did he?\""}},{"id":"p4b_goeman_2016","title":"Mishuana Goeman, \"In Memoriam: Patrick Wolfe (February 18, 2016),\" American Indian Culture and Research Journal 40, no. 1 (2016): ix–x.","citation":"Mishuana Goeman, \"In Memoriam: Patrick Wolfe (February 18, 2016),\" American Indian Culture and Research Journal 40, no. 1 (2016): ix–x.","url":"https://doi.org/10.17953/aicrj.40.1.goeman","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Read in full (eScholarship PDF, CC BY-NC 4.0; also published in Amerasia Journal 42:1, 2016). Passages: \"Patrick Wolfe's sudden passing in the winter of 2016 stunned us all in Native American and Indigenous studies\"; why his concept travelled \"when other texts and similar concepts had preceded his important book\": \"the readability of his article and the geographic breadth of his work\"; \"a key scholar in conceiving of a settler-colonial analytic in relation to Indigenous thinking and scholarship that exists far longer than settler nations\"; \"He critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner\"; Simpson's recollection of his Columbia seminar visit; note 6: The Settler Complex (UCLA AISC 2016) and Traces of History (Verso 2016)."}},{"id":"p4b_birch_2016","title":"Tony [Anthony] Birch, \"Good Patrick,\" Aboriginal History 40 (2016): 225–226.","citation":"Tony [Anthony] Birch, \"Good Patrick,\" Aboriginal History 40 (2016): 225–226.","url":"https://vuir.vu.edu.au/39225/","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Victoria University repository record read (ISSN 0314-8769; official link to ANU Press; file restricted to repository staff). Unread. The VU library guide places it in a section \"In Memoriam Patrick Wolfe 1949–2016\". Birch's phrase \"an openly emotional thinker\" is known only as quoted by Russell 2022."}},{"id":"p4b_russell_verso_2022","title":"Lynette Russell, \"Patrick Wolfe: Treading Carefully but Defiantly,\" Verso blog, 22 September 2022; excerpted from Susan Slyomovics and Lorenzo Veracini, eds., Race, Place, Trace: Essays in Honour of Patrick Wolfe (London: Verso).","citation":"Lynette Russell, \"Patrick Wolfe: Treading Carefully but Defiantly,\" Verso blog, 22 September 2022; excerpted from Susan Slyomovics and Lorenzo Veracini, eds., Race, Place, Trace: Essays in Honour of Patrick Wolfe (London: Verso).","url":"https://www.versobooks.com/blogs/news/5444-patrick-wolfe-treading-carefully-but-defiantly","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Publisher blog page read through a fetch summary (not verbatim) on 2026-10-23: Wolfe supervised Russell's doctorate; his work \"drew heavily on research and activist work by Indigenous people\"; phrases \"as a structure and not an event\" and \"trod carefully but defiantly\"; she quotes Birch's obituary (\"an openly emotional thinker\"); the book's imprint date is not on the page (blog date 2022)."}},{"id":"p4b_aq_forum_2017","title":"Sarah Franklin, Njoroge Njoroge and Suzanna Reiss, \"Tracing the Settler's Tools: A Forum on Patrick Wolfe's Life and Legacy,\" American Quarterly 69, no. 2 (2017): 235–247 (with Jean M. O'Brien, 249–255, and Saree Makdisi, 277–284).","citation":"Sarah Franklin, Njoroge Njoroge and Suzanna Reiss, \"Tracing the Settler's Tools: A Forum on Patrick Wolfe's Life and Legacy,\" American Quarterly 69, no. 2 (2017): 235–247 (with Jean M. O'Brien, 249–255, and Saree Makdisi, 277–284).","url":"https://doi.org/10.1353/aq.2017.0017","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref records (June 2017); Project MUSE returned a bot challenge; unread."}},{"id":"p4b_kelley_2017","title":"Robin D. G. Kelley, \"The Rest of Us: Rethinking Settler and Native,\" American Quarterly 69, no. 2 (2017): 267–276.","citation":"Robin D. G. Kelley, \"The Rest of Us: Rethinking Settler and Native,\" American Quarterly 69, no. 2 (2017): 267–276.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1353/aq.2017.0020","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record; unread; its argument (settler colonies built on the exploitation of Indigenous labour, South Africa) is known through Englert 2020 (read)."}},{"id":"p4b_makdisi_2017_ahs","title":"Saree Makdisi, review of Patrick Wolfe, Traces of History, Australian Historical Studies 48, no. 1 (2017): 117–118.","citation":"Saree Makdisi, review of Patrick Wolfe, Traces of History, Australian Historical Studies 48, no. 1 (2017): 117–118.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/1031461x.2017.1276553","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record (2 January 2017); unread."}},{"id":"p4b_smithers_2017_ahs","title":"Gregory D. Smithers, review of Patrick Wolfe, Traces of History and The Settler Complex, Australian Historical Studies 48, no. 1 (2017): 118–120.","citation":"Gregory D. Smithers, review of Patrick Wolfe, Traces of History and The Settler Complex, Australian Historical Studies 48, no. 1 (2017): 118–120.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/1031461x.2016.1273061","verification":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Crossref record (2 January 2017); unread."}},{"id":"p4b_vu_wolfe_collection","title":"Victoria University Library, \"Patrick Wolfe collection\" (special collections and archives; managed for the Aboriginal History Archive, Moondani Balluk Indigenous Academic Unit).","citation":"Victoria University Library, \"Patrick Wolfe collection\" (special collections and archives; managed for the Aboriginal History Archive, Moondani Balluk Indigenous Academic Unit).","url":"https://www.vu.edu.au/library/about-the-library/special-collections-archives/patrick-wolfe-collection","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Page read on 2026-10-23: \"Patrick Wolfe was a historian and scholar in the field of settler colonial studies\"; \"He held research and teaching positions at several Australian universities including Victoria University, University of Melbourne and La Trobe University\"; \"passed away in 2016\"; his personal library of about 1,400 books. No birth date, roles or years."}},{"id":"p4b_vu_libguide_wolfe","title":"Victoria University Library guide, \"Patrick Wolfe\" (VU Special Collections).","citation":"Victoria University Library guide, \"Patrick Wolfe\" (VU Special Collections).","url":"https://libraryguides.vu.edu.au/VUSpecialCollections/PatrickWolfe","verification":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Page read on 2026-10-23: born 1949, died 2016; works list including Sovereignty: Frontiers of Possibility (2013, ed. with Evans, Genovese and Reilly, University of Hawai'i Press) and a chapter in Stolen Lands, Broken Cultures: The Settler-Colonial Present (2012, Arena); \"In Memoriam Patrick Wolfe 1949–2016\" section in Aboriginal History 40 (2016)."}}]
source_addenda {"screq_wolfe_2006":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Read in full on 2026-10-23 in the Internet Archive capture of 28 June 2025 of the journal's own free-to-read full-text page (http://web.archive.org/web/20250628134148/https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14623520601056240; the live page returned 403; OpenAlex marks the article open on the publisher's site). Notes 1–80 present; the capture carries no page breaks, so page numbers follow Kauanui 2016's citations (pp. 387, 388, 390, 403) and Snelgrove et al. 2014 (p. 388). Wolfe's own words: \"Settler colonialism is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal\" (p. 387); \"a race cannot be taken as given. It is made in the targeting\"; \"Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element\"; \"settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event\" (p. 388; note 7 refers to the 1999 book, p. 2, and the 1994 essay, p. 96); \"Settler colonialism destroys to replace\"; \"elimination refers to more than the summary liquidation of Indigenous people, though it includes that\" (p. 390); Cole Harris 2004, p. 179, quoted (note 36); \"assimilation's Faustian bargain\"; \"Richard Pratt and Phillip Sheridan were both practitioners of genocide. The question of degree is not the definitional issue\"; \"settler colonialism is relatively impervious to regime change\"; \"the Holocaust, the non-paradigmatic paradigm\"; \"structural genocide\" (p. 403); \"settler colonialism is an indicator\". The earlier note's attestation through Kauanui's abstract is superseded. See data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md §1."},"screq_wolfe_2016":{"status":"bibliographic_metadata_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Imprint year confirmed as 2016 by the British Library record (p4b_wolfe_traces_bl_2016; electronic edition seen 27 January 2016) and by Goeman 2016, note 6. Contents known from the BL note; unread."},"screq_kauanui_2016":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Full text read on 2026-10-23 (open access, CC BY 4.0; unpaginated web text; notes 1–17). Wolfe \"insisted on making it clear time and again that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did\"; the phrase \"structure not an event\" \"often seems to stand-in for a serious engagement of his theory\"; \"Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies\"; indigeneity \"distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality\"; note 7 (Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973, Sayigh 1979, Sakai 1983, Trask, Fujikane and Okamura 2008, Veracini 2006 before Wolfe); note 8 (Wolfe's The Settler Complex answers the binary charge); Warrior's 2015 ASA count; NAISA founded 2008 (note 10); acknowledgements dated to \"the immediate wake of Patrick Wolfe's death\". She calls him \"the late English scholar\"; Wikidata records an Australian historian born in Yorkshire."},"screq_harris_2004":{"status":"supporting_page_checked","checked_on":"2026-10-23","note":"Wolfe 2006 (read in full) quotes p. 179 approvingly (note 36): \"Combine capital's interest in uncluttered access to land and settlers' interest in land as livelihood, and the principal momentum of settler colonialism comes into focus\"; Englert 2020 cites p. 172 for Indigenous incorporation into the workforce. The direction of use runs Harris → Wolfe."}}
