proposed_edges [{"proposal_id":"p4C_rel_01","source":"environment","target":"william_cronon","type":"critique","relationship_kind":"critique","directed":true,"relationship":"The wilderness debate: Hays found 'a world of abstracted ideas … divorced from the values and ideas inherent in wilderness action' (1996, as quoted by Foreman); Sessions, habitat underweighted (1996); Foreman, that the 'Received Wilderness Idea' of 'Callicott, Cronon and other postmodern deconstructionist scholars' is 'a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (1998; 2000)","map_label":"The trouble with Cronon's wilderness, 1996–2000","evidence_note":"Foreman 2000 read in full (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md §2.2): pp. 32–33 and the reference list p. 38. Hays's sentence is carried only as quoted by Foreman (p. 32); Hays's comment (Environmental History 1:1, 29–32) is unread and the quotation is marked for re-verification. Sessions 1996 read in full (graph record). Callicott and Nelson 1998 known by publisher description and Foreman's own reference. Cronon's 1996 response (pp. 47–55) is unread: this edge records the critics' words; his 1995 defence of wild-land protection and nonhuman autonomy is on cronon_wilderness (reflow pp. 12, 17–20). Source strand environment/wilderness_debate.","source_ids":["p4c_foreman_real_2000","p4c_eh_wilderness_forum_1996","sessions_cronon_reply","p4c_callicott_nelson_1998","cronon_wilderness"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review"},{"proposal_id":"p4C_rel_02","source":"william_cronon","target":"indigenous","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"Wilderness as the erasure of Indigenous dwelling: 'The removal of Indians to create an \"uninhabited wilderness\"' shows 'just how invented, just how constructed, the American wilderness really is' (1995); the Apostle Islands, 'inhabited by Ojibwe peoples for centuries', 'a wilderness that has long been a place of human dwelling' (2003)","map_label":"Uninhabited wilderness as invention, 1995–2003","evidence_note":"Own words. 1995: author-hosted reflow p. 10 (graph record cronon_wilderness, read in full 2026-10-13). 2003: 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands', read in full in OCR (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md §2.2, P6): the Ojibwe sentence on the scan's third page (Orion p. 38), 'a wilderness that has long been a place of human dwelling' and 'manage the Apostle Islands as a historical wilderness' (p. 39). His students Warren (1997) and Jacoby (2001) and Spence (1999) took the point into the archive (CV p. 11; Spence abstract read); no reader read here states that influence, so it is recorded as context, not as an edge. Target strand indigenous/land ('Land, identity and colonial change').","source_ids":["cronon_wilderness","p4c_cronon_apostle_2003","p4c_cronon_cv","p4c_spence_dispossessing_1999","p4c_jacoby_crimes_2001"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"indigenous/land"},{"proposal_id":"p4C_rel_03","source":"william_cronon","target":"publichistory","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"History for land managers and the public: the Apostle Islands should be managed 'as a historical wilderness' whose policy 'should equally protect stories—stories of wild nature, stories of human history' (2003); as AHA president he asked historians to 'connect our professional stories with the concerns of the wider public' (2013)","map_label":"Storied wilderness and public stories, 2003–13","evidence_note":"Own words. 2003 essay read in full in OCR (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md §2.2): the riddle passage (p. 39) and the closing paragraph ('It is a storied wilderness'). 2013 address: open AHA text, passages (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md §1.3). CV (read): Trust for Public Land board 2003–20; Wisconsin Historical Society curators 1997–2011; the 2012 column 'The Public Practice of History in and for a Digital Age' (title only). Target strand publichistory/place_preservation.","source_ids":["p4c_cronon_apostle_2003","p4c_cronon_storytelling_2013","p4c_cronon_cv"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review","target_strand":"publichistory/place_preservation"},{"proposal_id":"p4C_rel_04","source":"william_cronon","target":"digital_history","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"contribution","directed":true,"relationship":"'The digital revolution has created endless opportunities via blogs, websites, YouTube, and social media' for the historian's stories (2013); presidential columns 'The Public Practice of History in and for a Digital Age' and 'Scholarly Authority in a Wikified World' (2012); 'digital scholarship' named among his fields","map_label":"History in a digital age, 2012–13","evidence_note":"Moderate. The 2013 sentence is read (open AHA text, data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md §1.3); the 2012 Perspectives columns are known by title from the CV (p. 8) and his AHA-writings page; the CV (p. 1) lists 'digital scholarship' among his fields and (p. 10) the websites he built. The columns themselves are unread. No target strand: the entry's strands are per-work; the coordinator may attach it to the 2005 Cohen–Rosenzweig strand or leave it unattached.","source_ids":["p4c_cronon_storytelling_2013","p4c_cronon_cv"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review"},{"proposal_id":"p4C_rel_06","source":"aldo_leopold","target":"william_cronon","type":"connection","relationship_kind":"influence","directed":true,"relationship":"Leopold as teacher and model: the landscape-reading course he drew from Leopold (afterword, 2003), essays on Leopold and A Sand County Almanac (1998), and Leopold among the 'Classics and Precursors' of his reading list (2005)","map_label":"Leopold's example, 1994–2005","evidence_note":"Afterword pp. 173–175 (graph record cronon_changes_afterword, read: the Wisconsin landscape training and Leopold's course model); CV p. 9 (read): 'A Voice in the Wilderness: Aldo Leopold' (Wilderness, 1998) and 'A Sand County Almanac' in A Wisconsin Fifteen (1998), titles only; the 2005 reading list (graph record pe131_carson_cronon_readings_2005, read). Parallels edge_911 (leopold → environment), which already cites the afterword.","source_ids":["cronon_changes_afterword","p4c_cronon_cv","pe131_carson_cronon_readings_2005","leopold_almanac"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review"},{"proposal_id":"p4C_rel_11","source":"william_cronon","target":"donald_worster","type":"critique","relationship_kind":"critique","directed":true,"relationship":"'Modes of Prophecy and Production' answered Worster's 1990 agroecological programme (unread; reported by Isenberg 2013); 'A Place for Stories' (1992) read Dust Bowl as a story whose 'chief agent' is capitalism and whose plot 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy', set against Bonnifield's progressive plot","map_label":"Prophecy and production: reading Dust Bowl, 1990–92","evidence_note":"'A Place for Stories' pp. 1347–1351 read (graph record pe131_cronon_place_stories_1992; the Dust Bowl passages are quoted in donald_worster's description). The 1990 response (JAH 76:4, 1122–31) is unread (OUP 403) and is known through Isenberg 2013, pp. 88–89 (read, graph record pe131_crosby_isenberg_2013); Cronon's CV p. 7 confirms the citation. A critique by reading, not a rejection: Cronon's essay treats both plots as narrative choices.","source_ids":["pe131_cronon_place_stories_1992","pe131_cronon_modes_1990","pe131_crosby_isenberg_2013","worster_dust_bowl"],"classification_basis":"editorial_review"}]
