# Evidence: the archive as object (`archival_turn`), Ann Laura Stoler and Michel-Rolph Trouillot

Agent A of three (brief: `BRIEF.md` in this folder). Research phase only: no graph edits, no git, no site builds, no heavy jobs. Compiled 2026-10-23 against `historiography-1920-2000.json` as found on disk (200 nodes, 1,208 edges, 2,257 sources, 1,309 people; last revision_history entry dated 2026-10-23, i.e. 1.154). Network use kept light (one request at a time per host). No email address was sent to any service. Wikidata only via QLever SPARQL (the WikidataMCP endpoint failed to connect at session start and was not used).

Status: in progress; sections are written in research order and saved as they are finished.

---

## 0. Project state checked (2026-10-23)

**No entry for the archive as object exists.** No node id or label contains "archiv" as a field; the word appears only inside strands of other entries (listed below). `archival_turn` is free as an id.

**Ann Laura Stoler** is a people record only: `people['ann_laura_stoler']` = `{"id", "label"}` (no `node_id`, no identity note). `data/people-wikidata.json` carries her as `Q16203101`, status `accepted`, description "American anthropologist", method `bulk_roster_match_2026-09-22` ("applied by user decision on 2026-09-26 WITHOUT individual review"); the QID therefore needs the individual QLever check the brief asks for (section 10 below).

Roster rows that carry her (three nodes), all resting on one source, `stoler_desire` (Race and the Education of Desire, 1995; Duke page, publisher description only, "Full book … not read"):
- `gender` (contributor): "Makes colonial race and sexuality central to a critical extension of Foucault." Strand `gender/colonial_categories` (with Amadiume and Oyěwùmí).
- `queer` (historian): "Colonial archives and racial formation expose limits and possibilities in Foucauldian histories of sexuality." Strand `queer/colonial` ("Stoler both questions and develops Foucault using colonial archives").
- `post` (contributor): "Reworks Foucault through colonial archives, race and sexuality." Strands `post/colonial_intimacies` ("Race, sexuality and colonial archives"; focus: "Empire becomes part of the history of European bourgeois sexuality") and `post/metropole_colony` (Cooper and Stoler, Tensions of Empire 1997, with Catherine Hall; "the publisher's description and contents, not a passage-level check").

Edges that rest on her (none has her as an endpoint; she is a people record): edge_023 foucault → queer (influence; sources include `stoler_desire`); **edge_386 post → foucault (critique): "Stoler questions the marginal place of race and empire in Foucault's history of sexuality while using his 1976 lectures (1995)"**; edge_387 gender → post (contribution; Mohanty and Stoler); **edge_417 post → queer (contribution): "Stoler investigates sexuality and racial formation through colonial archives"**; edge_1023 post → adele_perry (influence; "Stoler and McClintock, as she and her reviewers name them"). When a person entry exists, edge_386 and edge_417 carry claims that properly belong to `ann_laura_stoler → foucault` and `ann_laura_stoler → queer`; see Corrections (section P.9) for the option of re-sourcing, as 1.152 did for edge_200.

Sources already in the graph for her: `stoler_desire` (1995; Duke page, supporting_page_checked, body unread); `cooper_stoler_tensions_1997` (UC Press page, contents and description only; "Chapter and volume bodies were not read"); `davin_imperialism_primary` (Davin 1978 as reprinted in Tensions of Empire). Absent: "Colonial archives and the arts of governance" (2002), Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002), "Tense and tender ties" (2001), Haunted by Empire (2006), Along the Archival Grain (2009), Imperial Debris (2013), Duress (2016), Capitalism and the Senses (ed., 2023). No chronology event anywhere names her.

**Michel-Rolph Trouillot**: no people record, no roster row, no source, no mention anywhere in the graph (grep on "trouillot" over nodes, people, sources and edges: zero hits). `rising-people-atlas.csv` in the cohorts folder already carries `Q6836249` for him with `in_atlas = False`.

**The archive inside existing entries** (what the new entry would gather and must not duplicate):
- `derrida` (person node, 4 events 1966–1994 including "Archive Fever, lecture" 1994): the description already says "His archive inquiry connects institutions, access, inscription and preservation with psychoanalytic questions." Source `derrida_archive_primary` (Archive Fever, "complete printed pp. 1–5 including p. 4 note 1, and pp. 7–15 read"; **its URL is a PDF on a mirror host** of the kind the 2026-10-19 provenance rule excludes; flagged in Corrections). Edges: edge_587 memory → derrida (Combe as a resource to Derrida), edge_588 derrida → memory ("Derrida contributes an account of archival authority and technical inscription"), edge_589 freud → derrida. Roster rows for Derrida on `language`, `gender`, `memory`, `freud`; strands `memory/archive_authority` (Combe and Derrida), `freud/archive_technical_memory`.
- `memory` strand `archive_authority` ("Archival authority, access and inscription": Sonia Combe, Archives interdites 1994; Derrida).
- `post`: strands `colonial_intimacies` and `metropole_colony` (above); `post` chronology: Said 1978, Chatterjee 1986, Spivak 1988, Chakrabarty 2000; coverage reviewed_through 2000.
- `subaltern` strand `local_memory` ("Local events, archives and remembrance"); source `w3_spivak_rani_1985` ("The Rani of Sirmur: An Essay in Reading the Archives", metadata only, unread).
- `atlantic` strand `diaspora_archives` (Dorothy Porter Wesley's collecting); `black` strand `archives` ("Collecting and diaspora bibliography"); `women` strands `archives_authority` and `rights_movement_boundaries`; `ranke` strand `diplomatics_authority`; `political/archives_institutions`; `ethnohistory/claims_archives`; `micro/local_research_collaboration`; `web_history` strand `digital10_hegarty_platformization_2024` ("reading social media collections along the archival grain": a 2024 title that borrows Stoler's phrase).
- `natalie_zemon_davis` (node): `nzd_fiction_1987` (Fiction in the Archives, 1987; OpenLibrary metadata only, unread). Davis wrote the foreword to Farge's The Allure of the Archives (2013).
- Carolyn Steedman is a people record (`carolyn_steedman`, Q13102029 checked 2026-10-22), on the `languages_of_class` roster for Landscape for a Good Woman only; Dust (2001) and "Something she called a fever" (2001) are absent.
- Absent people records: Trouillot, Farge, Hartman, Fuentes, Mbembe, Burton, Dirks, Zeitlyn, Joan M. Schwartz, Terry Cook, Richards, Arondekar, Diana Taylor, Cvetkovich, Manoff, Blouin, Rosenberg, David Scott, Laurent Dubois, Yannick Lowry. Present: `frederick_cooper`, `hazel_carby` ("Hazel V. Carby"), `sidney_mintz`, `clr_james` (node), `eric_williams` (node), `bernard_cohn`, `sonia_combe`, `pierre_nora`, `michel_de_certeau`, `walter_benjamin`, `gayatri_chakravorty_spivak` (node), `dipesh_chakrabarty` (node), `edward_said` (node), `foucault` (node; events 1969 Archaeology, 1971 Nietzsche, 1982 Subject and Power).

**Target entries named in the brief, with their state**: `post` (Teaching umbrella, period_3, 9 strands, 4 events, reviewed_through 2000); `language` (umbrella, period_2); `memory` (field, period_3; strand `archive_authority`); `foucault`, `derrida` (person nodes, no coverage block); `subaltern` (school, period_2); `atlantic` (umbrella, period_3); `indigenous` (field, period_3, 33 strands); `gender`, `queer` (fields, period_3); `anthropology` (field, 21 strands, 2 events); `global` (umbrella, period_3); `annales` (school, period_0, 11 strands). All group entries in scope are `reviewed_through: 2000`. Periods on disk run `period_0`–`period_3` ("Further turns, c. 1985–2000"); there is no `period_4` yet, so the new entry's `period` is proposed as `null` (as `languages_of_class` and `capitalismdebates` are) unless the coordinator mints period_4 in this wave.

**Edge pattern to follow** (1.152 precedent): persons contribute to the field/debate entry; critiques run from the person to the person or field answered; a field entry's cross-field arrows run field → field with `relationship_kind` contribution/comparison/influence. Existing arrows that the new entry must not duplicate: edge_588 derrida → memory, edge_386 post → foucault, edge_417 post → queer, edge_115 subaltern → post, edge_390 subaltern → memory.

### Dissertation figures to attach (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23)

From `summary/cohorts/README.md` §§2, 5, 6 and `field-engagement.csv` (seed list for `archive_as_object`: Stoler 389 theses, Burton 195, Trouillot 147, Farge 144, Derrida 137, Hartman 66, Steedman 49, Fuentes 33, Rosenberg 22, Blouin 8):

| Share of theses citing ≥2 archive-as-object seed authors | 1990–99 | 2000–09 | 2010–14 | 2015–19 | 2020–25 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Canada + US | 0 | 3.3 | 7.1 | 7.6 | 10.4 |
| Canada | 0 | 1.8 | 5.0 | 7.6 | 9.0 |
| US | 0 | 5.7 | 8.9 | 7.5 | 11.8 |
| Europe | — | 0 | 1.2 | 1.1 | 2.1 |
| (≥1 seed author, CA+US) | 3.6 | 9.5 | 19.8 | 24.8 | 26.7 |

- Stoler: cited by 3.2% of Canada + US theses in 2000–09 → 8.5% (2010–14) → 10.4% → 10.7% (2020–25); "the single strongest riser among people" 2015–25 vs 2000–09 (10.6% vs 3.2%, z = 5.3); at ≥3% in 11 of 18 TF-IDF families (mean family share 7%); co-cited with Thompson or Joan Scott by 2.5% of theses and with Foucault more; "the archive-as-object cluster (Stoler and Trouillot 1.7% and rising) has no opposing side" (§5). Along the Archival Grain 4.4% of theses 2015–25 (vs 0 in 2000–09); Carnal Knowledge 3.8% (vs 1.1%); Along the Archival Grain is also a 2020–25 entrant to the global/transnational snowball neighbourhood (lift 5.8). Robustness: Stoler (person and Along the Archival Grain) passes |z| ≥ 2 within-university.
- Trouillot: 0.8% (2000–09) → 3.3% → 3.2% → 5.9% (2020–25); Silencing the Past 4.3% of theses 2015–25 (vs 0.8%); at ≥3% in 5 families.
- Hartman: 0 → 0.8% → 1.4% → 4.3% (2020–25); Q7399969 in `rising-people-atlas.csv`, not in the atlas. Fuentes, Dispossessed Lives (2016): 3.1% of US theses 2020–25 (post2000-works.csv), a 2020–25 entrant to the US Indigenous and capitalism snowball neighbourhoods.
- Derrida: 1–1.5% of Canada + US theses in every cohort, "never together with Scott" (§5).
- Farge, Le goût de l'archive: a riser in the European (mostly French) theses (§2); 144 theses overall.
- §2's gloss: "the change is not a different population displacing the social historians, it is the same readers adding an archival and colonial layer"; §5's verdict: a framework "without an opposing side".

Per the brief, these figures go to `scope_note`, strand focus and `coverage` text, and are kept out of edge text for the international figures.

