Chicago page read (description: "an extended meditation on remembrance, religion, time, and technology"; blurbs from *The Guardian*, *Choice*, *Library Journal*, Jeremy Barris). The graph already holds `derrida_archive_primary` with pp. 1–5 and 7–15 read in 2026-09 — but through a PDF link on a mirror host of the kind the 2026-10-19 provenance rule excludes; proposed correction in P.9: replace the URL with the Chicago page and the Diacritics DOI, keep the reading record in the note. The lecture date (5 June 1994) is not on the Chicago page; it rests on the graph's existing note ("lecture 1994") and is not asserted to the day here.

## 8. Carolyn Steedman, *Dust* (Manchester University Press, 2001; US edition *Dust: The Archive and Cultural History*, Rutgers, 1 February 2002, 208 pp., Encounters: Cultural Histories) and "Something She Called a Fever: Michelet, Derrida, and Dust", *American Historical Review* 106:4 (October 2001): 1159–1180 (DOI 10.2307/2692943)
Rutgers page read (description: Steedman "calls the practice of paying attention to the archive 'archivization'"; the archive as the repository of "that which will not go away"; "the 'matter of history' can never go away or be erased"; author line "professor of history at the University of Warwick"). Warwick's repository record (wrap.warwick.ac.uk/11562) confirms the AHR citation, pp. 1159–1180, no full text. Manchester UP page 403. **Bodies unread**; her reply to Derrida (the "fever" as the historian's literal and figurative condition) is known to me only by title and description. Steedman's blurb on *Archive Stories* (Duke page): "Important and timely, this fascinating collection of tales from a multitude of repositories and record offices".

