Penn Press page 403; Google Books (CA) description read: "In the eighteenth century, Bridgetown, Barbados, was heavily populated by both enslaved and free women"; "Fuentes interrogates the archive and its historical production to expose the ongoing effects"; "Combining fragmentary sources with interdisciplinary methodologies that include black feminist theory"; "Fuentes challenges the way we write histories of vulnerable and often invisible subjects." No preview text; her phrase "reading along the bias grain" (a reply to Stoler's "along the grain") is known to me from memory of the book, not from a passage read this session, and is **not** used in any proposal. No open review found (H-Net and Reviews in History searches returned only passing citations).

