- Arlette Farge, *Le Goût de l'archive* (Seuil, 1989; Yale's page: "the original was published in 1989"); *The Allure of the Archives*, trans. Thomas Scott-Railton, foreword Natalie Zemon Davis (Yale, e-book 24 September 2013; paperback 24 February 2015, 152 pp., Lewis Walpole Series). Yale page read: "An exquisite appreciation of the distinctive rewards of historical research"; blurbs from Arnold Hunt (*TLS*: "an eloquent testimony to the materiality of the archive and its power to astonish and delight"), Jacob Soll, Scott McLemee, Brenda Wineapple ("a methodological handbook"), Kathleen Wellman, Kunal Parker, Richard Price, Kathryn Burns; second place, 2014 French-American Foundation translation prize. Body unread. The Seuil page 404.
- Antoinette Burton, ed., *Archive Stories: Facts, Fictions, and the Writing of History* (Duke, January 2006 per the Duke page; Crossref "issued 2005"; 408 pp.). Contents read: Introduction "Archive Fever, Archive Stories" (Burton, p. 1); Part I "Close Encounters: The Archive as Contact Zone": Durba Ghosh, "National Narratives and the Politics of Miscegenation: Britain and India" (p. 27); Jeff Sahadeo; Craig Robertson; Tony Ballantyne, "Mr. Peal's Archive" (p. 87); Horacio N. Roque Ramírez; Renee M. Sentilles; Part II "States of Art: 'Official' Archives and Counter-Histories": Jennifer S. Milligan, "'What Is an Archive?' in the History of Modern France" (p. 159); Peter Fritzsche; John Randolph; Laura Mayhall; Kathryn J. Oberdeck; Marilyn Booth; Part III "Archive Matters: The Past in the Present": Helena Pohlandt-McCormick (Soweto 1976); **Adele Perry, "The Colonial Archive on Trial: Possession, Dispossession, and History in *Delgamuukw v. British Columbia*" (p. 325)**; Ann Curthoys, "The History of Killing and the Killing of History" (p. 351). Description: "challenges the claims to objectivity associated with the traditional archive"; essays by historians who treat "actual encounters" with archives. Blurbs: Bonnie G. Smith, Carolyn Steedman. Bodies unread.
- Anjali Arondekar, *For the Record: On Sexuality and the Colonial Archive in India* (Duke, September 2009, 232 pp., Next Wave). Duke page read: Introduction "Without a Trace"; chapters on Burton's anthropology, sodomy law, "Archival Attachments: The Story of an India-Rubber Dildo", Kipling's Mutiny papers; blurbs by Durba Ghosh (*H-Net*: "It is a diagnosis and a provocation"), Mrinalini Sinha ("not a typical critique of the colonial archive"), Povinelli, Bishnupriya Ghosh, Sreenivas. Body unread.
