## 13. Readers of Trouillot and Mbembe's abstract

### 13a. Bastien Craipain (Louisiana State University), review of Trouillot, *Stirring the Pot of Haitian History*, trans. and ed. Mariana Past and Benjamin Hebblethwaite (Liverpool University Press, 2021), H-Caribbean, H-Net Reviews, October 2024, commissioned by Aaron Coy Moulton
URL https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=60011 (**read in full**, via curl; H-Net permits non-profit redistribution with attribution). Opening: "Three decades after its publication in 1995, Michel-Rolph Trouillot's *Silencing the Past: Power and the Production of History* remains an unavoidable reference for anyone navigating the streams of Black and Caribbean studies in academia and beyond." "With his critical indictment of Western historiography and its systematic disregard for Haiti's contribution to emancipatory struggles and decolonial politics in the Atlantic world, Trouillot not only offered an eye-opening analysis of the epistemological failures and ideological maneuverings intrinsic to history telling as a professional activity but also provided a crucial framework for thinking through the question of why Haiti has long needed 'new narratives'". Trouillot's words as Craipain quotes them: the "historical guild" and its facilitation of "the relegation to an historical backburner of the three themes to which [the Haitian Revolution] was linked: racism, slavery and colonialism" (*Silencing the Past*, Beacon 1995, p. 98, n. 3). *Ti difé boulé* was "originally written in Haitian Creole and published in 1977 by the independent Brooklyn-based Koléksion Lakansièl"; the Liverpool title was Évelyne Trouillot's rendering of the original's "dual meaning of 'clarification' and 'confrontation'" (Jonassaint's afterword, p. 182); the book "has remained virtually absent from bibliographies in North Atlantic academia despite the ubiquitous presence of its author's name on graduate and undergraduate syllabi"; "As Trouillot taught us, not all narratives or narrators are created equal." Lyonel Trouillot's preface, as quoted (p. xxiii): the group in 1970s New York "harbored an anti- or rather a counter-academism, with a vast knowledge of the 'academic' literature in the areas under study", insisting "on the originality of the knowledge to be produced … to oppose the knowledge embargo imposed by the 'official' venues". Notes: n. 1, Raoul Peck's *Exterminate All the Brutes* (HBO, 2021) "based in part on *Silencing the Past*"; n. 2, "The Odd and the Ordinary: Haiti, the Caribbean, and the World", *Cimarrón* 2:3 (1990): 3–12; n. 8, Laurent Dubois, "Éloge pour Michel-Rolph Trouillot", *Transition* 109 (2012): 27, and **Marlene L. Daut, "Beyond Trouillot: Unsettling Genealogies of Historical Thought", *Small Axe* 25:1 (March 2021): 136** (the one named critique found; unread; an H-Net review of Daut's *Awakening the Ashes* reports that she "urges readers to go beyond the book's account of Haitian history", search rendering only).

### 13b. Jared Davidson, "Silencing the past: reflections on remembering and forgetting", *Overland* (28 May 2020, updated 26 June 2020)
URL https://overland.org.au/2020/05/silencing-the-past-reflections-on-remembering-and-forgetting (open magazine essay by a New Zealand archivist-historian; **read in fetch summary**; it gives no page numbers). Trouillot's sentences as Davidson quotes them (all from *Silencing the Past*, 1995; "as quoted by Davidson", no pages): "The past – or, more accurately, pastness – is a position"; "in history, power begins at the source"; "archives are the institutions that organise facts and sources"; silencing is "an active and transitive process: one 'silences' a fact or an individual as a silencer silences a gun"; "Silences are produced and reproduced throughout any telling of a story"; "Silences enter the process of historical production at four crucial moments" (the block quotation; the four are not listed in the essay); "any historical narrative is a particular bundle of silences"; "Power is constitutive of the story"; the four moments "help us understand why not all silences are equal". These are the only sentences of chapter 1 available to me through an eligible route; each is marked "as quoted by Davidson (2020), unpaginated" wherever used.

