/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '95,165p' data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md" in /home/jic823/historiography
 succeeded in 0ms:
**Raben (first page only, p. 556).** "There was a time when colonial historiography was performed by going to the archives, reading what colonial administrators had to say about the topic, and writing it down. Later, there was a time when perusing those same archives was an extremely suspect, if inevitable, activity … Times have changed again. In line with the view that sources tell something primarily about themselves and their makers, Ann Laura Stoler has written a captivating account about reading the colonial archives." The rest (pp. 557–560, on chapters 3–5) is unread.

**Schulte Nordholt (read in full).** The summary: "Archives are no longer seen as simple repositories of historical data but are also approached as objects of research" (p. 560); "we can no longer speak of 'the colonial project' as a single and coherent phenomenon, as Stoler did in her earlier work" (p. 560); "Good historians … are accustomed to encountering contestations and contradictions during archival research, but they are less often accustomed to making archives into an object of ethnographic research. *Along the archival grain* is an eloquent plea to explore this approach more seriously" (p. 560). Stoler's words as he quotes them: archives "not just as repositories of state power, but as unquiet movements in a field of force, as restless realignments and readjustments of people and the beliefs to which they were tethered, as spaces in which the senses and the affective course through the seeming abstractions of political rationalities" (book pp. 32–33); her ambition to "look for the pulse of the archive in the quiescence and quickened pace of its own production, in the steady and feverish rhythms of repeated incantations, formulae, and frames" (book p. 35); the aim "to set a new standard for an ethnography of the archives (p. 45)" as against "doing an ethnography in the archives" (review pp. 561–562); the "politics of disregard" (ch. 7). His doubts: "The power of Stoler's rhetoric is also a bit confusing, and I am not quite sure how to identify these 'pulses'" (p. 560); "the case studies are examples of good fieldwork in the archives, and have not yet set a new standard" (p. 562); "Why were archives in Indonesia ignored in this ethnography? … no Indonesian scholars are mentioned in the opening pages of the book … *Along the archival grain* has, therefore, despite its innovative ambitions, an unexpected colonial flavour" (p. 562). The footnotes' references to "Heidegger, Kant, Nietzsche, Nussbaum, Rorty" (p. 560).

**Stoler's reply (first paragraph only, p. 562).** "There were times in working on *Along the archival grain* over the last some 20 years when I thought I could hear Jorge Luis Borges laughing at me … attempting to trace a map of the Dutch colonial archives as dense and extensive as they were themselves." The reply's answers to the three reviewers (pp. 563–567?) are unread.

**Use.** The strand "reading along the grain" and the Stoler edges can rest on the book's words as quoted by two named readers with page numbers (pp. 3, 5, 24, 32–33, 35, 41, 43, 45, 53, 98, 278), on the reviewers' statements of what the book does, and on their criticisms (Gouda: Dutch historiography skipped; Schulte Nordholt: the pulses hard to identify, Indonesian archives and scholars absent, a "colonial flavour"). The book itself remains unread; the 2002 article's "archive-as-source to archive-as-subject" is the only sentence of Stoler's own read directly.

## 3. Joan M. Schwartz and Terry Cook, "Archives, records, and power: the making of modern memory", *Archival Science* 2:1–2 (March 2002): 1–19

DOI 10.1007/BF02435628. **Read status: abstract, keywords and reference list only** (paywalled). The abstract, as displayed: it introduces "the first of two thematic issues of *Archival Science* on 'archives, records, and power'"; archives are often treated as neutral, but are established by the powerful and shape which stories are remembered; "Archives, then, are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated"; archivists' choices in appraisal, description, preservation and access shape collective memory and identity, and the authors call for these powers to be openly debated and held accountable (the last two clauses paraphrased by the fetch). Keywords: archival theory; archives and power relationships; identity formation; representation and reality; social memory. References cite Derrida (*Archive Fever*, n. 15; "Derridean Deconstruction", n. 17) and Verne Harris (nn. 5, 10, 17, 26, 27); not Foucault, Stoler or Trouillot. Schwartz (National Archives of Canada / Queen's) and Cook (University of Manitoba / Clio Consulting) are the Canadian archival-science side of the turn; the "archives, records, and power" issues frame the field's own reckoning with Derrida through Harris (South Africa). Body unread; a library copy would give the sentences on appraisal and on the postmodern archive.

## 4. Michel-Rolph Trouillot: life, works and identity

### 4a. University of Chicago News, "Michel-Rolph Trouillot, scholar of Caribbean history, 1949–2012" (William Harms, 10 July 2012)
URL https://news.uchicago.edu/story/michel-rolph-trouillot-scholar-caribbean-history-1949-2012. **Read in full (fetch summary).** Died 5 July 2012, aged 62. "Grew up in Haiti"; came to the United States to join the Haitian exile community in New York "during the Duvalier years of the late 1960s"; a songwriter and activist against the Duvalier dictatorship and against US treatment of undocumented Haitian immigrants. BA in Caribbean history and culture, City University of New York, 1978; PhD in anthropology, Johns Hopkins, 1985. Before Chicago, "Krieger/Eisenhower Distinguished Professor of Anthropology" and director of the Institute for Global Studies in Culture, Power and History at Johns Hopkins; joined the Chicago faculty in 1998 as professor of anthropology. Works named with dates: *Ti Dife Boule sou Istoua Ayiti* (1977), "a Haitian Creole history of the Haitian Revolution of 1791–1804"; *Peasants and Capital: Dominica in the World Economy* (1988); *Haiti: State against Nation: The Origins and Legacy of Duvalierism* (1990); *Silencing the Past: Power and the Production of History* (1995); *Open the Social Sciences* (1996; the Gulbenkian Commission report, of which he was a member); *Global Transformations: Anthropology and the Modern World* (2003). Award: the Caribbean Philosophical Association's 2011 Frantz Fanon Lifetime Achievement Award "for the originality of his interrogations in the human sciences". Colleagues quoted: Greg Beckett ("Rolph asked difficult questions, and he insisted others do the same"); Jean Comaroff; Yarimar Bonilla ("Rolph's work is the gold-standard for me intellectually"). A service in Port-au-Prince; a Chicago memorial planned for the autumn. Not given: birth date, Duke post, the 2002 aneurysm (which other obituaries mention; not read here).

### 4b. Wikidata check, Q6836249 (QLever SPARQL, qlever.dev/api/wikidata, 2026-10-23)
Query: the item's English label, description, P569, P570, P106 labels, P214, P19. Statements seen: label "Michel-Rolph Trouillot"; description "Haitian academic and anthropologist"; P569 1949-11-26; P570 2012-07-05; P106 historian, academic, anthropologist; P214 VIAF 2600615; P19 Port-au-Prince. **Verdict: correct** (death date matches the Chicago obituary; occupation and country match). The brief's "probably Q6836249" is confirmed; no namesake was found necessary to reject.

### 4c. *Trouillot Remixed: The Michel-Rolph Trouillot Reader*, ed. Yarimar Bonilla, Greg Beckett and Mayanthi L. Fernando (Duke University Press, December 2021; 456 pp.)
Duke page https://www.dukeupress.edu/trouillot-remixed, **publisher description and contents only.** Author line: "Michel-Rolph Trouillot (1949–2012) was Professor of Anthropology and Social Sciences at the University of Chicago". Contents: Prelude, Bonilla, "Remembering the Songwriter: The Life and Legacies of Michel-Rolph Trouillot"; Overture, "Trouillot Remixed"; Part I "Geography of Imagination": "Anthropology and the Savage Slot: The Poetics and Politics of Otherness", "The Odd and the Ordinary: Haiti, the Caribbean, and the World", "The Vulgarity of Power", "Good Day, Columbus: Silences, Power, and Public History (1492–1892)"; Part II "The Otherwise Modern": "The Otherwise Modern: Caribbean Lessons from the Savage Slot", "The Caribbean Region: An Open Frontier in Anthropological Theory", "Culture on the Edges: Creolization in the Plantation Context", "The Perspective of the World: Globalization Then and Now"; Part III "The Fields in Which We Work": "Making Sense: The Fields in Which We Work", "Caribbean Peasantries and World Capitalism: An Approach to Micro-level Studies", "The Anthropology of the State in the Age of Globalization: Close Encounters of a Deceptive Kind", "From Planters' Journals of Academia: The Haitian Revolution as Unthinkable History"; Part IV "A New Duty Arises": "Adieu, Culture: A New Duty Arises", "The Presence in the Past", "Abortive Rituals: Historical Apologies in the Global Era", "The Interrupted March to Democracy"; an interlude, "Ti Dife Boule: Radio Haiti Interview, 1977" (trans. Laura Wagner); "Liner Notes: A Comprehensive Bibliography". Blurbs: Christina Sharpe ("the clarity of Michel-Rolph Trouillot's arguments about power and the status of the historical"); David Scott, Columbia ("a thematically distilled selection of his work"); *New West Indian Guide*; *Journal of Global South Studies*.

**Correction to the brief.** David Scott's "The Theory of Haiti: *The Black Jacobins* and the Poetics of Universal History" is *Small Axe* 45 (November 2014): 35–51, an essay on C. L. R. James, not a chapter of *Trouillot Remixed* (whose contents, above, are all Trouillot's own texts plus the editors' prelude and overture). Scott's assessment of Trouillot appears on the Duke page only as a blurb. The named readers of Trouillot found instead: the *Journal of Haitian Studies* special issue 19:2 (Fall 2013; §4d), Hazel Carby's 2015 foreword (unread), Bonilla's 2021 prelude (unread), Colin Dayan (Boston Review and JHS 2013; 403 this session).

### 4d. *Journal of Haitian Studies* 19:2 (Fall 2013), special issue on Michel-Rolph Trouillot (guest editor Nadève Ménard, with Patrick Bellegarde-Smith)
Contents page read (UC Santa Barbara, engagedscholarship.ucsb.edu/journal-of-haitian-studies/volume-19-number-2); the articles are on Project MUSE (challenge page this session; unread). Items: Ménard, "Adieu, Gérard"; Colin Dayan, "Remembering Trouillot"; J. Michael Dash, "Neither Magical nor Exceptional: The Idea of the Ordinary in Caribbean Studies"; Ethel Hazard, "Alterity and History: The Cross-Current Contributions of Michel-Rolph Trouillot"; Drexel G. Woodson, "Le Rire particulier de Rolph Trouillot"; Greg Beckett, "The Ontology of Freedom: The Unthinkable Miracle of Haiti"; **Alyssa Goldstein Sepinwall, "Still Unthinkable? The Haitian Revolution and the Reception of Michel-Rolph Trouillot's *Silencing the Past*", pp. 75–103, DOI 10.1353/jhs.2013.0036 (Crossref: issued September 2013)**; Michel Acacia; Jemima Pierre, "Haiti and the 'Savage Slot'"; Sarah Quesada; Claudy Delné; Carolle Charles, "New York 1967–71, Prelude to *Ti difé boulé*: An Encounter with Liberation Theology, Marxism, and the Black National Liberation Movement"; Guy-Gérald Ménard; Bellegarde-Smith's afterword; reviews of *Ti dife boule* (Jocelyne Trouillot-Lévy) and *Global Transformations* (Jason Antrosio); Woodson, "Byen Pre Pa Lakay: Toward a Complete Bibliography of Michel-Rolph Trouillot's Writings". Sepinwall's title is the dated reception event for the entry; her findings are unread.


### 4e. University of Chicago Library, Hanna Holborn Gray Special Collections Research Center, finding aid "Michel-Rolph Trouillot Papers" (PDF generated 2025-09-19)
URL https://www.lib.uchicago.edu/ead/rlg/ICU.SPCL.TROUILLOTMR.pdf. **Biographical note read in full** (text layer extracted locally). "Michel-Rolph Trouillot (1949-2012) was an anthropologist known for his work on Caribbean history and culture, and his best-known publications include Silencing the Past (1995) and Global Transformations (2003)." "Trouillot grew up in Haiti and came to the United States in 1968 as part of a large wave of students who were fleeing the Duvalier regime. He graduated from Brooklyn College, City University of New York (CUNY) in 1978 with a Bachelor of Arts degree in Caribbean history and culture. Trouillot completed his PhD in 1985 at Johns Hopkins University where he studied under Sidney W. Mintz and Richard Price. From 1983-1988, Trouillot was an assistant professor of anthropology at Duke University, and in 1988, he joined Johns Hopkins as an associate professor where he was later appointed as the Krieger/Eisenhower Professor of Anthropology. There, he founded and was director of the Institute for Global Studies in Culture, History, and Power. In 1998 Rolph joined the faculty at the University of Chicago until his death in 2012." Folder titles seen in the inventory, usable as dated context: "Duke University Correspondence Concerning promotion, 1987"; "Krieger/Eisenhower Chair, Johns Hopkins University, 1993-1996"; "Harvard, Keynote, October 2-3, 1998"; "Apologies Project Talk, 1998"; "Fondation Mémoire Committee Notes, 1998". The finding aid does not give the birth date, the 2002 illness or the Fanon award.

### 4f. Rodrigo C. Bulamah, Júlia Vilaça Goyatá and Bethânia Pereira, "Beyond exceptionalism: notes on Michel-Rolph Trouillot's 'The odd and the ordinary'", *Vibrant: Virtual Brazilian Anthropology* 17 (2020), DOI 10.1590/1809-43412020v17j552 (open access; Redalyc HTML)
**Read status: fetch summary of the open HTML (paragraphs 1–7 and notes 2–4).** An introduction to the first Portuguese translation of Trouillot's "The Odd and the Ordinary: Haiti, the Caribbean, and the World", "originally published in 1990, in the third issue of the journal *Cimarrón: New Perspectives on the Caribbean*" (para. 1). Biographical details given: he "left Haiti in 1968 because of political persecution"; "taught at Duke from 1983, completed a PhD at Johns Hopkins in 1985, and joined the University of Chicago in 1998"; died in Chicago "at age 61 from a brain aneurysm" (note 4; the Chicago obituary says 62, which the 26 November 1949 birth date supports); his father Ernest Trouillot (1922–1987) and uncle Hénock Trouillot (1923–1988) were historians (note 3). The one sentence of *Silencing the Past* quoted: "I grew up in a family where history sat at the dinner table" (preface, cited as 1995, p. 15, in note 3). Their reading of *Silencing the Past*: the book shows that "the production of history, through both social practices and narratives, is shaped by situated power relations" (para. 3). On *Haiti: State against Nation* (1990): "the classic", "exerting a strong influence on later works" (para. 2). On *Global Transformations* (2003): concepts such as culture, modernity and globalization were called "North Atlantic universals" (para. 5). From "The odd and the ordinary" (cited as 1990a: 7): Haiti as the site of the West's "longest neocolonial experiment", its image "dominated by Christianity, capitalism, and whiteness"; the exceptionalist image works as a "shield" (paras 3–4). Bonilla's "Ordinary Sovereignty" (2013) cited as a continuation (para. 7). The "four moments" and the "unthinkable" are not discussed in the part read.

### 4g. Other Trouillot items checked (metadata only)
- *Global Transformations: Anthropology and the Modern World* (Palgrave Macmillan, © 2003; Springer record: hardcover and softcover 27 January 2004, x + 178 pp.). Contents (Springer): Introduction 1–5; "Anthropology and the Savage Slot: The Poetics and Politics of Otherness" 7–28 (DOI 10.1007/978-1-137-04144-9_2; its displayed abstract opens "Anthropology discovered globalization before the term became fashionable", and continues on the late-1970s change in global flows and the mid-1980s postmodern reassessment of ethnography; the chapter page does not note its first publication in Richard Fox, ed., *Recapturing Anthropology*, 1991, which is known only from the *Trouillot Remixed* contents and the Chicago obituary context); "North Atlantic Fictions: Global Transformations, 1492–1945" 29–46; "A Fragmented Globality" 47–78; "The Anthropology of the State in the Age of Globalization: Close Encounters of the Deceptive Kind" 79–96 (first in *Current Anthropology* 42:1, 2001; the Chicago journals page returned 403); "Adieu, Culture: A New Duty Arises" 97–116; "Making Sense: The Fields in which We Work" 117–139. Author line: "Professor of Anthropology at the University of Chicago"; the bio lists *Silencing the Past* as "(Beacon, 1997)", i.e. the paperback. Blurbs by Richard G. Fox and Sidney W. Mintz (not reproduced by the fetch).
- *Silencing the Past: Power and the Production of History* (Beacon Press, 1995; Kirkus gives the publication date as 21 November 1995; 20th-anniversary edition 17 March 2015 with a foreword by Hazel V. Carby, ISBN 9780807080535, 216 pp. on Google Books). Contents (Beacon): Acknowledgements; Preface; 1 "The Power in the Story"; 2 "The Three Faces of Sans Souci"; 3 "An Unthinkable History"; 4 "Good Day, Columbus"; 5 "The Presence in the Past"; Epilogue; Notes; Index. Google Books' structure for the 2015 edition: ch. 2 from p. 31, ch. 3 from p. 70, ch. 4 from p. 108, ch. 5 from p. 141, Epilogue p. 154, Notes p. 157. Beacon's description: "The 20th anniversary edition of a pioneering classic that explores the contexts in which history is produced"; "places the West's failure to acknowledge the most successful slave revolt in history" beside Holocaust denial, the Alamo and Columbus; the new foreword addresses "the book's influence and teaching value" (fetch paraphrase). **The body, including the "four moments" passage of chapter 1 and "An Unthinkable History", was not read**: Beacon's excerpt tab displayed no text, Google Books showed no preview text, and the only full copies found online were on excluded routes. The four moments are known to me only as later writers list them (e.g. the Cambridge Core summary of Jeff Strickland's *All for Liberty*, 2021, conclusion: "fact creation (the making of sources), fact assembly (the making of archives); fact retrieval (the making of narratives); and retrospective significance (the making of history in the final instance)", with no Trouillot page given). I therefore give no quotation of the four moments as Trouillot's words; the strand text must say "the four moments at which silences enter historical production (sources, archives, narratives, retrospective significance), as every later summary lists them", and the coordinator should settle the wording from a library copy (the passage is early in ch. 1; one web summary cites p. 26).
- *Haiti: State against Nation* (Monthly Review Press, 1990): the press page returned 404; dated from the Chicago obituary and the finding aid. *Peasants and Capital: Dominica in the World Economy* (Johns Hopkins University Press, 1988): dated from the obituary. *Ti dife boule sou istwa Ayiti* (1977): obituary, finding aid, JHS 2013 review title.
- "Culture on the Edges: Caribbean Creolization in Historical Context", in Brian Keith Axel, ed., *From the Margins: Historical Anthropology and Its Futures* (Duke, June 2002), p. 189 — the same volume whose Part 1, "Ethnography and the Archive", opens with Nicholas B. Dirks, "Annals of the Archive: Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History" (p. 47) and Talal Asad (p. 66), and whose Part 2 carries Stoler, "Developing Historical Negatives: Race and the (Modernist) Visions of a Colonial State" (p. 156, later ch. 4 of *Along the Archival Grain*). Duke page contents read; bodies unread. Mbembe's blurb on the Duke page: "*From the Margins* exemplifies the best of current thinking in anthropology."

## 5. Terry Cook, "Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth: Postmodernism and the Practice of Archives", *Archivaria* 51 (Spring 2001): 14–35
URL https://archivaria.ca/index.php/archivaria/article/view/12792 (open access; PDF read, pp. 14–35, text layer; **read in part: the opening, note *, pp. 21 and 31–32 in full, the rest skimmed by keyword**). Origin: "a plenary address delivered to the Association of Canadian Archivists in Winnipeg on 8 June 2001"; two other plenaries, by Verne Harris and Heather MacNeil, appear in the same issue; it complements Cook's "Archival Science and Postmodernism: New Formulations for Old Concepts", *Archival Science* 1:1 (2001): 3–24 (note *, p. 14). Own words, p. 21: "postmodernist writers themselves are now beginning to address archives directly in their writings, as institution, as activity, as records, as recording media, as collective memory, as social phenomenon. When Jacques Derrida, arguably the world's most famous living philosopher, devotes an entire book, his 1996 *Archive Fever*, to the very raison d'être of the archival profession, something significant is happening. In the Derrida aftershock, historians, geographers, philosophers, sociologists, anthropologists, psychologists, and cultural theorists are right now subjecting the archival world to a detailed critique such as it has never before experienced." Note 13 (p. 20–21) names the "pioneering postmodern archivists before Cook" as "also Canadian, Brien Brothman and Richard Brown", with Brothman's review of *Archive Fever* in *Archivaria* 43 (1997) and his "Declining Derrida" (*Archivaria* 48, 1999). On appraisal, pp. 31–32: the aim that archives "acquire in its holdings multiple voices, and not by default only the voices of the powerful"; "It is important, as Verne Harris notes, not to romanticize the marginalized"; the closing quotation of Harris (*Exploring Archives*, 2nd ed., 2000, p. 45) ending in Derrida's words, "'it opens out of the future'"; "the Canadian parallel way of archival remembering through macro-appraisal may speak strongly to archivists in this new century" (p. 32). Note 23 (p. 25) names Foucault's "historical methodology and Derrida's seminal volume" as his own exposures, with Brothman, Brown, Dodge, Harris among his interlocutors. Spivak is cited (note 33) as "a keen reading of Derrida within feminist and postcolonial studies". Cook's "What Is Past Is Prologue" (*Archivaria* 43, 1997: 17–63) was checked by its page only (abstract: "a new conceptual paradigm is emerging for the profession"; from "product-focused to … process-oriented").

**Use.** The Canadian strand: the archival profession's own postmodern turn (Brothman 1993–99, Cook 1997–2001, Schwartz and Cook 2002, with Harris in South Africa) ran beside and partly before the historians' and anthropologists' turn, and took Derrida directly. Cook's words date the "Derrida aftershock" to 1996–2001 from inside the profession.

## 6. Stoler's other books through named readers and publisher pages

### 6a. *Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power: Race and the Intimate in Colonial Rule* (University of California Press, 2002; 2nd ed. with a new preface, February 2010, 368 pp.)
UC Press page (2010 edition) read: contents 1 "Genealogies of the Intimate: Movements in Colonial Studies"; 2 "Rethinking Colonial Categories: European Communities and the Boundaries of Rule"; 3 "Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power: Gender and Morality in the Making of Race"; 4 "Sexual Affronts and Racial Frontiers: Cultural Competence and the Dangers of Métissage"; 5 "A Sentimental Education: Children on the Imperial Divide"; 6 "A Colonial Reading of Foucault: Bourgeois Bodies and Racial Selves"; 7 "Memory-Work in Java: A Cautionary Tale"; Epilogue "Caveats on Comfort Zones and Comparative Frames". Author line: "Willy Brandt Distinguished University Professor of Anthropology and Historical Studies" at The New School for Social Research.
**Dawn Ottevaere (Michigan State), review, H-Women, H-Net Reviews, June 2004** (https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=9492; **read in full**). Stoler's words as she quotes them, with pages: a "basic commitment to identifying the political stakes lodged in what is defined as public or private, to studying the quotidian shaping of racialized colonial worlds and their disparate sites of production" (p. 9); the "apparatus that kept potentially subversive white colonials in line" (p. 13); "students of colonialism, anthropologists in particular, have taken the politically constructed dichotomy colonizer/colonized as a given rather than as a historically shifting pair of social categories that needs to be explained" (p. 13); "argues for deeper historical engagement with the range of practices in which racisms were produced and thus with the cultural framing of political categories" (p. 15); "Ethnographies of empire should attend both to changing sensibilities and to sex, to racialized regimes that were realized on a macro and micro scale" (p. 78); on métissage, "the tension between a form of authority simultaneously predicated on incorporating and distancing" (p. 83); "why Foucault's elusive and suggestive treatment of race still remains so marginal to what colonial historians take from him today" (p. 141); chapter 7 "situates memories of domestic service by those who served against the density of the archives around them" (p. 162) and "invites more work on colonial memory itself while making the 'colonial' a subject rather than an assumed category of analysis" (p. 203). Her judgement: "Stoler relies heavily on theory and philosophy, particularly Foucault"; "Like Joan Scott, Stoler advocates the careful evaluation of historically designated categories"; "the strength of this text is not in its stories of colonial life … but in its instructive method"; "The weakness of this book is its inaccessibility to casual readers or undergraduates".

### 6b. *Haunted by Empire: Geographies of Intimacy in North American History* (ed.; Duke, May 2006, 568 pp.; American Encounters/Global Interactions)
Duke page read: Stoler's "Intimidations of Empire: Predicaments of the Tactile and Unseen" (p. 1) and "Tense and Tender Ties: The Politics of Comparison in North American History and (Post) Colonial Studies" (p. 23; first *Journal of American History* 88:3, December 2001, from p. 829, DOI 10.2307/2700385, Crossref), with contributors including Nayan Shah, Lisa Lowe ("The Intimacies of Four Continents", p. 191), Tiya Miles, Paul A. Kramer, Linda Gordon, Catherine Hall (commentary) and Nancy F. Cott (afterword). The volume brings "postcolonial critiques to North American history" (description, paraphrased). Bodies unread.

### 6c. *Imperial Debris: On Ruins and Ruination* (ed.; Duke, 2013, 384 pp.)
**Ronald Schultz (University of Wyoming), review, H-Empire, H-Net Reviews, February 2015, commissioned by Charles V. Reed** (https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=41934; **read in full**). Opening: "Ann Laura Stoler has always given us important things to think about. First it was Michel Foucault's Collège de France lectures about the biopolitics of race in *Race and the Education of Desire* … (1995). Then came several innovative works about the integral role of intimate relationships and sexuality in the formation and maintenance of colonial societies, culminating in *Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power* … (2002)." On the lead essay "The Rot Remains: From Ruins to Ruination": "as much a manifesto as an academic call to arms"; Stoler "challenges colonial scholars to move beyond the theoretical and empirical limitations of postcolonial and subaltern studies"; ruin is a "violent verb" (p. 7); ruination is "what people are left with" (p. 9). Contributors named: Nancy Rose Hunt, E. Valentine Daniel, Greg Grandin, Sharad Chari, John Collins, Ariella Azoulay, Gastón Gordillo. (*Duress: Imperial Durabilities in Our Times*, Duke 2016, was not checked; dated from Wikipedia's citation of the Duke page, orientation only.)

### 6d. Posts and identity (Stoler)
New School faculty page returned 429 three times; the search engine's rendering of newschool.edu pages gives: "Willy Brandt Distinguished University Professor of Anthropology and Historical Studies"; University of Michigan 1989–2003; at the New School for Social Research since 2004, "founding chair of its revitalized Anthropology Department"; director of the Institute for Critical Social Inquiry; PhD, Anthropology, Columbia. Wikipedia (orientation only): born 1949, Brooklyn; BA Barnard 1972, MA Columbia 1976, PhD Columbia 1982; Wisconsin–Madison 1983–89; Michigan 1989–2004 (the New School pages say 2003); Benda Prize 1992 for *Capitalism and Confrontation in Sumatra's Plantation Belt, 1870–1979* (Yale 1985; 2nd ed. Michigan 1995); *Duress* (Duke 2016); *Interior Frontiers* (OUP 2022); the French translations *La Chair de l'Empire* (2013) and *Au Coeur de l'Archive Coloniale* (EHESS 2019). Only the UC Press author line, the BKI by-line ("The New School for Social Research, New York", 2009) and the search rendering of the New School page are institutional confirmations; the 1983–89 Wisconsin post and the degrees rest on Wikipedia's citation of the ICSI page (404 this session).

**QLever check, Q16203101** (qlever.dev/api/wikidata, 2026-10-23): label "Ann Laura Stoler"; description "American anthropologist"; P569 1949-04-01 (the day may be a precision artefact; Wikipedia gives only 1949); P106 university teacher, historian, anthropologist; P214 VIAF 263247736. **Verdict: correct**; the bulk match of 2026-09-22 is now individually confirmed.

### 6e. Cooper and Stoler, "Introduction. Tensions of empire: colonial control and visions of rule", *American Ethnologist* 16:4 (November 1989): 609–621 (DOI 10.1525/ae.1989.16.4.02a00010, Crossref) and "Between Metropole and Colony: Rethinking a Research Agenda", in *Tensions of Empire* (UC Press, February 1997)
Metadata only; the 1989 special-issue introduction is the first joint statement, the 1997 chapter its expansion (`cooper_stoler_tensions_1997` in the graph: contents and description only). Neither body was read this session; the person entry dates both and claims nothing from them beyond their existence and titles.

## 7. Jacques Derrida, *Archive Fever: A Freudian Impression* (lecture, Freud Museum, London, 5 June 1994; *Diacritics* 25:2, Summer 1995, from p. 9, trans. Eric Prenowitz, DOI 10.2307/465144; University of Chicago Press, © 1996, viii + 114 pp., series Religion and Postmodernism, ISBNs 9780226502359 / 9780226851228)
Chicago page read (description: "an extended meditation on remembrance, religion, time, and technology"; blurbs from *The Guardian*, *Choice*, *Library Journal*, Jeremy Barris). The graph already holds `derrida_archive_primary` with pp. 1–5 and 7–15 read in 2026-09 — but through a PDF link on a mirror host of the kind the 2026-10-19 provenance rule excludes; proposed correction in P.9: replace the URL with the Chicago page and the Diacritics DOI, keep the reading record in the note. The lecture date (5 June 1994) is not on the Chicago page; it rests on the graph's existing note ("lecture 1994") and is not asserted to the day here.

## 8. Carolyn Steedman, *Dust* (Manchester University Press, 2001; US edition *Dust: The Archive and Cultural History*, Rutgers, 1 February 2002, 208 pp., Encounters: Cultural Histories) and "Something She Called a Fever: Michelet, Derrida, and Dust", *American Historical Review* 106:4 (October 2001): 1159–1180 (DOI 10.2307/2692943)
Rutgers page read (description: Steedman "calls the practice of paying attention to the archive 'archivization'"; the archive as the repository of "that which will not go away"; "the 'matter of history' can never go away or be erased"; author line "professor of history at the University of Warwick"). Warwick's repository record (wrap.warwick.ac.uk/11562) confirms the AHR citation, pp. 1159–1180, no full text. Manchester UP page 403. **Bodies unread**; her reply to Derrida (the "fever" as the historian's literal and figurative condition) is known to me only by title and description. Steedman's blurb on *Archive Stories* (Duke page): "Important and timely, this fascinating collection of tales from a multitude of repositories and record offices".

