# Proposals

Format follows `data/extension-2026/languages-of-class-2026-10-22/languages_of_class/entry-draft.json` (group entry) and `patrick_joyce/entry-draft.json` (person entry). Edge ids `p4A_rel_NN`; source ids `p4a_*`. Everything below is a proposal for the coordinator; nothing has been applied.

## P.1 New entry `archival_turn` — "The archive as object"

- `id`: `archival_turn`; `label`: "The archive as object"; `entry_kind`: group; `entry_type`: Research field; `layer`: historiographical_developments (as for the other fields); `hunt_core_paradigm`: false; `period`: null (there is no `period_4` on disk; if the coordinator mints one in this wave, this entry belongs to it: its readership is a 2000s–2010s formation); `aliases`: ["archival turn", "the archive as subject", "ethnography of the archive"].
- `date_label`: "Le goût de l'archive 1989 · Archive Fever 1994/1996 · Silencing the Past 1995 · Dust 2001 · Archival Science 2 'archives, records, and power' 2002 · Archive Stories 2005 · 'Venus in Two Acts' 2008 · Along the Archival Grain 2009 · Dispossessed Lives 2016 · Trouillot Remixed 2021"

**description** (1,196 characters):

"A way of reading that takes the archive as an object of study rather than a quarry of facts. Trouillot's Silencing the Past (1995) located the silences of history in the making of sources, archives, narratives and 'history in the final instance', and made the Haitian Revolution the case of an event 'unthinkable' as it happened. Stoler asked colonial historians to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002) and to read 'along the archival grain' before reading against it, finding in Dutch colonial records a 'repository of good taste and bad faith' (2009, as quoted by Gouda). Farge's Le goût de l'archive (1989), Derrida's Archive Fever (1994) and Steedman's Dust (2001) wrote the archive's allure, fever and dust; Schwartz and Cook, from the archival profession, called archives 'active sites where social power is negotiated' (2002); Burton's Archive Stories (2005) collected historians' encounters with their sources; Hartman's 'critical fabulation' (2008) and Fuentes (2016) confronted the violence of slavery's archive. No opposing side formed: the History PhD citation graph finds Stoler the strongest riser of 2015–25 and the cluster a framework without a debate."

**scope_note** (draft): "A Research-field entry gathering a readership rather than a school: in the History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23) the share of Canada + US theses citing two or more of Stoler, Trouillot, Farge, Derrida, Hartman, Steedman, Burton, Fuentes, Blouin and Rosenberg rose from 0 (1990s) to 3.3% (2000–09), 7.1%, 7.6% and 10.4% (2020–25; US 11.8%, Canada 9.0%; Europe 2.1%), with Stoler cited by 10.7% of theses in 2020–25 across 11 of 18 fields and Trouillot by 5.9%; no opposing side is co-cited (§5 of the cohorts README). Six strands: the archive as a site of power (Trouillot, Mbembe, Schwartz and Cook); reading along the grain (Stoler); allure, fever and dust (Farge, Derrida, Steedman); absence, violence and critical fabulation (Hartman, Fuentes); archive stories and the ethnography of archives (Burton, Dirks, Zeitlyn); the archivists' turn in Canada and South Africa (Cook, Schwartz, Harris, Brothman). Research of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md): read in full — Gouda (2009), Schulte Nordholt (2009), Ottevaere (2004), Schultz (2015), Craipain (2024), the Chicago obituary and the finding aid for Trouillot; in part — Cook (2001), the Bulamah–Goyatá–Pereira introduction (2020), Davidson (2020); abstracts and contents only — Stoler 2002, Schwartz and Cook 2002, Mbembe 2002, Manoff 2004, Trouillot 2003, Along the Archival Grain, Archive Stories, From the Margins, Haunted by Empire, Duress, For the Record, The Archive and the Repertoire. Unread: the bodies of Silencing the Past (the four-moments passage is given as later writers list it and as Davidson quotes it, unpaginated), Along the Archival Grain, Carnal Knowledge, Archive Fever, Dust and the AHR essay, Le goût de l'archive, 'Venus in Two Acts', Dispossessed Lives, The Imperial Archive, Processing the Past, Zeitlyn 2012, the HHS 1999 issue, Sepinwall 2013, Daut 2021, Carby's foreword. Indigenous data sovereignty and the digital archive are out of scope (see web_history for the 2024 uptake of Stoler's phrase)."

**representative_figures_and_works** (draft): "Founding texts: Arlette Farge, Le goût de l'archive (1989; The Allure of the Archives, 2013, foreword Natalie Zemon Davis); Thomas Richards, The Imperial Archive (1993); Jacques Derrida, Mal d'archive / Archive Fever (lecture 1994; Diacritics 1995; Chicago 1996); Michel-Rolph Trouillot, Silencing the Past (1995; 20th-anniversary edition with Hazel V. Carby's foreword, 2015); Saidiya Hartman, Scenes of Subjection (1997). The turn named: the History of the Human Sciences issue 'The Archive' (1999); Terry Cook, 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth' (Archivaria 51, 2001); Carolyn Steedman, Dust and 'Something She Called a Fever' (2001); Archival Science 2:1–2, 'archives, records, and power' (2002: Joan M. Schwartz and Terry Cook; Ann Laura Stoler, 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance'); Refiguring the Archive (2002: Achille Mbembe, 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits'; Stoler); From the Margins (2002: Nicholas B. Dirks, 'Annals of the Archive'); Marlene Manoff, 'Theories of the Archive from Across the Disciplines' (2004); Antoinette Burton, ed., Archive Stories (2005: Durba Ghosh, Tony Ballantyne, Adele Perry, Ann Curthoys); Diana Taylor, The Archive and the Repertoire (2003); Ann Cvetkovich, An Archive of Feelings (2003). The programme: Stoler, Along the Archival Grain (2009) and the Bijdragen debate (Gouda, Raben, Schulte Nordholt, 2009); Anjali Arondekar, For the Record (2009); Hartman, 'Venus in Two Acts' (Small Axe 26, 2008); Francis X. Blouin and William G. Rosenberg, Processing the Past (2011); David Zeitlyn, 'Anthropology in and of the Archives' (2012); Marisa J. Fuentes, Dispossessed Lives (2016); Trouillot Remixed (2021). Readers: Frances Gouda, Remco Raben, Henk Schulte Nordholt (2009); Alyssa Goldstein Sepinwall (2013); Marlene L. Daut (2021); Bastien Craipain (2024)."

**coverage**: `{reviewed_through: 2024, basis: "Surveyed 1989–2024 in the research of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md) through publisher pages, abstracts, open reviews and the History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23); the founding books themselves are unread beyond their contents and named readers, so the chronology's dates are firm and its characterisations rest on readers. The latest items are Craipain's 2024 review and the 2024 web-archives essay that borrows Stoler's phrase."}`

### P.1.1 Strands (all new; the entry is new)

1. `site_of_power` — "The archive as a site of power: Trouillot, Mbembe, Schwartz and Cook". Focus: "Trouillot's Silencing the Past (1995) holds that silences enter historical production at four moments — the making of sources, of archives, of narratives and of 'history in the final instance', as every later summary lists them — and that 'in history, power begins at the source' and 'archives are the institutions that organise facts and sources' (as quoted by Davidson, 2020, unpaginated). Craipain (2024) reads the book as a 'critical indictment of Western historiography' whose 'historical guild' relegated 'racism, slavery and colonialism' to 'an historical backburner' (Trouillot, p. 98, as quoted by Craipain). From the archival profession, Schwartz and Cook opened Archival Science's 'archives, records, and power' issues (2002) with the claim that archives 'are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated' (abstract); Mbembe's 'The Power of the Archive and its Limits' (2002) begins from the archive as 'a building, a symbol of a public institution' (abstract). The Haitian case and the archivists' statement are different routes to one claim: that the record is made by power before the historian reads it." person_ids: michel_rolph_trouillot, achille_mbembe, joan_m_schwartz, terry_cook. source_ids: p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995, p4a_davidson_overland_2020, p4a_craipain_hnet_2024, p4a_schwartz_cook_2002, p4a_mbembe_power_2002.

2. `along_the_grain` — "Reading along the grain: Stoler's ethnography of the colonial archive". Focus: "Stoler's 2002 essay asked anthropologists in colonial studies, whose 'archival activity tends to remain more an extractive than an ethnographic one', to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' and to ask 'what systems of classification and epistemology signal at specific times' (abstract). Along the Archival Grain (2009) treated Dutch colonial records 'not just as repositories of state power, but as unquiet movements in a field of force' (p. 32–33, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt), looked 'for the pulse of the archive in the quiescence and quickened pace of its own production' (p. 35) and aimed 'to set a new standard for an ethnography of the archives' (p. 45) rather than an ethnography in them. Gouda: she 'underscores the necessity to read along the grain before trying to read against it, as many postcolonial scholars in recent years have advocated' (2009, p. 552). The reviewers' doubts are part of the record: Schulte Nordholt was 'not quite sure how to identify these "pulses"' and found 'an unexpected colonial flavour' in a book that ignored archives in Indonesia and Indonesian scholars (pp. 560, 562); Gouda found 'so little engagement with the work of contemporary Dutch historians' (p. 555)." person_ids: ann_laura_stoler, frances_gouda, henk_schulte_nordholt, remco_raben. source_ids: p4a_stoler_archives_2002, p4a_stoler_archival_grain_2009, p4a_gouda_bki_2009, p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009, p4a_raben_bki_2009.

3. `allure_fever_dust` — "Allure, fever and dust: Farge, Derrida, Steedman". Focus: "Three texts made the historian's experience of the archive their subject. Farge's Le goût de l'archive (1989; English 2013, with Natalie Zemon Davis's foreword) is, in Yale's words, a reflection on the sensory and emotional experience of archival work in the judicial records of eighteenth-century Paris; Arnold Hunt called it 'an eloquent testimony to the materiality of the archive and its power to astonish and delight'. Derrida's Archive Fever (lecture 1994; Diacritics 1995; Chicago 1996), already on the derrida, memory and freud entries, is 'an extended meditation on remembrance, religion, time, and technology' that starts from archiving (publisher). Steedman's Dust (2001) and 'Something She Called a Fever: Michelet, Derrida, and Dust' (AHR 106:4, 2001) answered Derrida with the archive as the repository of 'that which will not go away' and named 'archivization' as the practice of attending to it (publisher description). Terry Cook dated the 'Derrida aftershock', in which 'historians, geographers, philosophers, sociologists, anthropologists, psychologists, and cultural theorists are right now subjecting the archival world to a detailed critique such as it has never before experienced', from inside the profession (2001, p. 21). Bodies of all three unread." person_ids: arlette_farge, derrida, carolyn_steedman, natalie_zemon_davis. source_ids: p4a_farge_allure_2013, p4a_derrida_archive_fever_chicago_1996, p4a_derrida_diacritics_1995, p4a_steedman_fever_2001, p4a_steedman_dust_2002, p4a_cook_archivaria_2001.

4. `absence_fabulation` — "Absence, violence and critical fabulation: Hartman, Fuentes". Focus: "Hartman's 'Venus in Two Acts' (Small Axe 26, June 2008) examines, by its own abstract as the journal lists it, a girl's presence and silence in the archive of Atlantic slavery; the MacArthur Foundation describes her 'speculative method she terms "critical fabulation," which questions historical archives as the sole source of credible information about the past' (2019). Scenes of Subjection (1997) 'traces continuities between pre- and post-emancipation eras' (MacArthur). Fuentes's Dispossessed Lives (2016), on enslaved and free women in eighteenth-century Bridgetown, 'interrogates the archive and its historical production to expose the ongoing effects' of slavery's violence, 'combining fragmentary sources with interdisciplinary methodologies that include black feminist theory' (publisher). Both are unread here; the strand records their place in the readership (Hartman 4.3% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25, from 0 in 2000–09; Fuentes a 2020–25 entrant to the US capitalism and Indigenous networks) and a reviewer's gloss that they urge historians 'to avoid inventing narrative where the archive is silent' (H-Net search rendering, not read)." person_ids: saidiya_hartman, marisa_j_fuentes. source_ids: p4a_hartman_venus_2008, p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019, p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016, p4a_cohorts_release_2026.

5. `archive_stories_ethnography` — "Archive stories and the ethnography of archives: Burton, Dirks, Zeitlyn". Focus: "Burton's Archive Stories (Duke, 2005) collected, in the press's words, ethnographies of the archival world by historians that challenge 'the claims to objectivity associated with the traditional archive', from Uzbekistan's state archive to Soweto 1976; its introduction is titled 'Archive Fever, Archive Stories', and its contributors include Durba Ghosh on miscegenation in Britain and India, Tony Ballantyne on 'Mr. Peal's Archive', Jennifer Milligan on 'What Is an Archive?' in modern France, Adele Perry on 'The Colonial Archive on Trial' in Delgamuukw v. British Columbia, and Ann Curthoys. Dirks's 'Annals of the Archive: Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History' opened Part 1, 'Ethnography and the Archive', of From the Margins (2002), the same volume that printed Stoler's 'Developing Historical Negatives' and Trouillot's 'Culture on the Edges'. Zeitlyn's 'Anthropology in and of the Archives' (Annual Review of Anthropology 41, 2012) and Manoff's survey of 'theories of the archive from across the disciplines' (2004), which found 'a confluence of interests among scholars, archivists, and librarians', mark the turn's consolidation. All bodies unread; the strand rests on contents and abstracts." person_ids: antoinette_burton, nicholas_b_dirks, durba_ghosh, adele_perry, david_zeitlyn, marlene_manoff. source_ids: p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005, p4a_from_the_margins_2002, p4a_zeitlyn_2012, p4a_manoff_2004.

6. `archivists_turn_canada` — "The archivists' turn: Canada and South Africa". Focus: "The profession's own turn ran beside and partly before the historians'. Cook names 'the pioneering postmodern archivists before Cook' as 'also Canadian, Brien Brothman and Richard Brown', with Brothman's review of Archive Fever in Archivaria 43 (1997) and 'Declining Derrida' (1999) (2001, n. 13). His plenary to the Association of Canadian Archivists (Winnipeg, 8 June 2001), printed as 'Fashionable Nonsense or Professional Rebirth', beside plenaries by Verne Harris and Heather MacNeil, argued that appraisal should let archives 'acquire in its holdings multiple voices, and not by default only the voices of the powerful', while, 'as Verne Harris notes', not romanticising the marginalised (pp. 31–32), and that 'the Canadian parallel way of archival remembering through macro-appraisal may speak strongly to archivists in this new century' (p. 32). Schwartz (National Archives of Canada / Queen's) and Cook (Manitoba) then edited the 'archives, records, and power' issues of Archival Science (2002), citing Derrida and Harris, not Foucault or Stoler (reference list). Harris's South African work and Refiguring the Archive (Wits, 2002) are the other pole. Perry's chapter on the colonial archive before the courts in Delgamuukw (2005) is the Canadian historians' link, unread. Indigenous data sovereignty is outside this entry." person_ids: terry_cook, joan_m_schwartz, brien_brothman, verne_harris, adele_perry. source_ids: p4a_cook_archivaria_2001, p4a_cook_archivaria_1997, p4a_schwartz_cook_2002, p4a_mbembe_power_2002, p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005.

