### P.1.4 Proposed edges for `archival_turn` (field-level)

| proposal_id | source → target | kind | relationship (≤300) | map_label | evidence_note | source_ids |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| p4A_rel_01 | post → archival_turn | influence | Colonial studies supplied the turn's founding texts: Stoler's call to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002) came from 'anthropologists engaged in post-colonial studies'; Dirks's 'Annals of the Archive' (2002), Burton's Archive Stories (2005) and Arondekar (2009) worked colonial archives | Colonial archives as the turn's first object, 2002–09 | Stoler 2002 abstract (read): the first sentence names post-colonial studies; From the Margins and Archive Stories contents (Duke pages read); Arondekar's Duke page. No body read; the claim is about where the texts came from, not their arguments. | p4a_stoler_archives_2002, p4a_from_the_margins_2002, p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005, p4a_arondekar_record_2009 |
| p4A_rel_02 | derrida → archival_turn | influence | Archive Fever (1994/1996) is the text the turn answers: Cook dated a 'Derrida aftershock' in which historians and anthropologists subjected 'the archival world to a detailed critique such as it has never before experienced' (2001); Steedman's AHR essay (2001) and Burton's introduction (2005) carry his title | The Derrida aftershock, 1996–2005 | Cook 2001, p. 21 (read); Steedman's and Burton's titles (Crossref; Duke contents); Stoler 2002, n. 21, and Schwartz–Cook 2002, nn. 15, 17, cite Archive Fever (reference lists read). Existing edge_588 (derrida → memory) is not repeated. | p4a_cook_archivaria_2001, p4a_steedman_fever_2001, p4a_burton_archive_stories_2005, p4a_stoler_archives_2002, p4a_schwartz_cook_2002 |
| p4A_rel_03 | foucault → archival_turn | influence | The Archaeology's 'The Statement and the Archive' and 'Nietzsche, Genealogy, History' are the Foucault the turn cites: Stoler's 2002 notes open with the latter and twice cite the former; Cook names 'Foucault's historical methodology' among his own exposures (2001); Manoff's survey begins from Derrida and Foucault (2004) | The archive after the Archaeology, 2001–04 | Stoler 2002 reference list (nn. 1, 30, 42; read); Cook 2001, n. 23 (read); Manoff abstract (DSpace record). Thin: rests on citations, not on a passage stating the debt. | p4a_stoler_archives_2002, p4a_cook_archivaria_2001, p4a_manoff_2004 |
| p4A_rel_04 | archival_turn → memory | contribution | Schwartz and Cook put the archivist's own choices into 'the making of modern memory': archives 'are not passive storehouses of old stuff, but active sites where social power is negotiated', and appraisal, description and access shape collective memory (2002); Trouillot's 'The Presence in the Past' and 'Abortive Rituals' (2000) addressed commemoration and apology | Archives in the making of memory, 2000–02 | Schwartz–Cook abstract (read); Trouillot chapter titles (Beacon and Duke contents). The memory entry's strand `archive_authority` (Combe, Derrida) is the existing hook; `target_strand`: memory/archive_authority. | p4a_schwartz_cook_2002, p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995, p4a_trouillot_remixed_2021 |
| p4A_rel_05 | archival_turn → atlantic | contribution | The archive of slavery became the turn's hardest case: Trouillot's 'unthinkable' Haitian Revolution (1995), Hartman's 'Venus in Two Acts' (2008) and 'critical fabulation', Fuentes's Dispossessed Lives (2016) on Bridgetown's enslaved women; Craipain: 'an unavoidable reference' for 'Black and Caribbean studies' (2024) | Slavery's archive, 1995–2016 | Craipain 2024 (read); MacArthur's description of critical fabulation (read); Fuentes's publisher description; Trouillot ch. 3 title. Hartman and Fuentes unread. | p4a_craipain_hnet_2024, p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019, p4a_hartman_venus_2008, p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016, p4a_trouillot_silencing_1995 |
| p4A_rel_06 | archival_turn → anthropology | contribution | 'An ethnography of the archives' rather than in them: Stoler's standard (2009, p. 45, as quoted by Schulte Nordholt), after her 2002 complaint that anthropologists' 'archival activity tends to remain more an extractive than an ethnographic one'; Dirks's 'Ethnographic Notes on the Sources of History' (2002); Zeitlyn's review (2012) | Ethnography of the archive, 2002–12 | Stoler 2002 abstract (read); Schulte Nordholt 2009, pp. 560–562 (read: 'less often accustomed to making archives into an object of ethnographic research'); Dirks and Zeitlyn titles (contents; Crossref). | p4a_stoler_archives_2002, p4a_schulte_nordholt_bki_2009, p4a_from_the_margins_2002, p4a_zeitlyn_2012 |
| p4A_rel_07 | archival_turn → language | comparison | The turn is the linguistic turn's successor in the archive: Cook presented the profession's engagement as 'postmodernism and the practice of archives' (2001); Manoff found 'a confluence of interests among scholars, archivists, and librarians' started by Derrida and Foucault (2004); but Stoler's programme reads 'along the grain' first, and Gouda notes it against 'reading against the grain' | After the linguistic turn: the archive, 2001–09 | Cook 2001 title and abstract (read); Manoff abstract; Gouda 2009, p. 552 (read). A comparison, not a critique: no text read names the linguistic turn as its target. | p4a_cook_archivaria_2001, p4a_manoff_2004, p4a_gouda_bki_2009 |
| p4A_rel_08 | subaltern → archival_turn | comparison | Reading 'against the grain' for the subaltern (Spivak's 'Rani of Sirmur', 1985) is the practice Stoler's 'along the grain' answers: Gouda, 'the necessity to read along the grain before trying to read against it, as many postcolonial scholars in recent years have advocated' (2009) | Against and along the grain, 1985–2009 | Gouda 2009, p. 552 (read); Spivak 1985 title (graph source w3_spivak_rani_1985, metadata only). Thin: Gouda does not name Subaltern Studies; the Spivak essay is unread. Hold if the coordinator wants a named target. | p4a_gouda_bki_2009, w3_spivak_rani_1985 |
| p4A_rel_09 | archival_turn → black | contribution | Hartman's 'critical fabulation' and Fuentes's black-feminist reading of 'fragmentary sources' (2016) take the archive's violence as the problem of Black history; Trouillot 'remains an unavoidable reference for anyone navigating the streams of Black and Caribbean studies' (Craipain, 2024) | The violence of the archive in Black history, 2008–16 | MacArthur description (read); Fuentes's publisher description (read); Craipain 2024 (read). The black entry's strand `archives` ("Collecting and diaspora bibliography") is the hook; `target_strand`: black/archives. | p4a_hartman_macarthur_2019, p4a_fuentes_dispossessed_2016, p4a_craipain_hnet_2024 |
| p4A_rel_10 | archival_turn → global | contribution | Along the Archival Grain joined the global/transnational neighbourhood of History PhDs in 2020–25 (History PhD citation graph); Trouillot's Global Transformations (2003) set 'North Atlantic universals' and the 'savage slot' against the anthropology of globalization | The archive in global history, 2003–25 | Snowball-members CAUS row (Stoler 2009, lift 5.8, new entrant 2020–25); Springer abstract of the Savage Slot chapter and the Bulamah et al. reading (para. 5). The data-based clause should stay out of the displayed relationship if the user's rule on PhD counts applies to field edges too. | p4a_cohorts_release_2026, p4a_trouillot_global_2003, p4a_trouillot_savage_slot_2003, p4a_bulamah_vibrant_2020 |

**Edges considered, not proposed.** archival_turn → indigenous: Perry's 'The Colonial Archive on Trial' (Delgamuukw) and Curthoys's chapter in Archive Stories are the evidence, contents only; held until a chapter is read. archival_turn → annales: Farge is placed as a precursor row here; no Annales statement read. archival_turn → gender / queer: Arondekar's For the Record (sexuality and the colonial archive) and Cvetkovich's An Archive of Feelings would carry them; both unread; held. michel_rolph_trouillot ↔ ann_laura_stoler: Stoler's 2002 notes cite Silencing the Past three times (nn. 24, 38, 49), but no sentence was read; held as a citation fact in the Stoler scope note.

## P.2 Person entry `ann_laura_stoler` (promotion of the existing people record)

- `id`: ann_laura_stoler; `label`: "Ann Laura Stoler"; `entry_kind`: person; `entry_type`: Individual thinker or historian; `layer`: historians (as for the 1.152 persons; she is an anthropologist by discipline, but the project's `intellectual_connections` layer is used for theorists; the coordinator decides); `period`: null.
- Life and posts (for the identity note, not events unless sourced): born 1949, Brooklyn (Wikidata P569 1949; Wikipedia, orientation); BA Barnard 1972, MA Columbia 1976, PhD Columbia 1982 (Wikipedia citing the New School and ICSI pages; the New School catalog entry confirms "PhD, Anthropology, Columbia University"); University of Wisconsin–Madison 1983–89 (Wikipedia); University of Michigan 1989–2003 (New School page, search rendering); The New School for Social Research from 2004, founding chair of the revitalised Anthropology Department, Willy Brandt Distinguished University Professor of Anthropology and Historical Studies, director of the Institute for Critical Social Inquiry (New School page, search rendering; UC Press author line). Benda Prize 1992 (uncited on Wikipedia; not asserted).
- `date_label`: "Capitalism and Confrontation 1985 · 'Tensions of Empire' (with Cooper) 1989 · Race and the Education of Desire 1995 · Tensions of Empire 1997 · 'Tense and Tender Ties' 2001 · 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' 2002 · Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power 2002 · Haunted by Empire 2006 · Along the Archival Grain 2009 · Imperial Debris 2013 · Duress 2016"

**description** (1,184 characters): "An American anthropologist of Dutch and French colonial rule whose work moved from the plantation belt of Sumatra to the colonial archive itself. With Frederick Cooper she set an agenda for reading metropole and colony together (1989; Tensions of Empire, 1997). Race and the Education of Desire (1995) asked why 'Foucault's elusive and suggestive treatment of race still remains so marginal to what colonial historians take from him' (2002, p. 141, as quoted by Ottevaere); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002) treated 'colonizer/colonized' not 'as a given' but as 'a historically shifting pair of social categories that needs to be explained' (p. 13). 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002) asked scholars to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject'; Along the Archival Grain (2009) read Dutch records 'along the grain' for 'the pulse of the archive' and 'the habits of a colonial heart'. Dutch reviewers found the pulses hard to identify and Indonesian archives absent. Imperial Debris (2013) and Duress (2016) turned to ruination and 'imperial durabilities'. Gouda: in postcolonial studies 'paying homage to Ann Stoler's work has become de rigueur' (2009)."

**scope_note** (draft): "Read on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md §§1–2, 6): the abstract and reference list of the 2002 Archival Science essay; the contents of Along the Archival Grain and three reviews from the Bijdragen debate (Gouda and Schulte Nordholt in full, Raben's first page) with the first paragraph of Stoler's reply; Ottevaere's H-Net review of Carnal Knowledge (in full) and the UC Press contents; Schultz's H-Net review of Imperial Debris (in full); the Duke pages for Haunted by Empire and Duress. Unread: every book's body, the 2002 essay's body, 'Tense and Tender Ties' (2001), the Cooper–Stoler introductions of 1989 and 1997 (metadata and contents only, as the graph already records), Imperial Formations (2007), Interior Frontiers (2022), and Stoler's reply to her Dutch critics beyond its opening. Quotations from the 2009 book are 'as quoted by' Gouda or Schulte Nordholt, from Carnal Knowledge 'as quoted by' Ottevaere, from Imperial Debris 'as quoted by' Schultz, with their page numbers. Wikidata Q16203101 checked by QLever on 2026-10-23 (born 1949; anthropologist, historian; VIAF 263247736). Posts from the New School's pages (search rendering; the page itself returned 429) and the UC Press author line; the Wisconsin years and degrees from Wikipedia's citation of New School pages, orientation only. History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23): cited by 10.7% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25, the strongest riser of 2015–25, in 11 of 18 fields; Along the Archival Grain 4.4% and Carnal Knowledge 3.8% of theses 2015–25 (figures for the scope note, not for arrows)."

**wikidata_check**: `{qid: "Q16203101", verdict: "correct", method: "QLever SPARQL (qlever.dev/api/wikidata, 2026-10-23; the uni-freiburg host now redirects there): the item's label, description, P569, P106 labels, P214; no wbsearchentities; the WikidataMCP endpoint failed to connect.", statements_seen: "Label 'Ann Laura Stoler'; description 'American anthropologist'; P569 1949-04-01 (day may be a precision artefact); P106 university teacher, historian, anthropologist; P214 VIAF 263247736. The 2026-09-22 bulk match is thereby individually confirmed."}`

**people_update**: `{id: ann_laura_stoler, node_id: ann_laura_stoler, new: false, label: "Ann Laura Stoler", identity_note: "American anthropologist and historian of Dutch colonial rule (The New School for Social Research; Willy Brandt Distinguished University Professor of Anthropology and Historical Studies), b. 1949, Brooklyn; Race and the Education of Desire (1995), Along the Archival Grain (2009). Node added in period-4 wave 1. Wikidata Q16203101 confirmed by QLever SPARQL on 2026-10-23 (data/people-wikidata.json already carries it as a bulk match)."}`

