## P.3 Person entry `michel_rolph_trouillot` (new people record and entry)

- `id`: michel_rolph_trouillot; `label`: "Michel-Rolph Trouillot"; `entry_kind`: person; `entry_type`: Individual thinker or historian; `layer`: historians (an anthropologist; same note as for Stoler); `period`: null.
- Life and posts: born 26 November 1949, Port-au-Prince (Wikidata P569/P19); son and nephew of the historians Ernest Trouillot (1922–87) and Hénock Trouillot (1923–88) (Bulamah et al. 2020, n. 3); left Haiti in 1968 "as part of a large wave of students who were fleeing the Duvalier regime" (finding aid); songwriter and activist in New York (Chicago obituary); BA, Brooklyn College, CUNY, 1978; PhD, Johns Hopkins, 1985, "under Sidney W. Mintz and Richard Price"; assistant professor, Duke, 1983–88; Johns Hopkins from 1988, later Krieger/Eisenhower Professor of Anthropology (chair folder 1993–96), founder and director of the Institute for Global Studies in Culture, Power and History; University of Chicago from 1998, Professor of Anthropology and Social Sciences (Duke author line); 2011 Frantz Fanon Lifetime Achievement Award, Caribbean Philosophical Association; died 5 July 2012, Chicago, aged 62, after an aneurysm (obituaries; the 2002 date of the illness is in other obituaries not read).
- `date_label`: "Ti dife boule sou istwa Ayiti 1977 · Peasants and Capital 1988 · Haiti: State against Nation 1990 · 'The Odd and the Ordinary' 1990 · 'Anthropology and the Savage Slot' 1991 · Silencing the Past 1995 · Global Transformations 2003 · Trouillot Remixed 2021"

**description** (1,172 characters): "A Haitian anthropologist and historian who left Port-au-Prince for New York in 1968 and wrote his first history of the Haitian Revolution in Creole (Ti dife boule, 1977). Peasants and Capital (1988) and Haiti: State against Nation (1990) studied Dominica's economy and Duvalierism; 'The Odd and the Ordinary' (1990) refused Haitian exceptionalism. Silencing the Past (1995) asked how power enters 'the production of history': silences enter at four moments, in the making of sources, archives, narratives and 'history in the final instance'; 'in history, power begins at the source' (as quoted by Davidson); the Haitian Revolution was 'unthinkable' as it happened, and the 'historical guild' relegated 'racism, slavery and colonialism' to 'an historical backburner' (p. 98, as quoted by Craipain). 'Anthropology and the Savage Slot' (1991) and Global Transformations (2003) turned the same question on anthropology's 'North Atlantic universals'. Craipain: three decades on, Silencing the Past 'remains an unavoidable reference' (2024); Daut asked readers to go 'beyond Trouillot' (2021). The 20th-anniversary edition (2015) carries Hazel Carby's foreword; Trouillot Remixed (2021) collects the essays."

**scope_note** (draft): "Read on 2026-10-23 (evidence-archive.md §§4, 13): the University of Chicago obituary (2012) and the Chicago Library finding aid's biographical note (in full); the contents and descriptions of Silencing the Past (Beacon), Global Transformations (Springer, with the 'Savage Slot' abstract) and Trouillot Remixed (Duke); the Journal of Haitian Studies 19:2 (2013) contents; Craipain's H-Net review of Stirring the Pot (2024, in full); the Bulamah–Goyatá–Pereira introduction to 'The Odd and the Ordinary' (Vibrant, 2020, in part); Davidson's Overland essay (2020), which quotes chapter 1 without page numbers. Unread: every book's body — the four-moments passage of Silencing the Past is given as later writers list it and as Davidson quotes it, unpaginated; chapter 3, 'An Unthinkable History', by title and Craipain's quotation (p. 98) only; Haiti: State against Nation and Peasants and Capital by obituary citations only; Sepinwall (2013), Daut (2021), Carby (2015), Bonilla's prelude (2021), Dayan (2012–13); David Scott has no essay on Trouillot in Trouillot Remixed (the brief's lead was wrong: 'The Theory of Haiti' is on C. L. R. James). Wikidata Q6836249 checked by QLever on 2026-10-23. History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23): cited by 5.9% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25 (0.8% in 2000–09), Silencing the Past by 4.3% in 2015–25, in 5 of 18 fields; a figure for the scope note, not for arrows."

**wikidata_check**: `{qid: "Q6836249", verdict: "correct", method: "QLever SPARQL (qlever.dev/api/wikidata, 2026-10-23): label, description, P569, P570, P106 labels, P214, P19; no wbsearchentities.", statements_seen: "Label 'Michel-Rolph Trouillot'; description 'Haitian academic and anthropologist'; P569 1949-11-26; P570 2012-07-05; P19 Port-au-Prince; P106 historian, academic, anthropologist; P214 VIAF 2600615. Death date matches the Chicago obituary."}`

**new people record**: `{id: michel_rolph_trouillot, node_id: michel_rolph_trouillot, new: true, label: "Michel-Rolph Trouillot", identity_note: "Haitian anthropologist and historian (1949–2012; Duke 1983–88, Johns Hopkins 1988–98, University of Chicago 1998–2012); Silencing the Past (1995). Added in period-4 wave 1. Wikidata Q6836249 checked by QLever SPARQL on 2026-10-23; to be recorded in data/people-wikidata.json in the usual way. Not to be confused with his sisters Évelyne Trouillot (novelist) and Jocelyne Trouillot-Lévy, or his brother Lyonel Trouillot (writer)."}`

