# Evidence: settler colonial studies after 2000, and Patrick Wolfe

Agent B of three (brief: `BRIEF.md` in this folder). Research phase only: no graph edits, no git, no site builds, no heavy local jobs. Compiled 2026-10-23 against `historiography-1920-2000.json` as found on disk (revision history ends 1.155, dated 2026-10-23; 200 nodes, 1,208 edges, 2,257 sources, 1,309 people). Network use kept light (one request at a time per host; publisher pages, open-access journal pages, QLever SPARQL). No email address was sent to any service. Wikidata via QLever SPARQL only (the WikidataMCP endpoint failed to connect this session; the REST name search was not used).

Status: complete (2026-10-23). Sections are in research order; the Proposals follow the readings.

---

## 0. Project state checked (2026-10-23)

### 0.1 The entry `settler_colonialism`

- **Classification.** `entry_kind` group; `entry_type` Research field; layer `historiographical_developments`; `period: period_4` on disk (revision 1.155, 2026-10-23, "Layering: new fields under a settled canon, c. 2000–"; the working copy of the graph changed between my first and second reads this session, so the `period_4` proposal the brief asks for is already applied; the Proposals section records this and proposes no change). `date_label`: "Earlier settler-society studies 1983–95 · Wolfe 1999 · selected later readings: Wolfe 2006, Veracini 2010, journal 2011, critiques 2013–20; field chronology unresolved". `date_span` 1983 (`earlier_roots`) → null (`open`).
- **Coverage.** `reviewed_through: 2000`; basis: "Reviewed through 2000 in the original 1920–2000 experiment. Later work is not yet surveyed for this entry; selected later interventions, where present, are dated individually."
- **Description** (1,200-character register, existing): Wolfe's "structure rather than an event"; Veracini and the journal (2011) "distinguished settler colonial from colonial phenomena"; comparative settler-capitalism and settler-society studies earlier; the Canadian historiography of dispossession, reserves, gender and the state; Indigenous political theory of recognition, sovereignty and resurgence; critiques from Indigenous scholars (recentring settlers), historians (structural inevitability, regional fit: commercial penetration, extractivism), Black studies (slavery). Ends: "Settler colonialism overlaps with Indigenous history, postcolonial critique and historical geography without being equivalent to any of them."
- **Scope note** (existing): "A selective research field whose theoretical consolidation came mostly after 2000; the entry covers its roots through 2000 and cites selected later works as readings … Roster inclusion is not adoption of the paradigm: Carter, Perry, Harris and Daschuk wrote earlier or beside it. No single founder, origin or Canadian consensus is asserted. Critics appear as critics. Francophone uptake in the PhD corpus is near zero."
