### 0.5 The earlier research (2026-10-12) and what it settled

`data/extension-2026/settler-colonialism-request-2026-10-12/README.md`: user decisions — settler colonialism is "a field now" (Research field, not approach); Indigenous theory works "can exist in more than one field" (dual roster placement allowed); Canadian relationships to be settled with the Canadian groups. The request found: core-work uptake among Canadian strict History PhDs 0.7% (2009–11) → 12.8% (2021–23); Wolfe 2006 cited by 31 PhDs (first 2013), Veracini 2010 by 25, Coulthard 23, A. Simpson 23, Wolfe 1999 17, Tuck–Yang 13, Byrd 10, Wolfe 2001 9, Kauanui 7; Daschuk 2013 the top co-citation with Wolfe/Veracini (19 PhDs, lift 10.2); every clustered core work sits in the "Indigenous peoples, fur trade & the West" co-citation field; francophone uptake near zero; Greer's CHA essay the most cited critique (8 PhDs). Open questions it left: whether the Indigenous theory strand belongs here or in `indigenous`; whether decolonization is part of it; the Black-studies critique edge; an imperial-history home. This file extends, not redoes, that work: its 51 source records are reused by id; new readings get `p4b_` ids.

### 0.6 Dissertation figures to attach (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23)

From `summary/cohorts/README.md` §§2, 5, 6 and the CSVs:
- **Field engagement** (two or more seed authors cited; `field-engagement.csv`): settler colonial studies, Canada + US, 0% (1990–99) → 3.2% (2000–09) → 4.2% (2010–14) → 9.0% (2015–19) → 11.0% (2020–25); Canada 15.2% and US 6.8% in 2020–25 — "the 2010s arrival still rising", in Canada overtaking environmental history (10.2%).
- **Rising works 2015–25 against 2000–09** (§2): Wolfe 2006 3.1% vs 0; Veracini 2010 2.4% vs 0; Daschuk 2013; *Mohawk Interruptus*; Coulthard; J. R. Miller, *Compact, Contract, Covenant* 2009; Carlson 2010; Raibmon 2005. Inside the Indigenous TF-IDF family Wolfe 0 → 28% of theses, Daschuk 0 → 24%, Tuhiwai Smith 10 → 29%. Wolfe and Veracini pass |z| ≥ 2 in the university-stratified check.
- **Two-sided citing** (§5, `debate-dyads.csv`): Wolfe and Veracini co-cited by 0 (2000–09) → 0.2 → 2.1 → 3.0% (2020–25); Wolfe and his Indigenous critics (Tuck and Yang, A. Simpson, Coulthard) 0 → 0 → 0.9 → 2.3%; Wolfe with Thompson or Joan Scott 1.1%. Wolfe cited by 0.5% (2000–09) → 3.5% → 6.6% (2020–25) of Canada + US theses, 31% of the Indigenous family; breadth 3 families at ≥ 3% (Veracini 2). The README's verdict: "the only post-2000 figure around whom a *two-sided* citing is forming … regional (Canada 4.2% of theses, US 3.6%, Europe 0.1% for the 2006 essay), not yet general."
- **Snowball** (§6.2; founders Wolfe 1999, 2006, Veracini 2010, Tuck–Yang 2012): Canada seed citers 3.1% (2015–19) → 8.5% (2020–25), equalised neighbourhood 51 works in both cohorts, recent works 13 → 17, median year 1999 → 2006; US 3.9% → 5.0%, neighbourhood 34 → 41, median 2005 → 2008 — "the largest neighbourhood of any field … with the most recent membership". Canadian entrants 2015–19: Perry, Van Kirk, Tuhiwai Smith, Miller, Lutz, Harris; 2020–25: Coulthard (support 11), Simpson, Carter (*Lost Harvests* 10, *Imperial Plots*, *Capturing Women*), Miller *Compact* (9), McKay, Daschuk (12), Kauanui (5), Greer 2019 (6), Harris 2004, Jacobs 2009, Hogue, Macdougall, Innes, Hill, Nickel, Stevenson, Hoy. US entrants 2015–19: *The Middle Ground*, Blackhawk, Witgen, Hämäläinen, O'Brien, Hixson; 2020–25: Jacobs, W. Johnson, Ostler, Miles, Chang, Saunt. Wolfe 1999 and 2006 and Tuck–Yang are the only 2020–25 entrants to the Canadian *environmental* history neighbourhood.
- **Atlas instruction in the README (§6.3)**: "Settler colonial studies is the 2010s field, still rising, Canada-led, with the largest and youngest network; the Indigenous critique (Coulthard, Simpson, Tuck and Yang) belongs inside the entry, as §5 found."
- User rule applied below: Canadian counts go in `scope_note`, strand focus or coverage text, not in edge text for international figures (Wolfe, Veracini, Kauanui, Tuck, Yang, Byrd, Moreton-Robinson, Rowse, Macoun, Strakosch, Englert).

### 0.7 What the entry therefore lacks

No event after 2011; no reading of Wolfe's own pages beyond pp. 1–2 of 1999; the Wolfe–Veracini relation unstated; the Indigenous critique read only through abstracts; no Wolfe death event, no journal history, no TRC, no Canadian uptake after 2013 beyond Burrill; no Englert, Moreton-Robinson, Hugill, Miller 2009, McCallum, Mawani, Stanger-Ross, Carter 2016, Perry 2015; person entry at 2 edges and 3 events; no QIDs.

