## 1. Patrick Wolfe, "Settler colonialism and the elimination of the native", *Journal of Genocide Research* 8:4 (December 2006): 387–409

**Citation.** DOI 10.1080/14623520601056240 (Crossref: issued 2006-12, pages 387–409, 4,510 Crossref citations; OpenAlex counts 6,460 citing works and marks it "bronze" open access on the publisher's site). **Read status: read in full** in the Wayback capture of the publisher's full-text page (notes 1–80 included; no page breaks in the capture). Page numbers below are "as given by Kauanui 2016" (notes 3–6: pp. 387, 388, 390, 403), "as given by Tuck and Yang 2012" (p. 387) or "as given by Snelgrove et al. 2014" (p. 388); otherwise the article's own section breaks (five asterisked sections after the opening) are used.

**The argument, in his words.**
- Opening: "The question of genocide is never far from discussions of settler colonialism. Land is life—or, at least, land is necessary for life." "In this article, I shall begin to explore, in comparative fashion, the relationship between genocide and the settler-colonial tendency that I term the logic of elimination. I contend that, though the two have converged … they should be distinguished. Settler colonialism is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal." (opening; p. 387 per Kauanui.) Note 1 cites his own "Nation and miscegeNation" (*Social Analysis* 36, 1994, pp. 93–152) and the 1999 book for the term.
- Race made in the targeting: "Indians and Black people in the US have been racialized in opposing ways that reflect their antithetical roles in the formation of US society. Black people's enslavement produced an inclusive taxonomy … the 'one-drop rule' … For Indians, in stark contrast, non-Indian ancestry compromised their indigeneity, producing 'half-breeds,' a regime that persists in the form of blood quantum regulations." (p. 387, as quoted by Tuck and Yang.) "a race cannot be taken as given. It is made in the targeting." "the primary motive for elimination is not race (or religion, ethnicity, grade of civilization, etc.) but access to territory. Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element."
- The structure sentence: "The logic of elimination not only refers to the summary liquidation of Indigenous people, though it includes that. In common with genocide as Raphaël Lemkin characterized it, settler colonialism has both negative and positive dimensions. Negatively, it strives for the dissolution of native societies. Positively, it erects a new colonial society on the expropriated land base—as I put it, settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event." (p. 388 per Kauanui and Snelgrove et al.; note 7 refers the formula back to the 1999 book, p. 2, and to "Nation and miscegeNation", p. 96.) "In its positive aspect, elimination is an organizing principal of settler-colonial society rather than a one-off (and superseded) occurrence. The positive outcomes of the logic of elimination can include officially encouraged miscegenation, the breaking-down of native title into alienable individual freeholds, native citizenship, child abduction, religious conversion, resocialization in total institutions such as missions or boarding schools, and a whole range of cognate biocultural assimilations."
- "Settler colonialism destroys to replace." (Herzl's *Altneuland*; Benvenisti; Tony Birch on Gariwerd.) "It is both as complex social formation and as continuity through time that I term settler colonization a structure rather than an event, and it is on this basis that I shall consider its relationship to genocide." (end of the first section; p. 390 per Kauanui for "elimination refers to more than the summary liquidation of Indigenous people, though it includes that.")
- Second section (discovery, preemption, the Cherokee removal): "the agency which reduced Indian peoples to this abjection was not some state instrumentality but irregular, greed-crazed invaders"; "Rather than something separate from or running counter to the colonial state, the murderous activities of the frontier rabble constitute its principal means of expansion"; "settler colonialism is an inclusive, land-centred project that coordinates a comprehensive range of agencies, from the metropolitan centre to the frontier encampment, with a view to eliminating Indigenous societies."
- Third section (modernity): "Settler colonialism was foundational to modernity." He quotes **Cole Harris** 2004 (note 36, p. 179): "Combine capital's interest in uncluttered access to land and settlers' interest in land as livelihood, and the principal momentum of settler colonialism comes into focus" — a direct citation of the Canadian geographer whom the project's `settler_colonialism` entry carries as a historian writing "beside" the paradigm (see Corrections).
- Fourth section (agriculture, the Five Civilized Tribes, assimilation): "Agriculture not only supports the other sectors. It is inherently sedentary and, therefore, permanent." "Indians were the original communist menace." "Here, in essence, is assimilation's Faustian bargain—have our settler world, but lose your Indigenous soul. Beyond any doubt, this is a kind of death." Pratt's "Kill the Indian in him and save the man" (1892).
- Fifth section (genocide as the killing of a *genos*): "Richard Pratt and Phillip Sheridan were both practitioners of genocide. The question of degree is not the definitional issue." Against "cultural genocide": "it confuses definition with degree."
- Sixth section (allotment, blood quantum, termination; Israel's Law of Return): "A major difference between this and the generality of non-colonial genocides is its sustained duration." "assimilation is one of a range of strategies of elimination that become favoured in particular historical circumstances."
- Seventh section ("Why, then, logic of elimination rather than genocide?"): "settler colonialism is relatively impervious to regime change. The genocide of American Indians or of Aboriginal people in Australia has not been subject to election results." Against hyphenated genocides ("never quite the real thing"); "the Holocaust, the non-paradigmatic paradigm". "When invasion is recognized as a structure rather than an event, its history does not stop—or, more to the point, become relatively trivial—when it moves on from the era of frontier homicide." "I suggest that the term 'structural genocide' avoids the questions of degree—and, therefore, of hierarchy among victims—that are entailed in qualified genocides, while retaining settler colonialism's structural induration" (p. 403 per Kauanui).
- Last section (prediction and prevention): "settler colonialism is an indicator"; "Since settler colonialism persists over extended periods of time, structural genocide should be easier to interrupt than short-term genocides"; Israel's "progressive dispensing with its reliance on Palestinian labour would seem to present an ominous case in point"; Colin Tatz's apartheid argument; "Gaza and the West Bank become less and less like Bantustans and more and more like reservations".
- Self-positioning: "Unpalatable though it is (to speak as a member of a settler society)". Note 20 announces a planned critique of US Indian sovereignty judgments. Note 62 quotes his own 2001 AHR essay (p. 894): "Difference … is not simply given. It is the outcome of differentiation, which is an intensely conflictual process."

**Readers he cites who matter to the project**: Harris 2004; Hoxie 1989 (*A Final Promise*); Limerick 1987; Deloria and Wilkins; Moses ed., *Genocide and Settler Society* (2005, notes 3, 34, 72); Lemkin 1944; Rodinson 1973 ("the collective mother country", note 35); Tony Birch 2003.

**Use.** Supports: edges `patrick_wolfe → settler_colonialism` (the 2006 programme, replacing the Kauanui-abstract attestation), `patrick_wolfe → racial_formation` (race "made in the targeting"), `patrick_wolfe → holocaust` or a genocide-studies comparison (no node exists; see Proposals), `patrick_wolfe → indigenous` (the programme as it bears on Indigenous history); the strand correction to `structure_and_elimination`; the 2006 chronology event's support text; the Harris correction.

## 2. J. Kēhaulani Kauanui, "'A Structure, Not an Event': Settler Colonialism and Enduring Indigeneity", *Lateral* 5:1 (Spring 2016)

**Citation.** DOI 10.25158/L5.1.7; open access (CC BY 4.0) at https://csalateral.org/issue/5-1/forum-alt-humanities-settler-colonialism-enduring-indigeneity-kauanui/. **Read status: read in full** (web text, unpaginated; located by paragraph; notes 1–17). Based on a 2014 ASA roundtable (note 1); "The last edits of this essay were completed in the immediate wake of Patrick Wolfe's death. He was not only a colleague; he was a friend" (acknowledgements). Responses by Beenash Jafri and Melissa Gniadek with a reply by Kauanui in *Lateral* 6:1 (2017) (editors' note).

**Positions.**
- Two senses of "enduring indigeneity" (para. 1): "that the operative logic of settler colonialism may be to 'eliminate the native,' as the late English scholar Patrick Wolfe brilliantly theorized, but that indigenous peoples exist, resist, and persist; and second, that settler colonialism is a structure that endures indigeneity, as it holds out against it." (She calls him "English"; Wikidata and the VU record say Australian; see Proposals.)
- On citation: Wolfe's 2006 essay "is often cited as the principal work representing the concept and theory of the settler colonial analytic. And although Wolfe insisted on making it clear time and again that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did—within the field of American Studies (as just one example), he tends to be most frequently cited as if he had" (para. 2). Note 7: "Wolfe did not coin the concept of settler colonialism"; she gives a genealogy: Fayez Sayegh, *Zionist Colonialism in Palestine* (1965); Rodinson 1973; Rosemary Sayigh 1979; J. Sakai 1983; Haunani-Kay Trask on "settlers of color" (1992/2000); Fujikane and Okamura 2008; Veracini, *Israel and Settler Society* (2006); Yuval-Davis 1995; Shohat 2000.
- The complaint: "the oft cited phrase from Wolfe's work—that settler colonialism is a structure not an event" "often seems to stand-in for a serious engagement of his theory and is also perhaps the most neglected aspect of his theory" (para. 4). "Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies" (para. 4). Alyosha Goldstein (ASA 2015) on the phrase's reduction and on how it can "foreclose or bracket other formations—such as franchise colonialism and slavery" (para. 5). Robert Warrior's 2015 position paper counting ASA panels: "the rise of Settler Colonial Studies has become … in some circles—an answer to the chronic need for more attention to and awareness of Native and Indigenous studies" (para. 6). NAISA's founding (2008) and her own co-founding role (note 10).
- "indigeneity is a category of analysis that is distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality—even as it entails elements of all three"; "taking up indigeneity as a category of analysis is not one and the same as the study of indigenous peoples".
- Closing: "Since settler colonialism is a structure and not an event, and because indigenous peoples are still subject to that structure—an ongoing genocidal project—NAIS must be engaged in relation to Settler Colonial Studies".
- Note 8 answers the binary charge: Wolfe's "discussion of this binary is specific to his discussion of the United States"; "This is not an Anglo-specific theory"; and "Wolfe responded to the charge of perpetuating a binary between settler and native with his recently edited volume, *The Settler Complex: Recuperating Binarism in Colonial Studies* (Los Angeles: UCLA American Indian Studies Center, 2016)."

exec
/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '116,215p' data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md" in /home/jic823/historiography
 succeeded in 0ms:
- Note 6 reproduces the 1999 formula with its page: "The colonizers come to stay—invasion is a structure not an event." (1999 book, p. 2; she cites a Continuum 1998 printing).

**Use.** A critique that is also an endorsement: she defends Wolfe's theory against its shallow citation and asks the field to attend to indigeneity and to NAIS. Supports `j_kehaulani_kauanui → patrick_wolfe` (critique, with the fairness point that she praises the theory), `settler_colonialism ↔ indigenous` (strengthening edge_881 with read text), the strand correction to `indigenous_scholars_critique`, chronology events 2008 (NAISA), 2015 (ASA panel), 2016 (essay), 2016 (*The Settler Complex*), 2017 (responses).

## 3. Eve Tuck and K. Wayne Yang, "Decolonization is not a metaphor", *Decolonization: Indigeneity, Education & Society* 1:1 (2012): 1–40

**Citation.** Journal page https://jps.library.utoronto.ca/index.php/des/article/view/18630 (published 2012-09-08; CC BY-NC 3.0; the PDF carries "pp. 1-40"; affiliations SUNY New Paltz and UC San Diego). **Read status: pages read 1–15, 19–20, 28–29, 35–36** (introduction, "The set of settler colonial relations", "Playing Indian", "Settler moves to innocence" I–II, part of III and IV, "Incommensurability is unsettling", "More on incommensurability", conclusion); moves V and VI and the "Third world decolonizations" and "Abolition" lists skimmed from the index only.

**Positions, in their words.**
- "Decolonization brings about the repatriation of Indigenous land and life; it is not a metaphor for other things we want to do to improve our societies and schools" (abstract, p. 1). "When metaphor invades decolonization, it kills the very possibility of decolonization; it recenters whiteness, it resettles theory, it extends innocence to the settler, it entertains a settler future" (p. 3). "Decolonization doesn't have a synonym" (p. 3).
- The triad: "settler colonialism is built upon an entangled triad structure of settler-native-slave" (abstract); "neither external nor internal colonialism adequately describe the form of colonialism which operates in the United States or other nation-states in which the colonizer comes to stay" (p. 5); "Settler colonialism is different from other forms of colonialism in that settlers come with the intention of making a new home on the land, a homemaking that insists on settler sovereignty over all things in their new domain" (p. 5).
- **The Wolfe citation**: "This violence is not temporally contained in the arrival of the settler but is reasserted each day of occupation. This is why Patrick Wolfe (1999) emphasizes that settler colonialism is a structure and not an event" (p. 5); the 2006 essay is quoted at length on the opposed racializations of Black and Indigenous people (p. 12, citing Wolfe 2006, p. 387; the reference list misdates it 2007).
- "Settlers are not immigrants" (p. 6); "settler nations are not immigrant nations" (p. 7). Footnote 7: "although the setter-native-slave triad structures settler colonialism, this does not mean that settler, native, and slave are analogs".
- "settler moves to innocence" (after Mawhinney 1998): "those strategies or positionings that attempt to relieve the settler of feelings of guilt or responsibility without giving up land or power or privilege" (p. 10): settler nativism, settler adoption fantasies, colonial equivocation, conscientization, at risk-ing/asterisk-ing, re-occupation and urban homesteading.
- "An ethic of incommensurability, which guides moves that unsettle innocence, stands in contrast to aims of reconciliation, which motivate settler moves to innocence. Reconciliation is about rescuing settler normalcy, about rescuing a settler future" (p. 35). "Decolonization is not accountable to settlers, or settler futurity. Decolonization is accountable to Indigenous sovereignty and futurity" (p. 35). "Decolonization is not an 'and'. It is an elsewhere" (p. 36).
- On the authors' positioning (note 1): "As an Indigenous scholar and a settler/trespasser/scholar writing together, we have used forward slashes to reflect our discrepant positionings".

**Use.** The essay adopts Wolfe's structure formula and the 2006 racialization argument; its target is the metaphorical use of "decolonization" in education and social justice, not Wolfe. The dissertation data's "Wolfe and his Indigenous critics" pairing is therefore, for Tuck and Yang, a pairing of a theorist with an essay that *builds on* him while redirecting the field toward land repatriation and Indigenous futurity. Supports: `eve_tuck`/`k_wayne_yang` roster corrections (argument now read in the authors' own words); strand `recognition_refusal_resurgence` correction; an `patrick_wolfe → settler_colonialism` evidence line; a comparison or contribution `settler_colonialism → anticolonial` (their "Third world decolonizations" section) is possible but not proposed. The Black-studies critique (Garba and Sorentino 2020, Sexton 2016) answers *this* essay's triad, as the existing strand `slavery_black_studies` says.

## 4. Lorenzo Veracini, "Introducing settler colonial studies", *Settler Colonial Studies* 1:1 (2011): 1–12

**Citation.** DOI 10.1080/2201473X.2011.10648799. **Read status: read in full** in the author's Swinburne deposit (figshare, DOI 10.25916/sut.26246930.v1, CC BY-NC-ND 3.0; the journal's own first-volume PDF, "© settler colonial studies 1 (2011), ISSN 1838-0743", pp. 1–12, with notes). The founding "was supported under Australian Research Council's Discovery Projects funding scheme (project number DP0986984)" (acknowledgements, p. 9); biographical note: "QE II Fellow at the Institute for Social Research, Swinburne University of Technology, Melbourne … He has authored *Settler Colonialism: A Theoretical Overview* (2010) and *Israel and Settler Society* (2006)" (p. 9).

**Positions.**
- "settler colonial studies aims to contribute to the consolidation of a new scholarly field. This process requires that colonial and settler colonial phenomena be analytically disentangled … colonialism and settler colonialism should be understood in their dialectical relation" (abstract, p. 1).
- The two demands: "if I come and say: 'you, work for me', it's not the same as saying 'you, go away'. This is why colonialism is not settler colonialism" (p. 1). "Colonisers and settler colonisers want essentially different things" (p. 1).
- Settler colonialism's self-supersession: "whereas colonialism reinforces the distinction between colony and metropole, settler colonialism erases it. If, as Patrick Wolfe remarked in a frequently quoted passage, that settler 'invasion is a structure' and not 'an event', it is also true that the structure persistently pursues a specific end point. Colonialism reproduces itself, and the freedom and equality of the colonised is forever postponed; settler colonialism, by contrast, extinguishes itself" (p. 3; note 11 cites the 1999 book, p. 163). "settler colonialism is most recognisable when it is most imperfect – say, 1950s Kenya or 1970s Zimbabwe – and least visible in the settler cities" (p. 3).
- Resistance and survival: "If the demand, by contrast, is to go away, it is indigenous persistence and survival that become crucial" (p. 3). "Fragility fundamentally defines the 'indigenous'" in settler discourse (p. 4).
- Why a field: "colonial, imperial and postcolonial studies have primarily focused on something else … these scholarly literatures have ultimately failed to detect the settler colonial 'situation' in its specific operation" (p. 5).
- "Decolonisation is not settler decolonisation" (section title, p. 5): "arguing against colonialism is not the same thing as condemning settler colonialism" (p. 5); "As Patrick Wolfe has concluded, settler colonialism has remained 'impervious to regime change'" (p. 5, note 22 — the phrase is in Wolfe 2006, §7); "if colonialism ends with the coloniser's departure … settler colonialism ends with an indigenous ultimate permanence" (p. 7); on the "politics of recognition": "an ostensible partnership does not alter the fundamental and original demand of settler colonialism, and indigenous alterities are still being subsumed/neutralised/extinguished" (p. 8); "Reconciliation should be a practice and not a process" (p. 9). Mamdani quoted: "in privileging the indigenous over the non-indigenous, we turned the colonial world upside down, but we did not change it" (p. 7–8).
- Note 3 gives his canon: Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis 1995; Wolfe 1999; Russell ed. 2001; Pearson 2001; Elkins and Pedersen 2005; Coombes ed. 2006; Pateman 2007; Goldstein and Lubin eds., *SAQ* 107:4 (2008); Belich 2009; Veracini 2010.
- The journal: "settler colonial studies will publish original research emanating from a variety of disciplinary and area studies backgrounds"; "settler colonialism is as much a thing of the past as a thing of the present" (p. 9).

**Use.** Veracini's own account of the Wolfe relation: he quotes the structure formula and adds that "the structure persistently pursues a specific end point" (self-supersession), which is the first statement of the difference the dissertation data registers as "Wolfe and Veracini". Supports `lorenzo_veracini → settler_colonialism` (contribution; journal founding), `patrick_wolfe → lorenzo_veracini` (influence, his own words), `settler_colonialism → post` (the claim that postcolonial studies "failed to detect the settler colonial 'situation'": a critique rather than a comparison, candidate revision of edge_882), the strand `theory_and_field` correction, the 2011 event's support text.

## 5. Lorenzo Veracini, "Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016)", *Settler Colonial Studies* 6:3 (2016): 189–190

**Citation.** DOI 10.1080/2201473x.2016.1176393 (Crossref: published online 2016-05-10). **Read status: read in full** (Wayback capture of the publisher page, 2024-03-26; the obituary's four paragraphs and closing).

**What it says.** "Patrick Wolfe was an unusual scholar. Always somewhat at the margins of academia, and yet holding at different times fellowships at Harvard and Stanford, he was able to contribute seminally to a variety of fields: anthropology, genocide studies, the historiography of race, indigenous studies, and the study of colonialism and imperialism." "Dialectics and binaries had ceased to be recognisable methodological approaches in scholarly pursuits decades ago, but they worked for him." "his work was eminently and inherently comparative and transnational. He was able to contribute to educating a generation of younger scholars working on a remarkable variety of national settings: Australia, Hawaii, North America, Brazil, and Palestine." "Patrick once confessed to me that he was a 'still a Marxist' … The referent here was the young Marx". On the 1999 book: "ostensibly a history of Australian anthropology. He once told me that 'settler colonialism' was only added at the very end and at the request of the publisher … And yet, to explain the evolution of anthropology Patrick defined settler colonialism as distinct from colonialism. No one had theorised it before and in a systematic way. Now a classic, Settler Colonialism practically established what is now a burgeoning scholarly subfield." On *Traces of History* (2016): "utilises his analysis of racism under settler colonialism in order to explain racism everywhere else (and vice versa)." "He was planning to work on territorialisation". "this anti-imperialist scholar kick started a scholarly field in a fit of absentmindedness. Or did he?"

**Use.** A named reader's statement of Wolfe's field-founding role and of the 1999 book's history, with the fairness point that Kauanui (note 7) and Wolfe himself (per Kauanui para. 2) deny that he created the field: both are recorded. Supports the death event (dated by Wikidata and the VU record, below), the "fellowships at Harvard and Stanford" (undated; not drawn as events), `patrick_wolfe → anthropology` (Veracini's reading that settler colonialism was defined "to explain the evolution of anthropology"), `patrick_wolfe → lorenzo_veracini` (influence: Englert 2020 calls Veracini Wolfe's "self-styled intellectual successor", §8).

## 6. Alissa Macoun and Elizabeth Strakosch, "The ethical demands of settler colonial theory", *Settler Colonial Studies* 3:3–4 (2013): 426–443

**Citation.** DOI 10.1080/2201473X.2013.810695. **Read status: read in the author-accepted manuscript** deposited at QUT ePrints (https://eprints.qut.edu.au/220228/, 20 pp., unpaginated against the published version; abstract, introduction, "The strengths of SCT", the critique section, the governmentality analogy and the conclusion read; the Northern Territory intervention case material skimmed). Affiliations: Indigenous Studies Research Network, QUT; Institute for Culture and Society, UWS.

**Positions.** "building on the theoretical contributions of Patrick Wolfe and Lorenzo Veracini, increasing numbers of scholars are beginning to think about settler colonialism as a specific political formation" (introduction). "settler colonial theory (SCT) remains a largely White attempt to think through contemporary colonial relationships"; "Indigenous scholars have not always embraced the theory" (introduction). The strength: "Exposing colonization as 'a structure not an event' confronts settlers with an account of contemporary colonialism that is difficult to avoid"; "as Patrick Wolfe famously put it, settlers 'come to stay' and this makes 'colonisation a structure not an event'". The critique: "By emphasizing continuities in colonial relationships between the past and the present, SCT can depict colonization as structurally inevitable, and can be deployed in ways that re-inscribe settler colonialism"; "Foundational scholar Patrick Wolfe has been labelled 'very much a structuralist stuck in a poststructuralist world'" (their note 63; the labeller is not identified in the manuscript text read); "This can lead to a theoretical and political impasse and result in a kind of colonial fatalism"; "Is it possible that settlers are particularly attracted to SCT precisely because it gives us a sense of being intellectually committed to the end of colonialism while simultaneously unable to act against our own privilege?" They cite Rowse ("that sorrowing form of attention in which defeat and marginality are highlighted at the expense of understanding the nature and limits of the Indigenous agency") and Joanne Barker ("a 'troubled focus within settler colonial studies on structure to the erasure of Indigenous experiences'"). Conclusion: "SCT is most usefully understood as providing non-Indigenous people in settler states with a better account of ourselves – rather than as an account of the entire settler–Indigenous relationship … Most clearly of all, it does not account for Indigenous lives"; "settler colonialism must be studied as an historical and empirical phenomenon as well as a conceptual framework"; "the fact that settler colonialism struggles to narrate its own ending does not mean that it cannot end." They quote Veracini approvingly: "'the decolonization of settler colonialism needs to be imagined before it is practised'".

**Use.** A critique from inside the theory, as the existing roster row says; now read. Supports `settler_colonialism → patrick_wolfe` (critique, Macoun and Strakosch named, with Rowse and Barker as they cite them), the strand `critiques_and_limits` correction, a 2013 event.

## 7. Corey Snelgrove, Rita Kaur Dhamoon and Jeff Corntassel, "Unsettling settler colonialism", *Decolonization* 3:2 (2014): 1–32

**Citation.** https://jps.library.utoronto.ca/index.php/des/article/view/21166 (CC BY-NC 3.0; the PDF carries "pp. 1-32"). **Read status: pages read 1–3, 7–14, 30–31** (abstract, introduction, "Disrupting the institutionalization of settler colonial studies", the first discussion, the OED section on "settler", conclusion); the sections on solidarity and resurgence (pp. 15–29) indexed only.

**Positions.** "Settler colonial studies as a distinct emerging field of study (rather than a site of struggle already critiqued by Indigenous peoples) has been centrally defined by Lorenzo Veracini's 2010 book … Patrick Wolfe's 1999 book … and his 2006 article … and more recently with articles in the *Settler Colonial Studies* journal" (p. 7). Four contributions and four weaknesses are listed (pp. 7–9), the latter: Joanne Barker's question about the specificity of settler colonialism; Macoun and Strakosch's "primarily a settler framework"; "an underlying 'colonial fatalism' (Macoun and Strakosch, 2013, p. 435) that posits a structural inevitability"; "over-characterizations of binary positions" and "treating settler colonialism as a meta-structure". The institutional history (p. 9): "the journal *Settler Colonial Studies* – first published in 2011 in an open access format (entirely run on volunteer labour) … moved to a large academic publishing house, Taylor & Francis, within two years of being established." "settler colonial studies can displace, overshadow, or even mask over Indigenous studies (for example, see Veracini, 2013)" (p. 9). Corntassel in the discussion: "A recent issue of *Settler Colonial Studies* (3:3) came out with a corresponding issue in *American Indian Culture and Research Journal* (37:2), though both issues were edited by Patrick Wolfe … is this a 'talking at?'" (p. 10); Snelgrove: "we can look at Veracini's history of the concept [of settler colonialism] where Indigenous studies and Indigenous resistance is pretty much erased. Veracini briefly names both on the second last page, but then immediately goes on to credit white historians" (p. 11); Corntassel on political science in the early 1990s: "when settlers take up these questions, it's suddenly considered a legitimate field of study" (p. 11). Conclusion: de-centring Indigenous articulations "has the potential to present settler colonialism as complete or transhistorical, as inevitable, rather than conditioned and contingent" (p. 31); Raibmon's "microtechniques of dispossession" (p. 31). Wolfe 2006 is quoted for "settler-colonizers come to stay" (p. 388) at p. 13.

**Use.** Replaces the search-summary basis of `screq_snelgrove_dhamoon_corntassel_2014` (verification correction); supports the strand `indigenous_scholars_critique`; a critique edge `settler_colonialism → lorenzo_veracini` (Snelgrove's reading of Veracini 2013) if Veracini has an entry; the 2013 institutional events (T&F move; the paired SCS/AICRJ issues).

## 8. Sai Englert, "Settlers, workers, and the logic of accumulation by dispossession", *Antipode* 52:6 (2020): 1647–1666

**Citation.** DOI 10.1111/anti.12659 (Wiley; open access; first published 20 July 2020; Leiden University). **Read status: abstract and opening read** (Wayback capture of the publisher's full text, 2023-08-21); body to be read in the next pass.

**Positions.** "This article offers a critique of the Wolfe-an model, which has become so dominant within contemporary Settler Colonial Studies (SCS). It focuses particularly on the central claim made by Patrick Wolfe, and others after him, that settler colonialism is categorically differentiated from other forms of colonialism by its drive to 'eliminate the native', instead of exploiting them" (abstract). "It has become customary to start discussions of settler colonialism … by engaging with Patrick Wolfe, whose work has defined much of the emergence of Settler Colonial Studies (SCS) as a separate academic field. Wolfe and others after him, primarily Lorenzo Veracini, his self-styled intellectual successor, analysed settler colonialism as structural, eliminatory, and land based". "Numerous settler societies were built on the exploitation of indigenous peoples … particularly in the South American and African continents. By assuming that exploitation, by definition, lays outside the realm of its field of study, SCS has privileged the analysis of the Anglo-settler world" (opening, citing Kelley 2017 and Speed 2017 from the AQ forum). His alternative: "focusing on accumulation by dispossession allows for an analysis of the specificity of settler social relations to emerge."

**Use.** A Marxist critique of the elimination/exploitation distinction: supports a critique edge `settler_colonialism → patrick_wolfe` (Englert named, with Kelley and Speed from the 2017 forum) and a `comparison` with `dependency` (accumulation by dispossession; Harvey) if the coordinator wants the brief's "comparison with dependency?" answered: the better answer is a strand line, not an edge (Englert's frame is Harvey's, not Frank's or Wallerstein's).

### 8a. Englert 2020, body (read after §8 was written)

**Read status now: read in full** (Wayback capture of the Wiley open-access page; sections "The Wolfe-an Model", "An Alternative Reading", "Accumulation by Dispossession, Settler Colonialism, and the Capitalist Transition", "Settler Accumulation and Settler Quietism", case studies, conclusion, endnotes; unpaginated in the capture; the printed range is 1647–1666).
- "SCS (re)emerged with the publication of Wolfe's (1999) *Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology*. (Re)emerged because … the study of settler colonialism (rather than the academic field of SCS) has a much longer genealogy" — the PLO's publications (Jabbour 1970; Sayegh), Said 1979, Rosemary Sayigh 1979 (via Barakat 2018).
- He quotes Wolfe 1999, p. 3: "were (are) premised on the elimination of the native societies. The split tensing reflects a determinate feature of settler colonisation. The colonizers come to stay—invasion is a structure not an event" (the project's existing reading of pp. 1–2 gives the same words; Englert's page is 3), and Wolfe 2006, p. 393 (his pagination): "settler colonialism is an inclusive, land-centred project that coordinates a comprehensive range of agencies, from the metropolitan centre to the frontier encampment, with a view to eliminating Indigenous societies" — "These lines, subsequently quoted ad nauseam by other authors, served as a foundational definition". "Wolfe's definitions captured three key aspects … because settler colonialism is about the control of land, it is ongoing, eliminatory, and structural."
- On Wolfe 1999, pp. 1–2 ("In contrast to the kind of colonial formation that Cabral or Fanon confronted settler colonies were not primarily established to extract surplus value from indigenous labour"): "Here a contradiction already emerges … Fanon was writing from the Algerian context—a French settler colony, which … depended on the exploitation of indigenous labour."
- On Veracini: 2010, p. 34 ("the former can be summarised as domination for the purpose of exploitation, the latter as domination for the purpose of transfer"); 2015, pp. 6, 27, 40 ("settler colonialism is not colonialism"); 2015, pp. 26–27, 40 ("[c]olonial and settler colonial forms are also intertwined in any actual situation"); "colonialism and settler colonialism should be interpreted as antithetical modes of domination" — all "as quoted by Englert". "Wolfe and Veracini draw a sharp analytical separation, between settler colonies as eliminatory formations and franchise colonies as exploitative ones. Yet it is difficult to see how this approach reflects the realities".
- On Coulthard: "Yellowknives Dene scholar Glen Coulthard (2014) … point[s] out the perpetual need for settler societies to define themselves in opposition to indigenous populations"; "Most important here is Coulthard … who, in his *Red Skins, White Masks*, has emphasised the centrality of primitive accumulation to the settler colonial world"; Coulthard 2014, p. 125 (as quoted by Englert): settler colonialism is "territorially acquisitive in perpetuity"; "In the specific context of Canadian settler-colonialism, although the means by which the colonial state has sought to eliminate Indigenous peoples in order to gain access to our lands and resources have modified over the last two centuries … the ends have always remained the same: to shore up continued access to Indigenous peoples' territories for the purposes of state formation, settlement, and capitalist development." Englert reads this as "complicating … the concept of elimination as an outcome rather than a strategy." He also cites Harris 2004, p. 172, for Indigenous incorporation into the BC workforce, and Nichols 2015/2017.
- Conclusion: "although the elimination of the native is key to some settler colonial regimes, in certain periods, it does not accurately define the overall logic of settler colonialism, as claimed by Wolfe"; the paper "proposes another analytic approach, which highlights the specificity of social relations within settler colonial contexts in which settler workers participate in the process of accumulation by dispossession". Endnote on the absence of Indigenous resistance from the paper: "a matter of space … rather than an epistemological choice" (citing Bhandar and Ziadah 2016).

## 9. Lorenzo Veracini, "'Settler Colonialism': Career of a Concept", *JICH* 41:2 (2013): 313–333

**Citation.** DOI 10.1080/03086534.2013.768099 (online 25 March 2013). **Read status: abstract, opening and the concluding section "Colonialism within 'Settler Colonialism': Settler Colonial Studies" read** in the author's accepted manuscript at Swinburne Research Bank (hdl 1959.3/353066; figshare DOI 10.25916/sut.26215358.v1; "posted here with the permission of the publisher"; unpaginated against the published version); the middle sections (the three earlier phases) skimmed. The project's existing record `screq_veracini_2013` carries the abstract.
- Opening definition: "Settler colonial phenomena – circumstances where colonisers 'come to stay' and to establish new political orders for themselves, rather than to exploit native labour – are inherently transnational and transcultural" (note 1 cites Wolfe 1999, p. 2).
- The fourth phase: "The indigenous peoples of the white settler nations – the 'Fourth World' – eventually began to militantly demand recognition and self-determination. In the context of renewed political contestations, another phase in the development of 'settler colonialism' as a concept thus began." "ethnohistory, anthropology, indigenous studies, and especially history became crucial disciplinary sites in the development of 'settler colonialism' as a conceptual category." "for different reasons, both the Canadian and US historiographies were late in adopting settler colonialism as a category of analysis" (note 52). "An initial emphasis on indigenous devastation in the 1970s and 1980s was in due course coupled with an appraisal of indigenous agency, resilience, and success in resisting settler domination"; "the recovery of a long lasting 'middle ground'"; the "history wars". Abstract: "beginning from approximately the mid-1990s, 'settler colonial studies' as an autonomous scholarly field could then consolidate."
- The passage Snelgrove et al. criticise (Indigenous studies named only on "the second last page" before crediting "white historians") is this concluding section: the manuscript names "indigenous studies" among the disciplinary sites and then turns to historians' "revolutions" and the "history wars". Both readings are recorded.

**Use.** Supports the `theory_and_field` and `earlier_settler_societies` strand corrections (the third phase is "settler/dominion capitalism", Denoon's book, which Veracini says wrote "indigenous people and indigenous history off the conceptual map"); the comparison with `dependency` (the third phase defines settler colonialism "as the opposite of colonialism and associated underdevelopment"); the Snelgrove critique.

## 10. Audra Simpson: *Mohawk Interruptus* (Duke 2014) and "The ruse of consent and the anatomy of 'refusal'" (*Postcolonial Studies* 20:1, 2017: 18–33)

**Read status.** The book: **publisher page only** (https://www.dukeupress.edu/mohawk-interruptus: May 2014; contents — 1 "Indigenous Interruptions: Mohawk Nationhood, Citizenship, and the State", 2 "A Brief History of Land, Meaning, and Membership in Iroquoia and Kahnawà:ke", 3 on Ely S. Parker and Lewis Henry Morgan, 4 "Ethnographic Refusal: Anthropological Need", 5 "Borders, Cigarettes, and Sovereignty", 6 "The Gender of the Flint", Conclusion "Interruptus"; prizes 2014–15 including the NAISA First Book Prize and the ASA Lora Romero award; Crossref DOI 10.1215/9780822376781 issued 2014; the description says she treats refusal "as distinct from the politics of cultural recognition", argues for a sovereign political order "nested within a sovereign state", and criticises anthropologists and political scientists "for assuming the colonial project is complete" (fetch summary of the page; not her words). The article: **abstract read** (Wayback capture of the publisher page; pp. 18–33; online 6 June 2017; Columbia): "This article takes the notion of 'refusal' to be an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society. This is argued as alternative with recourse to ethnographic examples that highlight the way in which 'consent' operates as a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession in historical cases from Indigenous North America and Australia … Indigenous life in these cases refused, did not consent to, and still refuses to be folded into a larger encompassing colonising and settler colonial narratives of acceptance, and in this, a governmental fait accompli." Goeman 2016 (read): "Audra Simpson remembers him as an historian of not only the comparative and global project of settler colonialism, but also of anthropology itself"; Wolfe "volunteered to speak to my graduate seminar in settler colonial studies" at Columbia.

**Use.** Her position is in her own words only at abstract level: refusal as an alternative to recognition, consent as "a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession". Enough for a roster-row correction and a strand line; not enough for a person entry this wave (see verdicts).

## 11. Glen Sean Coulthard, *Red Skin, White Masks* (Minnesota 2014)

**Read status: publisher page only** (https://www.upress.umn.edu/9780816679652/red-skin-white-masks/: eBook 15 August 2014, paperback 7 September 2014; series Indigenous Americas; contents — Introduction "Subjects of Empire", 1 "The Politics of Recognition in Colonial Contexts", 2 "For the Land: The Dene Nation's Struggle for Self-Determination", 3 "Essentialism and the Gendered Politics of Aboriginal Self-Government", 4 "Seeing Red: Reconciliation and Resentment", 5 "The Plunge into the Chasm of the Past: Fanon, Self-Recognition, and Decolonization", Conclusion "Lessons from Idle No More"; prizes 2015: Studies in Political Economy, Frantz Fanon Outstanding Book, C. B. Macpherson; author "(Yellowknives Dene) … First Nations Studies Program and the Department of Political Science at the University of British Columbia"; the description says he "challenges recognition as a method of organizing difference and identity in liberal politics" and draws on "a 'place-based' reworking of Marx's 'primitive accumulation'" and Fanon). Passages known through Englert 2020 (p. 125, quoted in §8a). Crossref DOI 10.5749/minnesota/9780816679645.001.0001.

**Use.** Roster-row correction (his argument is now attested in his own words through Englert's page-cited quotation, and in the publisher's description); strand lines on recognition and primitive accumulation; not enough for a person entry this wave.

## 12. Mishuana Goeman, "In Memoriam: Patrick Wolfe (February 18, 2016)", *AICRJ* 40:1 (2016): ix–x


exec
/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '216,300p' data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md" in /home/jic823/historiography
 succeeded in 0ms:
**Citation.** DOI 10.17953/aicrj.40.1.goeman; eScholarship (CC BY-NC 4.0); "also published in *Amerasia Journal* 42 no. 1 (Spring 2016)". **Read status: read in full.**
- "Patrick Wolfe's sudden passing in the winter of 2016 stunned us all in Native American and Indigenous studies." Why his concept travelled "when other texts and similar concepts had preceded his important book": "the readability of his article and the geographic breadth of his work, from Australia to New Zealand to the United States to Palestine and Canada"; "his clear articulation that colonialism is 'a structure not an event,' reliant upon various processes of eliminating Native peoples in the name of capitalism"; "his distinction between genocide and elimination".
- "Patrick Wolfe was a key scholar in conceiving of a settler-colonial analytic in relation to Indigenous thinking and scholarship that exists far longer than settler nations. He critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner … His work became a place to engage Indigenous studies concerns in relation to settler colonial studies that at times leaves out Indigenous epistemologies and ontologies".
- Sandy Grande: "a champion for the field and our work"; Kevin Bruyneel: "shaped the field of settler colonial studies, and he was unceasingly supportive of younger scholars"; Audra Simpson (quoted above). "two new books released around the same time as his passing" (note 6: *The Settler Complex*, UCLA AISC 2016; *Traces of History*, Verso 2016).

**Use.** The NAIS side's own statement of his standing, which, with Kauanui, balances Snelgrove et al.'s suspicion of the field: supports `patrick_wolfe → indigenous` (contribution) and the death event.

## 13. Wolfe's bibliography and reception, metadata level (Crossref, British Library, VU, Berghahn; checked 2026-10-23)

- "Nation and MiscegeNation: discursive continuity in the post-Mabo era", *Social Analysis* 36 (1994): 93–152 — **unread**; known as cited by Wolfe 2006 (n. 1, n. 7 p. 96, n. 66 p. 111, n. 70 pp. 110–118) and by the 2026-10-12 request (JSTOR blocked). No Crossref record.
- "Land, Labor, and Difference: Elementary Structures of Race", *AHR* 106:3 (June 2001): 866–905 (Crossref 10.1086/ahr/106.3.866; also 10.2307/2692330) — **unread**; one sentence known as quoted by Wolfe 2006 n. 62 (p. 894).
- "Structure and Event: Settler Colonialism, Time, and the Question of Genocide", ch. 4 (pp. 102–132) of A. Dirk Moses (ed.), *Empire, Colony, Genocide* (Berghahn, War and Genocide 12; hardback June 2008, paperback December 2009; chapter DOI 10.1515/9781782382140-005) — **unread**; contents page read on the Berghahn page (Veracini has ch. 6 in the same volume; Docker ch. 3 "Are Settler-Colonies Inherently Genocidal?"). This is the source of the brief's "Structure and event" title, not *Traces of History*.
- "After the Frontier: Separation and Absorption in US Indian Policy", *SCS* 1:1 (2011): 13–51 (10.1080/2201473x.2011.10648800) — unread (no Crossref abstract).
- "Purchase by Other Means: The Palestine *Nakba* and Zionism's Conquest of Economics", *SCS* 2:1 (2012): 133–171; and an Arabic translation of the 2006 essay, *SCS* 2:1 (2012): 226–252 — unread.
- Contributions to *Stolen Lands, Broken Cultures: The Settler-Colonial Present* (Arena, 2012; eds Hinkson, James, Veracini) and co-editorship of *Sovereignty: Frontiers of Possibility* (U Hawai'i Press, 2013; with Evans, Genovese, Reilly) — VU library guide only.
- "Recuperating Binarism: a heretical introduction", *SCS* 3:3–4 (November 2013): 257–279 (10.1080/2201473x.2013.830587), the special issue he edited with its paired *AICRJ* 37:2 (2013) issue (Snelgrove et al., p. 10; Crossref front matter 10.17953/aicr.37.2.c56388751726j752) — unread.
- David Lloyd and Patrick Wolfe, "Settler colonial logics and the neoliberal regime", *SCS* 6:2 (online 7 May 2015): 109–118 — unread.
- *Traces of History: Elementary Structures of Race* (Verso, London and New York, 2016; BL legal-deposit record ELD.DS.286697: "title from PDF title page (EBSCO, viewed January 27, 2016)"; ISBNs 9781781689172 pbk, 9781781689189/96 electronic; contents: Introduction; 1 Racialisation of Indigenous People in Australia; 2 Race and Democracy in the United States; 3 Antisemitism in Central Europe; 4 Africans, Natives and Colour in Brazil; 5 Dispossessing Indians, Making the United States; 6 Racialising Indians in the United States; 7 Dispossessing the Natives in Palestine; 8 Racialising the Jewish State; Conclusion) — **unread** beyond the contents note. The existing record `screq_wolfe_2016` asked for the imprint year to be confirmed: the BL record and Goeman 2016 give 2016. Reviews: Makdisi, *AHS* 48:1 (January 2017): 117–118; Smithers (with *The Settler Complex*), *AHS* 48:1: 118–120; Meneses, *Sociology* 51:1 (2017); Delmagori, *Socialism and Democracy* 31:3 (2017) — unread.
- (ed.) *The Settler Complex: Recuperating Binarism in Colonial Studies* (UCLA American Indian Studies Center, 2016) — Goeman n. 6 and Kauanui n. 8 ("Wolfe responded to the charge of perpetuating a binary between settler and native with his recently edited volume"); unread.
- *American Quarterly* 69:2 (June 2017), "Tracing the Settler's Tools: A Forum on Patrick Wolfe's Life and Legacy" (Franklin, Njoroge and Reiss, pp. 235–247; O'Brien, "Tracing Settler Colonialism's Eliminatory Logic in Traces of History", 249–255; Kelley, "The Rest of Us: Rethinking Settler and Native", 267–276; Makdisi, "Elimination as a Structure: Tracing and Racing Zionism with Patrick Wolfe", 277–284) — metadata only (Project MUSE blocked); Englert 2020 cites Kelley 2017 and Speed 2017 (*AQ* 69:4, "Structures of Settler Capitalism in Abya Yala") for the exploitation critique.
- Tony (Anthony) Birch, "Good Patrick", *Aboriginal History* 40 (2016): 225–226 (VU repository record 39225; ISSN 0314-8769; the file is restricted; the official link points to ANU Press) — unread; the VU library guide places it in a section "In Memoriam Patrick Wolfe 1949–2016". Lynette Russell's tribute (Verso blog, 22 September 2022, from *Race, Place, Trace: Essays in Honour of Patrick Wolfe*, ed. Susan Slyomovics and Lorenzo Veracini): "trod carefully but defiantly"; Wolfe "saw himself as a settler and newcomer, not an objective observer" (fetch summary); she quotes Birch's "an openly emotional thinker" — the Birch phrase is therefore "as quoted by Russell".
- **Life and posts.** Wikidata Q109284663 (QLever, 2026-10-23): "Australian historian"; born 1949-06-12, Yorkshire; died 2016-02-18, Melbourne; citizenship Australia and United Kingdom; educated at the London School of Economics and the University of Melbourne; occupation historian; VIAF 116565018, LC n98039450, GND 1089878249, ISNI 000000008185442X. VU collection page: "He held research and teaching positions at several Australian universities including Victoria University, University of Melbourne and La Trobe University"; "Patrick Wolfe passed away in 2016"; the collection (c. 1,400 books, his personal library) is managed for the Aboriginal History Archive, Moondani Balluk Indigenous Academic Unit. Veracini 2016: "fellowships at Harvard and Stanford" (undated). Goeman's title: "(February 18, 2016)". Kauanui calls him "the late English scholar"; the project's label should follow Wikidata ("Australian historian", born in Yorkshire).

## 14. The field's institutions (read evidence)

- **NAISA** founded 2008 after a 2005 steering committee (Kauanui, para. 6 and note 10, co-founder).
- **The journal**: *Settler Colonial Studies* 1:1 (2011), "first published in 2011 in an open access format (entirely run on volunteer labour)" and moved "to a large academic publishing house, Taylor & Francis, within two years" (Snelgrove et al., p. 9, citing Edmonds and Carey, "A new beginning for *Settler Colonial Studies*", *SCS* 3:1 (February 2013): 2–5, Crossref 10.1080/18380743.2013.771761); ARC grant DP0986984 (Veracini 2011, acknowledgements; Veracini 2013, acknowledgements).
- **ASA 2015**: panel "The Settler Colonialism Analytic: A Critical Reappraisal" (Goldstein) and Warrior's position paper "Settler Colonial Studies and Native American and Indigenous Studies" (Kauanui, paras 5–6).
- **The paired 2013 issues** edited by Wolfe (*SCS* 3:3 and *AICRJ* 37:2) (Snelgrove et al., p. 10).
- **The Routledge Handbook of the History of Settler Colonialism** (Cavanagh and Veracini eds): Crossref book DOI 10.4324/9781315544816 issued 2016-08-12 (the existing record says print 2017).

## 15. The Canadian uptake (publisher pages and open documents; nothing read in the authors' own words unless stated)

- **Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada, *Honouring the Truth, Reconciling for the Future: Summary of the Final Report* (2015)** — **pages 1–3 read** in the NCTR-hosted PDF (cat. no. IR4-7/2015E-PDF; ISBN 978-0-660-02078-5; "This report is in the public domain"): "For over a century, the central goals of Canada's Aboriginal policy were to eliminate Aboriginal governments; ignore Aboriginal rights; terminate the Treaties; and, through a process of assimilation, cause Aboriginal peoples to cease to exist as distinct legal, social, cultural, religious, and racial entities in Canada. The establishment and operation of residential schools were a central element of this policy, which can best be described as 'cultural genocide.'" (p. 1). "The Canadian government pursued this policy of cultural genocide because it wished to divest itself of its legal and financial obligations to Aboriginal people and gain control over their land and resources" (p. 3). Dates: the Executive Summary released "in June 2015, at the Commission's Closing Event in Ottawa"; the final event 15 December 2015; 94 Calls to Action (Prime Minister's backgrounder, 15 December 2015, read). The summary's "eliminate" vocabulary and its rejection of the vanishing-people narrative are the Canadian public text closest to Wolfe's; the summary does not cite him (pp. 1–3 only checked).
- **James Daschuk, *Clearing the Plains* (University of Regina Press, 2013; Crossref/De Gruyter issued 2013-12-31; new edition 2019 with a foreword by Elizabeth Fenn, an opening by Niigaanwewidam James Sinclair and "critical responses by Mary-Ellen Kelm, Ian Mosby, Susan Neylan, and Andrew Woolford")** — **publisher page only**: the description says the government "used a policy of starvation against Indigenous people to clear the way for settlement" and that the book "sparked widespread debate about genocide in Canada"; the page "does not use the terms 'settler colonialism' or 'colonialism' in the description"; prizes 2014 (Governor General's History Award, Clio, CHA best scholarly book, Aboriginal History Prize, Macdonald Prize). Kelm's commentary and the forum on the book in *JCHA* 26:2 (2015; Érudit ids 1037223ar–1037225ar) could not be read (Érudit returned a bot challenge). The project's "framing unchecked" caveat therefore stands, now with a positive note: the 2019 edition's responses are the place to check it.
- **J. R. Miller, *Compact, Contract, Covenant: Aboriginal Treaty-Making in Canada* (University of Toronto Press, 2009; ISBN 9780802097415 hbk, 9780802095152 pbk)** — **unread; metadata from library and retailer records only** (the UTP page was not found); "the first comprehensive history of treaty-making in Canada". Cited by 9 of the 2020–25 Canadian seed citers (snowball support 9, lift 5.3) and named in the cohorts README as a distinctive riser inside the Indigenous family. No evidence that the book uses the settler-colonial frame.
- **Renisa Mawani, *Colonial Proximities* (UBC Press, Law and Society, 15 May 2009)** — **publisher page only**: British Columbia as a "contact zone"; "Indigenous dispossession and Chinese exclusion were parts of the same colonial processes of racialization that built the settler state" (fetch summary); the page uses "settler regime" and "settler colonies", not "settler colonialism"; Perry's endorsement.
- **Mary Jane Logan McCallum, *Indigenous Women, Work, and History, 1940–1980* (University of Manitoba Press, May 2014; Critical Studies in Native History)** — **publisher page only**: Indigenous women who worked "in 'modern Native ways'"; state education and placement programs "served assimilation and the extinguishment of treaty obligations" (fetch summary); conclusion titled "The Wages of Whiteness and the Indigenous Historian"; the description does not use "settler colonialism". Snowball: a 2020–25 Canadian entrant to the *Indigenous* founders' neighbourhood (cohorts README §6.2).
- **Adele Perry, *Colonial Relations* (Cambridge University Press, Critical Perspectives on Empire; 2 April / 5 May 2015)** — **publisher page only** (contents: 1 "Empire, family, and archive" 1–19 … 9 "Conclusion: empire, colonies, and families" 254–263; shortlisted for the 2017 Macdonald Prize); the description places the book in "feminist scholarship on gender and intimacy, critiques of colonial archives, transnational and postcolonial history, and the 'new imperial history'" (fetch summary). The existing edge_1020 already carries her 2016 forum self-description.
- **Sarah Carter, *Imperial Plots* (University of Manitoba Press, October 2016)** — **publisher page only**: "the spadework of British colonialism"; British women homesteaders "took part in dispossessing Indigenous people, but … their complicity was limited and ambiguous" (fetch summary); 2017 Governor General's History Award, CHA best scholarly book, Clio (Prairies); the description does not use "settler colonialism". Snowball: a 2020–25 entrant (support 5).
- **Emma Battell Lowman and Adam J. Barker, *Settler* (Fernwood, October 2015)** — **publisher page only**: contents 2 "Introduction: Why Say Settler", 3 "Canada and Settler Colonialism", 4 "It's Always All About the Land", 7 "Decolonization and Dangerous Freedom"; Veracini's endorsement; replaced by a second edition.
- **Paulette Regan, *Unsettling the Settler Within* (UBC Press; EPUB 22 December 2010, paperback 25 January 2011)** — **publisher page only**: foreword Taiaiake Alfred; ch. 3 "Deconstructing Canada's Peacemaker Myth"; "non-Aboriginal Canadians must go through their own decolonization" (fetch summary); author "director of research for the Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada".
- **Jordan Stanger-Ross (ed.), *Landscapes of Injustice* (McGill-Queen's, August 2020; Rethinking Canada in the World)** — **publisher page only**: the internment reframed around "deliberate and permanent destruction of home" through dispossession; the description does not use "settler" or "colonial" (the H-Net review quoted on the page calls Canada a "settler colonial state"); Saywell Prize 2022.
- **David Hugill, "What is a settler-colonial city?", *Geography Compass* (May 2017)** — **abstract read** (Crossref): "attempting to urbanize recent insights in settler-colonial theory"; "societies where colonists have 'come to stay' and no formal process of decolonization has unfolded".
- **Fred Burrill 2019** and **Allan Greer 2019** are already in the graph with read evidence (edge_1012; `screq_burrill_2019`).

## 16. Aileen Moreton-Robinson, *The White Possessive* (Minnesota, 15 May 2015) and Jodi Byrd, *The Transit of Empire* (Minnesota 2011)

**Read status.** Moreton-Robinson: **publisher page only** (Indigenous Americas; "the nation is socially and culturally constructed as a white possession, with Australian national identity rooted in Britishness and colonization and built on the disavowal of Indigenous sovereignty" (fetch summary); contents in three parts, "Owning Property", "Becoming Propertyless", "Being Property"; NAISA Best Subsequent Book Award). Macoun and Strakosch (read) cite her "patriarchal white sovereignty" and "racial ventriloquism"; Snelgrove et al. (read, p. 8) credit her with situating "patriarchal white sovereignty as a constitutive feature of settler colonialism". Byrd: **unread** (the Minnesota page returned 301/404; the existing record `screq_byrd_2011` is metadata); Snelgrove et al. (p. 8): Byrd "centres the deployment of Indianness as a constitutive feature of settler colonialism"; Tuck and Yang list "the frontier as the first and always the site of invasion and war (Byrd, 2011)" (p. 29) and thank her (n. 23).

