# Proposals

All ids are proposals (`p4B_rel_NN` for edges, `p4b_` for sources); nothing below is applied. Source ids in brackets refer to existing graph records (`screq_…`, `cpl129_…`, `pe139_…`) or to the new `p4b_` records in P.8. Read status is as stated in the readings above; every quotation is from a passage read or is marked "as quoted by".

## P.1 Entry `settler_colonialism`: text revisions

**`period`**: already `period_4` on disk (revision 1.155); no change proposed.

**`date_label` (replace):** "Earlier settler-society studies 1983–95 · Wolfe 1999, 2006 · journal 2011 · Tuck and Yang 2012 · critiques 2013–14 · TRC 2015 · Wolfe d. 2016 · Englert 2020 · reviewed through 2024"

**`description` (replace; 1,190 characters):**
"Historical inquiry into colonial formations in which newcomers came to stay, took land and claimed sovereignty over Indigenous territories. Patrick Wolfe gave the field its programme: settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it is 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'invasion is a structure not an event' (1999, 2006). Lorenzo Veracini founded Settler Colonial Studies (2011) and distinguished colonialism ('you, work for me') from settler colonialism ('you, go away'), adding that the structure 'persistently pursues a specific end point': it 'extinguishes itself'. Two debates run through the field. Veracini's self-superseding structure and Wolfe's enduring one; and the Indigenous reading, which adopts Wolfe's formula while insisting that 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies' (Kauanui), that 'decolonization is not a metaphor' (Tuck and Yang) and that refusal, not recognition, answers the settler state (Simpson, Coulthard). Settler scholars warn against 'colonial fatalism' (Macoun and Strakosch); Marxists ask whether elimination or exploitation defines the form (Englert). In Canada the frame met an older historiography of reserves, treaties, gender and the state, and the TRC's finding of 'cultural genocide' (2015)."

**`scope_note` (replace):** "A research field whose consolidation came after 2000; reviewed through 2024 in the period-4 wave (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md). Read in full: Wolfe 2006 (in a Wayback capture of the publisher's free-to-read page), Veracini 2011 and his 2016 obituary of Wolfe, Kauanui 2016, Tuck and Yang 2012 (most pages), Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014 (most pages), Macoun and Strakosch 2013 (author manuscript), Englert 2020, Goeman 2016, the TRC summary pp. 1–3. Veracini 2013 in its author manuscript (abstract, opening, conclusion). Unread, known through publisher pages or named readers: Wolfe 1999 beyond pp. 1–2, 2001, 2008, 2016; Veracini 2010, 2015; Coulthard; A. Simpson (book; 2017 abstract read); Byrd; Moreton-Robinson; L. B. Simpson; Rowse; Konishi; Sexton; Garba and Sorentino; King; Daschuk; Miller 2009; Perry 2015; Carter 2016; McCallum; Mawani; Stanger-Ross; Lowman and Barker; Regan. Settler-colonial theory, Indigenous political thought, Canadian histories of dispossession and comparative imperial history have different genealogies; Veracini (2013) and Kauanui (2016) both record uses of the term before Wolfe (Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973, Denoon 1983, Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis 1995). Roster inclusion is not adoption of the paradigm: Carter, Perry, Harris, Miller, Daschuk and McCallum wrote earlier or beside it, and their publisher descriptions do not use the phrase. Critics appear as critics; Kauanui and Goeman defend Wolfe's theory against shallow citation while asking the field to attend to Indigenous studies. History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23: two or more of the field's authors are cited by 3.2% of Canada + US theses in 2000–09 and 11.0% in 2020–25 (Canada 15.2%, US 6.8%); Wolfe and Veracini are co-cited by 3.0% of theses in 2020–25 and Wolfe with Tuck and Yang, Simpson or Coulthard by 2.3%, the only rising two-sided citing after 2000; the Canadian neighbourhood of the founders is the largest and youngest of any field (51 works, median year 2006), its 2020–25 entrants Coulthard, Simpson, Carter, Miller, Daschuk, Kauanui and Greer. Francophone uptake in the corpus remains near zero."

**`representative_figures_and_works` (replace):** "Earlier: Sayegh (1965), Rodinson (1973), Denoon, Settler Capitalism (1983); Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis, eds. (1995). Wolfe, Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999), 'Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native' (2006), 'Structure and Event' (2008), Traces of History (2016); Veracini, Settler Colonialism: A Theoretical Overview (2010), 'Introducing settler colonial studies' (2011), 'Career of a Concept' (2013), The Settler Colonial Present (2015); Elkins and Pedersen, eds. (2005); Cavanagh and Veracini, eds. (2016). Indigenous critique and theory: Byrd (2011), L. B. Simpson (2011, 2017), Tuck and Yang (2012), Coulthard (2014), A. Simpson (2014, 2017), Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel (2014), Moreton-Robinson (2015), Kauanui (2016), Goeman (2016). Critiques: Macoun and Strakosch (2013), Rowse (2014), Greer (2019), Shoemaker (2019), Konishi (2019), Englert (2020), Garba and Sorentino (2020). Canada: Carter (1990, 2008, 2016), Harris (2002, 2004, 2020), Perry (2001, 2015), Miller (2009), Mawani (2009), Regan (2010), Daschuk (2013), McCallum (2014), Battell Lowman and Barker (2015), TRC Summary (2015), Hugill (2017), Burrill (2019), Stanger-Ross, ed. (2020)."

## P.2 Strands (corrections and new)

**Correction — `structure_and_elimination` (title "Structure, not event: the logic of elimination").** Focus (new): "Wolfe's programme, now read in his own pages. Settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it 'is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'the primary motive for elimination is not race … but access to territory. Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; 'settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event'; elimination has a 'positive' aspect (allotment, citizenship, child abduction, assimilation) that persists after the frontier, so that the form is 'relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' names its convergence with genocide 'without … hierarchy among victims' (2006). The formula goes back to the 1999 book (p. 2) and the 1994 essay (p. 96, per his note 7). Kauanui: the phrase 'often seems to stand-in for a serious engagement of his theory'." person_ids: patrick_wolfe, j_kehaulani_kauanui. source_ids: screq_wolfe_1999, cpl129_wolfe_1999_intro, screq_wolfe_2001, screq_wolfe_2006 (addendum), p4b_wolfe_2006_capture, p4b_wolfe_2008_structure_event, screq_kauanui_2016 (addendum), p4b_goeman_2016.

**Correction — `theory_and_field` (title "Veracini: the field, the journal and the self-superseding structure").** Focus (new): "Veracini founded Settler Colonial Studies (2011; open access, volunteer-run, on an ARC grant; to Taylor & Francis in 2013) 'to contribute to the consolidation of a new scholarly field'. Colonialism says 'you, work for me'; settler colonialism says 'you, go away'; the two 'should be understood in their dialectical relation'. Where Wolfe's structure endures, Veracini's 'persistently pursues a specific end point': 'settler colonialism, by contrast, extinguishes itself', is 'least visible in the settler cities', and 'ends with an indigenous ultimate permanence'; 'decolonisation is not settler decolonisation', and 'the politics of recognition' is 'not … a genuinely decolonising move' (2011). His history of the concept (2013) finds three earlier careers of the term and a consolidation 'from approximately the mid 1990s', with 'both the Canadian and US historiographies … late in adopting' it. Englert calls him Wolfe's 'self-styled intellectual successor'; Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel find in his history 'Indigenous studies and Indigenous resistance … pretty much erased'." person_ids: lorenzo_veracini, penelope_edmonds (new, optional), jane_carey (new, optional). source_ids: screq_veracini_2008, screq_veracini_2010, screq_veracini_2011 (addendum), p4b_veracini_2011_deposit, screq_veracini_2013 (addendum), p4b_veracini_2013_deposit, screq_veracini_2015, screq_cavanagh_veracini_2017, p4b_edmonds_carey_2013, p4b_englert_2020, screq_snelgrove_dhamoon_corntassel_2014.

**New strand — `wolfe_veracini_structure_debate` (title "Structure and its end: Wolfe and Veracini read together").** Focus: "The dissertation data register 'Wolfe and Veracini' as the field's first two-sided citing (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23: 0% of Canada + US theses in 2000–09, 3.0% in 2020–25). The difference in their own words: Wolfe's structure is 'a continuity through time' that 'does not stop' with frontier homicide and 'has not been subject to election results' (2006); Veracini accepts the 'frequently quoted passage' and adds that 'the structure persistently pursues a specific end point' and 'extinguishes itself', so that settler colonialism 'covers its tracks' (2011). Englert reads both as drawing 'a sharp analytical separation' between eliminatory settler and exploitative franchise colonies and finds the separation does not fit Africa or South America (2020); Macoun and Strakosch find in the structure a 'colonial fatalism' and recall the label 'very much a structuralist stuck in a poststructuralist world' (2013). Veracini's obituary records Wolfe as 'still a Marxist' of 'the young Marx' for whom 'things are not things in themselves but relationships' (2016)." person_ids: patrick_wolfe, lorenzo_veracini, sai_englert, alissa_macoun, elizabeth_strakosch. source_ids: p4b_wolfe_2006_capture, p4b_veracini_2011_deposit, p4b_englert_2020, p4b_macoun_strakosch_2013_qut, p4b_veracini_wolfe_obituary_2016. basis: editorial_distinction (new).

**Correction — `recognition_refusal_resurgence` (title "The Indigenous reading: land, refusal, incommensurability").** Focus (new): "Indigenous political theory that takes up Wolfe's formula and redirects the field. Tuck and Yang: settler colonialism's violence 'is not temporally contained in the arrival of the settler but is reasserted each day of occupation. This is why Patrick Wolfe (1999) emphasizes that settler colonialism is a structure and not an event'; 'decolonization brings about the repatriation of Indigenous land and life; it is not a metaphor'; the settler–native–slave triad; 'settler moves to innocence'; 'an ethic of incommensurability' against reconciliation, which 'is about rescuing settler normalcy' (2012). Simpson: 'refusal' as 'an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society', consent 'as a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession' (2017, abstract; the 2014 book is known through its publisher page). Coulthard: settler colonialism 'territorially acquisitive in perpetuity'; in Canada the means of elimination changed but 'the ends have always remained the same: to shore up continued access to Indigenous peoples' territories' (2014, p. 125, as quoted by Englert); a 'place-based' reworking of primitive accumulation (publisher). Byrd, L. B. Simpson and Moreton-Robinson remain at publisher-page level. These works have their own genealogies; the cohorts README (§6.3) finds that in Canadian and US theses they are read with Wolfe, and the user ruled (2026-10-12) that they may sit in more than one field." person_ids unchanged plus aileen_moreton_robinson. source_ids: existing plus screq_tuck_yang_2012 (addendum), p4b_simpson_ruse_2017, p4b_englert_2020, p4b_moreton_robinson_2015.

**Correction — `indigenous_scholars_critique` (title "Indigenous studies and the field: who made it, whom it displaces").** Focus (new): "Kauanui: Wolfe 'insisted … that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did', yet 'he tends to be most frequently cited as if he had'; 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies'; indigeneity is 'a category of analysis that is distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality'; Warrior counted ASA panels and feared the field had become 'an answer to the chronic need for more attention to … Native and Indigenous studies' (2016). Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel: the field 'can displace, overshadow, or even mask over Indigenous studies'; 'when settlers take up these questions, it's suddenly considered a legitimate field of study' (2014). Goeman answers from the same field: Wolfe 'critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner' and his work 'became a place to engage Indigenous studies concerns in relation to settler colonial studies' (2016). Konishi surveys Indigenous scholars who advocate, interrogate and challenge the field (2019, abstract)." person_ids: shino_konishi, j_kehaulani_kauanui, corey_snelgrove, rita_kaur_dhamoon, jeff_corntassel, mishuana_goeman. source_ids: screq_konishi_2019, screq_kauanui_2016 (addendum), screq_snelgrove_dhamoon_corntassel_2014 (addendum), p4b_goeman_2016.

**Correction — `critiques_and_limits` (title "Critiques: fatalism, region, period, exploitation").** Focus (new): "From inside the theory, Macoun and Strakosch: SCT 'posits a structural inevitability', 'can depict colonization as structurally inevitable', risks 'colonial fatalism' and 'does not account for Indigenous lives'; yet 'the fact that settler colonialism struggles to narrate its own ending does not mean that it cannot end' (2013). Rowse: a 'teleology of elimination' (2014, abstract). Greer: one mode among several, dominant in some periods and regions (2019). Shoemaker: universal theory or English heritage? (2019). Englert: 'the elimination of the native … does not accurately define the overall logic of settler colonialism'; settler colonies in Africa and South America 'were built on the exploitation of indigenous peoples'; accumulation by dispossession 'carried out by all classes of settlers, including by workers' (2020, with Kelley and Speed 2017)." person_ids: existing plus sai_englert, robin_d_g_kelley (optional, metadata). source_ids: existing plus p4b_macoun_strakosch_2013_qut, p4b_englert_2020, p4b_kelley_2017.

**Correction — `health_starvation` (keep title; comparison).** Focus (new): "Daschuk's Plains history is the work most co-cited with Wolfe and Veracini by Canadian PhDs (19 of 58, lift 10.2; a 2020–25 entrant with support 12). Its publisher describes 'a policy of starvation' that 'sparked widespread debate about genocide in Canada' and does not use 'settler colonialism'; the 2019 edition adds responses by Kelm, Mosby, Neylan and Woolford. His own framing remains unchecked; the TRC summary's 'cultural genocide' (2015) is the public text beside which the book is read." source_ids: screq_daschuk_2013 (addendum), p4b_daschuk_urp_2019, p4b_trc_summary_2015, p4b_kelm_2015_jcha (not_checked).


