## 1. William Cronon: bibliographic frame, identity and the AHA address (checked 2026-10-23)

### 1.1 Curriculum vitae (author's own site)

**Read status: read in full** (8-page PDF, `biography/wjc_cv.pdf` on williamcronon.net, page revision 2018; the site answers only to a browser user-agent — HTTP 300/406 otherwise). The CV prints a personal e-mail address; it was not used or recorded. Facts taken from it (page numbers of the PDF's running heads "William Cronon, Vita, Page n"):
- Born 11 September 1954, New Haven; B.A. Wisconsin–Madison 1976; Rhodes Scholar 1976–78; D.Phil. Oxford 1981 ("British urban and economic history"; thesis *Energy and the Growth of an English Town: Coventry 1860–1950*, unpublished); Ph.D. Yale, December 1990 (p. 1, p. 10).
- Posts: assistant professor Yale 1981–86, associate 1986–91 (tenured 1988), professor 1991–92; Frederick Jackson Turner Professor of History, Geography and Environmental Studies, Wisconsin–Madison, 1992–2003; Frederick Jackson Turner and Vilas Research Professor 2003–2020; emeritus 2020– (p. 1).
- Honours and offices: MacArthur Fellowship 1985–90; President, American Society for Environmental History, 1989–93; Guggenheim 1995; Bancroft Prize 1992 for *Nature's Metropolis* (also the ASEH's George Perkins Marsh Prize 1992, the Forest History Society's Weyerhaeuser Award 1993 and a Pulitzer nomination 1992); Francis Parkman Prize 1984 for *Changes in the Land*; "A Place for Stories" won the OAH ABC-Clio Prize 1993; elected American Philosophical Society 1999, American Academy of Arts and Sciences 2006; AHA Vice-President (Professional Division) 2002–05 and principal drafter of the 2005 *Statement on Standards of Professional Conduct*; ASEH Distinguished Scholar Award 2008; **President of the American Historical Association, 2012–13**; Robert Marshall Award of The Wilderness Society 2014; Society of American Historians' Schlesinger Award 2017; ASEH Lisa Mighetto service award 2022 (pp. 2, 14–15).
- Series: **Weyerhaeuser Environmental Books (University of Washington Press), series editor 1994–2014, "acquiring, editing, and writing forewords for all titles; Paul Sutter succeeded me in this role in 2014"** (p. 2); the CV lists 60-odd forewords, among them to Pyne's *Vestal Fire* (1997), Fiege's *Irrigated Eden* (1999), Lowenthal's *Marsh* (2000), Sutter's *Driven Wild* (2002, "Why Worry about Roads"), Thrush's *Native Seattle* (2007), Weisiger's *Dreaming of Sheep* (2009), Fiege's *Republic of Nature* (2012, foreword titled "Environmental History Comes of Age"), Turner's *Promise of Wilderness* (2012), Feldman's *A Storied Wilderness* (2011) (pp. 2–4). Also co-editor of Yale's Lamar Series in Western History (publishing Hämäläinen's *Comanche Empire* 2008 and Madley's *An American Genocide* 2016 among others) and the Histories of the American Frontier series (pp. 4–6); editor of *John Muir: Nature Writings* (Library of America 1996) and *Loren Eiseley: Collected Essays* (2016) (p. 6).
- Publications after 1995 (pp. 6–8): *Uncommon Ground: Toward Reinventing Nature* (Norton 1995; paperback as *Rethinking the Human Place in Nature*, 1996); "The Trouble with Wilderness" in *Environmental History* 1:1 (January 1996), 7–55, "with comments by Samuel P. Hays, Michael P. Cohen, Thomas R. Dunlap, and a response by William Cronon"; "Only Connect" (1998); "Why the Past Matters" (2000); "Reading the Palimpsest" (2001); "The Riddle of the Apostle Islands: How Do You Manage a Wilderness Full of Human Stories?" (*Orion*, 2003); *Changes in the Land* 20th-anniversary edition with new afterword (2003); "Getting Ready to Do History" (2004/2006); nine *Perspectives on History* presidential columns (2012: "The Public Practice of History in and for a Digital Age", "Scholarly Authority in a Wikified World", "Two Cheers for the Whig Interpretation of History", "How Long Will People Read History Books?" …); "Storytelling", AHR 118:1 (February 2013), 1–19; French selection *Nature et Récits* (Éditions Dehors, 2016). Works in progress: *The Making of the American Landscape* and a history of Portage, Wisconsin (p. 11).
- Doctoral students (pp. 11–14), relevant to the absorption question: Louis Warren (Yale 1994; *The Hunter's Game*, 1997), Philip Deloria (Yale 1994; *Playing Indian*, 1998), **Karl Jacoby (Yale 1997; *Crimes Against Nature*, 2001, Marsh Prize and AHA Littleton-Griswold Prize)**, Samuel Truett (1997), Thomas Andrews (2003; *Killing for Coal*, 2008), Coll Thrush's *Native Seattle* is a series foreword, not a dissertation. Spence (Mark David Spence, *Dispossessing the Wilderness*, 1999) is not listed as his student.

