### 1.3 "Storytelling", AHA presidential address (AHR 118:1, February 2013, 1–19; DOI 10.1093/ahr/118.1.1)

**Read status: read in part** — the AHA's open text at https://www.historians.org/presidential-address/william-cronon/ (the page heads it "2012" and says it was "delivered at the 127th annual meeting of the American Historical Association, held in New Orleans in 2013"; the AHA's January 2013 *Perspectives* notice and the Wisconsin department's gallery date the delivery to 4 January 2013), passages extracted by prompt rather than the whole; the OUP page (paywalled) confirms title, section "Presidential Address", pages 1–19 and online date 4 February 2013. Quotations: "There can be few more daunting assignments in our profession than the one that lies before me" (opening, OUP extract); past addresses "tend to cluster into certain recurring genres" — Gordon Wright's "manifestoes", "the monographic addresses", and a small autobiographical group; "I was elected two years ago as the first self-described environmental historian ever to serve as president of the AHA"; "How do we make the past come alive? By telling stories about it"; "Our core business is resurrection: helping the dead past live again"; "The digital revolution has created endless opportunities via blogs, websites, YouTube, and social media" to "connect our professional stories with the concerns of the wider public"; closing: "Nothing is more important, for only by the neverending telling of such stories is the dead past reborn". Footnote 14 refers the reader to "A Place for Stories" (JAH 1992) for "additional thoughts on this theoretical background". Tribute to his undergraduate teacher Dick Ringler in the body ("Dick Ringler changed my life forever").

**Edge use.** Own words, open text: supports a contribution edge `william_cronon → truthdebate` revision or a new edge to `narrative`/`public history` (the entry ids to be chosen by the coordinator: the graph has `truthdebate`; check for a public/digital history entry), dated 2013, and the chronology events "elected AHA president (2011, for 2012–13)" and "Storytelling delivered 4 Jan 2013 / published Feb 2013". It also documents his self-description as "the first self-described environmental historian" to hold the office, an institution-level fact for the `environment` chronology (context track).

## 2. The wilderness debate, 1995–2000

### 2.1 The 1996 *Environmental History* forum (vol. 1, no. 1, January 1996)

**Read status: bibliographic metadata checked** (Crossref listing of the issue, 2026-10-23; the OUP pages are paywalled, "content only available as a PDF"): Cronon, "The Trouble with Wilderness: Or, Getting Back to the Wrong Nature", pp. 7–28, DOI 10.2307/3985059; Samuel P. Hays, "Comment: The Trouble with Bill Cronon's Wilderness", pp. 29–32, DOI 10.2307/3985060; Michael P. Cohen, "Comment: Resistance to Wilderness", pp. 33–42, DOI 10.2307/3985061; Thomas R. Dunlap, "Comment: But What Did You Go Out into the Wilderness to See?", pp. 43–46, DOI 10.2307/3985062; Cronon, "The Trouble with Wilderness: A Response", pp. 47–55, DOI 10.2307/3985063. Same issue: Hal Rothman, "From the Editor", p. 6 (the first number of the merged journal), Robert Marks on South China, José Drummond on Brazil's Tijuca forest. The journal itself: the graph's venue catalogue already records (source `journal_source_oup_environmental_merger`, read 2026-09-16) that *Environmental History Review* (1990–95; earlier *Environmental Review* 1976–89) merged with *Forest & Conservation History* in 1996 to form *Environmental History* (journal node `journal_b8bc3aaecbd1501f`; LoC record: Durham, N.C., "c1996-", ASEH and Forest History Society; later OUP and Chicago). The 1996 texts of Hays, Cohen, Dunlap and Cronon's response are **unread** here; Hays is quoted below only as Foreman quotes him.

The 1995 chapter itself: the graph's `cronon_wilderness` record (author-hosted reflow read in full, 2026-10-13) already carries the words the strands rest on ("quite profoundly a human creation", "a flight from history", "The removal of Indians to create an 'uninhabited wilderness'", the defence of wild-land protection at reflow pp. 12, 17–20). Not re-read; nothing here contradicts it. Cronon's CV adds the reprint record: *Best American Essays 1996*; Miller and Rothman, *Out of the Woods* (1997); abridged in the *New York Times Magazine* 13 August 1995 and *Utne Reader* May–June 1996 (CV p. 7) — the essay reached a public beyond the field within a year.

### 2.2 Dave Foreman, "The Real Wilderness Idea" (1999/2000)

**Read status: read in full.** Dave Foreman, "The Real Wilderness Idea", in David N. Cole, Stephen F. McCool, Wayne A. Freimund and Jennifer O'Loughlin, comps., *Wilderness Science in a Time of Change Conference, Volume 1: Changing Perspectives and Future Directions; 1999 May 23–27; Missoula, MT*, Proceedings RMRS-P-15-VOL-1 (Ogden, UT: USDA Forest Service, Rocky Mountain Research Station, 2000), pp. 32–38; a US government publication, read from the federal wilderness.net document archive (https://www.wilderness.net/NWPS/documents/science1999/Volume1/Foreman_1-5.pdf; 7-page PDF with text layer; page numbers from the printed folios). Foreman signs as "Chairman, The Wildlands Project".

The other side in its own words:
- Abstract (p. 32): "In recent years, some philosophers, historians and literary critics have condemned the 'Received Wilderness Idea.' Close examination reveals that this Received Wilderness Idea is a literary/philosophical construct little related to the Real Wilderness Idea that conservationists have used to establish the National Wilderness Preservation System."
- Opening (p. 32): "I come not to praise The Received Wilderness Idea, but to bury it. … If the Wilderness Idea that Baird Callicott, Bill Cronon and other postmodern deconstructionist scholars so eagerly banish with Milton's Lucifer has been received, I think it has been received as they hold hands in a darkened room around a seance table, trying to hear voices from the misty shades of Jonathan Edwards and Henry David Thoreau."
- p. 32: "This Real Wilderness Idea is very different from The Received Wilderness Idea invented and then lambasted by Baird Callicott, Bill Cronon and other deconstructionist social scientists. The literary and philosophical writings they draw from have had little influence in the wilderness protection movement"; conservationists since 1920 "have been motivated primarily by two things: One, they like a particular wilderness; two, they see a need to protect it from development and exploitation."
- p. 32, quoting Hays: "As Samuel Hays (1996), the great historian of resource conservation, Nature conservation and environmentalism, writes, 'Cronon's wilderness is a world of abstracted ideas…but divorced from the values and ideas inherent in wilderness action.'" (Hays 1996, pp. 29–32, **as quoted by Foreman**; Hays's text unread.)
- p. 32: "This Received Wilderness Idea is a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground. It is not the idea of wilderness that led to the Wilderness Act and the National Wilderness Preservation System … When one fights a phantom, it is easy to claim you have mortally wounded the monster."
- p. 33: "I am not going to respond point by point to the academic left's complaints about wilderness. I've done it before, most recently in the Callicott/Nelson anthology, The Great New Wilderness Debate, and I have not noticed anyone rebutting my specific points (Foreman 1998)." His reference list gives: Foreman, "Wilderness areas for real", in Callicott and Nelson, eds., *The Great New Wilderness Debate* (Athens: University of Georgia Press, 1998), 395–407. He then sets out "the Real Wilderness Idea of the citizen conservation movement", "blending both experiential and ecological values" (p. 33), under the heading "Self-Willed Land" (etymology of *wilderness*, Vest 1983).

**Edge use.** A named critique in the critic's own words, read in full: supports `environment → william_cronon` (critique: Foreman, with Hays, Sessions, Callicott as the named critics) and a new strand `wilderness_debate` on `environment`. Foreman's own dating ("Foreman 1998", the anthology) and his federal-proceedings statement (1999, printed 2000) are both events for the reception track.

