## 4. The Sutter forum, *Journal of American History* 100:1 (June 2013)

**Read status: abstract and forum listing read; bodies unread.** Paul S. Sutter, "The World with Us: The State of American Environmental History", JAH 100:1 (June 2013), 94–119, DOI 10.1093/jahist/jat095 (OUP page paywalled, 2026-10-23; 144 Web of Science citations shown). The OUP extract (opening paragraph): the field grew from "frontier historiography, American studies, the Annales school, historical geography, cultural ecology, and conservation politics", coalesced with the environmental movement of the 1960s–70s and was shaped by social history's widening of sources and subjects; it names Worster's lead essay in the 1990 round table as calling for attention to "the role and place of nature in human life". The OAH's issue page (Wayback capture of 23 October 2013, read via curl; the live OAH page returns 403) gives the editors' summary: "Since the Journal of American History last published a round table on the subject in 1990, American environmental history has seen explosive growth. Paul S. Sutter takes us on a selective tour of this expansive field, paying particular attention to questions of environmental causation and the ways environmental historians have replaced the once-firm categories of nature and culture with various approaches to environmental hybridity. That hybrid turn, Sutter suggests, has been analytically essential, yet it has also left the field at a moral crossroads." Responses (same page; Crossref DOIs where found): David Igler, "On Vital Areas, Categories, and New Opportunities", 120–123; Christof Mauch, "Which World Is with Us? A Tocquevillian View on American Environmental History", 124–127 (jat097); Gregg Mitman, "Living in a Material World", 128–130; Linda Nash, "Furthering the Environmental Turn", 131–135 (jat098); Helen M. Rozwadowski, "The Promise of Ocean History for Environmental History", 136–139; Bron Taylor, "'It's Not All about Us': Reflections on the State of American Environmental History", 140–144 (jat099); Sutter, "Nature Is History", 145–148 (jat096). A named reader: Jonathan Zasloff, "The Past Isn't Dead…", Legal Planet, 7 June 2013 (read in full; open blog): "it's not clear to me that 'hybridity' can do quite as much work as Sutter says". The word "declensionist" and Sutter's own sentences on hybridity are **not** available here; the strand text must quote the editors' summary, not Sutter. The OAH made the forum free in 2013 (Zasloff); the free links pointed to jah.oxfordjournals.org, whose Wayback captures could not be checked (archive.org rate-limited, 429). A library copy would settle Sutter's wording on "hybridity" and "declension" and the respondents' positions.

Sutter's identity: QLever Q126183373 "Paul Shriver Sutter", "History professor", P108 University of Colorado Boulder (probable: the only historian among four namesakes; no birth date). Cronon's CV: Sutter succeeded him as Weyerhaeuser series editor in 2014; *Driven Wild: How the Fight against Automobiles Launched the Modern Wilderness Movement* (UW Press 2002, foreword by Cronon, "Why Worry about Roads"). His Colorado profile page returned 404.

