**Read status: series page read** (ubcpress.ca, 2026-10-23): "devoted to the publication of high-quality scholarship in environmental history and allied fields", "essential concern is with the interface between nature and society"; 41 titles; general editor Graeme Wynn; no founding date on the page. Wynn's identity: QLever Q58217341, "Canadian geographer and environmental historian", P569 1946-09-28, P108 University of British Columbia (the namesake Q5592427 is a rugby player). His *Canada and Arctic North America: An Environmental History* (ABC-CLIO, Nature and Human Societies; Crossref 10.5040/9798400623141 dated 2006; reviewed by Binnema in *Environmental History* 12:4, October 2007, 1015–1016): publisher page unreachable (Bloomsbury blocked); **unread**. His 2001 Acadiensis essay and 2004 BC Studies introduction are already in the graph (read).

### 6.3 The books (publisher pages read 2026-10-23 unless stated)

- **Alan MacEachern, *Natural Selections: National Parks in Atlantic Canada, 1935–1970*** (McGill-Queen's, Crossref 10.1515/9780773569010, issued 23 April 2001). MQUP and De Gruyter Brill pages unreachable (404/405); **metadata only**. MacEachern: ungrounded on Wikidata (no label match); NiCHE director (above); co-editor with William J. Turkel of *Method and Meaning in Canadian Environmental History* (Nelson, 2009; reviewed with Turkel's *Archive of Place* by Jennifer Bonnell, CHR 90:4, Dec. 2009, 766–770); co-editor with Evenden of the Canadian special issue of *Environmental History* 12:4 (October 2007; "From the Editors", 755–758, DOI 10.1093/envhis/12.4.755; unread).
- **Tina Loo, *States of Nature: Conserving Canada's Wildlife in the Twentieth Century*** (UBC Press; hardcover 24 May 2006; Nature | History | Society, foreword Wynn): "Since the early days of the fur trade, wildlife has been powerfully and inspiringly emblematic of Canada"; CHA Sir John A. Macdonald Prize 2007 (the UBC profile calls it the "CHA Best Scholarly Book in Canadian History Prize"), Harold Adams Innis Prize 2008; Bill Waiser (*The Beaver*): "Loo uses the history of Canadian wildlife conservation as a lens through which to view the changing attitude of Canadians". **"Disturbing the Peace: Environmental Change and the Scales of Justice on a Northern River"**, *Environmental History* 12:4 (October 2007), 895–919 (DOI 10.1093/envhis/12.4.895; abstract read): "examines the environmental and social impacts of damming the Peace River in northern British Columbia" to explore "the challenges associated with doing environmental justice", which "is not simply a matter of rectifying the distribution" of harms and benefits but "requires coming to terms with the different scales" at which environmental change is apprehended, "scales that have sensual, spatial, and temporal dimensions". ***Moved by the State: Forced Relocation and Making a Good Life in Postwar Canada*** (UBC Press, 1 June 2019; McLean Family Canadian Studies Series): "the Canadian government relocated people, often against their will, in order to improve their lives"; CHA Best Book in Political History 2020; chapter "Decolonizing the North" (Inuit resettlement); Penny Bryden's blurb reads it "through the lens of high modernism". UBC profile (read): University Killam Professor; PhD UBC 1990; FRSC 2016; the CHR best-article prize 2011 (with Meg Stanley, on the Peace and Columbia rivers). QLever: Q97585539 "Canadian historian", P569 1962-10-07 — confirmed (namesake Q136396384, a Naturalis project officer, rejected). The graph's 2014 "Missed Connections" is already read (`pe131_extras_loo_2014`).
- **Liza Piper, *The Industrial Transformation of Subarctic Canada*** (UBC Press; hardcover 10 March 2009; Nature | History | Society, foreword Wynn): "Between 1921 and 1960, these lakes comprised a seam in the Canadian interior where industrial economies took root"; CHA Clio Prize (North) 2010, K. D. Srivastava Prize 2010; Stephen Bocking (*Northern Review*): "This history has dirt under its fingernails". With John Sandlos, **"A Broken Frontier: Ecological Imperialism in the Canadian North"**, *Environmental History* 12:4 (October 2007), 759–795 (DOI 10.1093/envhis/12.4.759; abstract read): "Ecological imperialism is one of the most enduring models of past global environmental change"; the authors extend it beyond the temperate zones "where it worked best" and find that "the Canadian North was never subject to the broad ecological transformation that occurred further to the south" — a limited, not total, process. (Crosby is not named on the page; the title and abstract take his term.) Piper: QLever Q131147375, "Canadian researcher", historian, P569 1978 (year precision), P108 University of Alberta — probable.
- **John Sandlos, *Hunters at the Margin: Native People and Wildlife Conservation in the Northwest Territories*** (UBC Press; hardcover 8 May 2007; Nature | History | Society, foreword Wynn, "The Enigmatic North"): game laws, parks and sanctuaries "helped the state assert authority over traditional hunting cultures"; CHA Clio (North) 2008 and the Forest History Society's Weyerhaeuser Award 2008; blurbed by **Karl Jacoby** ("a powerful new interpretation of Canada's conservation policies in the Northwest Territories") and reviewed by William R. Morrison in *Environmental History* 12:4. QLever Q57413196, "environmental historian", P569 1970 (year precision), P108 Memorial University — confirmed.
- **Matthew Evenden, *Fish versus Power: An Environmental History of the Fraser River*** (Cambridge, Studies in Environment and History; print 3 May 2004; DOI 10.1017/CBO9780511512032): "a case study of a river basin where development decisions did not ultimately dam the river, but rather conserved its salmon". With Stéphane Castonguay, eds., ***Urban Rivers: Remaking Rivers, Cities, and Space in Europe and North America*** (Pittsburgh, History of the Urban Environment, May 2012): Christof Mauch's blurb, "Hands down, Urban Rivers is the best environmental history of urban industrial rivers". QLever Q123023434, "Canadian geographer and environmental historian", P569 1971-10-13 — confirmed.
- **George Colpitts, *Pemmican Empire: Food, Trade, and the Last Bison Hunts in the North American Plains, 1780–1882*** (Cambridge, Studies in Environment and History; hardback 5 October 2014, paperback 27 October 2014; the brief's "2015" is the US publication year): the pemmican market hunt "nearly wiped out the Canadian bison herds north of the Missouri River"; Elliott West: "an utterly fresh and revealing look at the oft-studied story of the plains bison"; Brenda Macdougall (*Environmental History*). Ungrounded on Wikidata (no label match).
- **Joy Parr, *Sensing Changes*** (UBC Press, 1 July 2010) — already in the graph with an edge (edge_963); a roster row on `environment` is missing and is proposed below.
- **Theodore Binnema and Melanie Niemi, "'Let the Line Be Drawn Now': Wilderness, Conservation, and the Exclusion of Aboriginal People from Banff National Park in Canada"**, *Environmental History* 11:4 (October 2006), 724–750 (DOI 10.1093/envhis/11.4.724; abstract read, paraphrased by the tool): traces the exclusion of the Stoney from Rocky Mountains (Banff) Park, 1890–1920, and argues that in Canada, "and probably the United States", exclusion served game conservation, sport hunting, tourism and Indian assimilation rather than an ideal of uninhabited wilderness. Cronon and Spence are not named on the page.
- **James Daschuk, *Clearing the Plains: Disease, Politics of Starvation, and the Loss of Indigenous Life*** (University of Regina Press, 2013; Crossref 10.1515/9780889772977; new edition March 2019 with foreword by Elizabeth A. Fenn; the 2019 title replaces "Aboriginal" with "Indigenous"): "the roles that Old World diseases, climate, and, most disturbingly, Canadian politics" played in Indigenous deaths; Governor General's History Award, CHA Clio Prize and Sir John A. Macdonald Prize 2014; blurbed by **J. R. McNeill**. Agent B's territory for the settler-colonial side; here the hinge.

## 7. The absorption: dispossession histories in the United States

- **Karl Jacoby, *Crimes against Nature: Squatters, Poachers, Thieves, and the Hidden History of American Conservation*** (University of California Press, 2001; 2014 edition with new afterword, Crossref 10.1525/9780520957930). Publisher pages unreachable (UC Press template, De Gruyter 405); **unread**. Cronon's CV (p. 11): Jacoby's Yale dissertation "The Recreation of Nature: A Social and Environmental History of American Conservation, 1872–1919" (1997, co-directed with Howard Lamar); the book won the ASEH's George Perkins Marsh Prize and the AHA's Littleton-Griswold Prize for 2001. Jacoby: QLever Q54917887, "American historian", P569 1965-05-29, P108 Columbia University — confirmed (four namesakes rejected by dates). Dissertation data: *Crimes Against Nature* enters the US founders' neighbourhood in 2010–14 (support 9, lift 6.9) and stays in 2015–19; enters the Canadian neighbourhood in 2015–19.
- **Mark David Spence, *Dispossessing the Wilderness: Indian Removal and the Making of the National Parks*** (OUP; OSO record "Published: 2 November 2000", print ISBN 9780195142433; the hardback is cited as 1999 in the WHQ and *Public Historian* reviews of 2000). Abstract read (OUP, 2026-10-23): "examines the ideal of wilderness preservation in the United States from the antebellum era" and shows how "the early conception of the wilderness as the place where Indians lived (or should live)" gave way to "the idealization of uninhabited wilderness", through removal at Yellowstone, Glacier and Yosemite, 1870s–1930s; chapters include "First Wilderness: America's Wonderland and Indian Removal from Yellowstone National Park" and "Yosemite Indians and the National Park Ideal, 1916–1969". Cronon is not named in the abstract. Ungrounded on Wikidata. Dissertation data: enters the US neighbourhood in 2010–14 (support 8, lift 7.4).
- **Louis Warren, *The Hunter's Game: Poachers and Conservationists in Twentieth-Century America*** (Yale, 1997): Cronon's student (CV p. 11; dissertation 1994, co-directed with Lamar); reviews in WHQ, AHR and JAH 1998–99 (Crossref). Unread; metadata only.

Reading of the absorption: the works that took Cronon's 1995 argument (wilderness as a cultural creation that erased Indigenous inhabitants) into the archive — Warren 1997, Spence 1999, Jacoby 2001 — came from his own seminar at Yale (two of three), and the Canadian parallel (Binnema and Niemi 2006, Sandlos 2007, with Jacoby's endorsement) argued the same removal from the conservation side. The dissertation data then show the founders' neighbourhood filling with Indigenous and settler-colonial works after 2015 (§0.5). The chain is documentary for Warren and Jacoby (CV) and bibliographic for Spence; no reader read here says "this is Cronon's influence", so the influence edge below is proposed at reduced strength.

## 8. Donald Worster after 1990: career, later books, readers

- **Identity and posts.** QLever Q1240502, "American historian", P569 1941 (year precision), P108 University of Kansas and Renmin University of China, VIAF 66479787 — confirmed; the sheet's bulk match is reviewed. Origins (Ohio State) author page (read): Ph.D. Yale 1971; "Hall Distinguished Professor of American History" at Kansas "from 1989 until his retirement"; now "Distinguished Foreign Expert and senior professor in the School of History at Renmin University of China"; "a former president of the American Society for Environmental History". The search snippets give the retirement as May 2012 and the Renmin post as honorary director of its Ecological History Research Center (the Renmin page itself returned 404; the KU archives finding aid refused the connection) — carried as "probable, decade precision not needed: 2012 from a Portuguese-language interview snippet, unverified". Earlier posts (Brandeis, Hawaii, Yale) are not verified here.
- **Prizes.** Columbia's Bancroft Prize list (read 2026-10-23) lists "Dust Bowl: The Southern Plains in the 1930s" under 1979 (the list's year is the book year; the award was made in 1980 — the page does not say which, so the event below is dated 1980 with the Columbia entry as support and the ambiguity stated). *A River Running West* does not appear on Columbia's list; the brief's "Bancroft 2002" is **not confirmed** and is not proposed. AHA Award for Scholarly Distinction: "2012: Donald Worster, Univ. of Kansas" (with Alfred Crosby the same year; presented at the January 2013 meeting per the search snippet).
- **Books after 1990 (metadata).** *The Wealth of Nature: Environmental History and the Ecological Imagination* (OUP, 1993 hardback per the graph's existing record; OUP's online edition is the paperback "Published: 8 December 1994", DOI 10.1093/oso/9780195092646.001.0001): contents read on OUP — "The Nature We Have Lost", "Paths Across the Levee", "History as Natural History", **"Transformations of the Earth" (pp. 45–63, DOI …003.0007; the chapter abstract on OUP is the opening paragraph: Leopold's call for "an ecological interpretation of history" and the Kentucky canebrakes)**, "Arranging a Marriage: Ecology and Agriculture", "The Kingdom, the Power, and the Water", "Thinking Like a River", "The Ecology of Order and Chaos", "John Muir and the Roots of American Environmentalism", "The Wealth of Nature". *An Unsettled Country* (1994) not checked. *A River Running West: The Life of John Wesley Powell* (OUP, 2001; reviews: Goetzmann, *Isis* 92:4, Dec. 2001; Jacoby in JAH 89:1, June 2002; WHQ 33:2, 2002). "John Muir and the Modern Passion for Nature", *Environmental History* 10:1 (January 2005), 8–19. *A Passion for Nature: The Life of John Muir* (OUP, 2008; reviews: Harvey, *Oregon Historical Quarterly* 110:2, 2009 — MUSE blocked; PHR 78:4, 2009). *Shrinking the Earth: The Rise and Decline of American Abundance* (OUP, 2016; OUP's page is behind a bot wall).
- **Named readers of *Shrinking the Earth*, read in full (open journals).** Jean-Daniel Collomb, *Miranda* 15 (2017; online 20 September 2017; DOI 10.4000/miranda.10800): Worster treats the "Second Earth" of 1500 as having given settlers "the false impression that there was no limit" (para. 2); the book avoids "anachronistic self-righteousness, one of the pitfalls of environmental history" (para. 3); the energy chapter is "environmental history at its best" (para. 8); but Worster is "very short on details about the human capacity for adaptation", so his objection "sound[s] more like an act of faith than a convincing argument" (para. 12) and his "eventual plea for an earth ethic almost rings hollow" (para. 13); the book offers "reflections of the greatest US environmental historian on humankind's future and the Anthropocene" (para. 14). Joseph Michael Gratale, *European Journal of American Studies* (reviews; online 1 August 2017; DOI 10.4000/ejas.12175): "a critical and important contribution to environmental history", "fascinating to read, erudite, and engrossing" (para. 11); "Equilibrium became the word of the period" of the conservationists (para. 10); objects to the word "discovery" (para. 9).
- **The 1990 round table** remains as the graph has it: Worster's JAH text unread (OUP 403 / JSTOR challenge, 2026-10-13); Cronon's "Modes of Prophecy and Production" (JAH 76:4, 1122–31) unread; White's response unread; Merchant's read in full. A Portuguese translation ("Transformações da terra", *Ambiente & Sociedade*, 2003) exists in an open journal but the SciELO/orgprints pages could not be reached this session.

## 9. The Anthropocene and climate: what was read

- Chakrabarty 2009, 2015, 2021 and the Malm–Hornborg and LeCain critiques are already in the graph (edges 918, 1147; sources `pe132_chakrabarty_climate_2009`, `w3_chak_*`, `pe132_whose_anthropocene_2016`); not re-read.
- **Andreas Malm, *Fossil Capital: The Rise of Steam Power and the Roots of Global Warming*** (Verso, January 2016; page read): "Contrary to established views, steam offered neither cheaper nor more abundant energy"; steam gave manufacturers control over labour; Michael W. Howard's review (quoted on the page) criticises the "Anthropocene narrative" that makes the fossil economy the inevitable result of human fire-use; Mike Davis's blurb: the logic of capital, not technology as such, drove warming. Body unread.
- **Christophe Bonneuil and Jean-Baptiste Fressoz, *The Shock of the Anthropocene: The Earth, History and Us*** (Verso; trans. David Fernbach; the Verso page lists the paperback February 2017; the English edition is 2016 per library records; French *L'Événement Anthropocène*, Seuil 2013): "The Earth has entered a new epoch: the Anthropocene"; refutes "the convenient view of a 'human species' that upset the Earth system, unaware of what it was doing"; "the first critical history of the Anthropocene" (publisher description, read). Body unread.
- **Jason W. Moore, *Capitalism in the Web of Life*** (Verso, 2015): metadata only (Crossref reviews 2017: Larrère in *Annales* 72:2; Thomas, *Journal of Political Ecology*). Unread.
- **McNeill and Engelke, *The Great Acceleration*** (2016): §3.2.
- Dissertation data (§0.5): Chakrabarty 4.5% of Canada + US theses in 2020–25, three families; Latour 3.5%; Haraway 1.5%, "never above 7% even in the environment family". The Anthropocene strand is therefore a strand of the historiography, not of PhD reading.

