## P1. `environment`: text revisions

- **label:** unchanged ("Environmental history"). **period:** unchanged (`period_2`).
- **date_label (proposed):** "1970s consolidation · Environmental History (journal) 1996 · NiCHE 2004 · EH Canadian issue 2007 · Sutter forum 2013 · Oxford Handbook 2014 · reviewed through 2024"
- **description (revised draft, 1,176 characters):**
  "Explains historical relations among people and the nonhuman world through ecological exchange, agriculture, property, commodity flows, conservation and cultural ideas of nature; the approaches disagree about causal emphasis, and cases from New England, the Great Plains, Mexico, India, colonial islands and South Africa connect ecological processes with power without one universal model. After 1995 the field argued with itself and its neighbours. Cronon's 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995) called wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'; Foreman answered that this 'Received Wilderness Idea is a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (2000), while Spence (1999) and Jacoby (2001) took the removal of Indigenous people from 'uninhabited wilderness' into the archive. A merged journal (1996), McNeill's world synthesis (2000) and a Canadian network (NiCHE, 2004; Loo, Piper, Sandlos, Evenden) widened the field; by 2013 Sutter found it had replaced 'the once-firm categories of nature and culture' with 'environmental hybridity' (editors' summary) and stood 'at a moral crossroads'. Chakrabarty's 'climate of history' (2009) and the Anthropocene histories of McNeill, Malm, Bonneuil and Fressoz (2016) set its questions at planetary scale."
- **representative_figures_and_works (append):** "… After 2000: J. R. McNeill, Something New Under the Sun (2000) and 'Observations' (2003); Jacoby, Crimes against Nature (2001); Spence, Dispossessing the Wilderness (1999); Sutter, 'The World with Us' (JAH 2013); Isenberg, ed., Oxford Handbook of Environmental History (2014); in Canada, Wynn (series editor), MacEachern, Loo, Piper, Sandlos, Evenden, Colpitts, Parr; the Anthropocene: Chakrabarty (2009), McNeill and Engelke, Malm, Bonneuil and Fressoz (2016). Institutions: Environmental History (journal, 1996), NiCHE (2004)."
- **scope_note (append):** "Extended to 2024 on 2026-10-23 (evidence-environment.md). Read in full: Foreman's 'The Real Wilderness Idea' (2000), Cronon's CV, 'Storytelling' (2013, passages) and 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (2003), the NiCHE and UBC Press series pages, publisher pages and abstracts for the Canadian books and articles, two open reviews of Worster's Shrinking the Earth. Unread: the 1996 comments by Hays, Cohen and Dunlap and Cronon's response; Sutter's 2013 essay and its six responses (the editors' summary is quoted instead); McNeill 2000 and 2003 (the latter known from the earlier record); the 2005 'What's Next' forum including Linda Nash on agency; the bodies of every Canadian book; Malm, Moore, Bonneuil and Fressoz beyond descriptions. The History PhD citation graph (release 2026-10-23) dates the field's readership: 1.6% of Canada + US theses cited two or more of its authors in the 1990s, 5.7% in 2000–09, 9.5% in 2010–14, 11.3% in 2015–19, 9.9% in 2020–25; the network of works co-cited with Cronon, Worster, White, Crosby and Merchant peaked in Canada in 2010–14 (37 works) and halved by 2020–25 (17), its new members being Wolfe and Tuck and Yang; in the US it is stable (17–19 works) and its entrants are Indigenous, borderlands and slavery works. The field is read with its neighbours rather than renewed from within."

