{"node": "settler_colonialism", "field": "description", "new": "Historical inquiry into colonial formations in which newcomers came to stay, took land and claimed sovereignty over Indigenous territories. Patrick Wolfe gave the field its programme: settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it is 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'invasion is a structure not an event' (1999, 2006). Lorenzo Veracini founded Settler Colonial Studies (2011) and distinguished colonialism ('you, work for me') from settler colonialism ('you, go away'), adding that the structure 'persistently pursues a specific end point': it 'extinguishes itself'. Two debates run through the field. Veracini's self-superseding structure and Wolfe's enduring one; and the Indigenous reading, which adopts Wolfe's formula while insisting that 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies' (Kauanui), that 'decolonization is not a metaphor' (Tuck and Yang) and that refusal, not recognition, answers the settler state (Simpson, Coulthard). Settler scholars warn against 'colonial fatalism' (Macoun and Strakosch); Marxists ask whether elimination or exploitation defines the form (Englert). In Canada the frame met an older historiography of reserves, treaties, gender and the state, and the TRC's finding of 'cultural genocide' (2015).", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.1: description rewritten from the texts read in full; every quotation attributed."}
{"node": "settler_colonialism", "field": "scope_note", "new": "A research field whose consolidation came after 2000; reviewed through 2024 in the period-4 wave (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md). Read in full: Wolfe 2006 (in an Internet Archive capture of the journal's free-to-read page), Veracini 2011 and his 2016 obituary of Wolfe, Kauanui 2016, Englert 2020, Goeman 2016; in most pages, Tuck and Yang 2012 and Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014; Macoun and Strakosch 2013 in the authors' version; Veracini 2013 in its author manuscript (abstract, opening, conclusion); the TRC summary pp. 1–3. Unread, known through publisher pages or named readers: Wolfe 1999 beyond pp. 1–2, 2001, 2008, 2016; Veracini 2010, 2015; Coulthard; A. Simpson (book; 2017 abstract read); Byrd; Moreton-Robinson; L. B. Simpson; Rowse; Konishi; Sexton; Garba and Sorentino; King; Daschuk; Miller 2009; Perry 2015; Carter 2016; McCallum; Mawani; Stanger-Ross; Lowman and Barker; Regan. Settler-colonial theory, Indigenous political thought, Canadian histories of dispossession and comparative imperial history have different genealogies; Veracini (2013) and Kauanui (2016) both record uses of the term before Wolfe (Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973, Denoon 1983, Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis 1995). Roster inclusion is not adoption of the paradigm: Carter, Perry, Harris, Miller, Daschuk and McCallum wrote earlier or beside it, and their publisher descriptions do not use the phrase. Critics appear as critics; Kauanui and Goeman defend Wolfe's theory against shallow citation while asking the field to attend to Indigenous studies. History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23: two or more of the field's authors are cited by 3.2% of Canada + US theses in 2000–09 and 11.0% in 2020–25 (Canada 15.2%, US 6.8%); Wolfe and Veracini are co-cited by 3.0% of theses in 2020–25 and Wolfe with Tuck and Yang, Simpson or Coulthard by 2.3%, the only rising two-sided citing after 2000; the Canadian neighbourhood of the founders is the largest and youngest of any field (51 works, median year 2006), its 2020–25 entrants Coulthard, Simpson, Carter, Miller, Daschuk, Kauanui and Greer; among Canadian History PhDs 2009–26, 54 cite a work of Wolfe's and 17 the 1999 book (first in 2012), figures that say more about Canadian history than about him. Bibliographies record consultation, not influence. Francophone uptake in the corpus remains near zero.", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.1: scope note rewritten; the dissertation figures (and the counts struck from edge_892, 881 and 882) live here."}
{"node": "patrick_wolfe", "field": "description", "new": "An Australian historian of settler colonialism, race and the history of anthropology (1949–2016). His first book read Victorian and Australian anthropology through 'the elimination of native societies': 'the colonizers come to stay—invasion is a structure not an event' (1999). The 2006 essay made the formula a programme: settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it is 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; after the frontier, elimination 'turned inwards' through allotment, blood quantum and assimilation, so the form stays 'relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' names its convergence with genocide without 'hierarchy among victims'. Race, he argued, 'is made in the targeting' (2001, 2006; Traces of History, 2016). Veracini called him the field's founder 'in a fit of absentmindedness'; Kauanui records that he 'insisted … that he did not create the field … that Native scholars did'. Critics find in his structure a 'colonial fatalism' (Macoun and Strakosch) and ask whether exploitation, not elimination, defines settler colonies (Englert).", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7: description rewritten from the 2006 essay (read in full) and named readers."}
{"node": "patrick_wolfe", "field": "scope_note", "new": "Read on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md): the 2006 essay in full (an Internet Archive capture of the journal's free-to-read page; page numbers from Kauanui's citations), the 1999 introduction pp. 1–2 (earlier), Veracini's obituary, Goeman's memorial, Kauanui's essay, Macoun and Strakosch, Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel, Englert, in full or in most pages. Unread: the 1994 essay (JSTOR), the 1999 chapters (contents and catalogue checked), the 2001 AHR essay beyond a sentence he quotes himself, the 2008 chapter, the Settler Colonial Studies essays, Traces of History (British Library contents note only), The Settler Complex, Birch's obituary (restricted file), the 2017 forum, Russell's chapter. Life dates and places from Wikidata Q109284663 (checked by QLever SPARQL); posts from the Victoria University collection page (Victoria University, University of Melbourne, La Trobe University; undated) and Veracini's obituary (fellowships at Harvard and Stanford; undated), so no post is drawn as an event; Kauanui's 'English scholar' is noted against Wikidata's Australian historian born in Yorkshire. He did not coin the term (Veracini 2013; Kauanui 2016, n. 7) and said he did not found the field; the entry presents a programme, not an origin. Canadian PhD figures are in the settler_colonialism scope_note, not here.", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.7."}
{"node": "environment", "field": "description", "new": "Explains historical relations among people and the nonhuman world through ecological exchange, agriculture, property, commodity flows, conservation and cultural ideas of nature; the approaches disagree about causal emphasis, and cases from New England, the Great Plains, Mexico, India, colonial islands and South Africa connect ecology with power without one model. Bullard and the Huggins life histories add unequal burdens and authority over accounts; the Dust Bowl debates set capitalism, technology, policy and survival against one another. After 1995 the field argued with itself and its neighbours: Cronon called wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'; Foreman answered that this 'Received Wilderness Idea is a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (2000); Spence (1999) and Jacoby (2001) took the removal of Indigenous people from 'uninhabited wilderness' into the archive. A merged journal (1996), McNeill's world synthesis (2000) and a Canadian network (NiCHE, 2004) widened it; by 2013 Sutter found nature and culture replaced by 'environmental hybridity' (editors' summary). Chakrabarty (2009) and the Anthropocene histories of 2016 set its questions at planetary scale.", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1: the field after 1995."}
{"node": "environment", "field": "scope_note", "new": "Ecological exchange, political economy, and feminist critiques of nature overlap; institutional society history does not document every intellectual route. Crosby’s temperate-settler argument differs from Melville’s Mexican case; Gadgil and Guha’s resource conflicts differ from Grove’s conservation networks and Carruthers’s park politics. Cronon’s cultural account of wilderness does not deny nonhuman nature. The selection remains incomplete, especially in environmental justice and Indigenous scholarship. A bounded second-pass addition now compares Bullard and the Huggins collaboration; primary-excerpt, publisher-description and metadata limits remain distinct, and neither selection represents every community. Extended to 2024 on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md). Read in full: Foreman's 'The Real Wilderness Idea' (2000), Cronon's CV, 'Storytelling' (2013, passages) and 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (2003), the NiCHE and UBC Press series pages, publisher pages and abstracts for the Canadian books and articles, two open reviews of Worster's Shrinking the Earth. Unread: the 1996 comments by Hays, Cohen and Dunlap and Cronon's response; Sutter's 2013 essay and its six responses (the editors' summary is quoted instead); McNeill 2000 and 2003 (the latter known from the earlier record); the 2005 'What's Next' forum including Linda Nash on agency; the bodies of every Canadian book; Malm, Moore, Bonneuil and Fressoz beyond descriptions. The History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23 dates the field's readership: 1.6% of Canada + US theses cited two or more of its authors in the 1990s, 5.7% in 2000–09, 9.5% in 2010–14, 11.3% in 2015–19, 9.9% in 2020–25; the network of works co-cited with Cronon, Worster, White, Crosby and Merchant peaked in Canada in 2010–14 (37 works) and halved by 2020–25 (17), its new members being Wolfe and Tuck and Yang; in the US it is stable (17–19 works) and its entrants are Indigenous, borderlands and slavery works. The field is read with its neighbours rather than renewed from within.", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1: reading limits and the PhD citation figures."}
{"node": "william_cronon", "field": "description", "new": "A historian of North American environments who treats ecological change as a two-way relation between people and the rest of nature, worked out through property, markets and ideas of nature. Changes in the Land (1983) assumes that 'the interactions of the two are dialectical' and finds in colonial New England not a choice between landscapes with and without people but 'two human ways of living, two ways of belonging to an ecosystem'. Nature’s Metropolis (1991) follows grain, lumber and meat between Chicago and its hinterland on the premise that 'city and country have a common history, so their stories are best told together'. 'A Place for Stories' (1992) asks environmental historians for 'stories about stories about nature', and 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995) calls wilderness 'quite profoundly a human creation'. Reviewers found the Chicago book distant from farmers and townspeople, or moralising; he replied that he saw 'no pure heroes or pure villains'. After 1995 the essay drew its own debate: Hays found in it 'a world of abstracted ideas' (1996, as quoted by Foreman), and Foreman told a wilderness-science conference that the 'Received Wilderness Idea' of 'Callicott, Cronon and other postmodern deconstructionist scholars' was 'a straw dog; it does not exist on the ground' (2000). Cronon answered in practice, asking that the Apostle Islands be managed 'as a historical wilderness', 'a storied wilderness' (2003). He edited the Weyerhaeuser Environmental Books (1994–2014) and, as 'the first self-described environmental historian ever to serve as president of the AHA', told the profession that 'Our core business is resurrection: helping the dead past live again' (2013).", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P1/P6: the debate and the later career."}
{"node": "william_cronon", "field": "scope_note", "new": "This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-13: chapter 1 of Changes in the Land in an unpaginated web transcription; 'A Place for Stories' (JAH 1992; pp. 1347–1351 and 1369–1376 read) and 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993; OCR of the author’s scan, journal pagination); 'Kennecott Journey' (1992); passages of 'The Trouble with Wilderness' in the author’s reflow; and the 1992 Annals of Iowa forum on Nature’s Metropolis, with his response read in full. Nature’s Metropolis itself is unread: its words appear here only as forum contributors quote them with page numbers. 'Modes of Prophecy and Production', his 1990 response to Worster in the JAH round table, is paywalled and unread. He is not presented as a founder. He places environmental history among the 'new' histories 'born or reenergized in the wake of the 1960s' (1993, p. 2), names Nash, Hays, Worster, Merchant, Tarr and Pyne as practitioners (pp. 2–3), footnotes lessons to Crosby, Richard White, McEvoy and others (pp. 8–10), and echoes Leopold (n. 11). On Mahoney’s reading (1992, p. 492), Nature’s Metropolis demonstrates 'this fundamental urban historical premise' rather than inventing it. His approaches were contested in period: Danbom (1992, p. 503) finds 'environmental moralism'; Mahoney (p. 491) that urban and economic history 'seem only to serve the interests of environmental history'; Sessions (1996) that the wilderness essay underweights wild habitat. His 2003 afterword, McNeill (2003) and Canadian uses after 2000 are later readings. Extended on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): his CV read in full; 'The Riddle of the Apostle Islands' (2003) read in full in OCR; 'Storytelling' (2013) read in passages from the AHA's open text; Foreman's 'The Real Wilderness Idea' (2000) read in full. Unread: his 1996 response to Hays, Cohen and Dunlap (paywalled), whose side of the second round is therefore missing; the 2012 Perspectives columns (titles only); Callicott and Nelson's anthology (publisher description). Hays's 1996 sentence is known only as Foreman quotes it. Wikidata Q8007305 confirmed by QLever.", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): reading record."}
{"node": "donald_worster", "field": "description", "new": "A historian of the American Great Plains and arid West who set out a three-level model of environmental history. In 'Doing Environmental History' (1988) he wrote that 'there are three levels on which the new history proceeds': 'understanding nature itself', 'the socioeconomic realm as it interacts with the environment', and 'the purely mental or intellectual', which together 'constitute a single dynamic inquiry'. His 1990 Journal of American History essay turned to 'that middle level, the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena', chiefly the capitalist transformation of agriculture, which Dust Bowl (1979) had called 'the decisive factor in this nation's use of nature'; Rivers of Empire (1985) described the West as 'a modern hydraulic society'. Merchant's 1990 response called the levels 'a significant framework for research and writing in this emerging field' but found that the essay 'lacks a gender analysis'. Cronon (1992) read Dust Bowl as a story whose 'chief agent' is capitalism and whose plot 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy'. After the round table he turned to lives and to limits: biographies of John Wesley Powell (2001) and John Muir (2008; the Muir essay of 2005), and Shrinking the Earth (2016), which Collomb read as a history of the 'Second Earth' of 1500 that gave settlers 'the false impression that there was no limit', by 'the greatest US environmental historian' — while finding its 'plea for an earth ethic almost rings hollow'. He held the Hall chair at Kansas (1989–2012) and received the AHA's Award for Scholarly Distinction (2012).", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) P6/P7: the later career."}
{"node": "donald_worster", "field": "scope_note", "new": "This entry presents the three-level model in Worster's 1988 wording, checked in an abridged reprint and by full-text search of the 1988 edition, and the 1990 essay in his own 1993 reprint; the Journal of American History pages of 1990 were not reachable and are given only where another writer cites them (Isenberg 2013). Dust Bowl and Rivers of Empire are quoted at the pages given by Cronon (1992) and Hennessey (1987), with the wording confirmed by search of the original editions. He is not presented as the founder of the field or the sole author of the scheme: he grounds the idea in Aldo Leopold's call for 'an ecological interpretation of history', and Arthur McEvoy's ecology, production and cognition triad appeared in the same 1988 volume. The scheme was contested at once: Merchant (1990) added gender and 'a fourth analytical level, that of reproduction', Cronon (1992) treated Dust Bowl as a declensionist narrative, and McDean (1986) criticised its generalisation across the Plains. The 1990 objections of White and Cronon in the round table are known here only through Isenberg (2013), a later reading. Extended on 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): the Oxford record of The Wealth of Nature (contents; the opening paragraph of 'Transformations' as its abstract), the Origins author page, Columbia's Bancroft list and the AHA award list read; Collomb (2017) and Gratale (2017) on Shrinking the Earth read in full. The biographies of Powell and Muir and 'John Muir and the Modern Passion for Nature' (2005) are known by metadata only, so no edge rests on them; the retirement year (2012) and the Renmin appointment rest on a search snippet and the Origins page; the ASEH presidency has no years found. The Internet Archive routes in the older records for Nature's Economy, Rivers of Empire and The Wealth of Nature are not reused; those records now point to publisher pages, their earlier reading notes kept. Wikidata Q1240502 confirmed by QLever.", "reason": "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md): reading record."}
