{"id": "pe131_worster_wealth_1993", "title": "Donald Worster, The Wealth of Nature: Environmental History and the Ecological Imagination (New York: Oxford University Press, 1993).", "url": "https://archive.org/details/wealthofnatureen00wors", "citation": "Donald Worster, The Wealth of Nature: Environmental History and the Ecological Imagination (Oxford University Press, 1993), ch. 4, “Transformations of the Earth” (begins p. 45), and Notes, pp. 221–244.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "Internet Archive wealthofnatureen00wors (OUP 1993; Open Library OL1713212M, LCCN 92015360, ISBN 0195076249), lending-restricted; page images not seen. Checked by the archive's full-text search, which returns short snippets with running heads. Contents: ch. 3 'History as Natural History' p. 30; ch. 4 'Transformations of the Earth' p. 45; Notes p. 221; Index p. 245. Chapter 4 reprints the 1990 essay (the snippets match the Portuguese translation and Isenberg's JAH quotations); whether it was revised was not checked. Snippets used: 'There are three levels on which the new history proceeds, each drawing on a range'; 'at once materialist and idealist; they commonly maintain that the'; 'the second level of inquiry I mentioned'; 'that middle level, the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena, and particularly'; 'who has gained and who has lost power as modes of production have changed'; 'I submit that the single most important task for scholars in the history of modern agroecology is to trace what Karl Polanyi has called “the great transformation,”'; 'as sweeping and revolutionary a set of land-use changes as did the Neolithic revolution'; 'The reorganization of nature, not merely of society'. The index gives modes of production at 50, 52–63 and the great transformation at 56, 57–58. Chapter 4, note 5 (Annales passage) is on p. 226: the running head '226 Notes' precedes the end of chapter 3's notes (n. 32), and 'Notes 227' precedes chapter 4's n. 9, so chapter 4's notes 1–8 fall on p. 226. Its full wording is in NOTES.md."}, "scope_note": "Worster's 1993 reprint of the 1990 essay: source of the English wording quoted here."}
ADDENDUM {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-23', 'note': "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md §8): OUP's online edition is the paperback ('Published: 8 December 1994'); 'Transformations of the Earth' occupies pp. 45–63 (DOI 10.1093/oso/9780195092646.003.0007). The 1993 first edition is retained. The record's url now points to the publisher (batch source_field_sets); the earlier reading note is kept."}
{"id": "pe131_worster_natures_economy_1977", "title": "Donald Worster, Nature's Economy: The Roots of Ecology (San Francisco: Sierra Club Books, 1977).", "url": "https://archive.org/details/natureseconomyro0000wors", "citation": "Donald Worster, Nature's Economy: The Roots of Ecology (Sierra Club Books, 1977); second edition, Nature's Economy: A History of Ecological Ideas (Cambridge University Press, 1994).", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "Internet Archive natureseconomyro0000wors (Sierra Club Books, 1977), lending-restricted; full-text search snippets only. LoC authority n84043788 cites 'His Nature's economy, 1984, c1977'. Snippets: 'The first was an “arcadian” stance toward nature, epitomized by Gilbert White, the parson-naturalist of Selborne'; 'The second, an “imperial” tradition, is best represented in the work of Carl [Linnaeus]'; 'Our central focus in this history of ecology has been moral'; index entry 'Imperial tradition, 2, 29–55'. The book was not read; it supports the date and subject of the work only. [Also checked under pe131_carson_worster_1977: Read through the Internet Archive full-text search of the borrow-only scan (identifier natureseconomyro0000wors): each passage was rebuilt from overlapping phrase searches of the OCR text, so the wording is the scan's own, with OCR hyphenation removed and obvious OCR letter errors corrected (e.g. 'Carson s' read as 'Carson’s'). The page images were not seen. Pages from the 1977 index ('Carson, Rachel, 284, 331, 340; mentioned, vii, 23-24, 55'; 'Silent Spring, 23, 340'; 'Nuclear age, 339-341') and the running heads 'The Age of Ecology « 339' and '« 341'. pp. 23–24: '… nature writer who more than any other person launched the recent ecology movement, Rachel Carson. Her early writing on the sea and the tidal zone … are nature essays squarely in the John Burroughs and Gilbert White tradition. But in her book Silent Spring (published in 1962), which marshaled substantial scientific evidence of the threat to life posed by the persistent pesticides, Carson went on to inaugurate the literature of ecological apocalypse.' p. 339 opens the chapter: 'One kind of fallout from the atomic bomb was the beginnings of widespread, popular ecological concern around the globe.' p. 340: 'It also became clear, with the publication in 1962 of Rachel Carson’s Silent Spring, that the atomic bomb was only the most obvious threat to the sanctity of life. Carson found in the new persistent pesticides a more subtle but perhaps equally devastating kind of weapon', followed by her p. 8 sentence; the note cites 'Rachel Carson, Silent Spring, p. 8'. The 1994 edition's index adds pp. 347–350 for Carson (not read). The page of each sentence on pp. 23–24 is located by the index, not seen on the page image. Fix pass 2026-10-13 (sol-review-1.131): because the page images were not seen, the atlas text now gives these passages as attributed paraphrase, with pages marked as observed (running head) or inferred (index). A re-try to reach page images failed: the scans are borrow-only, and the archive's full-text search endpoint returned 403 on 2026-10-13.]"}, "scope_note": "In-period book: history of ecological ideas; identity and subject only."}
ADDENDUM {'status': 'bibliographic_metadata_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-23', 'note': "Period-4 wave 1: the record's url now points to the Oxford record of the 1994 second edition; the 1977 Sierra Club first edition is retained in the citation; the earlier snippet reading is kept in the note."}
{"id": "pe131_worster_rivers_1985", "title": "Donald Worster, Rivers of Empire: Water, Aridity, and the Growth of the American West (New York: Pantheon Books, 1985).", "url": "https://archive.org/details/riversofempirewa00wors", "citation": "Donald Worster, Rivers of Empire: Water, Aridity, and the Growth of the American West (Pantheon Books, 1985), x + 402 pp.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "Internet Archive riversofempirewa00wors (Pantheon 1985), lending-restricted; checked by full-text search snippets only, page images not seen. Contents snippets: 'TAXONOMY: The Flow of Power in History 17', 'Wittfogel, Marx, and the Ecology of Power 22', 'The Agrarian State Mode 36', 'The Capitalist State Mode 48'. Text snippets: the West 'can best be described as a modern hydraulic society, which is to say, a social order'; 'the argument made by Karl Wittfogel, a twentieth-century scholar of Chinese'; 'Water in the capitalist state has no intrinsic value'. Pages for quotations come from Hennessey's 1987 review (pe131_worster_hennessey_1987): 'a land of authority and restraint, of class and exploitation, and ultimately of imperial power' (p. 4); 'social order based on the intensive, large-scale manipulation of water' (p. 7); 'Water had indeed made this desert bloom, and the crop was oligarchy' (p. 206)."}, "scope_note": "In-period book: the hydraulic-society argument, checked by search and through a review."}
