{"id": "pe131_extras_mcneill_2003", "title": "J. R. McNeill, “Observations on the Nature and Culture of Environmental History,” History and Theory 42, no. 4 (December 2003): 5–43.", "url": "https://www.williamcronon.net/writing/H701_McNeill.pdf", "citation": "J. R. McNeill, “Observations on the Nature and Culture of Environmental History,” History and Theory 42:4 (2003): 5–43. doi:10.1046/j.1468-2303.2003.00255.x.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "PDF re-downloaded and read for pp. 8, 14–16 with notes 15, 34–35, 39, 42. Quoted: Hays 'Among the American pioneers in this field' (p. 8; n. 15 lists Conservation and the Gospel of Efficiency and Beauty, Health, and Permanence); 'the Annales historians were, many of them, strangely blind to evolving environments'; Braudel 'normally imagined it as an unchanging force'; Le Roy Ladurie 'embraced environmental change in two of his major books' (n. 35: Les paysans de Languedoc; Times of Feast, Times of Famine); 'To a very limited extent, then, Annales historians were engaged in environmental history avant la lettre'; 'Their general approach however proved inspirational' (all p. 14); n. 39 (p. 15): Nash 'in 1970 offered one of the very first courses in environmental history' (McNeill cites PHR '363 (1974)', a slip for 41, 1972); n. 42 (p. 16): 'The most important book in this vein probably was Roderick Nash, Wilderness and the American Mind'. Fix pass 2026-10-13 (sol-review-1.131): text-layer PDF re-downloaded and pp. 14 and 37–39 re-read. p. 14 as quoted above. p. 37: 'The most cogent was probably the one elaborated in slightly different versions by both Donald Worster and Arthur McEvoy', and such theories 'have sparked no groundswells of enthusiasm nor attracted sizable schools of followers'; pp. 38–39: Merchant's scheme 'has not attracted wide imitation within environmental history'. [Also checked under pe131_crosby_mcneill_2003: Later reading. Copy hosted on williamcronon.net (downloaded 2026-10-13 for the canon evidence file); pp. 18–19, 24, 32–33 and 42 re-read for Crosby, page assignment from running heads. Crossref 10.1046/j.1468-2303.2003.00255.x. 'the American historians, especially Worster, Cronon, and Crosby, were read almost wherever environmental history aroused interest' (p. 19). The Columbian Exchange is 'one of the landmark works in environmental history' (p. 24). Ecological Imperialism is 'Perhaps the best-known among the thematically defined global-scale environmental histories' (p. 32) and 'contains one of the most elegant arguments found anywhere in environmental history'; 'Some readers felt that Crosby had relocated historical agency (and responsibility for genocide and colonialism) from humans to wheat and whooping cough' (p. 33). Crosby 'did not have much to say about the transplanting of agronomic practice' (p. 42). Other drafts in this batch may cite McNeill 2003 under their own ids; merge at build if so.] [Also checked under pe131_richard_white_mcneill_2003: Full text (williamcronon.net H701_McNeill.pdf), read for canon-evidence-international.md; the White passage re-read: 'Richard White’s concept of “middle ground,” which he developed in the context of Native American–white relations in North America, struck historians of Japan and medieval Austria as useful for their own studies' (p. 37), n. 131 citing The Middle Ground and Brett Walker, The Conquest of Ainu Lands (2001). Crossref 10.1046/j.1468-2303.2003.00255.x: History and Theory 42:4, 5–43.]"}, "scope_note": "Later reading (2003): international self-account of the field; used only as a labelled later reading."}
ADDENDUM {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-23', 'note': "Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md §3.1): Crossref record confirmed (History and Theory 42:4, pp. 5–43, issued 7 November 2003); the record's url now points to the publisher's DOI (batch source_field_sets); the pages read on 2026-10-13 stand on the earlier note."}
{"id": "cronon_changes_afterword", "title": "William Cronon, afterword to Changes in the Land: Indians, Colonists, and the Ecology of New England (1983; anniversary edition 2003): 171–186.", "citation": "William Cronon, afterword to Changes in the Land: Indians, Colonists, and the Ecology of New England (1983; anniversary edition 2003): 171–186.", "url": "https://www.williamcronon.net/courses/460/handouts/cronon_changes_in_the_land_2003_ed_afterword.pdf", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "Downloaded primary retrospective consulted at pp. 173–175 for Wisconsin landscape training and Leopold’s course model; pp. 182–183 for learning property analysis from anthropology, economics and legal theory; pp. 184–185 for the author’s criticism of source asymmetries, schematic capitalism and neglected colonial spirituality. These concern the 1983 book; no full-book review or post-2000 extension of the mapped field. Addendum 2026-10-13: Re-read for the 1.131 Leopold entry. The Leopold passages are on pp. 174–175 (the existing note's pp. 173–175 range covers them): the 'reading the landscape' workshops at the university arboretum 'were in fact modeled on a famous course taught by Aldo Leopold as early as the 1930s'; the Almanac, 'usually read today as one of the greatest twentieth-century contributions to American nature writing', 'is no less a meditation on the history of human interactions with the American land'; the teachers he names include the geographers Lawrence Martin, Glenn Trewartha and Andrew Hill Clark, who 'may not have called themselves environmental historians'; his high-school reconstruction of presettlement vegetation was in 'the county where Aldo Leopold's famous “Shack” is located' (p. 175)."}}
ADDENDUM None
{"id": "pe131_cronon_place_stories_1992", "title": "William Cronon, “A Place for Stories: Nature, History, and Narrative,” Journal of American History 78, no. 4 (March 1992): 1347–1376.", "url": "https://www.williamcronon.net/writing/cronon_place_for_stories_1991.pdf", "citation": "William Cronon, “A Place for Stories: Nature, History, and Narrative,” Journal of American History 78:4 (1992): 1347–1376. doi:10.2307/2079346.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-18", "note": "Author-hosted PDF of the journal pages (file name says 1991; running heads read 'The Journal of American History March 1992', pp. 1347–1376); Crossref 10.2307/2079346 agrees. Read closely: pp. 1347–1351 (Bonnifield and Worster; 'the postmodernist assault on narrative'; n. 5 on longue-durée histories; n. 6 reading list including Hayden White, Ricoeur, Mink and Clifford and Marcus; the chronicle of the Plains) and pp. 1369–1376 (people at the centre of environmental narratives; criteria for good history; 'only willing to follow the postmodernists so far'; the three bounds of facts, ecology and community; 'nature coauthors our stories'; 'stories about stories about nature'; closing questions). pp. 1352–1368 (the survey of Great Plains narratives: Turner, Webb, Malin, the New Deal, Plenty Coups) were not read closely. [Also checked under pe131_worster_cronon_1992: Author-hosted PDF of the journal pages (running heads 'The Journal of American History March 1992'); text layer read for pp. 1347–1349 and 1363–1364. pp. 1347–1348: Bonnifield's and Worster's Dust Bowl books of 1979 'agreed on most of their facts, and yet their conclusions could hardly have been more different'; Cronon quotes Worster, Dust Bowl, p. 4: 'It came about because the culture was operating in precisely the way it was supposed to.... The Dust Bowl ... was the inevitable outcome of a culture that deliberately, self-consciously, set itself [the] task of dominating and exploiting the land for all it was worth.' p. 1363: Worster 'is with Webb the most powerful narrator'; the Plains are 'a paradigmatic case in a larger story that might be called “the rise and fall of capitalism”'; Cronon quotes Dust Bowl, p. 5: 'Capitalism, it is my contention, has been the decisive factor in this nation's use of nature'; 'the chief agent of the story is not “the pioneers” or “civilization” or “man”; it is capitalism'; the tale 'proclaims an apocalyptic prophecy that inverts the prophecy of progress found in earlier frontier narratives' (p. 1363). p. 1364: Worster 'acknowledges the prior presence of Indians in the region' but 'devotes only a few pages to them'. The Cronon packet in this batch records the same article as pe131_cronon_place_stories_1992; the two records should be merged at release.] Addendum 2026-10-18: Revision 1.147 (Hayden White edges) newly read pp. 1350–1351 and 1368–1369 in journal pagination, so p. 1368 is no longer among the pages not read closely. p. 1350 n. 6: reading list naming White's Metahistory (1973), Tropics of Discourse (1978) and The Content of the Form (1987). p. 1351 and n. 7: Great Plains history as \"a chronicle, a simple chronological listing of events\"; \"This distinction between chronicle and narrative is more fully analyzed in White, Metahistory, 5-7; White, Tropics of Discourse, 109-11; Louis O. Mink\". p. 1368: \"One group, which includes Hayden White and the late Louis Mink as well as many of the deconstructionists, argues that narrative is so basic to our cultural beliefs that we automatically impose it on a reality that bears little or no relation to the plots we use in organizing our experience\" (Cronon's summary of their position); n. 39 calls White's 1984 History and Theory survey \"useful, if biased\". pp. 1368–1369: the alternative \"most recently defended by David Carr\"; \"Carr's position will undoubtedly be attractive to most historians\" and \"gives us a way of absorbing the lessons of narrative theory without feeling we have abandoned all ties to an external reality.\""}, "scope_note": "In-period essay: narrative, environmental history and the postmodern challenge."}
ADDENDUM None
{"id": "pe131_crosby_isenberg_2013", "title": "Andrew C. Isenberg, “From the Periphery to the Center: North American Environmental History,” Global Environment 6, no. 12 (2013): 80–101.", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3197/ge.2013.061205", "citation": "Andrew C. Isenberg, “From the Periphery to the Center: North American Environmental History,” Global Environment 6, no. 12 (2013): 80–101. doi:10.3197/ge.2013.061205.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "Later reading. Full text from environmentandsociety.org (downloaded 2026-10-13 for the canon evidence file); pp. 85–86 and n. 13 re-read. Crossref 10.3197/ge.2013.061205. 'The materialist approach was exemplified by the work of Alfred W. Crosby' (p. 85). 'As Crosby expanded the scope of his analysis, he increasingly depicted people as utterly at the mercy of ecological forces'; he quotes Ecological Imperialism (1986, p. 192, per n. 13) that European colonists 'were seldom masters of the biological changes they triggered' and that 'their role was less often a matter of judgment and choice than of being downstream of a bursting dam'; 'In short, over the course of two decades Crosby's analysis devolved into little more than ecological determinism' (p. 86). The 1986 passage is known here only through this quotation. [Also checked under pe131_richard_white_isenberg_2013: Full text from the Environment & Society Portal, read for canon-evidence-international.md; re-read in that copy at pp. 87–89. p. 87: 'Environmental history emerged as a distinct field in the 1970s and early 1980s when a group of historians, including Wiliam [sic] Cronon, Thomas Dunlap, Carolyn Merchant, Arthur McEvoy, Richard White, and Donald Worster, synthesized the material and cultural approaches to the environment'. pp. 88–89: 'In a response to the essay, Richard White summarized Worster’s argument thusly: “Environmental history has a base (natural history), a structure (production relations or modes of production) and a superstructure (culture and ideology)”. This scheme, White argued, reduced culture to “superstructure in the old vulgar Marxist sense”', n. 21: White, JAH 76 (1990), pp. 1112–1113; 'White and Cronon urged environmental historians to pay more attention to culture'. Crossref 10.3197/ge.2013.061205: Global Environment, pp. 80–101.]"}, "scope_note": "Later reading (2013), labelled as such: the ecological-determinism critique."}
ADDENDUM None
{"id": "pe131_worster_jah_1990", "title": "Donald Worster, “Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History,” Journal of American History 76, no. 4 (March 1990): 1087–1106.", "url": "https://doi.org/10.2307/2936586", "citation": "Donald Worster, “Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History,” Journal of American History 76:4 (1990): 1087–1106, opening “A Round Table: Environmental History”. doi:10.2307/2936586.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "English JAH text unread: the DOI resolves to the OUP page, which returned 403, and JSTOR returned a bot challenge. Crossref 10.2307/2936586 gives JAH 76:4, first page 1087; Crossref lists the round table's responses: Crosby 'An Enthusiastic Second' (1107), White 'Environmental History, Ecology, and Meaning' (1111), Merchant 'Gender and Environmental History' (1117), Cronon 'Modes of Prophecy and Production' (1122), Pyne 'Firestick History' (1132) and Worster's reply 'Seeing Beyond Culture' (1142). The whole article and notes were read in Portuguese translation ('Transformações da terra', Ambiente & Sociedade 5:2/6:1, 2002–03, pp. 23–44). The English wording quoted in this packet comes from Worster's reprint of the essay in The Wealth of Nature (1993, ch. 4; see pe131_worster_wealth_1993), checked by full-text search. JAH pages are given only where Isenberg 2013 cites them: p. 1091 for 'at once materialist and idealist' and 'the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena', both matching the 1993 wording. Worster's reply and the White, Cronon, Crosby and Pyne responses are unread. [Also checked under pe131_leopold_worster_1990: The English original was not seen (JSTOR blocked; no open copy found). Crossref 10.2307/2936586 confirms JAH 76:4 (March 1990), first page 1087. The Portuguese translation was read in the PDF of orgprints eprint 20347 (Worster_transformações.pdf), whose first-page note says the article was first published in the Journal of American History in March 1990. Quoted from the translation: p. 23, 'Há quarenta anos atrás, um homem sábio e visionário, o conservacionista e biólogo da vida selvagem, Aldo Leopold, de Wisconsin, reivindicou uma “interpretação ecológica da História”, pela qual propunha usar as idéias e a pesquisa do emergente campo da ecologia para ajudar a explicar porque os fatos que constituem o passado desenvolveram-se da maneira como os conhecemos'; 'Levou algum tempo para os historiadores darem atenção ao conselho de Leopold, mas finalmente o campo da história ambiental começou a ganhar forma e seus praticantes estão tentando construir a partir de sua iniciativa.' p. 24: 'os fatos no caso são mais complicados do que Leopold poderia ter explorado nos limites de seu ensaio, e eles exigem mais do que uma forma simples de determinismo ambiental, que é o que um leitor menos atento poderia encontrar em seu exemplo'; Kentucky bluegrass 'não era uma espécie nativa, mas uma importação européia', followed by Crosby's 'imperialismo ecológico'. Notes, p. 42: n. 1 cites Leopold, A Sand County Almanac, and Sketches Here and There (1949; reprinted New York, 1987), p. 205; n. 3 says Leopold knew the story was more complex and quotes him (p. 206) that 'we do not even know' where bluegrass came from. English glosses in the atlas are the editor's, not Worster's words. A Bowdoin course guide paraphrasing the essay was seen and not used.] [Also checked under pe131_richard_white_worster_1990: Only the Portuguese translation was read (orgprints eprint 20347 PDF). Note 5 (translation p. 42): 'O melhor esforço para traçar a emergência do campo, pelo menos em uma parte influente do mundo, é o de WHITE, R. “American Environmental History: The Development of a New Historical Field” … White argumenta que o estudo da fronteira e da história do oeste tem sido a influência formativa neste campo. Uma outra fonte de idéias importante, realmente retirada da influência de Frederick Jackson Turner, tem sido a dos historiadores e geógrafos franceses, particularmente Fernand Braudel, Lucien Febvre e Emmanuel Le Roy Ladurie, todos associados à revista dos Annales.' The English wording and JAH page of the note are unchecked.] Addendum 2026-10-13: Re-read for edge_111 (extras packet): note 5 in the Portuguese translation (Ambiente & Sociedade 5:2/6:1, 2002–03, p. 42, n. 5), from the orgprints PDF, names 'Fernand Braudel, Lucien Febvre e Emmanuel Le Roy Ladurie, todos associados à revista dos Annales' as 'Uma outra fonte de idéias importante'. It is attached to the sentence that introduces the new field of ecological or environmental history. The English JAH page remains unread."}, "scope_note": "In-period programmatic essay; English pages not seen."}
ADDENDUM None
