{"node":"memory","field":"focus","strand":"archive_authority","new":"Combe’s inquiry into access supplies a named resource to Derrida. His account differentiates archives from spontaneous memory and examines institutional authority and technical inscription. From the archival profession, Schwartz and Cook called archives 'active sites where social power is negotiated' whose appraisal and access shape collective memory (2002, abstract); the wider readership is on the entry archival_turn.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md P.9 4): cross-reference and the Schwartz–Cook abstract (read)."}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","new":"Structure, not event: the logic of elimination","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"structure_and_elimination"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"Wolfe's programme, now read in his own pages. Settler colonialism 'destroys to replace'; it 'is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'the primary motive for elimination is not race … but access to territory. Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; 'settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event'; elimination has a 'positive' aspect (allotment, citizenship, child abduction, assimilation) that persists after the frontier, so that the form is 'relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' names its convergence with genocide without 'hierarchy among victims' (2006). The formula goes back to the 1999 book (p. 2) and the 1994 essay (p. 96, per his note 7). Kauanui: the phrase 'often seems to stand-in for a serious engagement of his theory'.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: the 2006 essay is read in full; the earlier caveat is withdrawn.","strand":"structure_and_elimination"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","new":"Veracini: the field, the journal and the self-superseding structure","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"theory_and_field"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"Veracini founded Settler Colonial Studies (2011; open access, volunteer-run, on an ARC grant; to Taylor & Francis in 2013) 'to contribute to the consolidation of a new scholarly field'. Colonialism says 'you, work for me'; settler colonialism says 'you, go away'; the two 'should be understood in their dialectical relation'. Where Wolfe's structure endures, Veracini's 'persistently pursues a specific end point': 'settler colonialism, by contrast, extinguishes itself', is 'least visible in the settler cities', and 'ends with an indigenous ultimate permanence'; 'decolonisation is not settler decolonisation', and the politics of recognition 'cannot be considered a genuinely decolonising move' (2011). His history of the concept (2013) finds three earlier careers of the term and a consolidation 'from approximately the mid 1990s', with 'both the Canadian and US historiographies … late in adopting' it. Englert calls him Wolfe's 'self-styled intellectual successor'; Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel find in his history 'Indigenous studies and Indigenous resistance … pretty much erased'.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Veracini 2011 read in full, 2013 in its author manuscript.","strand":"theory_and_field"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","new":"The Indigenous reading: land, refusal, incommensurability","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"recognition_refusal_resurgence"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"Indigenous political theory that takes up Wolfe's formula and redirects the field. Tuck and Yang: settler colonialism's violence 'is not temporally contained in the arrival of the settler but is reasserted each day of occupation. This is why Patrick Wolfe (1999) emphasizes that settler colonialism is a structure and not an event'; 'decolonization brings about the repatriation of Indigenous land and life; it is not a metaphor'; the settler–native–slave triad; 'settler moves to innocence'; 'an ethic of incommensurability' against reconciliation, which 'is about rescuing settler normalcy' (2012). Simpson: 'refusal' as 'an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society', consent 'as a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession' (2017, abstract; the 2014 book is known through its publisher page). Coulthard: settler colonialism 'territorially acquisitive in perpetuity'; in Canada the means of elimination changed but 'the ends have always remained the same: to shore up continued access to Indigenous peoples' territories' (2014, p. 125, as quoted by Englert); a 'place-based' reworking of primitive accumulation (publisher). Byrd, L. B. Simpson and Moreton-Robinson remain at publisher-page level. These works have their own genealogies; in Canadian and US theses they are read with Wolfe (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23), and the user ruled (2026-10-12) that they may sit in more than one field.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Tuck and Yang read; Simpson and Coulthard in their own words at abstract or quoted level.","strand":"recognition_refusal_resurgence"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","new":"Indigenous studies and the field: who made it, whom it displaces","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"indigenous_scholars_critique"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"Kauanui: Wolfe 'insisted … that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did', yet 'he tends to be most frequently cited as if he had'; 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies'; indigeneity is 'a category of analysis that is distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality'; Warrior counted ASA panels and feared the field had become 'an answer to the chronic need for more attention to … Native and Indigenous studies' (2016). Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel: the field 'can displace, overshadow, or even mask over Indigenous studies'; 'when settlers take up these questions, it's suddenly considered a legitimate field of study' (2014). Goeman answers from the same field: Wolfe 'critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner' and his work 'became a place to engage Indigenous studies concerns in relation to settler colonial studies' (2016). Konishi surveys Indigenous scholars who advocate, interrogate and challenge the field (2019, abstract).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Kauanui, Snelgrove et al. and Goeman read.","strand":"indigenous_scholars_critique"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"title","new":"Critiques: fatalism, region, period, exploitation","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2.","strand":"critiques_and_limits"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"From inside the theory, Macoun and Strakosch: SCT 'posits a structural inevitability', 'can depict colonization as structurally inevitable', risks 'colonial fatalism' and 'does not account for Indigenous lives'; yet 'the fact that settler colonialism struggles to narrate its own ending does not mean that it cannot end' (2013). Rowse: a 'teleology of elimination' (2014, abstract). Greer: one mode among several, dominant in some periods and regions (2019). Shoemaker: universal theory or English heritage? (2019). Englert: 'the elimination of the native … does not accurately define the overall logic of settler colonialism'; settler colonies in Africa and South America 'were built on the exploitation of indigenous peoples'; accumulation by dispossession 'carried out by all classes of settlers, including by workers' (2020, with Kelley and Speed 2017).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: Macoun and Strakosch and Englert read.","strand":"critiques_and_limits"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"Daschuk's Plains history is the work most co-cited with Wolfe and Veracini by Canadian PhDs (19 of 58, lift 10.2; a 2020–25 entrant with support 12; History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23). Its publisher describes 'a policy of starvation' that 'sparked widespread debate about genocide in Canada' and does not use 'settler colonialism'; the 2019 edition adds responses by Kelm, Mosby, Neylan and Woolford. His own framing remains unchecked; the TRC summary's 'cultural genocide' (2015) is the public text beside which the book is read. A comparison strand.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2: publisher page read; framing still unchecked.","strand":"health_starvation"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"Comparative settler economies (1983) and gender, race, ethnicity and class in settler societies (1995) are earlier comparative scholarship, before the field's theoretical consolidation; Veracini's history of the concept records decolonization-era usage of the term itself. Veracini (2013) reads the 'settler/dominion capitalism' literature as writing 'indigenous people and indigenous history off the conceptual map'; Kauanui (2016, n. 7) adds Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973, Sayigh 1979 and Trask 1992/2000 before Wolfe.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor.","strand":"earlier_settler_societies"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"How settler-colonial and decolonial frameworks place slavery and Black people. Wolfe 2006 (read) sets out the opposed racialisations of Black and Indigenous people in the United States; Tuck and Yang's settler–native–slave triad (read) is what Garba and Sorentino answer. Both authors' words are now read; the critics' remain at abstract level.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor.","strand":"slavery_black_studies"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"works","new":"Adele Perry, On the Edge of Empire: Gender, Race, and the Making of British Columbia, 1849–1871 (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 2001).; Sarah Carter, The Importance of Being Monogamous: Marriage and Nation Building in Western Canada to 1915 (Edmonton: University of Alberta Press / Athabasca University Press, 2008).; Margaret D. Jacobs, White Mother to a Dark Race: Settler Colonialism, Maternalism, and the Removal of Indigenous Children in the American West and Australia, 1880–1940 (Lincoln: University of Nebraska Press, 2009).; Adele Perry, Colonial Relations: The Douglas-Connolly Family and the Nineteenth-Century Imperial World (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2015).; Sarah Carter, Imperial Plots: Women, Land, and the Spadework of British Colonialism on the Canadian Prairies (Winnipeg: University of Manitoba Press, 2016).","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor: publisher pages.","strand":"gender_family_nation"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"focus","new":"Settler self-examination after residential schools and in contemporary Canada; Regan's contents page names the 'peacemaker myth'. The TRC summary (2015, pp. 1–3 read) finds that residential schools were part of a policy 'which can best be described as \"cultural genocide\"'; Regan is described by her publisher as the Commission's director of research.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.2 minor.","strand":"settler_identity_reconciliation"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","new":"Gave the field its programme: 'destroys to replace'; 'inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal'; 'invasion is a structure not an event'; 'structural genocide' (2006, read in full; the formula from 1994 and 1999). Denied that he created the field, per Kauanui. Australian historian (1949–2016); also on the Indigenous, racial formation, Holocaust and Indigenous–newcomer entries.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"patrick_wolfe"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","new":"Founded the journal (2011) and the theory of a self-superseding structure: colonialism says 'you, work for me', settler colonialism 'you, go away'; 'settler colonialism … extinguishes itself'; 'decolonisation is not settler decolonisation' (2011, read in full). His history of the concept (2013) dates the field's consolidation to the mid-1990s. Wikidata Q112150999; a person entry is deferred to wave 2.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"lorenzo_veracini"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","new":"Rejects 'the colonial politics of recognition' and reworks Marx's primitive accumulation as 'place-based' (publisher); settler colonialism 'territorially acquisitive in perpetuity', its Canadian means changing while 'the ends have always remained the same' (2014, p. 125, as quoted by Englert). Political scientist, UBC; the publisher identifies him as Yellowknives Dene. Book unread; Wikidata Q91710018; a person entry is deferred to wave 2.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"glen_coulthard"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","new":"'Refusal' as 'an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society'; consent as 'a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession' (2017, abstract read); Mohawk Interruptus (2014) known through its publisher page (nested sovereignty, ethnographic refusal, prizes 2014–15). Anthropologist, Columbia; Wikidata Q112536616 describes her as a Kahnawà:ke Mohawk anthropologist; a person entry is deferred to wave 2.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"audra_simpson"}
{"node":"settler_colonialism","field":"context","new":"'Decolonization brings about the repatriation of Indigenous land and life; it is not a metaphor'; adopts Wolfe's 'structure and not an event' (1999) and his 2006 account of opposed racialisations; 'settler moves to innocence'; 'an ethic of incommensurability' (2012, read). SUNY New Paltz on the article, Toronto per Wikidata (Q28924966). The article's note 1 describes its authors as 'an Indigenous scholar and a settler/trespasser/scholar writing together'; no further identity assertion.","reason":"Period-4 wave 1, 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md) P.3: roster row amended from the texts read.","person_id":"eve_tuck"}
