/bin/bash -lc "python3 - <<'PY'
import json
p=json.load(open('data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/release/editorial-additions.json'));g=json.load(open('historiography-1920-2000.json'))
for s in g['sources']:
 if s['id'] in ['screq_greer_2019_jcha','screq_greer_2019_wmq','screq_ostler_shoemaker_2019','screq_shoemaker_2019']:print(s)
for e in g['edges']:
 if e['id'] in ['edge_111','edge_911','edge_112','edge_386','edge_417','edge_882']:print(e)
PY" in /home/jic823/historiography
 succeeded in 236ms:
{'id': 'screq_greer_2019_jcha', 'title': 'Allan Greer, "Settler Colonialism and Beyond," Journal of the Canadian Historical Association / Revue de la Société historique du Canada 30, no. 1 (2019): 61–86.', 'url': 'https://doi.org/10.7202/1070631ar', 'citation': 'Allan Greer, "Settler Colonialism and Beyond," Journal of the Canadian Historical Association / Revue de la Société historique du Canada 30, no. 1 (2019): 61–86.', 'verification': {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Abstract read: the settler approach has much to offer, but across 500 years of invading Indigenous North America the settler variety dominates some periods and regions and fades in others. 'Imperial/Commercial Penetration' and 'Extractivism' are other modes; extractivism has become predominant in recent decades. Addendum 2026-10-14: Read in full for the 1.139 Allan Greer person entry (Érudit PDF served to an ordinary request; text layer; folios 61–79, notes 80–86). Based on a keynote address to the CHA annual meeting (first-page note). Passages used: settler colonial theory 'does indeed have much to offer historians' but 'the settler variety stands out as a dominant force in some periods and regions, but it fades to insignificance in others' (p. 62); 'The late Patrick Wolfe, still the most important theorist of settler colonialism' (p. 62); the hazard of 'temporal flattening' 'when settler colonialism is casually invoked in ways that suggest a transhistorical, always-already quality, as though the settler-colonial condition had no discernible beginning and as if it could never end' (p. 63); 'Let me assure you that I am not here to trash or dismiss settler-colonial theory. I personally think it has a great deal to offer anyone trying to understand the present and the past of a country such as Canada' (p. 63); historians 'need to take on board the critique of settler-national historiography sketched out by Wolfe and developed more fully by others such as Lorenzo Veracini' (p. 63); 'However, that doesn't mean we need to find settler colonialism everywhere and all the time' (p. 63); 'My objective is to try to historicize settler colonialism' (p. 63); 'Imperial/Commercial Penetration' and 'Extractivism' as 'coexisting and overlapping versions of colonialism' (p. 64); early settlements 'hardly functioned as instruments of elimination and replacement except in quite limited, localized ways' (p. 64); settler colonization 'emerged as an important independent force' only from the last quarter of the eighteenth century (p. 70); 'The time may be right, then, for a renewed history of the Confederation era, one that unites the history of state formation with that of settler colonialism' (p. 73), with n. 41: 'A prime example of this problem would be Colonial Leviathan; devoted to questions of state formation, it neglected everything to do with Indigenous peoples and processes of elimination' (p. 84); 'To those who insist that Canada is, was, and always has been a settler-colonial phenomenon, I offer qualified support' (p. 73); 'settlers as such are no longer the main threat, for the invasion has adopted a different guise' (p. 74); 'Settler colonialism stands out also as a fundamental force behind the creation of a territorial nation-state' and 'An order based on territorial dispossession is not, and never has been, normal, natural, and inevitable' (p. 79). The source's page range 61–86 includes the notes."}}
{'id': 'screq_greer_2019_wmq', 'title': 'Allan Greer, "Settler Colonialism and Empire in Early America," William and Mary Quarterly 76, no. 3 (2019): 383–390.', 'url': 'https://doi.org/10.5309/willmaryquar.76.3.0383', 'citation': 'Allan Greer, "Settler Colonialism and Empire in Early America," William and Mary Quarterly 76, no. 3 (2019): 383–390.', 'verification': {'status': 'bibliographic_metadata_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-12', 'note': "Opening read (Australia from 1788 under modern conceptions of territory and property). The full argument is unread; the page range is from the forum's pagination and should be confirmed."}}
{'id': 'screq_ostler_shoemaker_2019', 'title': 'Jeffrey Ostler and Nancy Shoemaker, "Settler Colonialism in Early American History: Introduction," William and Mary Quarterly 76, no. 3 (2019): 361–368.', 'url': 'https://doi.org/10.5309/willmaryquar.76.3.0361', 'citation': 'Jeffrey Ostler and Nancy Shoemaker, "Settler Colonialism in Early American History: Introduction," William and Mary Quarterly 76, no. 3 (2019): 361–368.', 'verification': {'status': 'bibliographic_metadata_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-12', 'note': 'The forum includes Glassburn, Greer, Miles, Ostler, Romney, Shoemaker, Smallwood, Spear, Truett and Witgen. The end page is to confirm.'}}
{'id': 'screq_shoemaker_2019', 'title': 'Nancy Shoemaker, "Settler Colonialism: Universal Theory or English Heritage?" William and Mary Quarterly 76, no. 3 (2019): 369–374.', 'url': 'https://doi.org/10.5309/willmaryquar.76.3.0369', 'citation': 'Nancy Shoemaker, "Settler Colonialism: Universal Theory or English Heritage?" William and Mary Quarterly 76, no. 3 (2019): 369–374.', 'verification': {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-12', 'note': 'Opening read: if settler colonialism is a universal theory, why do studies concentrate on former British colonies in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries? She proposes testing it as an early modern English ideology of expansion. The end page is to confirm.'}}
{'id': 'edge_111', 'source': 'annales', 'target': 'environment', 'relationship': 'Annales geography and multiple time scales as a source of ideas and a temporal affinity for environmental history, acknowledged by Worster (1990) and Cronon (1993); McNeill (2003) later reassessed the Annales treatment of environmental change while crediting their general approach as inspirational', 'type': 'connection', 'relationship_kind': 'influence', 'directed': True, 'source_ids': ['pe131_worster_jah_1990', 'pe131_worster_wealth_1993', 'pe131_cronon_uses_1993', 'pe131_extras_mcneill_2003', 'gurevich_annales'], 'evidence_note': "In-period acknowledgements first. Donald Worster, 'Transformations of the Earth' (JAH 1990), note 5. Witnesses: the Portuguese translation (Ambiente & Sociedade 5:2/6:1, 2002–03, p. 42, n. 5; PDF read in full) and Internet Archive full-text-search snippets of Worster's 1993 reprint in The Wealth of Nature (ch. 4, n. 5; p. 226 inferred from the running heads on either side; page images not seen; whether the reprint revised the note was not checked). The English JAH page was not reached. Because the English wording is known only from joined search snippets, it is given here as paraphrase: after reporting White's view that frontier and western history had been the formative influence on the field, Worster names French historians and geographers associated with Annales, particularly Braudel, Febvre and Le Roy Ladurie, as a further important source of ideas, distinct from the Turnerian tradition. The translation reads: 'Uma outra fonte de idéias importante, realmente retirada da influência de Frederick Jackson Turner, tem sido a dos historiadores e geógrafos franceses, particularmente Fernand Braudel, Lucien Febvre e Emmanuel Le Roy Ladurie, todos associados à revista dos Annales' (p. 42, n. 5). William Cronon, 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993; OCR of the author-hosted page scan, journal pagination), p. 14: 'Perhaps this is why we feel some kinship with a Braudelian vision of history in which the different time scales of la longue durée, la vie matérielle, and l'histoire événementielle weave together to form the tapestry of the past. Although our general bias is often toward the longue durée, we understand that the interactions of environment, economy, political institutions, social norms, cultural values, and natural processes are endlessly complex' (n. 29 cites The Mediterranean). Both acknowledge a source of ideas and a kinship, not a lineage, and neither disputes it. Later reading, not evidence for the 1990–93 claim: J. R. McNeill (2003; text-layer PDF, p. 14 re-read) reassesses the Annales treatment of environmental change, writing that 'the Annales historians were, many of them, strangely blind to evolving environments'; Braudel 'normally imagined it as an unchanging force', while Le Roy Ladurie 'embraced environmental change'; 'To a very limited extent, then, Annales historians were engaged in environmental history avant la lettre'; yet 'Their general approach however proved inspirational for many who would become environmental historians'. Gurevich's chapter (publisher abstract only) supports the Annales side: geography among the school's concerns; it says nothing about environmental history. Earlier wording (an undirected comparison of affinities) is replaced because Worster and Cronon describe influence running from the Annales to the field, not a comparison between equals.", 'map_label': 'Annales sources and temporal affinities, 1990–93', 'classification_basis': 'editorial_review'}
{'id': 'edge_112', 'source': 'environment', 'target': 'global', 'relationship': 'Crosby and Melville connect introduced organisms with colonial expansion; Grove traces conservation through colonial networks', 'type': 'connection', 'relationship_kind': 'contribution', 'directed': True, 'source_ids': ['global', 'cronon', 'crosby_ecological', 'melville_plague', 'grove_green', 'subrahmanyam_connected'], 'evidence_note': 'Ecological change and cross-border material relations provide one route beyond national containers; examples require attention to scale. Crosby’s temperate regions, Melville’s Mexican case and Grove’s island networks operate at different scales. Their books provide resources for histories across borders; this does not prove that all global historians adopted them. Subrahmanyam738n10 explicitly cites Crosby and Grove, adding named primary reception for this route; it does not establish every proposed Melville reception.', 'classification_basis': 'editorial_review', 'map_label': 'Organisms, conquest and colonial networks'}
{'id': 'edge_386', 'source': 'post', 'target': 'foucault', 'type': 'critique', 'directed': True, 'relationship_kind': 'critique', 'relationship': "Stoler questions the marginal place of race and empire in Foucault's history of sexuality while using his 1976 lectures (1995)", 'evidence_note': "The checked publisher description of Race and the Education of Desire (1995) records a critique of the marginal place of empire, and of race in the making of bourgeois sexuality, in Foucault alongside productive use of his 1976 lectures and Dutch and French colonial archives; the book itself is unread. Spivak's critique of Foucault is the separate edge from her person entry.", 'source_ids': ['stoler_desire'], 'map_label': 'Stoler: race, sexuality and empire in Foucault, 1995', 'classification_basis': 'editorial_review'}
{'id': 'edge_417', 'source': 'post', 'target': 'queer', 'relationship': 'Stoler investigates sexuality and racial formation through colonial archives', 'type': 'connection', 'relationship_kind': 'contribution', 'directed': True, 'source_ids': ['stoler_desire'], 'evidence_note': 'The 1995 project both uses and questions Foucault. This contribution is the specific colonial-historical intervention described by the publisher, not the origin of queer history or proof that all queer historians adopted it.', 'classification_basis': 'editorial_review', 'map_label': 'Colonial archives and sexual categories'}
{'id': 'edge_882', 'source': 'post', 'target': 'settler_colonialism', 'relationship': 'Colonial and settler colonial phenomena distinguished', 'type': 'connection', 'relationship_kind': 'comparison', 'directed': False, 'source_ids': ['screq_veracini_2011', 'screq_hoxie_2008'], 'evidence_note': "Veracini argues that colonial and settler colonial phenomena must be analytically disentangled and read in dialectical relation. Hoxie presents settler colonialism as the most promising aspect of a postcolonial critique. Alternative for review: a directed contribution post → Settler colonialism on Hoxie's account. PhD context: 32 of 86 core-citing 2009–2026 PhDs also engage the 'Empire, colonialism & postcolonial theory' co-citation field (37% vs 24% of all; lift 1.58). Bibliographies record consultation, not influence.", 'classification_basis': 'editorial_review', 'map_label': 'Postcolonial critique and settler colonialism', 'proposal_id': 'screq_rel_03'}
{'id': 'edge_911', 'source': 'aldo_leopold', 'target': 'environment', 'relationship': "Environmental historians cite Leopold's call for 'an ecological interpretation of history' (1949) as a precedent for allying history with ecology, and his essays as a moral and literary voice", 'type': 'connection', 'relationship_kind': 'influence', 'directed': True, 'source_ids': ['leopold_almanac', 'pe131_worster_ends_1988', 'pe131_worster_jah_1990', 'pe131_cronon_uses_1993', 'cronon_environment_syllabus_1994', 'pe131_extras_nash_1967', 'cronon_changes_afterword', 'pe131_leopold_wynn_2001'], 'evidence_note': "Leopold's text, 'The Land Ethic', A Sand County Almanac (1949), read in two unpaginated transcriptions: 'That man is, in fact, only a member of a biotic team is shown by an ecological interpretation of history. Many historical events, hitherto explained solely in terms of human enterprise, were actually biotic interactions between people and land. The characteristics of the land determined the facts quite as potently as the characteristics of the men who lived on it.' After the Kentucky example: 'Is history taught in this spirit? It will be, once the concept of land as a community really penetrates our intellectual life.' Worster (1990, n. 1) and Wynn (2001, n. 55) cite the history passage at p. 205 of the 1949 edition; a paginated 1949 copy was not seen. In-period readings by historians. (1) Worster, 'Doing Environmental History' (1988), abridged reprint read (Merchant reader, 2012, pp. 2–9; reprint pages): 'Take away plant ecology and environmental history loses its foundation, its coherence, its first step' (p. 5); some scholars speak of 'ecological history' or 'historical ecology', meaning 'to insist on a tighter alliance with the science. Some years back the scientist and conservationist Aldo Leopold projected such an alliance when he spoke of “an ecological interpretation of history.”' (pp. 5–6). He then retells the Kentucky example and quotes Leopold's question about 'the plant succession inherent in this dark and bloody ground' (p. 6). (2) Worster, 'Transformations of the Earth', JAH 76 (1990), read only in Portuguese translation (Ambiente & Sociedade, 2002–03): the essay opens with Leopold, who 'reivindicou uma “interpretação ecológica da História”', and continues 'Levou algum tempo para os historiadores darem atenção ao conselho de Leopold, mas finalmente o campo da história ambiental começou a ganhar forma' (p. 23; gloss: it took historians some time to heed Leopold's advice, but the field of environmental history finally began to take shape). The English wording is unchecked. (3) Cronon, 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993), read in full: 'Environmental history tries to reconstruct the endless layers of change that we and the earth have traced upon each other. It is the history recorded in Aldo Leopold’s tree rings, the history recorded by the marks of his saw upon the good oak as he cut it down' (p. 19; n. 32, p. 22: 'The echo here is that of Leopold’s “Good Oak,” Sand County Almanac, 6-18'); n. 11 (p. 21): 'At this and several other points in this essay, I trust that readers will hear my echoes of Aldo Leopold's Sand County Almanac'. (4) Cronon's History 901 syllabus (Fall 1994) assigns 'Good Oak' in the session 'Reading the Landscape, Part I' (p. 4) and 'Thinking Like a Mountain' with Part IV, 'The Upshot', beside Nash's Wilderness and the American Mind (p. 6). (5) Nash's Wilderness and the American Mind (1967), checked at contents level only, gives him a chapter, 'Aldo Leopold: Prophet'. Bounded: Worster dates 'the idea of environmental history' to the 1970s and the environmental movement (1988, reprint p. 3), and in 1990 warns that the Kentucky facts need more than 'uma forma simples de determinismo ambiental' (p. 24; gloss: a simple form of environmental determinism). Later reading, not evidence for this edge: Cronon's 2003 afterword (pp. 174–175) recalls 'reading the landscape' workshops 'modeled on a famous course taught by Aldo Leopold as early as the 1930s' and calls the Almanac 'no less a meditation on the history of human interactions with the American land'.", 'classification_basis': 'editorial_review', 'map_label': 'Ecological interpretation of history, 1949', 'proposal_id': 'pe131_leopold_rel_01'}

exec
/bin/bash -lc "python3 -B - <<'PY'
import json
from scripts.apply_person_entries import Batch
b=Batch('data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23');p=json.load(open(b.packet));g=b.transform(json.load(open('historiography-1920-2000.json')),p)
for n in g['nodes']:
 if n['id']=='michel_rolph_trouillot':
  for e in n['chronology']['events'][:18]:print(e['id'],e['date'],e['label'],e['source_ids'],e['support'])
print('MULTI_SOURCE_EVENTS')
