p4b_daschuk_urp_2019 https://uofrpress.ca/Books/C/Clearing-the-Plains bibliographic_metadata_checked Publisher page read on 2026-10-23: contents (an "Introduction to the 2013 Edition" and to the 2019 edition; critical responses by Kelm, Mosby, Neylan and Woolford); prizes 2014 (Governor General's History Award, Clio, CHA Best Scholarly Book, Aboriginal History Prize, Macdonald Prize). The description (fetch summary) speaks of "a policy of starvation" and says the book "sparked widespread debate about genocide in Canada"; it does not use "settler colonialism" or "colonialism". Book unread.
p4b_edmonds_carey_2013 https://doi.org/10.1080/18380743.2013.771761 bibliographic_metadata_checked Crossref record (February 2013); unread. Cited by Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014, p. 9 (read), for the journal's move from a volunteer-run open-access format to Taylor & Francis.
p4b_englert_2020 https://doi.org/10.1111/anti.12659 supporting_page_checked Open access (Wiley). Read in full on 2026-10-23 in the Internet Archive capture of 21 August 2023 of the publisher's full-text page (the live page returned 403); unpaginated in the capture. Passages: "a critique of the Wolfe-an model, which has become so dominant within contemporary Settler Colonial Studies"; Veracini "his self-styled intellectual successor"; Wolfe 1999, p. 3 and 2006, p. 393 quoted as "a foundational definition"; "Wolfe and Veracini draw a sharp analytical separation, between settler colonies as eliminatory formations and franchise colonies as exploitative ones"; Veracini 2010, p. 34 and 2015, pp. 6, 26–27, 40, 93 quoted; Coulthard 2014, p. 125 quoted ("territorially acquisitive in perpetuity"); conclusion: elimination "does not accurately define the overall logic of settler colonialism, as claimed by Wolfe"; accumulation by dispossession "carried out by all classes of settlers, including by workers".
p4b_goeman_2016 https://doi.org/10.17953/aicrj.40.1.goeman supporting_page_checked Read in full (eScholarship PDF, CC BY-NC 4.0; also published in Amerasia Journal 42:1, 2016). Passages: "Patrick Wolfe's sudden passing in the winter of 2016 stunned us all in Native American and Indigenous studies"; why his concept travelled "when other texts and similar concepts had preceded his important book": "the readability of his article and the geographic breadth of his work"; "a key scholar in conceiving of a settler-colonial analytic in relation to Indigenous thinking and scholarship that exists far longer than settler nations"; "He critically engaged our field in a respectful and nuanced manner"; Simpson's recollection of his Columbia seminar visit; note 6: The Settler Complex (UCLA AISC 2016) and Traces of History (Verso 2016).
p4b_hugill_2017 https://doi.org/10.1111/gec3.12315 supporting_page_checked Abstract read (Crossref, May 2017): the article "seeks to answer these questions by attempting to urbanize recent insights in settler-colonial theory" for "societies where colonists have 'come to stay' and no formal process of decolonization has unfolded". Body unread.
p4b_jafri_2017 https://doi.org/10.25158/l6.1.7 bibliographic_metadata_checked Crossref record (May 2017); the editors' note on Kauanui's 2016 page names the responses; unread.
p4b_kelley_2017 https://doi.org/10.1353/aq.2017.0020 bibliographic_metadata_checked Crossref record; unread; its argument (settler colonies built on the exploitation of Indigenous labour, South Africa) is known through Englert 2020 (read).
p4b_kelm_2015_jcha https://id.erudit.org/iderudit/1037223ar not_checked Not read: Érudit returned a bot challenge on 2026-10-23 for both the PDF and HTML routes. Its placement of the book within settler colonialism is known only from a search summary and is not relied on.
p4b_lloyd_wolfe_2015 https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2015.1035361 bibliographic_metadata_checked Crossref record (online 7 May 2015; volume 6, issue 2); unread.
p4b_macoun_strakosch_2013_qut https://eprints.qut.edu.au/220228/ supporting_page_checked Author's version (20 pp., unpaginated against the published version) read on 2026-10-23: abstract, introduction, "The strengths of SCT", the critique section, the governmentality analogy and the conclusion; the Northern Territory intervention material skimmed. Passages: "building on the theoretical contributions of Patrick Wolfe and Lorenzo Veracini"; SCT "remains a largely White attempt to think through contemporary colonial relationships"; "can depict colonization as structurally inevitable"; "Foundational scholar Patrick Wolfe has been labelled 'very much a structuralist stuck in a poststructuralist world'" (their note 63; the labeller not identified in the text read); "a kind of colonial fatalism"; "it does not account for Indigenous lives"; "the fact that settler colonialism struggles to narrate its own ending does not mean that it cannot end"; Rowse and Joanne Barker as cited by them.
