p4b_russell_verso_2022 https://www.versobooks.com/blogs/news/5444-patrick-wolfe-treading-carefully-but-defiantly supporting_page_checked Publisher blog page read through a fetch summary (not verbatim) on 2026-10-23: Wolfe supervised Russell's doctorate; his work "drew heavily on research and activist work by Indigenous people"; phrases "as a structure and not an event" and "trod carefully but defiantly"; she quotes Birch's obituary ("an openly emotional thinker"); the book's imprint date is not on the page (blog date 2022).
p4b_simpson_ruse_2017 https://doi.org/10.1080/13688790.2017.1334283 supporting_page_checked Abstract read on 2026-10-23 in the Internet Archive capture of 19 July 2025 of the publisher's page (the live page returned 403; online 6 June 2017; Columbia University): "This article takes the notion of 'refusal' to be an alternative to recognition politics in settler colonial society"; "'consent' operates as a technique of recognition and simultaneous dispossession"; Indigenous life "refused, did not consent to, and still refuses to be folded into a larger encompassing colonising and settler colonial narratives of acceptance". Body unread.
p4b_smithers_2017_ahs https://doi.org/10.1080/1031461x.2016.1273061 bibliographic_metadata_checked Crossref record (2 January 2017); unread.
p4b_stanger_ross_2020 http://www.mqup.ca/isbn/9780228001720 bibliographic_metadata_checked Publisher page read (August 2020; contents with contributors; Saywell Prize 2022; Basil Stuart-Stubbs Prize shortlist 2021). The description (fetch summary) reframes the internment around "deliberate and permanent destruction of home" through dispossession and does not use "settler" or "colonial"; an H-Net review quoted on the page calls Canada a "settler colonial state". Unread.
p4b_trc_summary_2015 https://nctr.ca/records/reports/ supporting_page_checked Imprint page and pp. 1–3 read on 2026-10-23 in the PDF served from the National Centre for Truth and Reconciliation's reports page ("This report is in the public domain"). p. 1: "the central goals of Canada's Aboriginal policy were to eliminate Aboriginal governments; ignore Aboriginal rights; terminate the Treaties; and, through a process of assimilation, cause Aboriginal peoples to cease to exist as distinct legal, social, cultural, religious, and racial entities in Canada. The establishment and operation of residential schools were a central element of this policy, which can best be described as 'cultural genocide.'" p. 3: "The Canadian government pursued this policy of cultural genocide because it wished to divest itself of its legal and financial obligations to Aboriginal people and gain control over their land and resources." The rest unread; the pages read do not cite Wolfe.
p4b_veracini_2011_deposit https://doi.org/10.25916/sut.26246930.v1 supporting_page_checked Read in full on 2026-10-23 (the journal's own 2011 PDF, "© settler colonial studies 1 (2011), ISSN 1838-0743", deposited by the author under CC BY-NC-ND 3.0; handle 1959.3/156165). Passages: "you, work for me" against "you, go away" (p. 1); "settler colonialism, by contrast, extinguishes itself" and "the structure persistently pursues a specific end point" (p. 3, note 11 citing Wolfe 1999, p. 163); postcolonial studies "failed to detect the settler colonial 'situation' in its specific operation" (p. 5); "Decolonisation is not settler decolonisation" (p. 5); "impervious to regime change" quoted from Wolfe (p. 5); "settler colonialism ends with an indigenous ultimate permanence" (p. 7); the politics of recognition "cannot be considered a genuinely decolonising move" (p. 8); "Reconciliation should be a practice and not a process" (p. 9); ARC grant DP0986984 (acknowledgements).
p4b_veracini_2013_deposit https://doi.org/10.25916/sut.26215358.v1 supporting_page_checked Author's accepted manuscript (handle 1959.3/353066; "posted here with the permission of the publisher") read in its abstract, opening and concluding section ("Colonialism within 'Settler Colonialism': Settler Colonial Studies") on 2026-10-23; the middle sections skimmed; unpaginated against the published version. Passages: "ethnohistory, anthropology, indigenous studies, and especially history became crucial disciplinary sites in the development of 'settler colonialism' as a conceptual category"; "both the Canadian and US historiographies were late in adopting settler colonialism as a category of analysis" (note 52); the settler-capitalism literature wrote "indigenous people and indigenous history off the conceptual map"; consolidation "beginning from approximately the mid 1990s" (abstract).
p4b_veracini_wolfe_obituary_2016 https://doi.org/10.1080/2201473x.2016.1176393 supporting_page_checked Read in full on 2026-10-23 in the Internet Archive capture of 26 March 2024 of the publisher's page (http://web.archive.org/web/20240326050636/https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/2201473X.2016.1176393; the live page returned 403). Crossref: online 10 May 2016. Passages: "fellowships at Harvard and Stanford"; "Dialectics and binaries … worked for him"; "still a Marxist" of "the young Marx"; the 1999 book "ostensibly a history of Australian anthropology", "settler colonialism" "only added at the very end and at the request of the publisher"; "No one had theorised it before and in a systematic way"; "practically established what is now a burgeoning scholarly subfield"; "kick started a scholarly field in a fit of absentmindedness. Or did he?"
p4b_vu_libguide_wolfe https://libraryguides.vu.edu.au/VUSpecialCollections/PatrickWolfe supporting_page_checked Page read on 2026-10-23: born 1949, died 2016; works list including Sovereignty: Frontiers of Possibility (2013, ed. with Evans, Genovese and Reilly, University of Hawai'i Press) and a chapter in Stolen Lands, Broken Cultures: The Settler-Colonial Present (2012, Arena); "In Memoriam Patrick Wolfe 1949–2016" section in Aboriginal History 40 (2016).
p4b_vu_wolfe_collection https://www.vu.edu.au/library/about-the-library/special-collections-archives/patrick-wolfe-collection supporting_page_checked Page read on 2026-10-23: "Patrick Wolfe was a historian and scholar in the field of settler colonial studies"; "He held research and teaching positions at several Australian universities including Victoria University, University of Melbourne and La Trobe University"; "passed away in 2016"; his personal library of about 1,400 books. No birth date, roles or years.
