environment roster james_daschuk Disease, climate and 'Canadian politics' in the loss of Indigenous life on the Plains (2013; GG History Award, Macdonald and Clio prizes); blurbed by McNeill; in Canadian PhD bibliographies read beside the environmental founders from 2015 and with Wolfe after 2020 (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23). The settler-colonial reading is on settler_colonialism.
settler_colonialism roster james_daschuk Disease, starvation policy and the loss of Indigenous life on the Plains; the work most co-cited with Wolfe and Veracini by Canadian PhDs. The publisher's description speaks of 'a policy of starvation' and a 'debate about genocide', not of settler colonialism; whether the book uses the framework is unchecked (2019 edition with responses by Kelm, Mosby, Neylan and Woolford).
settler_colonialism roster james_rodger_miller Treaty-making in Canada (2009); unread; a distinctive riser among Canadian PhDs inside the Indigenous family (History PhD citation graph, release 2026-10-23); no use of the frame attested.
archival_turn description A way of reading that takes the archive as an object of study rather than a quarry of facts. Trouillot's Silencing the Past (1995) located the silences of history in the making of sources, archives, narratives and 'history in the final instance', and made the Haitian Revolution the case of an event 'unthinkable' as it happened. Stoler asked colonial historians to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject' (2002) and to read 'along the archival grain' before reading against it, finding in Dutch colonial records a 'repository of good taste and bad faith' (2009, as quoted by Gouda). Farge's Le goût de l'archive (1989), Derrida's Archive Fever (1994) and Steedman's Dust (2001) wrote the archive's allure, fever and dust; Schwartz and Cook, from the archival profession, called archives 'active sites where social power is negotiated' (2002); Burton's Archive Stories (2005) collected historians' encounters with their sources; Hartman's 'critical fabulation' (2008) and Fuentes (2016) confronted the violence of slavery's archive. No opposing side formed: the History PhD citation graph finds Stoler the strongest riser of 2015–25 and the cluster a framework without a debate.
EDGE p4A_rel_10 evidence_note Springer abstract of the Savage Slot chapter (read) and the Bulamah, Goyatá and Pereira reading (para. 5, read); Duke description of Haunted by Empire (read). The dissertation finding that Along the Archival Grain entered the global/transnational neighbourhood of PhDs in 2020–25 is kept in the scope note, not here.
EDGE p4B_rel_16 evidence_note Wolfe 2006 (read in full), fourth section: "Of itself, however, modernity cannot explain the insatiable dynamic whereby settler colonialism always needs more land. The answer that springs most readily to mind is agriculture"; "Agriculture not only supports the other sectors. It is inherently sedentary and, therefore, permanent"; "The reproach of nomadism renders the native removable." Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014, p. 30 (read): "land is the irreducible element of settler colonialism, and … environmental degradation has often proceeded through and in support of settler colonialism". Tuck and Yang 2012, p. 5 (read): "Within settler colonialism, the most important concern is land/water/air/subterranean earth". The dissertation evidence for the pairing is in the entry's scope_note, not here. Drawn by Agent B by the coordinator's decision; Agent C does not duplicate it. (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md)
EDGE ('patrick_wolfe', 'settler_colonialism') evidence_note Revision of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_01): the 2006 essay, read in full in the Internet Archive capture of the journal's free-to-read page (screq_wolfe_2006 addendum), joins the 1999 introduction (pp. 1–2, read earlier: settler colonies 'were (are) premised on the elimination of native societies'; 'invasion is a structure not an event'; contents and catalogue checked). Wolfe 2006, in his words: 'Settler colonialism is inherently eliminatory but not invariably genocidal' (p. 387, pages per Kauanui 2016); 'Territoriality is settler colonialism's specific, irreducible element'; 'settler colonizers come to stay: invasion is a structure not an event' (p. 388; his note 7 refers the formula to the 1999 book, p. 2, and to 'Nation and MiscegeNation', 1994, p. 96); 'Settler colonialism destroys to replace'; 'elimination refers to more than the summary liquidation of Indigenous people, though it includes that' (p. 390); 'settler colonialism is relatively impervious to regime change'; 'structural genocide' (p. 403). A contribution, not a founding: earlier settler-society studies (1983, 1995) and earlier usage (Veracini 2013; Kauanui 2016, note 7: Sayegh 1965, Rodinson 1973) precede it, and Kauanui records that Wolfe 'insisted … that he did not create the field of settler colonial studies—that Native scholars did'. The earlier note's Canadian PhD counts are moved to the entry's scope_note (user rule, 2026-10-23); bibliographies record consultation, not influence.
EDGE ('post', 'settler_colonialism') evidence_note Revision of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_11): the arrow changes from an undirected comparison to a critique from the field toward the umbrella, in three read texts' own words. Veracini 2011, p. 5 (read in full): 'colonial, imperial and postcolonial studies have primarily focused on something else … these scholarly literatures have ultimately failed to detect the settler colonial "situation" in its specific operation'; p. 1: 'colonial and settler colonial phenomena be analytically disentangled'. Tuck and Yang 2012, p. 5 (read): 'relying solely on postcolonial literatures or theories of coloniality that ignore settler colonialism will not help to envision the shape that decolonization must take in settler colonial contexts'. Macoun and Strakosch 2013 (author version, read), introduction: 'For many decades, postcolonial theory has shaped global scholarship of colonialism, and this has tended to obscure the ongoing hierarchies of settler states'; 'postcolonial does not "mean the same thing as post-settler colonial"'. Counter-reading kept: Hoxie 2008 (abstract) presents settler colonialism as the most promising aspect of a postcolonial critique. The earlier note's Canadian PhD counts are moved to the entry's scope_note.
EDGE ('indigenous', 'settler_colonialism') evidence_note Konishi surveys Indigenous scholars who advocate, interrogate and challenge settler colonial studies (abstract). Revision of 2026-10-23 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-settler-colonial.md P.6, p4B_rel_12) adds the read words on both sides. Kauanui 2016 (read in full): 'Settler Colonial Studies does not, should not, and cannot replace Indigenous Studies'; Wolfe 'insisted … that Native scholars did' create the field; indigeneity 'a category of analysis that is distinct from race, ethnicity, and nationality'. Snelgrove, Dhamoon and Corntassel 2014, pp. 9–11 (read): the field 'can displace, overshadow, or even mask over Indigenous studies'; 'when settlers take up these questions, it's suddenly considered a legitimate field of study'. Goeman 2016 (read in full): Wolfe's work 'became a place to engage Indigenous studies concerns in relation to settler colonial studies that at times leaves out Indigenous epistemologies and ontologies'. Veracini 2013 (author manuscript, read): 'indigenous studies, and especially history became crucial disciplinary sites in the development of "settler colonialism" as a conceptual category'. Comparison only: Indigenous history is not a subset of the paradigm, and the paradigm did not originate there. The earlier note's Canadian PhD counts are moved to the entry's scope_note.
EDGE pe131_cronon_rel_01 evidence_note Cronon’s own in-period statements. Changes in the Land (1983), ch. 1 'The View from Walden', read in full in an unpaginated web transcription (page numbers not verified): 'The replacement of Indians by predominantly European populations in New England was as much an ecological as a cultural revolution'; 'An ecological history begins by assuming a dynamic and changing relationship between environment and culture, one as apt to produce contradictions as continuities. Moreover, it assumes that the interactions of the two are dialectical'; 'The choice is not between two landscapes, one with and one without a human influence; it is between two human ways of living, two ways of belonging to an ecosystem'; 'The pig was not merely a pig but a creature bound among other things to the fence, the dandelion, and a very special definition of property'; a 'world capitalist system' draws people 'into trade and market relations which lie well beyond the boundaries of their local ecosystems'. He sets this against species-transfer accounts 'as Alfred Crosby and William H. McNeill have done', which are 'bound to be incomplete', and against functionalist ecological anthropology, which risks 'a static and ahistorical view'. In 1992 he calls Nature’s Metropolis 'a natural sequel' to that book, which 'described a comparable set of environmental changes that occurred in New England as that region became linked to London’s markets' (Annals of Iowa forum, p. 517). 'The Uses of Environmental History' (1993): the lesson 'When people buy and sell things in a market, they link together ecosystems and encourage change' is 'a central concern of my own work' (p. 8, n. 14, citing Changes in the Land, Nature’s Metropolis and 'Kennecott Journey'); 'The chief moral of my own version of environmental history is the one I tried to embed in the title of my book Changes in the Land. To live as human beings on this planet is to change the world around us' (p. 19); 'the material history of environmental change is simultaneously a spiritual history of human consciousness and a political economic history of human society' (p. 19); 'All environmental knowledge is culturally constructed and historically contingent—including our own' (p. 14), held together with the claim that 'the natural world exists quite apart from what we believe about it' (p. 15). 'Kennecott Journey' (1992, p. 33): 'The special task of environmental historians is to tell stories that carry us back and forth across the boundary between people and nature to reveal just how culturally constructed that boundary is—and how dependent upon natural systems it remains.' 'The Trouble with Wilderness' (1995, author’s reflow p. 1): wilderness is 'quite profoundly a human creation'. A contribution, not a founding: he makes the claim for materialist analysis that tries 'to finesse a full-blown determinism' on behalf of the field ('One important contribution of environmental history', 1993, p. 13), and places the field among the 'new' histories of the 1960s (p. 2). Contested in period: Danbom (1992) reads Nature’s Metropolis for its 'environmental moralism' (p. 503) and as 'a book about evil unleavened by good' (p. 506); Cronon answers 'I see no pure heroes or pure villains in the stories I tell' (p. 522). His 1990 JAH response to Worster is unread. The 2003 afterword (pp. 182–185: property analysis learned from anthropology, economics and legal theory; his own criticism of schematic capitalism and source asymmetry) is a later reading. edge_342 (anthropology → environment) and edge_343 (environment → culture) stay: they record anthropology as a resource and environmental history’s contribution to cultural history; this arrow is his own framework. Period-4 wave 1 (data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-environment.md) (edge_901 revision): CV pp. 2, 14–15 read in full; the AHA's open text of 'Storytelling' read in passages.

