representative_people [{"person_id": "edward_said", "role": "contributor", "context": "Studies imperial authority through texts and institutions, combining discourse analysis with hegemony and attention to particular writers.", "works": "Orientalism (1978)", "source_ids": ["said_orientalism"], "basis": "source_review", "edge_id": "edge_968"}, {"person_id": "gayatri_chakravorty_spivak", "role": "contributor", "context": "Questions intellectual representation through colonialism, labor and gender; uses deconstruction critically.", "works": "“Can the Subaltern Speak?” (1988); A Critique of Postcolonial Reason (1999)", "source_ids": ["spivak_speak", "pe137_spivak_cpr_1999"], "basis": "source_review", "edge_id": "edge_983"}, {"person_id": "dipesh_chakrabarty", "role": "historian", "context": "Tests European social-science categories against South Asian political histories while retaining their critical uses.", "works": "Provincializing Europe (2000); edition with a new preface responding to critics (2007); “In Defense of Provincializing Europe” (2008)", "source_ids": ["post", "w3_chak_pe_2007_edition", "w3_chak_reply_dietze_2008"], "basis": "source_review", "edge_id": "edge_914"}, {"person_id": "partha_chatterjee", "role": "contributor", "context": "Develops colonial historical difference through an explicit disagreement with Anderson.", "works": "Nationalist Thought and the Colonial World (1986); The Nation and Its Fragments (1993)", "source_ids": ["chatterjee", "chatterjee_fragments"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "homi_k_bhabha", "role": "contributor", "context": "Examines ambivalence, mimicry and hybrid cultural production; these concepts are debated interpretations.", "works": "The Location of Culture (1994)", "source_ids": ["bhabha_location"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "chandra_talpade_mohanty", "role": "contributor", "context": "Criticizes homogenizing accounts of women and develops historically situated feminist solidarity.", "works": "“Under Western Eyes” (1984; revised 1991)", "source_ids": ["mohanty_western"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "ann_laura_stoler", "role": "contributor", "context": "Reworks Foucault through colonial archives, race and sexuality.", "works": "Race and the Education of Desire (1995)", "source_ids": ["stoler_desire"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "bernard_cohn", "role": "contributor", "context": "Historical anthropology examines the colonial production of authoritative knowledge in British India.", "works": "Colonialism and Its Forms of Knowledge (1996)", "source_ids": ["cohn_knowledge"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "ronald_inden", "role": "historian", "context": "His criticism of idealized and denigrating images of India supplies a specific resource for Guha’s environmental argument. Original article remains to be distinguished from its documented reception.", "works": "“Orientalist Constructions of India” (1986), especially442, through Guha (1989)", "source_ids": ["guha_deep_ecology_1989"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "gyan_prakash", "role": "historian", "context": "Historian whose essays set out postcolonial historiography from Indian historiography and read Subaltern Studies as postcolonial criticism. His Subaltern Studies context remains distinct.", "works": "“Writing Post-Orientalist Histories of the Third World” (1990); “Subaltern Studies as Postcolonial Criticism” (1994)", "source_ids": ["prakash_post_orientalist_1990", "prakash_subaltern_postcolonial_1994"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "stuart_hall", "role": "contributor", "context": "Cultural theorist whose essays on diasporic identity and on the category of the “post-colonial” are standard resources in postcolonial critique. His cultural-studies context remains separate.", "works": "“Cultural Identity and Diaspora” (1990); “When Was ‘the Post-colonial’? Thinking at the Limit” (1996)", "source_ids": ["hall_diaspora", "hall_postcolonial_1996"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "paul_gilroy", "role": "contributor", "context": "The Black Atlantic criticizes national and racial closure and reads modernity through the Black Atlantic; a standard postcolonial and diaspora text for historians. His Atlantic and diaspora contexts remain.", "works": "The Black Atlantic (1993)", "source_ids": ["gilroy"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "frederick_cooper", "role": "contributor", "context": "Coedited Tensions of Empire with Ann Laura Stoler (1997) and coauthored its opening research-agenda chapter. The publisher describes a historical and anthropological inquiry into relations between metropole and colony and changing categories of colonial rule. This contextual inclusion does not identify Cooper as an adherent of every postcolonial theory; his later criticisms require a separate dated claim.", "works": "Tensions of Empire (1997), coedited with Ann Laura Stoler; opening chapter, “Between Metropole and Colony: Rethinking a Research Agenda”", "source_ids": ["cooper_stoler_tensions_1997"], "basis": "source_review"}, {"person_id": "catherine_hall", "role": "historian", "context": "Metropole and colony in one frame: Birmingham abolitionists, Baptist missionaries and Jamaica, 1830–1867; read as postcolonial history by Woollacott (2006).", "works": "Civilising Subjects (2002)", "source_ids": ["w1_woollacott_hall_2006", "w1_hall_civilising_subjects_2002"], "basis": "source_review", "edge_id": "edge_1066"}, {"person_id": "joan_wallach_scott", "role": "historian", "context": "Reads the French headscarf ban and the discourse of secularism as civilizational claims about gender equality, in the accounts of her reviewers Laborde and Whitney and her 2018 interview; Laborde finds the normative premises unclear, and O’Grady objects that the later book holds nineteenth-century Europeans to present moral standards. Scott asks how to critique secularism without rejecting it (2018 interview).", "works": "The Politics of the Veil (2007); Sex and Secularism (2017)", "source_ids": ["w2_scott_laborde_veil_2008", "w2_scott_whitney_lrc_2018", "w2_scott_borderlines_2018", "w2_scott_ogrady_the_2018"], "basis": "source_review", "edge_id": "edge_1125"}, {"person_id": "ranajit_guha", "role": "contributor", "context": "Takes Hegel’s World-history to task as a statist frame that, in Guha’s words as Michael quotes them, reached India as an instrument of the East India Company, and asks historians to learn from literature the historicality of everyday life (2002), as Michael and Chakrabarty read the book; Michael fears the state may be lost “as an object of critique” and finds the argument largely conceptual.", "works": "History at the Limit of World-History (2002)", "source_ids": ["w3_guha_michael_2004", "w3_guha_cup_limit", "w3_guha_chakrabarty_aah_2024"], "basis": "source_review", "edge_id": "edge_1152"}]
settler_colonialism
description Historical inquiry into colonial formations in which newcomers came to stay, acquired land and claimed sovereignty over Indigenous territories. Patrick Wolfe's account of settler colonialism as a structure rather than an event treats invasion as a continuing relation rather than a completed frontier past. Lorenzo Veracini's work and the journal Settler Colonial Studies (2011) distinguished settler colonial from colonial phenomena. Comparative studies of settler capitalism and settler societies, and decolonization-era uses of the term, came earlier. In Canadian historical writing the framework meets an older historiography of dispossession, reserves, gender and the state in the West and British Columbia. It also meets Indigenous political theory of recognition, sovereignty and resurgence. Its uses extend to labour, science, memory and settler identity. The field is contested from several directions. Indigenous scholars ask whether it recentres settlers and displaces Indigenous studies and Indigenous-authored histories. Historians question its structural inevitability and its fit across regions and periods, proposing other modes such as commercial penetration and extractivism. Black studies scholars question how it places slavery. Settler colonialism overlaps with Indigenous history, postcolonial critique and historical geography without being equivalent to any of them.
scope_note A selective research field whose theoretical consolidation came mostly after 2000; the entry covers its roots through 2000 and cites selected later works as readings. Settler-colonial theory, Indigenous political thought, Canadian histories of dispossession and comparative imperial history have different genealogies. Roster inclusion is not adoption of the paradigm: Carter, Perry, Harris and Daschuk wrote earlier or beside it. No single founder, origin or Canadian consensus is asserted. Critics appear as critics. Francophone uptake in the PhD corpus is near zero.
representative_figures_and_works Earlier: Denoon, Settler Capitalism (1983); Stasiulis and Yuval-Davis, eds., Unsettling Settler Societies (1995). Wolfe, Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999) and “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006); Elkins and Pedersen, eds. (2005); Veracini, Settler Colonialism (2010) and Settler Colonial Studies (2011). Read with Coulthard (2014), A. Simpson (2014), L. B. Simpson (2011, 2017), Byrd (2011) and Tuck and Yang (2012). Canadian historiography: Carter (1990, 2008), Harris (2002, 2004, 2020), Perry (2001), Regan (2010), Daschuk (2013), Battell Lowman and Barker (2015), Burrill (2019). Critiques: Macoun and Strakosch (2013), Rowse (2014), Kauanui (2016), Greer (2019), Shoemaker (2019), Konishi (2019), Garba and Sorentino (2020).
patrick_wolfe
description Reads the history of anthropology through settler colonialism. Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology (1999) studies virgin birth in anthropology and in Australian settler discourse, and Victorian anthropology on mother-right, totemism and E. B. Tylor. Later essays address land, labour and race (2001) and the elimination of the native (2006).
scope_note This entry presents the 1999 book. Its introduction (pp. 1–2, read) distinguishes settler from franchise colonies, holds that settler colonies 'were (are) premised on the elimination of native societies' and states that 'invasion is a structure not an event'; the rest of the book is checked at contents and catalogue level. The 2001 and 2006 essays are later readings. He did not originate the term, which has earlier usage (Veracini 2013), and he is not presented as the field's founder.
representative_figures_and_works Patrick Wolfe (1949–2016); Settler Colonialism and the Transformation of Anthropology: The Politics and Poetics of an Ethnographic Event (1999). Later readings: “Land, Labor, and Difference: Elementary Structures of Race” (2001); “Settler Colonialism and the Elimination of the Native” (2006).

