data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/ann_laura_stoler/entry-draft.json:13:    "description": "An American anthropologist of Dutch and French colonial rule whose work moved from the plantation belt of Sumatra to the colonial archive itself. With Frederick Cooper she set an agenda for reading metropole and colony together (1989; Tensions of Empire, 1997). Race and the Education of Desire (1995) asked why 'Foucault's elusive and suggestive treatment of race still remains so marginal to what colonial historians take from him' (2002, p. 141, as quoted by Ottevaere); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002) treated 'colonizer/colonized' not 'as a given' but as 'a historically shifting pair of social categories that needs to be explained' (p. 13). 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002) asked scholars to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject'; Along the Archival Grain (2009) read Dutch records 'along the grain' for 'the pulse of the archive' and 'the habits of a colonial heart'. Dutch reviewers found the pulses hard to identify and Indonesian archives absent. Imperial Debris (2013) and Duress (2016) turned to ruination and 'imperial durabilities'. Gouda: in postcolonial studies 'paying homage to Ann Stoler's work has become de rigueur' (2009).",
data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md:145:**Dawn Ottevaere (Michigan State), review, H-Women, H-Net Reviews, June 2004** (https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=9492; **read in full**). Stoler's words as she quotes them, with pages: a "basic commitment to identifying the political stakes lodged in what is defined as public or private, to studying the quotidian shaping of racialized colonial worlds and their disparate sites of production" (p. 9); the "apparatus that kept potentially subversive white colonials in line" (p. 13); "students of colonialism, anthropologists in particular, have taken the politically constructed dichotomy colonizer/colonized as a given rather than as a historically shifting pair of social categories that needs to be explained" (p. 13); "argues for deeper historical engagement with the range of practices in which racisms were produced and thus with the cultural framing of political categories" (p. 15); "Ethnographies of empire should attend both to changing sensibilities and to sex, to racialized regimes that were realized on a macro and micro scale" (p. 78); on métissage, "the tension between a form of authority simultaneously predicated on incorporating and distancing" (p. 83); "why Foucault's elusive and suggestive treatment of race still remains so marginal to what colonial historians take from him today" (p. 141); chapter 7 "situates memories of domestic service by those who served against the density of the archives around them" (p. 162) and "invites more work on colonial memory itself while making the 'colonial' a subject rather than an assumed category of analysis" (p. 203). Her judgement: "Stoler relies heavily on theory and philosophy, particularly Foucault"; "Like Joan Scott, Stoler advocates the careful evaluation of historically designated categories"; "the strength of this text is not in its stories of colonial life … but in its instructive method"; "The weakness of this book is its inaccessibility to casual readers or undergraduates".
data/extension-2026/period4-wave1-2026-10-23/evidence-archive.md:326:**description** (1,184 characters): "An American anthropologist of Dutch and French colonial rule whose work moved from the plantation belt of Sumatra to the colonial archive itself. With Frederick Cooper she set an agenda for reading metropole and colony together (1989; Tensions of Empire, 1997). Race and the Education of Desire (1995) asked why 'Foucault's elusive and suggestive treatment of race still remains so marginal to what colonial historians take from him' (2002, p. 141, as quoted by Ottevaere); Carnal Knowledge and Imperial Power (2002) treated 'colonizer/colonized' not 'as a given' but as 'a historically shifting pair of social categories that needs to be explained' (p. 13). 'Colonial Archives and the Arts of Governance' (2002) asked scholars to move 'from archive-as-source to archive-as-subject'; Along the Archival Grain (2009) read Dutch records 'along the grain' for 'the pulse of the archive' and 'the habits of a colonial heart'. Dutch reviewers found the pulses hard to identify and Indonesian archives absent. Imperial Debris (2013) and Duress (2016) turned to ruination and 'imperial durabilities'. Gouda: in postcolonial studies 'paying homage to Ann Stoler's work has become de rigueur' (2009)."

