{"id": "pe131_crosby_isenberg_2013", "title": "Andrew C. Isenberg, “From the Periphery to the Center: North American Environmental History,” Global Environment 6, no. 12 (2013): 80–101.", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3197/ge.2013.061205", "citation": "Andrew C. Isenberg, “From the Periphery to the Center: North American Environmental History,” Global Environment 6, no. 12 (2013): 80–101. doi:10.3197/ge.2013.061205.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "Later reading. Full text from environmentandsociety.org (downloaded 2026-10-13 for the canon evidence file); pp. 85–86 and n. 13 re-read. Crossref 10.3197/ge.2013.061205. 'The materialist approach was exemplified by the work of Alfred W. Crosby' (p. 85). 'As Crosby expanded the scope of his analysis, he increasingly depicted people as utterly at the mercy of ecological forces'; he quotes Ecological Imperialism (1986, p. 192, per n. 13) that European colonists 'were seldom masters of the biological changes they triggered' and that 'their role was less often a matter of judgment and choice than of being downstream of a bursting dam'; 'In short, over the course of two decades Crosby's analysis devolved into little more than ecological determinism' (p. 86). The 1986 passage is known here only through this quotation. [Also checked under pe131_richard_white_isenberg_2013: Full text from the Environment & Society Portal, read for canon-evidence-international.md; re-read in that copy at pp. 87–89. p. 87: 'Environmental history emerged as a distinct field in the 1970s and early 1980s when a group of historians, including Wiliam [sic] Cronon, Thomas Dunlap, Carolyn Merchant, Arthur McEvoy, Richard White, and Donald Worster, synthesized the material and cultural approaches to the environment'. pp. 88–89: 'In a response to the essay, Richard White summarized Worster’s argument thusly: “Environmental history has a base (natural history), a structure (production relations or modes of production) and a superstructure (culture and ideology)”. This scheme, White argued, reduced culture to “superstructure in the old vulgar Marxist sense”', n. 21: White, JAH 76 (1990), pp. 1112–1113; 'White and Cronon urged environmental historians to pay more attention to culture'. Crossref 10.3197/ge.2013.061205: Global Environment, pp. 80–101.]"}, "scope_note": "Later reading (2013), labelled as such: the ecological-determinism critique."}
{"id": "pe131_worster_jah_1990", "title": "Donald Worster, “Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History,” Journal of American History 76, no. 4 (March 1990): 1087–1106.", "url": "https://doi.org/10.2307/2936586", "citation": "Donald Worster, “Transformations of the Earth: Toward an Agroecological Perspective in History,” Journal of American History 76:4 (1990): 1087–1106, opening “A Round Table: Environmental History”. doi:10.2307/2936586.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-13", "note": "English JAH text unread: the DOI resolves to the OUP page, which returned 403, and JSTOR returned a bot challenge. Crossref 10.2307/2936586 gives JAH 76:4, first page 1087; Crossref lists the round table's responses: Crosby 'An Enthusiastic Second' (1107), White 'Environmental History, Ecology, and Meaning' (1111), Merchant 'Gender and Environmental History' (1117), Cronon 'Modes of Prophecy and Production' (1122), Pyne 'Firestick History' (1132) and Worster's reply 'Seeing Beyond Culture' (1142). The whole article and notes were read in Portuguese translation ('Transformações da terra', Ambiente & Sociedade 5:2/6:1, 2002–03, pp. 23–44). The English wording quoted in this packet comes from Worster's reprint of the essay in The Wealth of Nature (1993, ch. 4; see pe131_worster_wealth_1993), checked by full-text search. JAH pages are given only where Isenberg 2013 cites them: p. 1091 for 'at once materialist and idealist' and 'the analysis of modes of production as ecological phenomena', both matching the 1993 wording. Worster's reply and the White, Cronon, Crosby and Pyne responses are unread. [Also checked under pe131_leopold_worster_1990: The English original was not seen (JSTOR blocked; no open copy found). Crossref 10.2307/2936586 confirms JAH 76:4 (March 1990), first page 1087. The Portuguese translation was read in the PDF of orgprints eprint 20347 (Worster_transformações.pdf), whose first-page note says the article was first published in the Journal of American History in March 1990. Quoted from the translation: p. 23, 'Há quarenta anos atrás, um homem sábio e visionário, o conservacionista e biólogo da vida selvagem, Aldo Leopold, de Wisconsin, reivindicou uma “interpretação ecológica da História”, pela qual propunha usar as idéias e a pesquisa do emergente campo da ecologia para ajudar a explicar porque os fatos que constituem o passado desenvolveram-se da maneira como os conhecemos'; 'Levou algum tempo para os historiadores darem atenção ao conselho de Leopold, mas finalmente o campo da história ambiental começou a ganhar forma e seus praticantes estão tentando construir a partir de sua iniciativa.' p. 24: 'os fatos no caso são mais complicados do que Leopold poderia ter explorado nos limites de seu ensaio, e eles exigem mais do que uma forma simples de determinismo ambiental, que é o que um leitor menos atento poderia encontrar em seu exemplo'; Kentucky bluegrass 'não era uma espécie nativa, mas uma importação européia', followed by Crosby's 'imperialismo ecológico'. Notes, p. 42: n. 1 cites Leopold, A Sand County Almanac, and Sketches Here and There (1949; reprinted New York, 1987), p. 205; n. 3 says Leopold knew the story was more complex and quotes him (p. 206) that 'we do not even know' where bluegrass came from. English glosses in the atlas are the editor's, not Worster's words. A Bowdoin course guide paraphrasing the essay was seen and not used.] [Also checked under pe131_richard_white_worster_1990: Only the Portuguese translation was read (orgprints eprint 20347 PDF). Note 5 (translation p. 42): 'O melhor esforço para traçar a emergência do campo, pelo menos em uma parte influente do mundo, é o de WHITE, R. “American Environmental History: The Development of a New Historical Field” … White argumenta que o estudo da fronteira e da história do oeste tem sido a influência formativa neste campo. Uma outra fonte de idéias importante, realmente retirada da 