codex
**Accept with fixes.**

1. **`edges[edge_882].source`, `.target`.** The arrow is reversed: its text criticises postcolonial studies, but its endpoints make postcolonial studies the critic. Set `source: "settler_colonialism"` and `target: "post"`. The change to directed critique is supported; retain the Hoxie qualification.

2. **`nodes[settler_colialism].description`; `nodes[settler_colonialism].strands[wolfe_veracini_structure_debate].focus`; `.strands[theory_and_field].focus`.** Co-citation becomes an asserted Wolfe–Veracini dispute. Veracini develops Wolfe’s formula; these readings establish different emphases, not a documented exchange between opponents. Replace “Two debates run through the field…” with: **“Two questions organise this selection: how elimination persists while pursuing its own completion, and how settler-colonial analysis relates to Indigenous sovereignty, refusal and resurgence.”** Explain that self-supersession means the settler project’s attempted elimination of the relation, not successful decolonisation. [Veracini, 2011](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.1080/2201473X.2011.10648799)

3. **`edges[p4B_rel_07].relationship`; `nodes[settler_colonialism].scope_note`.** “The field’s critics answer Wolfe by name” misplaces Kauanui’s criticism of *Wolfe’s reception* onto Wolfe himself. Replace that opening with: **“Critiques address both Wolfe’s model and its subsequent uses: Macoun and Strakosch question structural inevitability; Rowse questions a teleology of elimination; Englert directly challenges elimination as the defining logic. Kauanui defends Wolfe against formulaic citation while rejecting the replacement of Indigenous Studies.”** Treat “Wolfe and his Indigenous critics” as the dataset’s category, not every author’s declared position. [Kauanui](https://csalateral.org/issue/5-1/forum-alt-humanities-settler-colonialism-enduring-indigeneity-kauanui/)

4. **`nodes[environment].strands[conservation_dispossession].title`, `.focus`; `.scope_note`; `.coverage.basis`; `release/README.md`.** “Absorbed” and “rather than renewed from within” exceed the citation measure. Replace the title with **“Conservation, dispossession and neighbouring historiographies.”** Replace the scope-note conclusion with: **“The History PhD citation graph interprets these sampled co-citation neighbourhoods as diffusion into neighbouring historiographies. This does not establish declining scholarly production or the field’s own understanding of its development.”** Mention the founder selection, lift threshold and repeated samples of fifteen seed-citing theses. Replace “shows the consequence” with **“records an associated readership pattern.”**

5. **`nodes[archival_turn].description`, `.representative_figures_and_works`; `edges[p4A_rel_02].relationship`.** The entry’s display over-unifies distinct projects despite its qualified scope note. Begin: **“This editorial grouping brings together distinct inquiries into archives as institutions, practices, sources and objects of interpretation.”** Delete “No opposing side formed”; move the readership finding exclusively into the scope note. Change “Founding texts,” “The turn named,” and “The programme” to **“Earlier works,” “Selected interventions,” and “Later contributions.”** Replace “the text the turn answers” with **“a text explicitly engaged by Cook, Steedman and Burton.”**

6. **`nodes[environment].description`; `.strands[conservation_dispossession].focus`; `edges[p4C_rel_02].evidence_note`; `edges[p4C_rel_41].relationship`.** Cronon’s supervision of Warren and Jacoby does not establish that their books implemented his wilderness argument. The drafts admit that no reader establishes this transmission. Delete “carried the point into the archive” and equivalent causal wording. In the description use: **“Spence (1999) examined Indian removal in the making of national parks.”** Remove Warren and Jacoby from the substantive Indigenous-dispossession edge until an argument-bearing passage or explicit reader supports that claim.

7. **`edges[p4A_rel_25].relationship`; `evidence-archive.md §4f`.** The quotation’s referent is wrong. Christianity, capitalism and whiteness describe the surrounding **world**, not Haiti’s exceptionalist image. Replace the second clause with: **“Bulamah, Goyatá and Pereira read ‘The Odd and the Ordinary’ as challenging an exceptionalist image that isolates Haiti from its surrounding world (2020, paragraph 5).”** [Published introduction](https://www.scielo.br/j/vb/a/sKXrJd6LmyhHBPdgKtSyGpz/)

